{"MINDMAP_V2_NOTES_N3":{"n3-001||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning — 'even ~ too'","sub":"〜だって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だって","patternReading":"だって","coreMeaning":"Even ~; anyone/anything ~; because... (colloquial)","slug":"core","definition":"だって is a **colloquial emphatic particle** placed after a noun or question word. It expresses **'even ~'** or **'even something as extreme as ~'**. The speaker deliberately chooses an extreme or surprising case, then says the conclusion still applies to it. This pattern belongs to conversation, chats, and family talk — **not formal writing**.","nuance":"Compared with 〜でも, だって sounds much **more casual, emotional, and natural**; it often conveys emphasis, a retort, or mild protest. For the same basic meaning, formality decreases: **さえ → でも → だって**. At the start of a sentence, however, だって can mean **'because...'** as an excuse, which is completely different from its mid-sentence use.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"even... too"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"くらい","r":"くらい","m":"to this extent"},{"t":"分かる","r":"わかる","m":"understand"},{"t":"よ","m":"you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even a child can understand this much.","scene":"You reply when an adult says children cannot understand"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"even... too"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"たまに","r":"たまに","m":"sometimes"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休みたい","r":"やすみたい","m":"want to rest"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even I want to take a break sometimes.","scene":"You mildly protest when family always makes you work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-001||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & combined forms","sub":"〜だって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だって","patternReading":"だって","coreMeaning":"Even ~; anyone/anything ~; because... (colloquial)","slug":"forms","definition":"In form, だって attaches **directly** after a noun, question word, or small quantity; no extra particle is needed. With verbs, use **〜たって** (＝ 〜ても), and **never Vる + だって**. だって can also function as a conjunction at the start of a sentence.","nuance":"This form is colloquial and has no separate polite version. For formal speech or writing, use 〜でも. Position is the key distinction: **mid-sentence = particle**, **sentence-initial = conjunction**.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + だって + V","note":"Even N ~; no extra particle."},{"lhs":"疑問詞","rhs":"誰/何/いつ/どこ + だって + V","note":"Anyone/anything/anytime/anywhere ~; works in affirmative and negative sentences."},{"lhs":"数量詞","rhs":"一度/一円 + だって + Vない","note":"Not even one time/yen ~; must be followed by a negative."},{"lhs":"Conjunction","rhs":"だって、〜から / 〜もん","note":"Because... as an excuse; sentence-initial."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"too"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"fail"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everyone fails sometimes.","scene":"Comforting a friend after a mistake"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"one time"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"even... too"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"be late"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"experience"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have never been late, not even once.","scene":"Proudly describing your punctuality"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-001||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & real-life situations","sub":"〜だって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だって","patternReading":"だって","coreMeaning":"Even ~; anyone/anything ~; because... (colloquial)","slug":"usage","definition":"だって has **four main uses**: 1) emphasizing an extreme case — 'even N'; 2) 疑問詞 + だって — 'anyone/anything/anytime'; 3) a small quantity + だって + negative — 'not even one'; and 4) sentence-initial だって — 'because...' as an excuse. The same form changes meaning according to its position.","nuance":"In conversation, だって feels **close, lively, and personally expressive**. Use 4 often appears with 〜もん and sounds like **talking back or whining**, especially among children and close friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"even... too"},{"t":"間違える","r":"まちがえる","m":"make mistakes"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"event"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even teachers make mistakes sometimes.","scene":"Reassuring someone who was criticized too harshly"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","r":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"anytime"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"call"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ね","m":"okay"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Call me anytime, okay?","scene":"A close friend is moving far away"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-001||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜だって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だって","patternReading":"だって","coreMeaning":"Even ~; anyone/anything ~; because... (colloquial)","slug":"compare","definition":"Four patterns are easily confused with だって: **でも / さえ / たって / って**. The key points are: だって is the colloquial alternative to でも; さえ is a formal written form emphasizing surprise at a minimum case; たって is the casual form of 〜ても after Vて; and って is a quoting particle. Remember that sentence-initial だって is an excuse meaning 'because'.","nuance":"Compared with でも, だって is **less formal but more emotional**. Compared with さえ, だって does **not specifically emphasize surprise that even the minimum case qualifies**. Position determines which meaning だって has.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"too"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can do"},{"t":"よ","m":"you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can do it too!","scene":"You push back after someone underestimates you"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜でも","point":"Both can mean 'even,' but でも is neutral in speech and writing; だって is colloquial and emotional. 子供でも分かる / 子供だって分かる."},{"vs":"〜さえ","point":"さえ is formal and emphasizes surprise at a minimum case; だって is casual and conversational. In writing: 子供さえ分かる."},{"vs":"〜たって","point":"だって follows N or a question word; たって follows a verb in て-form (＝ても). 食べるだって❌ → 食べたって⭕."},{"vs":"〜って","point":"って is the spoken form of と/という for quoting, reporting, or naming; だって emphasizes 'even.' 田中って人 vs 田中だって行く."},{"vs":"conjunction だって","point":"When sentence-initial and followed by a reason, it means 'because' as an excuse, not an emphatic particle. だって、眠いんだもん."}]}},"n3-001||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜だって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だって","patternReading":"だって","coreMeaning":"Even ~; anyone/anything ~; because... (colloquial)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include attaching だって directly to a verb or adjective in its basic form instead of using たって/くたって, using だって in writing, and confusing the particle with the sentence-initial conjunction. Remember: **だって attaches directly only to nouns, question words, and quantities**; verbs and adjectives must change to たって/くたって.","nuance":"だって sounds **argumentative and personally emotional**. In an email or report, it can make the sentence seem unprofessional.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"too"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"fail"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everyone fails sometimes.","scene":"Reminding yourself or friends not to feel too much pressure"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べるだって美味しい。 → ✅ 食べたって美味しい。 (Vて + たって; not Vる + だって)","❌ 忙しいだって行く。 → ✅ 忙しくたって行く。 (For い-adjectives, 'even if...' uses 〜くたって＝くても; do not attach だって directly to the adjective)","❌ Essay: 学生だって頑張っている。 → ✅ 学生でも頑張っている。 (Use でも/さえ in writing)","❌ Confusing particle だって with sentence-initial conjunction: だって、頭が痛かった = 'because I had a headache' — not 'even.'"],"compare":null}},"n3-001||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜だって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だって","patternReading":"だって","coreMeaning":"Even ~; anyone/anything ~; because... (colloquial)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: だって is like conversational **'but / too / even'** — it can emphasize a case or give an excuse. Remember this formula: if an extreme case CAN do something, ordinary cases naturally can too.","nuance":"Associate だって with **friends, text messages, and anime**, not with formal workplace writing.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-001||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"〜だって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だって","patternReading":"だって","coreMeaning":"Even ~; anyone/anything ~; because... (colloquial)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed expressions include **誰だって** (everyone), **何だって** (anything), **いつだって** (always), **一度だって〜ない** (not even once), and **だって〜もん** (because..., as an excuse).","nuance":"In JLPT questions, combinations such as 疑問詞 + だって and 否定 + だって are often tested. They are highly conversational, so avoid them in essays.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"anything"},{"t":"だって","r":"だって","m":"too"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can do"},{"t":"よ","m":"you know"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"君","r":"きみ","m":"you"},{"t":"なら","r":"なら","m":"if it is you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can do anything.","scene":"Encouraging a friend who lacks confidence"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-002||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜こそ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜こそ","patternReading":"こそ","coreMeaning":"It is precisely ~; exactly ~ (emphasis and exclusion)","slug":"core","definition":"〜こそ is an emphatic particle that puts one object in an exclusive position: 'X, not anything else.' It is much stronger than は and carries firm affirmative emotion. The speaker rules out all other options and selects exactly what they want to emphasize.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with no fixed level of formality. こそ has emotional weight, so do not overuse it in ordinary descriptions. To simply introduce a topic, use は.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"こそ","m":"precisely"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"始める","r":"はじめる","m":"begin"},{"t":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Now is precisely the time to start studying.","scene":"Telling yourself this after delaying"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"こそ","m":"exactly"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"探して","r":"さがして","m":"looked for"},{"t":"いた","m":"had"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is exactly the book I was looking for.","scene":"Finally finding the right book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-002||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures and forms","sub":"〜こそ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜こそ","patternReading":"こそ","coreMeaning":"It is precisely ~; exactly ~ (emphasis and exclusion)","slug":"forms","definition":"The position of こそ determines its meaning: **after a noun** → 'exactly N'; **after Vて** → a prerequisite; **after だから/から** → a reason. Nが/を + こそ exists in formal writing but is uncommon.","nuance":"Vて + こそ often appears in proverbs and advice. N + こそ and だからこそ are natural in daily conversation. To build momentum, use 今こそ.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + こそ","note":"Emphasizes 'exactly N'—the correct answer among many choices."},{"lhs":"N が/を","rhs":"N が/を + こそ","note":"Extremely rare and formal; the original particle remains before こそ."},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"Vて + こそ","note":"'Only after V...'—a prerequisite for a good result."},{"lhs":"N / なA","rhs":"N/なA + だから + こそ","note":"'Precisely because...'—emphasizes the cause."},{"lhs":"いA","rhs":"いA + から + こそ","note":"'Precisely because it is (adjective)...'—emphasizes the cause."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardship"},{"t":"して","m":"go through"},{"t":"こそ","m":"only then"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"成長する","r":"せいちょうする","m":"grow"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only by going through hardship can people grow.","scene":"A proverb-like reminder to yourself"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"だからこそ","r":"だからこそ","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"truth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"んだ","r":"んだ","m":"I do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because you are my friend, I will tell you the truth.","scene":"Explaining why you must speak frankly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-002||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜こそ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜こそ","patternReading":"こそ","coreMeaning":"It is precisely ~; exactly ~ (emphasis and exclusion)","slug":"usage","definition":"The three main structures with こそ serve three situations: **selecting an answer** (Nこそ), **stating a necessary condition** (Vてこそ), and **emphasizing a reason** (だからこそ). In addition, こちらこそ is a polite reply to thanks or a request in Japanese social interaction.","nuance":"In workplace communication, Japanese speakers often respond with こちらこそ to show humility. 今こそ / 今年こそ are familiar motivational phrases in speeches and can be used to inspire immediate action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","m":"my side"},{"t":"こそ","m":"precisely"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よろしく","m":"favor"},{"t":"お願いします","r":"おねがいします","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am the one who looks forward to working with you.","scene":"Returning a request when meeting a partner"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今年","r":"ことし","m":"this year"},{"t":"こそ","m":"precisely"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"目標","r":"もくひょう","m":"goal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"達成","r":"たっせい","m":"achieve"},{"t":"しましょう","m":"let's"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"This is the year we will achieve our goals!","scene":"New Year's speech to the company"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-002||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrasts and common confusions","sub":"〜こそ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜こそ","patternReading":"こそ","coreMeaning":"It is precisely ~; exactly ~ (emphasis and exclusion)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch untangles patterns often confused with こそ, as well as its three main meanings. The key point: こそ is not the topic particle (は), a limiter (だけ), or a minimum-level marker (さえ).","nuance":"When comparing them, remember: こそ = 'this exact one.' Nearby patterns mean は = topic, だけ = only, and さえ = even/at least.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"こそ","m":"precisely"},{"t":"チャンス","m":"chance"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Now is precisely the opportunity.","scene":"Realizing the decisive moment has arrived"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜は","point":"〜は marks a neutral topic; こそ emphasizes 'exactly, precisely' — 今は忙しい (I am busy now) vs 今こそチャンスだ (now is exactly the opportunity)."},{"vs":"〜だけ","point":"だけ limits scope as 'only'; こそ highlights 'exactly, the standout' — これだけ欲しい (I only want this) vs これこそ欲しかったものだ (this is exactly what I wanted)."},{"vs":"〜さえ","point":"さえ marks a minimum, 'even...' or 'if only'; こそ asserts the exact target — 子供さえ分かる (even a child understands) vs あなたこそ分かるべきだ (you are the one who should understand)."},{"vs":"Vてこそ / だからこそ","point":"Do not mix them up: N+こそ emphasizes a noun, Vてこそ a condition, and だからこそ a reason — 今こそ日本語を勉強する (study Japanese now), 努力してこそ夢が叶う (only effort makes dreams come true), 好きだからこそ厳しい (strict precisely because I care)."}]}},"n3-002||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Errors to avoid","sub":"〜こそ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜こそ","patternReading":"こそ","coreMeaning":"It is precisely ~; exactly ~ (emphasis and exclusion)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often overuse こそ or put it in the wrong position. Remember that こそ calls for an affirmative sentence and comes directly after the word being emphasized. To emphasize a reason, から/だから is required.","nuance":"These errors appear frequently on JLPT questions; avoiding them can earn easy points.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"だからこそ","r":"だからこそ","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"厳しく","r":"きびしく","m":"strictly"},{"t":"する","m":"be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because I care, I am strict.","scene":"A parent explaining discipline"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私こそ来ない。 → ✅ 私こそ来ます。／私は来ません。 (こそ does not suit negative sentences)","❌ これは本こそです。 → ✅ これこそ本です。 (こそ comes directly after the emphasized word)","❌ 勉強したらこそ、上手になる。 → ✅ 勉強してこそ、上手になる。 (Vてこそ = necessary condition)","❌ 好きこそ厳しい。 → ✅ 好きだからこそ厳しい。 (to emphasize a reason, から/だから is required)"],"compare":null}},"n3-002||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜こそ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜こそ","patternReading":"こそ","coreMeaning":"It is precisely ~; exactly ~ (emphasis and exclusion)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a finger pointing straight at one thing: 'THIS exact one, not another.' After a noun, it identifies the answer; after Vて, a condition; after だから, a reason.","nuance":"Remember the three magnets: これこそ, 今こそ, こちらこそ—they appear constantly in tests and daily life.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-002||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜こそ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜こそ","patternReading":"こそ","coreMeaning":"It is precisely ~; exactly ~ (emphasis and exclusion)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases with こそ appear as fixed units in JLPT materials and conversation: 今こそ, これこそ, こちらこそ, だからこそ, and Vてこそ.","nuance":"Among them, こちらこそ is a polite reply, so memorize it as a set phrase.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こちらこそ","r":"こちらこそ","m":"I should say that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よろしく","m":"favor"},{"t":"お願いします","r":"おねがいします","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am the one who looks forward to working with you.","scene":"Returning a greeting when starting work with a partner"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-003||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜さえ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ","patternReading":"さえ","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜さえ is an emphatic particle with two main meanings: (1) emphasizing a **MINIMUM** or **EXTREME** case—“even ~”; and (2) stating the minimum condition needed for a result, often with 〜ば—“as long as ~.” The key point: unlike でも/だって, さえ focuses on the **minimum** (“just X, or even X, is enough for ~”).","nuance":"Used naturally in both speech and writing, from casual conversation to formal contexts—not as register-limited as すら, which strongly favors written language. It can suggest that even the smallest thing is enough, or that a minimum condition is sufficient.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"truth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言わ","r":"いわ","m":"tell"},{"t":"なかった","m":"did not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He did not even tell his family the truth.","scene":"A man hides something important from his family."},{"tokens":[{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何日","r":"なんにち","m":"several days"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"生きられる","r":"いきられる","m":"can survive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as there is water, people can survive for several days.","scene":"A conversation about wilderness survival."},{"tokens":[{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even children know that.","scene":"Rejecting an attempt to hide the obvious."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-003||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and patterns","sub":"〜さえ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ","patternReading":"さえ","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"forms","definition":"The form of さえ depends on its position. After a noun: **N + さえ** (replacing が/を/は). For an extreme subject, **N + でさえ** is common. After another particle, keep the particle and add さえ (にさえ, からさえ…). With verbs: **Vます-stem + さえすれば** (as long as one does ~) or **Vて + さえ + いれば** (as long as one remains ~).","nuance":"〜でさえ is stronger and more formal than 〜さえ, especially in writing. When the noun is a subject such as “teacher” or “parent,” でさえ sounds much more natural than bare さえ.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + さえ","note":"Replaces が/を/は; emphasizes “even N”"},{"lhs":"N (subject)","rhs":"N + でさえ","note":"Stronger than さえ; often used for extreme subjects"},{"lhs":"N + particle","rhs":"N + particle + さえ","note":"Keep the particle to show its role: にさえ, からさえ"},{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"Vます-stem + さえすれば","note":"“As long as one V...”; must include ば"},{"lhs":"Vて-form","rhs":"Vて + さえ + いれば","note":"“As long as one keeps V-ing...”; maintained state"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"言わ","r":"いわ","m":"tell"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will not even tell him.","scene":"Keeping an absolute secret."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ボタン","m":"button"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"押し","r":"おし","m":"press"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"すれば","r":"すれば","m":"if one does"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"機械","r":"きかい","m":"machine"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"動きます","r":"うごきます","m":"works"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you press the button, the machine will run.","scene":"Showing a new employee how to operate a machine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-003||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and contexts","sub":"〜さえ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ","patternReading":"さえ","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Two main situations: (1) **Without ば** → “even ~” for an unexpected or extreme case; (2) **With ば / なければ** → “as long as ~” for a minimum condition. Check whether ば is present to identify the meaning quickly.","nuance":"Natural with friends and in formal writing such as reports and proposals—さえ is not register-limited. だって is the distinctly colloquial, more casual form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"降ら","r":"ふら","m":"fall"},{"t":"なければ","m":"if not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"予定通り","r":"よていどおり","m":"as scheduled"},{"t":"行います","r":"おこないます","m":"hold"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as it does not rain, the match will take place as planned.","scene":"Planning an outdoor event."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ついた","r":"ついた","m":"told"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He even lied to his parents.","scene":"Talking about a dishonest friend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"いて","r":"いて","m":"are with me"},{"t":"くれれば","r":"くれれば","m":"if you would"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"いらない","m":"do not need"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you are by my side, I need nothing else.","scene":"A romantic confession in a film or song."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-003||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing でも・だって","sub":"〜さえ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ","patternReading":"さえ","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish さえ from similar patterns. Key point: **さえ emphasizes the MINIMUM** and often takes ば; **でも/だって** only emphasize an extreme example and do not combine with ば. **さえも** is an emphatic form of さえ, used only for “even,” not “as long as.”","nuance":"さえ and でも are neutral and work in speech and writing; だって is distinctly colloquial and suits casual conversation. The wrong register can make a sentence sound unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こんな","m":"such"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"分かる","r":"わかる","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even children understand a story like this.","scene":"Emphasizing that something is obvious."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"N + でも","point":"でも marks a neutral “extreme example,” not “as long as,” and does not take ば. 子供でも知っている = “Even children know it”—it does not imply that children alone are enough."},{"vs":"N + だって","point":"だって is colloquial and emotional, used in casual conversation. 子供だって知っている sounds natural with friends; 子供さえ知っている is also natural but less emotionally surprised."},{"vs":"〜さえも","point":"さえも is an emphatic form of さえ meaning “even...,” common in writing, and NOT used for “as long as.”"}]}},"n3-003||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜さえ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ","patternReading":"さえ","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors: keeping は/が/を before さえ, putting other particles in the wrong order, forgetting ば when expressing “as long as,” and confusing さえ with だけ (“only”).","nuance":"さえ always expresses an extreme case or minimum condition—quite different from だけ. With particles, は/が/を disappear before さえ, while に/で/から/と stay before it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"言わ","r":"いわ","m":"tell"},{"t":"なかった","m":"did not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I did not even tell my friends.","scene":"A correct sentence emphasizing に before さえ."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼はさえ来なかった → ✅ 彼さえ来なかった (drop は/が/を before さえ; do not keep both)","❌ 友達さえに言わない → ✅ 友達にさえ言わない","❌ 水さえある、生きられる → ✅ 水さえあれば、生きられる","❌ 果物さえ好きです (meaning “I only like fruit”) → ✅ 果物だけ好きです (さえ expresses an extreme/minimum, not the plain limit だけ)"],"compare":null}},"n3-003||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜さえ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ","patternReading":"さえ","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick reminder: さえ has two translations—“Even X...” (surprise) and “As long as X...” (minimum condition, + ば). Memory line: **“With ば, use ‘as long as’; without ば, use ‘even.’”**","nuance":"Picture a lever: さえ is the smallest fulcrum that can still lift a large load. あなたさえいてくれれば evokes pure love in J-pop.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-003||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜さえ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ","patternReading":"さえ","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases common in tests and daily life: **〜さえあれば** (as long as there is), **〜さえすれば** (as long as one does), **〜さえいれば** (as long as one stays), and **〜さえ降らなければ** (as long as it does not rain).","nuance":"あなたさえいてくれれば is a classic romantic phrase in song lyrics; Japanese listeners immediately associate it with unconditional love.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何とか","r":"なんとか","m":"somehow"},{"t":"生きられる","r":"いきられる","m":"can survive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as there is water, we can somehow survive.","scene":"A familiar line about survival skills."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-004||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜さえ（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ（も）","patternReading":"さえも","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜さえ（も） is an emphatic particle centered on the idea of a **minimum or extreme case**. Meaning 1: “even ~” — it presents an unbelievable case to support the conclusion. Meaning 2: “as long as ~” — it states the minimum condition, often with 〜ば. Unlike でも/だって, さえ focuses on the **minimum level** (“if only X, or even X—which is normally insufficient—is enough”).","nuance":"Natural in both casual conversation and formal writing, but clearly **more emphatic** than でも/だって. With an extreme subject, でさえ is often used instead of さえ. It can express surprise, disbelief, or determination in conditional sentences.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Discussing something everyone knows.","tokens":[{"t":"そんな"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"でさえ","m":"even"},{"t":"知っている","r":"しっている","m":"know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even children know that."},{"scene":"Discussing a survival condition.","tokens":[{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何日","r":"なんにち","m":"several days"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"生きられる","r":"いきられる","m":"can survive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as there is water, people can survive for several days."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-004||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜さえ（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ（も）","patternReading":"さえも","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic connection patterns for 〜さえ. After a noun, さえ replaces が/を/は. After other particles, the particle remains before さえ. With verbs, use the Vます stem + さえすれば or Vて + さえいれば.","nuance":"でさえ is commonly used for subjects and adds stronger emphasis. さえも is an even more emphatic variant.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + さえ","note":"Replaces が/を/は; emphasizes “even N”"},{"lhs":"N (subject)","rhs":"N + でさえ","note":"Stronger emphasis; common in writing"},{"lhs":"N + に/から...","rhs":"N + particle + さえ","note":"Keep the particle: にさえ・からさえ"},{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"Vます + さえすれば","note":"As long as you do V..."},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"Vて + さえいれば","note":"As long as you are V-ing..."}],"examples":[{"scene":"Giving machine operating instructions.","tokens":[{"t":"ボタン","m":"button"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"押し","r":"おし","m":"press"},{"t":"さえすれば","m":"just do"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"機械","r":"きかい","m":"machine"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"動きます","r":"うごきます","m":"will run"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just press the button and the machine will run."},{"scene":"Discussing a secret you will not reveal.","tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"言わない","r":"いわない","m":"not tell"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I won’t tell even my friends."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-004||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜さえ（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ（も）","patternReading":"さえも","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Keep the two situations distinct. **Extreme**: no ば in the sentence; さえ means “even.” **Minimum condition**: the sentence has ば/なければ; さえ means “as long as.”","nuance":"さえ is natural in casual speech as well as writing, reports, and presentations. It is less register-restricted than すら, which leans strongly toward written language.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Describing someone hiding the truth from family.","tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"truth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言わなかった","r":"いわなかった","m":"did not tell"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He didn’t even tell his family the truth."},{"scene":"Discussing the condition for holding a game.","tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"降らなければ","r":"ふらなければ","m":"if it does not rain"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"game"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"予定通り","r":"よていどおり","m":"as scheduled"},{"t":"行います","r":"おこないます","m":"will hold"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as it doesn’t rain, the game will go ahead as scheduled."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-004||compare":{"title":"⚖️ さえ vs でも・だって","sub":"〜さえ（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ（も）","patternReading":"さえも","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Three easily confused forms: さえ, でも, and だって. さえ emphasizes a **minimum level** or **unexpected extreme**; でも simply gives a neutral extreme example; だって is casual and emotional.","nuance":"On tests, さえ + ば means “as long as”; without ば it means “even.” でさえ is more literary and emphatic.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Commenting on a difficult question.","tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"でさえ","m":"even"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"does not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even the teacher doesn’t know."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜でも","point":"でも is neutral: it simply gives an extreme example, without minimum emphasis and without ば. Example: 子供でも知っている (Even children know it)."},{"vs":"〜だって","point":"だって is casual and emotional, used only in familiar spoken language. Example: 子供だって知ってるよ."},{"vs":"〜さえ〜ば","point":"さえ〜ば is a separate pattern meaning “as long as,” and requires ば; さえ alone means “even.”"}]}},"n3-004||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜さえ（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ（も）","patternReading":"さえも","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with さえ: confusing its meanings, misplacing particles, forgetting ば, and using it in the wrong context.","nuance":"Notice which particles disappear (は/が/を) and which remain (に/で/から...), and check whether ば is present before choosing the meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Encouraging someone worried about money.","tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何とか","r":"なんとか","m":"somehow"},{"t":"なる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as we have money, we’ll manage somehow."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 水さえあるよ。(intended: “as long as there is water”) → ✅ 水さえあれば、生きられる。","❌ 友達さえに言わない。 → ✅ 友達にさえ言わない。","❌ 彼はさえ来なかった。 → ✅ 彼さえ来なかった。 (Drop は/が/を before adding さえ; do not keep both.)"],"compare":null}},"n3-004||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜さえ（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ（も）","patternReading":"さえも","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: さえ has two meanings linked to the presence of ば. If ば/なければ follows さえ, it means “as long as.” Without it, it means “even.” Picture one finger holding a heavy object: the minimum is enough.","nuance":"Checking quickly for ば is the key to understanding a さえ sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-004||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"〜さえ（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ（も）","patternReading":"さえも","coreMeaning":"Even ~ / As long as ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed expressions with さえ often appear on tests and in conversation. Learning them helps you identify the meaning quickly: 〜さえあれば (as long as there is), 〜さえすれば (as long as you do), and 〜てさえいれば (as long as you keep doing). **あなたさえいてくれれば** is a classic lyric phrase with an affectionate tone, often expressing pure love.","nuance":"All these expressions convey a minimum condition and usually combine with ば, giving a gentle, somewhat formal feel.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"A love confession in a song.","tokens":[{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"さえ","m":"as long as"},{"t":"いて","r":"いて","m":"be here"},{"t":"くれれば","m":"if you would"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"いらない","m":"do not need"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you’re by my side, I don’t need anything else."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-005||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜として · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜として","patternReading":"として","coreMeaning":"As ~, in the role of ~, regard ~ as ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜として expresses the **role, status, or standpoint** of a person or thing. The speaker puts a “role hat” on the subject and views X according to function Y. This common pattern is often translated as “in the role of ~.”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with an objective, unemotional tone. To emphasize a personal viewpoint, use にとって. For extra formality, use としまして.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"として","r":"として","m":"in the role of"},{"t":"働いて","r":"はたらいて","m":"working"},{"t":"いる","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He works as a doctor.","scene":"Talking about someone’s profession"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"留学生","r":"りゅうがくせい","m":"international student"},{"t":"として","r":"として","m":"in the role of"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came to Japan as an international student.","scene":"Sharing why you came to Japan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-005||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜として · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜として","patternReading":"として","coreMeaning":"As ~, in the role of ~, regard ~ as ~","slug":"forms","definition":"〜として has **four main variations**: N + として directly modifies what follows, N + としての + N2 modifies a noun, N + としては adds an evaluation, and N + としても expresses concession. Polite forms such as としまして / と致しまして are also used.","nuance":"All forms must follow a noun. In workplace communication, としまして signals that an official position is about to be stated.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + として","rhs":"V / Adj","note":"Directly modifies the predicate and states the subject’s actual role."},{"lhs":"N + としての","rhs":"N2","note":"Modifies a noun: “N2 as N.”"},{"lhs":"N + としては","rhs":"Comment / evaluation","note":"Judges by N’s role-based standard, often with mild contrast."},{"lhs":"N + としても","rhs":"Conclusion","note":"Even in the role of N, the result remains unchanged."},{"lhs":"N + としまして / と致しまして","rhs":"Formal sentence","note":"Polite / highly honorific; often used in meetings, emails, and speeches."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"指導者","r":"しどうしゃ","m":"instructor"},{"t":"としての","r":"としての","m":"as a"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"感じる","r":"かんじる","m":"feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I feel the responsibility of an instructor.","scene":"Talking about responsibility after receiving a task"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新人","r":"しんじん","m":"new employee"},{"t":"としては","r":"としては","m":"for a new employee"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"well"},{"t":"できて","r":"できて","m":"manage to do"},{"t":"いる","m":"are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a new employee, you’re doing very well.","scene":"A manager praises a new employee"},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","r":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"としても","r":"としても","m":"even as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"それ","r":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"許せない","r":"ゆるせない","m":"cannot forgive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even as a friend, I can’t forgive that.","scene":"Talking about a close friend’s betrayal"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-005||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜として · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜として","patternReading":"として","coreMeaning":"As ~, in the role of ~, regard ~ as ~","slug":"usage","definition":"〜として identifies a role or status in an event, evaluates something by that role’s standard, modifies a noun, expresses concession, or adds formality in business settings. Meaning ① is the most common.","nuance":"Each variation has its own nuance: としては compares with a standard, としても states a hypothetical concession, and としまして is formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"通訳","r":"つうやく","m":"interpreter"},{"t":"として","r":"として","m":"as"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"conference"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"参加した","r":"さんかした","m":"participated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He participated in the conference as an interpreter.","scene":"A partner meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"大学生","r":"だいがくせい","m":"college student"},{"t":"としては","r":"としては","m":"for a college student"},{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"幼すぎる","r":"おさなすぎる","m":"too childish"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a college student, that’s a little too childish.","scene":"Commenting on a college student"},{"tokens":[{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issue"},{"t":"として","r":"として","m":"as"},{"t":"取り上げられた","r":"とりあげられた","m":"was raised"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was raised as an issue.","scene":"Discussing a social issue"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-005||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from にとって・という","sub":"〜として · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜として","patternReading":"として","coreMeaning":"As ~, in the role of ~, regard ~ as ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜として from easily confused patterns にとって and という. The key is: として = actual role, にとって = evaluative viewpoint, and という = definition or name.","nuance":"Learners often confuse として with にとって because both can be translated as “as” or “for.” Remember: if the subject ACTUALLY performs a function → として; if it is only a viewpoint → にとって.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"母親","r":"ははおや","m":"mother"},{"t":"として","r":"として","m":"as"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"育てた","r":"そだてた","m":"raised"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She raised her children as a mother.","scene":"Talking about a mother"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にとって","point":"として: the subject’s actual role. にとって: a person’s viewpoint or perspective. Ex: 彼は医者として働く (he works as a doctor) ↔ 彼にとって仕事は大切だ (work is important to him)."},{"vs":"〜という","point":"として: status or function. という: definition or name. Ex: 先生として話す (speak as a teacher) ↔ 先生という人 (a person called a teacher)."},{"vs":"〜としても","point":"として: states an actual role. としても: concessive “even if...” Ex: 教師として働く (work as a teacher) ↔ 教師としても反対だ (even as a teacher, I oppose it)."}]}},"n3-005||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner mistakes","sub":"〜として · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜として","patternReading":"として","coreMeaning":"As ~, in the role of ~, regard ~ as ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with 〜として include attaching it to a verb, confusing it with にとって, and overusing としては. Golden rule: ACTUAL ROLE → として; FEELING/VIEWPOINT → にとって.","nuance":"The first mistake comes from translating “as” and placing the form after a verb. Always check whether the word before として is a noun.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"として","r":"として","m":"as"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強して","r":"べんきょうして","m":"studying"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am studying Japanese as a student.","scene":"Introducing yourself"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べるとして話します。 → ✅ 食べることは別として話します。","❌ 私としてこの問題は難しいです。（use にとって for a subjective evaluation） → ✅ 私にとってこの問題は難しいです。","❌ 学生としても難しい問題だ。（meaning “difficult for students”; としても means CONCESSION “even as a student...,” unlike としては, an evaluation by the standard） → ✅ 学生としては難しい問題だ。","❌ 私は学生にとって日本語を勉強します。 → ✅ 私は学生として日本語を勉強します。"],"compare":null}},"n3-005||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜として · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜として","patternReading":"として","coreMeaning":"As ~, in the role of ~, regard ~ as ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip for 〜として: picture “putting on a role hat.” When you want to say that someone acts in a specific role, imagine them wearing a hat labeled with that role.","nuance":"として comes from と (with/as) + する (do/regard). The phrase として means “regard as” or “view as.” In business, としまして is the polite version and often signals an official position.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-005||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜として · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜として","patternReading":"として","coreMeaning":"As ~, in the role of ~, regard ~ as ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"〜として appears in many fixed phrases, such as 〜をはじめとして (starting with ~), 〜を中心として (centered on ~), 〜を前提として (on the premise of ~), 〜を基として (based on ~), and 〜をきっかけとして (taking ~ as an opportunity).","nuance":"These phrases are often used in formal writing, reports, and presentations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"はじめとして","r":"はじめとして","m":"starting with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多く","r":"おおく","m":"many"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"社員","r":"しゃいん","m":"employees"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"参加した","r":"さんかした","m":"participated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting with him, many employees participated.","scene":"Describing a company gathering"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-006||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜において／においては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜において／においては","patternReading":"において／においては","coreMeaning":"At/in ~; in the field of ~ (formal written style)","slug":"core","definition":"〜において means **at / in** a context: the location of an event, an era, or a specialized field. It is a **formal written pattern**, common in reports, academic papers, government notices, and speeches. In meaning, it is close to で/に, but much more formal.","nuance":"Avoid it in casual conversation because it sounds stiff. **Typical settings**: administrative and academic writing, formal news. It cannot replace で everywhere—only when で marks a context or scope, not when で marks a means or tool. It often appears with formal verbs such as 開かれる, 行われます, 果たす, 必要だ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"conference"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"において","m":"at"},{"t":"開催された","r":"かいさいされた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The conference was held in Tokyo.","scene":"Reading news or a report about an international conference"},{"tokens":[{"t":"法律","r":"ほうりつ","m":"law"},{"t":"においては","m":"in"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"推定無罪","r":"すいていむざい","m":"presumption of innocence"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"原則","r":"げんそく","m":"principle"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In law, there is a presumption of innocence.","scene":"Explaining a legal concept in an essay"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-006||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & forms","sub":"〜において／においては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜において／においては","patternReading":"において／においては","coreMeaning":"At/in ~; in the field of ~ (formal written style)","slug":"forms","definition":"These are the four main forms of 〜において. All of them **follow a noun**, not a verb.","nuance":"Adding **は** in **においては** emphasizes the scope: “as for in/at N...”. **における** comes only before a noun and modifies it. Adding **も** in **においても** means “even in N...”.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + において","rhs":"V / A / S","note":"Marks the place, time, or field where something occurs."},{"lhs":"N + においては","rhs":"S","note":"Emphasizes the scope: “specifically in N...”"},{"lhs":"N + における","rhs":"N","note":"Modifies the following noun."},{"lhs":"N + においても","rhs":"S","note":"“Even in/at N...”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"スポーツ","m":"sports"},{"t":"における","m":"in the field of"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"実力","r":"じつりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"high"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Japan's strength in sports is high.","scene":"Analysis in a sports article"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issue"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"国際社会","r":"こくさいしゃかい","m":"international society"},{"t":"においても","m":"even in"},{"t":"重要","r":"じゅうよう","m":"important"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This issue is also important in the international community.","scene":"A debate in an academic essay"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-006||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"〜において／においては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜において／においては","patternReading":"において／においては","coreMeaning":"At/in ~; in the field of ~ (formal written style)","slug":"usage","definition":"Meaning 1: LOCATION—the place where a formal event occurs. Meaning 2: TIME/ERA—a historical period or era. Meaning 3: FIELD/SCOPE—a specialized area or aspect of activity. における modifies a noun, while においても emphasizes “even in.”","nuance":"It always carries a formal tone. In JLPT questions, において often appears in reading passages and opening clauses such as 現代においては…. In everyday conversation, use で.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"式典","r":"しきてん","m":"ceremony"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大ホール","r":"だいホール","m":"large hall"},{"t":"において","m":"at"},{"t":"行われます","r":"おこなわれます","m":"will be held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The ceremony will be held in the main hall today.","scene":"An official announcement at a ceremony"},{"tokens":[{"t":"近代","r":"きんだい","m":"modern era"},{"t":"においては","m":"in"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教育","r":"きょういく","m":"education"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"重要性","r":"じゅうようせい","m":"importance"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高まった","r":"たかまった","m":"increased"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In the modern era, the importance of education increased.","scene":"An essay introduction about education"},{"tokens":[{"t":"医療","r":"いりょう","m":"medicine"},{"t":"においては","m":"in"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"患者","r":"かんじゃ","m":"patient"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"同意","r":"どうい","m":"consent"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In medicine, patient consent is necessary.","scene":"A presentation on medical ethics"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-006||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"〜において／においては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜において／においては","patternReading":"において／においては","coreMeaning":"At/in ~; in the field of ~ (formal written style)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish において from で, に, にとって, and に関して. The key point is that において locates a **context, space, or scope**, while the other patterns have different functions.","nuance":"において is a formal version of で/に, but only within a context or scope. にとって marks a viewpoint; に関して marks a topic; において marks the setting in which something occurs.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"科学","r":"かがく","m":"science"},{"t":"においては","m":"in"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"証拠","r":"しょうこ","m":"evidence"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In science, evidence is necessary.","scene":"A sentence in a presentation on research methods"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"で","point":"で: neutral and usable in speech or writing. において: formal, used in writing or speeches—using it for a casual place like a park sounds stiff. 「公園で遊ぶ」(natural) ↔「国際会議において意見を述べる」(formal and appropriate)."},{"vs":"に","point":"に: a specific time or destination. において: the context in which an event occurs. 「東京に行く」↔「東京において会議が開かれる」."},{"vs":"にとって","point":"にとって: from someone's viewpoint. において: within a place, field, or scope. 「私にとって簡単だ」↔「この分野において重要だ」."},{"vs":"に関して","point":"に関して: about a topic. において: the spatial, temporal, or field context. 「環境問題に関して議論する」↔「環境問題において対策が必要だ」."}]}},"n3-006||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜において／においては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜において／においては","patternReading":"において／においては","coreMeaning":"At/in ~; in the field of ~ (formal written style)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with において include attaching it to a verb, using it in casual conversation, using it for transportation or tools, and confusing it with にとって.","nuance":"It sounds most awkward in everyday situations. Ask yourself: Is this a formal text? Is N a noun? Is N a means or tool?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経済","r":"けいざい","m":"economy"},{"t":"においては","m":"in"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"growth"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"求められる","r":"もとめられる","m":"is expected"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In economics, growth is expected.","scene":"A claim in an economic analysis"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べるにおいて → ✅ 食事において (において follows nouns, not verbs)","❌ コンビニにおいてジュースを買った → ✅ コンビニでジュースを買った (use で for casual locations)","❌ 電車において行く → ✅ 電車で行く (use で for transportation)","❌ 私において大切だ → ✅ 私にとって大切だ (use にとって for personal viewpoints)"],"compare":null}},"n3-006||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜において／においては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜において／においては","patternReading":"において／においては","coreMeaning":"At/in ~; in the field of ~ (formal written style)","slug":"memory","definition":"Visualize において as **で/に wearing a suit**—it still means “at/in,” but looks much more formal. The older form's kanji 於 (お) means “at,” so において evokes an official setting or stage.","nuance":"When you see formal writing such as a report, academic paper, or speech, think of において. In conversation, “take off the suit” and use で/に.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-006||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"〜において／においては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜において／においては","patternReading":"において／においては","coreMeaning":"At/in ~; in the field of ~ (formal written style)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common collocations in formal texts and JLPT questions that help you recognize the pattern while reading.","nuance":"These phrases appear in articles, government reports, and JLPT reading passages.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"現代","r":"げんだい","m":"modern times"},{"t":"においては","m":"in"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"インターネット","r":"インターネット","m":"internet"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"欠かせない","r":"かかせない","m":"indispensable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In modern times, the internet is indispensable.","scene":"A sentence in an essay about modern society"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-007||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜をはじめ（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をはじめ（として）","patternReading":"をはじめ（として）","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying many others","slug":"core","definition":"〜をはじめ（として） uses one representative member as the starting point, then expands to the whole group. The N before をはじめ is the group's “star.” Common translations are “starting with ~” and “especially ~.”","nuance":"This pattern sounds formal and is common in speeches, reports, and writing. Unlike など (“and so on”), をはじめ highlights rank: the first person or thing is the most important representative.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全員","r":"ぜんいん","m":"everyone"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"参加した","r":"さんかした","m":"participated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting with the company president, everyone participated.","scene":"Event notice led by the president."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"アジア","m":"Asia"},{"t":"各国","r":"かっこく","m":"various countries"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"文化","r":"ぶんか","m":"culture"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"学んで","r":"まなんで","m":"study"},{"t":"います","m":"am studying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am studying the cultures of Asian countries, starting with Japan.","scene":"Introducing research on Asian cultures."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-007||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & structures","sub":"〜をはじめ（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をはじめ（として）","patternReading":"をはじめ（として）","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying many others","slug":"forms","definition":"There are four main patterns. **N + をはじめ** comes before a full sentence; **N + をはじめとして** is more formal; **N + をはじめとする + N2** modifies a group noun; **N + をはじめとした + N2** is an alternative form.","nuance":"All forms work in polite speech and writing. In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often use など or とか instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + をはじめ","rhs":"、＋ Câu","note":"Name representative N, then mention the other members or activities."},{"lhs":"N + をはじめとして","rhs":"、＋ Câu","note":"More formal than をはじめ."},{"lhs":"N + をはじめとする","rhs":"＋ N2 (tập hợp)","note":"Modifies the noun that follows."},{"lhs":"N + をはじめとした","rhs":"＋ N2","note":"Alternative form of とする."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"フランス語","r":"フランスご","m":"French"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting with English, I can also speak French.","scene":"Showing off language skills at a party."},{"tokens":[{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"をはじめとする","r":"をはじめとする","m":"especially"},{"t":"大都市","r":"だいとし","m":"major cities"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"人口","r":"じんこう","m":"population"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"集まって","r":"あつまって","m":"concentrates"},{"t":"いる","m":"is concentrated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The population is concentrated in major cities, especially Tokyo.","scene":"Sentence from an economic article."},{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"をはじめとして","r":"をはじめとして","m":"starting with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社員","r":"しゃいん","m":"employees"},{"t":"全員","r":"ぜんいん","m":"all"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出席した","r":"しゅっせきした","m":"attended"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting with the company president, all employees attended.","scene":"Reporting on an important meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-007||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜をはじめ（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をはじめ（として）","patternReading":"をはじめ（として）","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying many others","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when introducing a group that has one standout representative. That representative comes directly before をはじめ. など often appears at the end to signal “there are many more.”","nuance":"Sentences with をはじめ often sound ceremonial and suit thank-you speeches, company achievements, and news articles. Avoid it in casual chats with close friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"山田先生","r":"やまだせんせい","m":"Professor Yamada"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teachers"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"参加された","r":"さんかされた","m":"attended (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Many teachers attended, especially Professor Yamada.","scene":"Yamada is the school's most respected teacher."},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"感謝しています","r":"かんしゃしています","m":"am grateful to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am grateful to many people, especially my parents.","scene":"Graduation ceremony thank-you speech."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-007||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜をはじめ（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をはじめ（として）","patternReading":"をはじめ（として）","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying many others","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish をはじめ from similar patterns: **〜など** (an unranked list), **〜だけでなく** (“not only X but also Y”), and the verb **〜をはじめる** (to start doing something).","nuance":"If learners remember only “and so on,” they may overuse など; をはじめ requires choosing the right **representative**. だけでなく always has two clearly contrasted parts.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"各地","r":"かくち","m":"various regions"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"人気","r":"にんき","m":"popular"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is popular throughout Japan, especially in Tokyo.","scene":"Discussing a famous local dish."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜など","point":"など lists “and so on” without ranking; をはじめ highlights the first member as the standout. Example: 日本や韓国など (equal members) vs 日本をはじめ、アジア各国 (Japan is representative)."},{"vs":"〜だけでなく","point":"だけでなく means “not only X but also Y,” with Y often notable or unexpected; をはじめ simply puts X first in a list. 東京だけでなく全国で人気だ stresses nationwide popularity; 東京をはじめ、全国で人気だ presents Tokyo as representative."},{"vs":"〜をはじめる（動詞）","point":"をはじめる is the verb “to start doing” (勉強をはじめる = start studying); をはじめ is a listing pattern meaning “especially.” After をはじめ, look for members of the same group."}]}},"n3-007||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜をはじめ（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をはじめ（として）","patternReading":"をはじめ（として）","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying many others","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three classic mistakes: (1) putting a non-representative member before をはじめ; (2) confusing it with the verb **をはじめる**; (3) using it when there is only one member.","nuance":"Japanese communication is sensitive to rank, so choosing the wrong representative can feel **disrespectful**. Always check: is the first member the “star” of the group?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"韓国","r":"かんこく","m":"South Korea"},{"t":"や"},{"t":"中国","r":"ちゅうごく","m":"China"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"訪れた","r":"おとずれた","m":"visited"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I visited Japan, South Korea, and China, starting with Japan.","scene":"Talking about an Asia trip."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 弟をはじめ、家族で旅行した。 → ✅ 父をはじめ、家族で旅行した。 (Father is the highest-ranking family member.)","❌ 日本をはじめ行きました。 → ✅ 日本をはじめ、韓国や中国を訪れました。 (List members of the same group, such as Asian countries.)","❌ 勉強をはじめ、日本語を勉強しています。 → ✅ 日本語をはじめ、アジアの言語を勉強しています。 (The verb をはじめる ≠ the listing pattern をはじめ.)"],"compare":null}},"n3-007||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜をはじめ（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をはじめ（として）","patternReading":"をはじめ（として）","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying many others","slug":"memory","definition":"Use this visual mnemonic: **X stands at the front of a line** and leads the group behind it. 始め means “beginning,” so をはじめ means “starting with X and bringing in the whole group,” not the verb をはじめる.","nuance":"When you see をはじめ, ask yourself: “Is this a list?” If not, it may be the verb.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-007||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜をはじめ（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をはじめ（として）","patternReading":"をはじめ（として）","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying many others","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases in JLPT questions and everyday life include **家族をはじめ** (“starting with my family”), **日本をはじめとするアジア諸国** (“Asian countries, especially Japan”), and **社長をはじめ、全員が参加** (“starting with the president, everyone participated”).","nuance":"These phrases often appear in reading passages and formal text. Memorizing the whole phrase can help you translate quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"especially"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"友人","r":"ゆうじん","m":"friends"},{"t":"たち"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"感謝","r":"かんしゃ","m":"thanks"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"伝えたい","r":"つたえたい","m":"want to express"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to thank my family and friends, starting with my family.","scene":"Speech at an anniversary ceremony."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-008||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜について／に関して · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜について／に関して","patternReading":"について・にかんして","coreMeaning":"About ~; concerning ~; introducing a topic before its content","slug":"core","definition":"〜について and 〜に関して both mean **about ~ / concerning ~** and are used to **introduce a topic** before presenting information. The difference is **formality**: について is common in both conversation and writing, while に関して is formal and often appears in reports, contracts, and academic writing. When introducing a topic, consider who you are speaking to and the setting.","nuance":"This is a **topic-setting signal** in Japanese: omitting について when opening a topic can sound abrupt. について is neutral and relaxed; に関して has a **broader, more indirect** tone and is mainly used in writing. With close friends or in chats, について is the most natural choice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"将来","r":"しょうらい","m":"future"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"について","r":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話し","r":"はなし","m":"talk"},{"t":"ましょう","r":"ましょう"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's talk about our dreams for the future.","scene":"Inviting a coworker to start a conversation."},{"tokens":[{"t":"環境問題","r":"かんきょうもんだい","m":"environmental issue"},{"t":"に関して","r":"にかんして","m":"concerning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"法律","r":"ほうりつ","m":"law"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"作られた","r":"つくられた","m":"was created"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A new law was created concerning environmental issues.","scene":"Reading news about new policy."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-008||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & structure","sub":"〜について／に関して · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜について／に関して","patternReading":"について・にかんして","coreMeaning":"About ~; concerning ~; introducing a topic before its content","slug":"forms","definition":"Both structures combine directly only with **nouns**. Before a verb: N + について/に関して + V. Before a noun: N + について**の** / N + に関**する** + N. Add は (については/に関しては) to **focus the topic** and set it apart for discussion.","nuance":"Never write 'NについてN' directly — の is required. With に関して + N, do not use の; change it to に関する. については opens a sentence with the meaning 'as for X...'","structure":[{"lhs":"N + について","rhs":"+ V / sentence","note":"About N — most common"},{"lhs":"N + についての","rhs":"+ N","note":"Modifies following noun"},{"lhs":"N + については","rhs":"+ conclusion","note":"Focuses the topic"},{"lhs":"N + に関して","rhs":"+ V / sentence","note":"About N — formal"},{"lhs":"N + に関する","rhs":"+ N","note":"Modifies noun — written style"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"について","r":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話した","r":"はなした","m":"talked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I talked about the trip.","scene":"Telling a friend about yesterday's conversation."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"についての","r":"についての","m":"about"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読んだ","r":"よんだ","m":"read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I read a book about Japan.","scene":"Answering what I did yesterday."},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"に関して","r":"にかんして","m":"concerning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"専門家","r":"せんもんか","m":"specialist"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"相談した","r":"そうだんした","m":"consulted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I consulted a specialist about my health.","scene":"Talking about seeing a doctor for advice."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-008||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜について／に関して · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜について／に関して","patternReading":"について・にかんして","coreMeaning":"About ~; concerning ~; introducing a topic before its content","slug":"usage","definition":"Choose the form by **context**: ① With communication/thought verbs: 話す, 考える, 調べる, 説明する. ② Before **report, email, or notice titles**. ③ Asking opinions: 「〜についてどう思いますか？」. ④ For very formal honorific language (VIP customers, official notices) → **につきまして**. ⑤ To focus the topic, add は.","nuance":"Japanese communication often puts the **topic first, then the content**. In business email, 「〇〇につきまして」 at the beginning signals official content. Using the wrong level of formality (such as に関して with a close friend) sounds awkward.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"件","r":"けん","m":"matter"},{"t":"について","r":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"どう"},{"t":"思い","r":"おもい","m":"think"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"What do you think about this matter?","scene":"Asking a coworker's opinion about an incident."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今回","r":"こんかい","m":"this time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ご注文","r":"ごちゅうもん","m":"order"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"つきまして","r":"つきまして","m":"concerning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご確認","r":"ごかくにん","m":"confirmation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"お願い","r":"おねがい","m":"request"},{"t":"いたします","r":"いたします"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regarding this order, please confirm it.","scene":"Standard B2B email to a business partner."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-008||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with に対して・をめぐって","sub":"〜について／に関して · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜について／に関して","patternReading":"について・にかんして","coreMeaning":"About ~; concerning ~; introducing a topic before its content","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish these immediately: **について** = state a topic; **に対して** = direct something toward a target, often a reaction or criticism; **をめぐって** = revolve around a controversial issue, with verbs such as 議論する and 争う. について and に関して differ only in formality.","nuance":"Although all can sometimes translate as 'about,' に対して requires an **action directed at** a target, をめぐって implies **multiple parties**, while について simply introduces a topic.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"toward"},{"t":"批判","r":"ひはん","m":"criticize"},{"t":"している","r":"している"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is criticizing Japan.","scene":"Discussing an acquaintance's attitude."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"に対して","point":"について marks a topic; に対して directs a reaction or emotion toward a target."},{"vs":"をめぐって","point":"について simply states a topic; をめぐって emphasizes debate or dispute."}]}},"n3-008||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜について／に関して · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜について／に関して","patternReading":"について・にかんして","coreMeaning":"About ~; concerning ~; introducing a topic before its content","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make five mistakes: **①** using について when に対して is needed. **②** using に関して in casual conversation, which sounds excessive. **③** omitting の in the modifier form (❌ について本). **④** confusing it with にとって, which gives a viewpoint-based evaluation. **⑤** opening without setting the topic with について, making the sentence less polite in the workplace.","nuance":"Always check: are you **talking about** something (について), or **directing something toward/evaluating** it (に対して/にとって)? Is the setting formal or casual? Does a noun modifier need の or する?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"について","r":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"confirm"},{"t":"したい"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As for next week's meeting, I have something to confirm.","scene":"Politely opening a question to the boss."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私について大切だ → ✅ 私にとって大切だ (にとって: evaluation from a viewpoint, not a topic)","❌ 環境で話す → ✅ 環境について話す (で marks a place, not a topic)","❌ 日本語について本を読んだ → ✅ 日本語についての本を読んだ","❌ 友達と環境問題に関して話した → ✅ 友達と環境問題について話した (casual conversation uses について)","❌ 会議、確認したいことがあります → ✅ 会議について、確認したいことがあります"],"compare":null}},"n3-008||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜について／に関して · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜について／に関して","patternReading":"について・にかんして","coreMeaning":"About ~; concerning ~; introducing a topic before its content","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **ついて** as a sticker that **sticks to** the topic under discussion; **関** is the kanji in 関係 ('relationship'), emphasizing a **connection** with a field. The formality scale is について (ordinary) → に関して (formal) → につきまして (highly honorific, used in VIP-customer emails).","nuance":"Remember the golden rule: casual conversation → について; reports/contracts → に関して; official emails/notices → につきまして. When unsure, choose について, since it is less awkward than using the wrong formal level.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-008||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜について／に関して · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜について／に関して","patternReading":"について・にかんして","coreMeaning":"About ~; concerning ~; introducing a topic before its content","slug":"phrases","definition":"In JLPT questions and daily life, these fixed phrases are common: **話す・考える・調べる** after について; **本・情報** after についての; **お知らせ・資料・質問** after に関する; and **〜につきましては** at the beginning of formal emails.","nuance":"These combinations are almost **fixed collocations**. In reports, use 〜に関する資料 and 〜に関する調査. In conversation, 〜について話す is the most natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"環境","r":"かんきょう","m":"environment"},{"t":"に関する","r":"にかんする","m":"concerning"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"material"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見てください","r":"みてください","m":"please look at"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please look at the materials related to the environment.","scene":"A manager hands out materials in a meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-009||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜に対して／に対する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に対して／に対する","patternReading":"にたいして／にたいする","coreMeaning":"Toward ~ (directed action/feeling); in contrast to ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜に対して expresses the **direction of an action or feeling** toward a specific target (a person, organization, or thing), often translated as “toward ~” or “to ~.” It can also show **contrast/opposition** between A and B, translated as “whereas A, B.” This pattern combines with nouns; に対する + N modifies a noun.","nuance":"Usable in both speech and writing, but in conversation it often sounds more **formal or attitude-focused** than the particle に. に対して does not normally express subjective praise or criticism; with the contrast meaning, the sentence must contain **two clearly opposing clauses**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"toward"},{"t":"失礼","r":"しつれい","m":"rude"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"say"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ended up saying something rude to my teacher.","scene":"Regretting having spoken disrespectfully to a teacher."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今年","r":"ことし","m":"this year"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"黒字","r":"くろじ","m":"profit"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"whereas"},{"t":"去年","r":"きょねん","m":"last year"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"赤字","r":"あかじ","m":"loss"},{"t":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This year was profitable, whereas last year ran at a loss.","scene":"Comparing two years in a company financial report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-009||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & structures","sub":"〜に対して／に対する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に対して／に対する","patternReading":"にたいして／にたいする","coreMeaning":"Toward ~ (directed action/feeling); in contrast to ~","slug":"forms","definition":"All forms of に対して combine with a **noun**. ～に対して comes before a verb or adjective describing an action or attitude directed toward the target. ～に対する comes before a noun to modify it. ～のに対して connects two contrasting clauses.","nuance":"Use に対しては to highlight “this particular case of X” as a topic. Use に対する instead of に対しての in formal writing; に対しての still occurs in conversation, but に対する is more natural before a noun.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に対して + V / A","note":"Direct an action/feeling toward N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に対する + N2","note":"N2 toward N; noun modifier"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に対しては + S","note":"Emphasize N specifically"},{"lhs":"Aい/V + の / N・Aな + なの","rhs":"に対して B","note":"Contrast: whereas A, B (N/Aな needs な before の)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"toward"},{"t":"優しい","r":"やさしい","m":"kind"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Be kind to children.","scene":"Talking about how to treat children."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customers"},{"t":"に対する","r":"にたいする","m":"for"},{"t":"サービス","r":"サービス","m":"service"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"充実","r":"じゅうじつ","m":"extensive"},{"t":"している","m":"is provided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The customer service is comprehensive.","scene":"A hotel highlights its full range of guest services."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-009||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜に対して／に対する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に対して／に対する","patternReading":"にたいして／にたいする","coreMeaning":"Toward ~ (directed action/feeling); in contrast to ~","slug":"usage","definition":"The main use is showing **directed impact**: the speaker highlights which target receives an action, reaction, or attitude. The second use is **contrast**, joining two clauses with opposing content, especially in writing. It can also express a ratio: “X compared with Y.”","nuance":"For reactions, it often appears with verbs such as 反対する・賛成する・応える・答える. With the contrast meaning, two opposing clauses are required; with only one clause, it means direction toward a target.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意見","r":"いけん","m":"opinion"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"toward"},{"t":"賛成","r":"さんせい","m":"agreement"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cannot agree with her opinion.","scene":"Disagreeing with a coworker’s proposal in a meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"兄","r":"あに","m":"older brother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"背","r":"せ","m":"height"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"tall"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"whereas"},{"t":"弟","r":"おとうと","m":"younger brother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"低い","r":"ひくい","m":"short"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The older brother is tall, whereas the younger brother is short.","scene":"Commenting on the brothers’ different heights."},{"tokens":[{"t":"収入","r":"しゅうにゅう","m":"income"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"compared with"},{"t":"支出","r":"ししゅつ","m":"expenses"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Expenses are high compared with income.","scene":"Discussing monthly spending that exceeds income."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-009||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜に対して／に対する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に対して／に対する","patternReading":"にたいして／にたいする","coreMeaning":"Toward ~ (directed action/feeling); in contrast to ~","slug":"compare","definition":"This comparison branch helps distinguish に対して from common related patterns. The key difference is: に対して means “directed toward / the target receiving an action,” while について means “the topic being discussed.” に is similar, but に対して adds **emphasis on attitude or contrast**.","nuance":"In formal writing, に対して is often preferred over に when clarifying the affected target. に対する cannot express the contrast meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"について","r":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Talk about the teacher.","scene":"Telling a story related to a teacher."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"について","point":"について = topic “talk ABOUT X”; に対して = target “talk TO X.” 先生に対して話す = talk to the teacher; 先生について話す = talk about the teacher."},{"vs":"に","point":"に is neutral and natural; に対して emphasizes the attitude or target. 先生に質問する is more natural than 先生に対して質問する."},{"vs":"に反して","point":"に反して = contrary to / against expectations, wishes, or rules, with strong opposition; に対して = directed toward a target or neutral contrast."},{"vs":"にとって","point":"にとって = evaluates something from X’s viewpoint; に対して = the subject’s action or attitude is directed toward X."}]}},"n3-009||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜に対して／に対する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に対して／に対する","patternReading":"にたいして／にたいする","coreMeaning":"Toward ~ (directed action/feeling); in contrast to ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: overusing に対して, which makes sentences stiff; confusing it with について when saying “about a topic”; and using に対しての instead of に対する before a noun. For contrast, make sure both opposing clauses are present.","nuance":"In everyday conversation, に is usually more natural. Use に対して when emphasizing an attitude or in formal writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"質問","r":"しつもん","m":"question"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"した","m":"asked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked the teacher a question.","scene":"Speaking naturally with friends."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生に対して質問した → ✅ 先生に質問した (more natural)","❌ 日本の文化に対して話す → ✅ 日本の文化について話す (the topic, not the target)","❌ 外国人に対してのサービス → ✅ 外国人に対するサービス (use に対する before a noun)","❌ 彼は元気だ。に対して、弟は疲れている。 → ✅ 彼は元気なのに対して、弟は疲れている。 (の is needed to connect clauses)"],"compare":null}},"n3-009||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜に対して／に対する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に対して／に対する","patternReading":"にたいして／にたいする","coreMeaning":"Toward ~ (directed action/feeling); in contrast to ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine 対 as two people standing face to face. に対して is like “shooting” your action or feeling toward the target. With the contrast meaning, picture **two arrows pointing in opposite directions** in clauses A and B.","nuance":"This trick helps you decide quickly: one direction of impact → “toward.” Two opposing subjects → “in contrast.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-009||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"〜に対して／に対する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に対して／に対する","patternReading":"にたいして／にたいする","coreMeaning":"Toward ~ (directed action/feeling); in contrast to ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"Remembering these fixed expressions helps you use に対して naturally. In the workplace, one common phrase is お客様に対して誠実に対応する (respond sincerely to customers).","nuance":"Expressions with emotion verbs often sound formal and are less common in casual conversation. に対する措置・対応 frequently appears in administrative notices.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"顧客","r":"こきゃく","m":"customer"},{"t":"に対して","r":"にたいして","m":"toward"},{"t":"誠実","r":"せいじつ","m":"sincere"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"対応","r":"たいおう","m":"respond"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Respond sincerely to customers.","scene":"A customer-service principle in a Japanese company."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-010||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","patternReading":"をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a turning point / take the opportunity ~ to cause change","slug":"core","definition":"〜をきっかけに（／をきっかけとして）describes event X as a turning point that opens the way to a new change or decision Y. きっかけ basically means a “spark” or “catalyst”—X sets Y in motion. This is not a simple cause expression; it highlights the starting point of lasting change. It is often used when describing an experience that changed a person, habit, or life.","nuance":"Usable in both speech and writing; をきっかけとして is more formal and common in journalism and announcements. The following clause often uses 〜ようになる、〜始める、or 〜変わる, showing a lasting result rather than a momentary action. きっかけ is neutral: it works for positive changes (quitting smoking) and negative ones (depression, bankruptcy), as long as X is a true TURNING POINT, not an immediate action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"気をつける","r":"きをつける","m":"watch"},{"t":"ように","m":"as a habit"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After getting sick, I began paying more attention to my health.","scene":"Describing a lifestyle change after an illness."},{"tokens":[{"t":"転職","r":"てんしょく","m":"job change"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強し始めた","r":"べんきょうしはじめた","m":"started studying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I took changing jobs as an opportunity to start studying English.","scene":"Telling a friend why you started studying English."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-010||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","patternReading":"をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a turning point / take the opportunity ~ to cause change","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern consists of N + をきっかけに and variants: をきっかけとして (formal) and をきっかけにして (casual). When the turning point is a completed action, use Vたこと + をきっかけに; in conversation, this can shorten to Vたのをきっかけに. The following clause gives the resulting change. This form is common when describing why someone began studying a language or changed jobs.","nuance":"をきっかけとして sounds formal and often describes social or historical events. をきっかけにして is common in conversation. Vたことをきっかけに is especially common in self-introductions because the turning point is a specific experience or action.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"+ をきっかけに / をきっかけとして / をきっかけにして","note":"N is a specific event: 病気, 転職, 出会い, 結婚..."},{"lhs":"Vたこと","rhs":"+ をきっかけに / をきっかけとして","note":"Use a whole clause as the turning point: 見たことをきっかけに"},{"lhs":"Vたの","rhs":"+ をきっかけに","note":"Natural spoken variant replacing こと"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"+ がきっかけで + Result","note":"Common parallel form: あの出会いがきっかけで..."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本映画","r":"にほんえいが","m":"Japanese film"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見た","r":"みた","m":"watched"},{"t":"こと","m":"act of"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"興味","r":"きょうみ","m":"interest"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持った","r":"もった","m":"developed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Watching a Japanese film sparked my interest in Japanese.","scene":"Introducing why you study Japanese."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"事件","r":"じけん","m":"incident"},{"t":"をきっかけとして","r":"をきっかけとして","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"法律","r":"ほうりつ","m":"law"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"改正された","r":"かいせいされた","m":"was amended"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This incident became the turning point for the law to be amended.","scene":"A sentence from a current-affairs report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-010||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","patternReading":"をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a turning point / take the opportunity ~ to cause change","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when event or experience X (illness, a trip, a meeting, marriage, etc.) becomes the starting point for change Y. Asking about someone’s “turning point” is common: 日本語を勉強するきっかけは何ですか. The pattern often combines with 〜ようになる to emphasize a process of change.","nuance":"The following clause describes a lasting new result—a habit, career, or way of life. Do not use it for a one-time action. In speech, Vたことをきっかけに and 〜がきっかけで are popular; をきっかけとして is more literary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"引っ越し","r":"ひっこし","m":"moving"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"wake up"},{"t":"ように","m":"as a habit"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After moving, I developed a habit of getting up early.","scene":"Discussing a lifestyle change with a coworker."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"誘われた","r":"さそわれた","m":"was invited"},{"t":"こと","m":"act of"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ジム","r":"じむ","m":"gym"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"通い始めた","r":"かよいはじめた","m":"started going regularly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Being invited by a friend became the reason I started going to the gym.","scene":"Explaining why you started exercising."},{"tokens":[{"t":"震災","r":"しんさい","m":"earthquake disaster"},{"t":"をきっかけとして","r":"をきっかけとして","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ボランティア","r":"ぼらんてぃあ","m":"volunteer"},{"t":"活動","r":"かつどう","m":"activities"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"広がった","r":"ひろがった","m":"spread"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The earthquake disaster became a turning point, and volunteer activities spread.","scene":"A comment quoted in a newspaper article."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-010||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compared with similar patterns","sub":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","patternReading":"をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a turning point / take the opportunity ~ to cause change","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜をきっかけに with patterns that can be confused with it in the area of “cause and opportunity for change.” The focus is: をきっかけに highlights a turning point starting change; ために states cause and effect; おかげで/せいで express positive or negative evaluation; を機に is formal and lighter; ことから explains a reason or origin. Knowing each focus helps you choose naturally.","nuance":"Choose をきっかけに when X is a catalyst starting change; ために for cause and effect; おかげで/せいで for positive or negative evaluation; を機に for a formal, mild “on the occasion of”; and ことから for explaining an origin or name.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"タバコ","r":"たばこ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やめた","r":"やめた","m":"quit"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Getting married became the turning point that made me quit smoking.","scene":"A friend proudly says they quit smoking."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ために（原因）","point":"ために states cause and effect; をきっかけに marks a turning point starting change: 病気のために働けない (cannot work because of illness) ≠ 病気をきっかけに生活を見直した (used illness as a catalyst)."},{"vs":"〜おかげで / 〜せいで","point":"おかげで/せいで assign positive/negative value to a cause; をきっかけに is NEUTRAL and only highlights the starting point, so it works for both positive and negative changes."},{"vs":"〜を機に","point":"Both can mean “on the occasion of,” but を機に is formal, written, and lighter; をきっかけに emphasizes a “catalyst causing major change.”"},{"vs":"〜ことから","point":"ことから explains a reason or origin, often for a name or definition; をきっかけに describes a turning point in time."}]}},"n3-010||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","patternReading":"をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a turning point / take the opportunity ~ to cause change","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with 〜をきっかけに include confusing it with ために, using it for a one-time action, mixing the particle pairs を…に and が…で, and forgetting たこと when the turning point is a verb. Mastering these prevents grammar errors on tests and in conversation.","nuance":"This pattern highlights the start of a change, not its deeper cause. For a direct cause such as 雨が降ったので..., another pattern is more natural. きっかけ is neutral and works with either positive or negative results.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大病","r":"たいびょう","m":"serious illness"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生活習慣","r":"せいかつしゅうかん","m":"lifestyle habits"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見直した","r":"みなおした","m":"reviewed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I used a serious illness as a turning point and reviewed my lifestyle habits.","scene":"Describing a lifestyle change after serious illness."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降ったのをきっかけに、傘を買った。 → ✅ 雨が降ったので、傘を買った。 (Buying an umbrella is a one-time action, not lasting change.)","❌ 病気のために、働き方を見直した。（sounds forced; no clear ACTIVE turning point） → ✅ 病気をきっかけに、働き方を見直した。（clearly a catalyst for an intentional change）","❌ 病気がきっかけに、生活を見直した。 → ✅ 病気をきっかけに、生活を見直した。（or 病気がきっかけで — を goes with に, が with で; do not mix them）","❌ 日本へ行くをきっかけに、日本語を勉強した。 → ✅ 日本へ行ったことをきっかけに、日本語を勉強した。 (Before をきっかけに use N or Vたこと, not the dictionary form.)"],"compare":null}},"n3-010||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","patternReading":"をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a turning point / take the opportunity ~ to cause change","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: きっかけ = a “spark”—a small stone rolls and creates an avalanche. Imagine a small event X triggering a chain of major changes Y. When you encounter a life-changing decision, ask yourself: “What was the catalyst that pushed me?” That is きっかけ.","nuance":"This memory aid helps you choose the pattern: if there is a “catalyst,” use をきっかけに; if it is merely an “occasion,” use を機に.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-010||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","patternReading":"をきっかけに／をきっかけとして","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a turning point / take the opportunity ~ to cause change","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common phrases with をきっかけに in conversation and JLPT questions, helping learners respond naturally. Patterns such as 〜との出会いをきっかけに, 〜での経験をきっかけに, and 〜の事故をきっかけに appear often. Interviews also commonly ask きっかけは何ですか, answered with 〜をきっかけに〜ようになりました. Learning these chunks makes your sentences more natural.","nuance":"These phrases often appear in reading passages and conversations, especially when describing the past. In speech, がきっかけで is also very common.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"出会い","r":"であい","m":"meeting"},{"t":"をきっかけに","r":"をきっかけに","m":"as a turning point"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人生","r":"じんせい","m":"life"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"変わった","r":"かわった","m":"changed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Meeting him became the turning point that changed my life.","scene":"Describing someone important who changed you."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-011||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜に加えて／に加え · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に加えて／に加え","patternReading":"にくわえて／にくわえ","coreMeaning":"In addition, besides ~, there is also ~ (adding in the same direction)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に加えて expresses **adding one more factor** to something that already exists, like addition A + B. The added factor (B) must be **in the same direction** as what already exists (A): both positive or both negative. The tone is neutral, without stressing that B is surprising or extreme.","nuance":"Usable in both speech and writing, with moderate formality. Compared with だけでなく/ばかりか, に加えて simply **adds factors together**; it does not imply that “A is insufficient” or that “B is especially surprising.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"に加えて","r":"にくわえて","m":"in addition"},{"t":"風","r":"かぜ","m":"wind"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"強く","r":"つよく","m":"strong"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides the rain, the wind also became stronger.","scene":"Watching heavy rain and strong winds."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"美しさ","r":"うつくしさ","m":"beauty"},{"t":"に加え","r":"にくわえ","m":"in addition"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"intelligence"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides her beauty, she is also intelligent.","scene":"Commenting on a friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-011||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜に加えて／に加え · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に加えて／に加え","patternReading":"にくわえて／にくわえ","coreMeaning":"In addition, besides ~, there is also ~ (adding in the same direction)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern is most commonly used with **nouns**: N + に加えて + clause. With verbs or adjectives, nominalize with の／こと before に加えて (Vる/Vた + の／ことに加えて), though this is less common. **に加え** is a shortened form of に加えて, often used in documents and news.","nuance":"Connect noun A directly to に加えて; the following part must clearly state added factor B. To modify a noun, use **に加えての + N**.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に加えて + S","note":"Common in speech and writing"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に加え + S","note":"Shorter, formal, journalistic"},{"lhs":"V（普通形/た形）","rhs":"の／ことに加えて + S","note":"Less common; nouns preferred"},{"lhs":"N + に加えて","rhs":"の + N","note":"Modifies a noun: 雨に加えての強風"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"製品","r":"せいひん","m":"product"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"品質","r":"ひんしつ","m":"quality"},{"t":"に加えて","r":"にくわえて","m":"in addition"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"価格","r":"かかく","m":"price"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"魅力的","r":"みりょくてき","m":"attractive"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides its quality, this product also has an attractive price.","scene":"Introducing a product to customers."},{"tokens":[{"t":"少子化","r":"しょうしか","m":"low birthrate"},{"t":"に加え","r":"にくわえ","m":"in addition"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"高齢化","r":"こうれいか","m":"aging population"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"進んで","r":"すすんで","m":"progressing"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides the low birthrate, population aging is also progressing.","scene":"News about Japanese society."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-011||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜に加えて／に加え · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に加えて／に加え","patternReading":"にくわえて／にくわえ","coreMeaning":"In addition, besides ~, there is also ~ (adding in the same direction)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜に加えて groups two events pointing in the same direction to give an overall evaluation. With negative problems, it suggests that “things get even worse”; with positive points, it suggests that “the qualities reinforce each other.”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, but **に加え** (without て) sounds more formal and often appears in news and reports. Japanese media often uses it for “double trouble,” such as 物価高に加えて賃金低下.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"に加えて","r":"にくわえて","m":"in addition"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"地震","r":"じしん","m":"earthquake"},{"t":"まで","m":"even"},{"t":"起きた","r":"おきた","m":"occurred"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides the typhoon, an earthquake also occurred.","scene":"Hearing disaster news."},{"tokens":[{"t":"才能","r":"さいのう","m":"talent"},{"t":"に加えて","r":"にくわえて","m":"in addition"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"あって","r":"あって","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides his talent, his hard work helped him succeed.","scene":"Commenting on a successful young man."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-011||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Nuance comparison","sub":"〜に加えて／に加え · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に加えて／に加え","patternReading":"にくわえて／にくわえ","coreMeaning":"In addition, besides ~, there is also ~ (adding in the same direction)","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns can mean “besides A, also B,” but their **nuances differ**. に加えて is neutral addition; だけでなく stresses that A is insufficient; ばかりか stresses that B escalates surprisingly; のほかに simply lists items neutrally.","nuance":"In writing or presentations, choose **に加え** for a formal tone; in conversation, use だけでなく to stress “not enough”; use ばかりか for criticism or surprise.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"能力","r":"のうりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"に加えて","r":"にくわえて","m":"in addition"},{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides ability, there is also experience.","scene":"Evaluating a job applicant."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜だけでなく","point":"だけでなく stresses “A is not enough; B is also needed”; に加えて simply adds B neutrally. E.g.: 雨に加えて風も… / 雨だけでなく風も…"},{"vs":"〜ばかりか","point":"ばかりか: B is more surprising/extreme than A, with escalation; に加えて: B is an ordinary addition. E.g.: 雨ばかりか嵐まで…"},{"vs":"〜のほかに","point":"のほかに simply lists “besides A” without accumulation; に加えて stresses the combined effect of A + B."}]}},"n3-011||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜に加えて／に加え · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に加えて／に加え","patternReading":"にくわえて／にくわえ","coreMeaning":"In addition, besides ~, there is also ~ (adding in the same direction)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes by pairing **contrasting** clauses, omitting B, or placing a verb/adjective directly before に加えて. Remember that A and B must point in the same direction, and a main clause must follow に加えて.","nuance":"When A and B contrast, use が / のに / にもかかわらず; に加えて requires them to point in the same direction.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"に加えて","r":"にくわえて","m":"in addition"},{"t":"雷","r":"かみなり","m":"thunder"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"鳴った","r":"なった","m":"rumbled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides the rain, thunder also rumbled.","scene":"A thunderstorm."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨に加えて、晴れた。 → ✅ 雨に加えて、風も強くなった。 (A and B must point in the same negative direction)","❌ 彼は頭がいいに加えて、努力した。 → ✅ 彼は頭のよさに加えて、努力もした。 (頭がいい must become the noun 頭のよさ before に加えて; if keeping a verb/adjective, use Vる/Vた+ことに加えて)","❌ 雨に加えて。 → ✅ 雨に加えて風も強い。 (a clear B clause is required)"],"compare":null}},"n3-011||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜に加えて／に加え · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に加えて／に加え","patternReading":"にくわえて／にくわえ","coreMeaning":"In addition, besides ~, there is also ~ (adding in the same direction)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember 〜に加えて, break down 加(くわえる): it means “to add,” like addition in math. When you see this pattern, picture a plus sign (+) joining factors A and B. Mnemonic: “A is already there; B ADDS on in the same direction,” which recalls both the meaning and the key condition that A and B must point the same way.","nuance":"The mnemonic highlights “adding” (加) and “same direction” to prevent using the pattern with conflicting factors.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-011||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜に加えて／に加え · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に加えて／に加え","patternReading":"にくわえて／にくわえ","coreMeaning":"In addition, besides ~, there is also ~ (adding in the same direction)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in exams and daily life include: “Aに加えてBも,” “Aに加えB (written),” and “Aに加えてのB.”","nuance":"In formal Japanese, the form **に加え** makes the sentence shorter and more polished.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"給与","r":"きゅうよ","m":"salary"},{"t":"に加えて","r":"にくわえて","m":"in addition"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"住宅手当","r":"じゅうたくてあて","m":"housing allowance"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"is paid"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides the salary, a housing allowance is also provided.","scene":"Hearing about company benefits."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-013||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜にわたって／にわたる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にわたって／にわたる","patternReading":"にわたって・にわたる","coreMeaning":"Spanning a long time or wide scope, emphasizing scale","slug":"core","definition":"〜にわたって／にわたる describes an action or state that continues throughout a long period, or covers a broad area, field, or scope. The period or scope must be sufficiently large; this pattern is not used for short periods or small areas.","nuance":"Formal style, more common in writing than speech: news, reports, and official notices. In daily conversation, Japanese speakers often use ずっと or 〜間ずっと instead. にわたって emphasizes large scale and continuity without interruption.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"10年","r":"じゅうねん","m":"10 years"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"throughout"},{"t":"研究","r":"けんきゅう","m":"research"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"続けた","r":"つづけた","m":"continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He continued his research for ten years.","scene":"Introducing a professor with a long research career."},{"tokens":[{"t":"全国","r":"ぜんこく","m":"nationwide"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"across"},{"t":"大雨","r":"おおあめ","m":"heavy rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降った","r":"ふった","m":"fell"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Heavy rain fell across the entire country.","scene":"A weather report warning of widespread heavy rain."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-013||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"〜にわたって／にわたる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にわたって／にわたる","patternReading":"にわたって・にわたる","coreMeaning":"Spanning a long time or wide scope, emphasizing scale","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the connecting forms of the pattern. The N before it is always a noun indicating time, scope, a field, or a number of occasions.","nuance":"In terms of formality, にわたり, the continuative form often seen in writing and news, is more formal than にわたって. にわたる/にわたった are not simply more formal; they modify a noun and cannot end a sentence. A noun must follow.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (期間・時間)","rhs":"にわたって + V","note":"An action continues throughout a long period. Example: 3年にわたって"},{"lhs":"N (範囲・地域)","rhs":"にわたって + V","note":"A broad geographical scope. Example: 全国にわたって"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にわたる + N2","note":"Modifies a noun; no て. Example: 長年にわたる努力"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にわたった + N","note":"Past form used as a modifier. Example: 3日間にわたった会議"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にわたり + V/N","note":"More formal written form than にわたって."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"工事","r":"こうじ","m":"construction"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"3年","r":"さんねん","m":"three years"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"lasting"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"was carried out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This construction project was carried out over three years.","scene":"An announcement about a project completed after three years."},{"tokens":[{"t":"長年","r":"ながねん","m":"many years"},{"t":"にわたる","r":"にわたる","m":"long-standing"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"実","r":"み","m":"fruit"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"結んだ","r":"むすんだ","m":"bore"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Years of effort finally paid off.","scene":"Emphasizing success achieved through long-term effort."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-013||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜にわたって／にわたる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にわたって／にわたる","patternReading":"にわたって・にわたる","coreMeaning":"Spanning a long time or wide scope, emphasizing scale","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main situations: ① extended time, such as 3年 or 長期間; ② broad space, such as 全国 or 広範囲; and ③ many occasions or fields, such as 複数回 or 多岐. All emphasize something large, long, or wide.","nuance":"It is uncommon in speech but frequent in formal writing. When you see “for X” on a test, check whether X represents a sufficiently large period or scope.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"長期","r":"ちょうき","m":"long-term"},{"t":"にわたる","r":"にわたる","m":"long-running"},{"t":"交渉","r":"こうしょう","m":"negotiations"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"末","r":"すえ","m":"end"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"合意","r":"ごうい","m":"agreement"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"達した","r":"たっした","m":"was reached"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After lengthy negotiations, an agreement was reached.","scene":"A report on the end of trade negotiations."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"全6科目","r":"ぜんろっかもく","m":"all six subjects"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"across"},{"t":"実施される","r":"じっしされる","m":"will be administered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This exam covers all six subjects.","scene":"An official notice about the exam's subject coverage."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-013||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: にわたって and 3 patterns","sub":"〜にわたって／にわたる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にわたって／にわたる","patternReading":"にわたって・にわたる","coreMeaning":"Spanning a long time or wide scope, emphasizing scale","slug":"compare","definition":"This section distinguishes 〜にわたって from three easily confused patterns: 〜にかけて, 〜を通じて, and 〜の間に. Understanding the key difference helps you choose the right grammar pattern for the context.","nuance":"The deciding factor is the focus: にわたって emphasizes the ENTIRE period or scope; にかけて emphasizes two endpoints A→B; を通じて emphasizes continuity or a medium; の間に is neutral and does not emphasize scale.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"地域","r":"ちいき","m":"area"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"5年","r":"ごねん","m":"five years"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"for"},{"t":"支援","r":"しえん","m":"support"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"続けられた","r":"つづけられた","m":"was maintained"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This area received continuous support for five years.","scene":"Contrast: にわたって means the whole span, without interruption."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にかけて","point":"にかけて: from point A to point B, with two endpoints. にわたって: the entire period or scope. Example: 春から夏にかけて ↔ 5年にわたって."},{"vs":"〜を通じて","point":"を通じて: throughout, without emphasizing size, or “through” an intermediary. Example: 1年を通じて (throughout the year) ↔ 5年にわたって (over five years)."},{"vs":"〜の間に","point":"の間に: a neutral time period, usable for both short and long periods. Example: 3年の間に留学した ↔ 3年にわたって研究した."}]}},"n3-013||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜にわたって／にわたる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にわたって／にわたる","patternReading":"にわたって・にわたる","coreMeaning":"Spanning a long time or wide scope, emphasizing scale","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This section summarizes typical mistakes learners make with 〜にわたって: using it for short periods or one-off events, and confusing it with 〜にかけて. Knowing these mistakes helps you avoid losing points.","nuance":"The issue is not only grammatical but also naturalness: using にわたって for a small event can sound exaggerated and unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"調査","r":"ちょうさ","m":"investigation"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"3日間","r":"みっかかん","m":"three days"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"over"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"was conducted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The investigation was conducted over three days.","scene":"A correct example using the pattern for a sufficiently long period."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 5分にわたって話した → ✅ 5分間話した","❌ 1時間にわたって勉強した → ✅ 1時間勉強した (unless in a formal report)","❌ 東京から大阪にわたって → ✅ 東京から大阪にかけて (describes two endpoints)","❌ 1回にわたって会議した → ✅ 1回会議した (not used for a single event)"],"compare":null}},"n3-013||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜にわたって／にわたる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にわたって／にわたる","patternReading":"にわたって・にわたる","coreMeaning":"Spanning a long time or wide scope, emphasizing scale","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember 渡る (わたる), meaning “to cross.” Think of にわたって as a red carpet stretching from beginning to end: a long uninterrupted period or something covering an entire area.","nuance":"Use this tip in reading and listening questions: when you see a large number or broad scope in a formal context, にわたって is a strong candidate.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-013||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜にわたって／にわたる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にわたって／にわたる","patternReading":"にわたって・にわたる","coreMeaning":"Spanning a long time or wide scope, emphasizing scale","slug":"phrases","definition":"This section introduces fixed expressions commonly used with 〜にわたって/〜にわたる, such as 長年にわたって, 全国にわたる, 広範囲にわたる, and 多岐にわたる. Memorize them to use the pattern naturally.","nuance":"These expressions sound most natural as complete phrases. 多岐にわたる is especially common in formal documents and reports.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"多岐","r":"たき","m":"many fields"},{"t":"にわたる","r":"にわたる","m":"spanning"},{"t":"分野","r":"ぶんや","m":"fields"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"活躍","r":"かつやく","m":"be active"},{"t":"している","r":"している","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is active in many different fields.","scene":"Introducing a talented person active in many fields."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-014||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる","patternReading":"をめぐって・をめぐる","coreMeaning":"Around ~ (debate/dispute)","slug":"core","definition":"〜をめぐって describes multiple parties caring about, debating, or disputing an issue N. The speaker emphasizes that N is the center of the debate or disagreement. Translate it as “around ~” or “about the disputed topic ~.”","nuance":"Unlike について, this pattern is not used for neutral topics. It always suggests conflict or opposing views. It is common in writing, journalism, and news, and often conveys tension or confrontation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"相続","r":"そうぞく","m":"inheritance"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"around"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"兄弟","r":"きょうだい","m":"siblings"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"争った","r":"あらそった","m":"disputed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The two brothers argued over the inheritance.","scene":"Two brothers dispute how to divide an inheritance"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"政策","r":"せいさく","m":"policy"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"around"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"活発な","r":"かっぱつな","m":"lively"},{"t":"議論","r":"ぎろん","m":"debate"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"なされた","r":"なされた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A lively debate took place over the new policy.","scene":"A policy meeting with opposing views"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-014||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる","patternReading":"をめぐって・をめぐる","coreMeaning":"Around ~ (debate/dispute)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜をめぐって follows noun N directly and comes before a verb. 〜をめぐる modifies the noun that follows. Other forms include 〜をめぐっての + N and 〜をめぐり, a formal written form.","nuance":"をめぐって is the most common form in conversation and writing. をめぐり is literary and uncommon in speech. をめぐる and をめぐっての both modify nouns; をめぐる is direct, while をめぐっての adds emphasis.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + をめぐって","rhs":"+ V","note":"V is a debate/conflict verb: 争う, 議論する, 対立する"},{"lhs":"N + をめぐる","rhs":"+ N2","note":"N2 is often 議論, 対立, 問題, 争い"},{"lhs":"N + をめぐっての","rhs":"+ N","note":"Formal modifying variant"},{"lhs":"N + をめぐり","rhs":"+ V","note":"Written and more formal than をめぐって"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"領土問題","r":"りょうどもんだい","m":"territorial issue"},{"t":"をめぐる","r":"をめぐる","m":"around"},{"t":"対立","r":"たいりつ","m":"conflict"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"続いている","r":"つづいている","m":"continues"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The conflict over the territorial issue is continuing.","scene":"News about an international territorial dispute"},{"tokens":[{"t":"予算","r":"よさん","m":"budget"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"与野党","r":"よやとう","m":"ruling and opposition"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"激しく","r":"はげしく","m":"fiercely"},{"t":"対立した","r":"たいりつした","m":"clashed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The ruling and opposition parties clashed fiercely over the budget.","scene":"News of disagreement over the national budget"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-014||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる","patternReading":"をめぐって・をめぐる","coreMeaning":"Around ~ (debate/dispute)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use it for an issue that many people care about and view differently. It often appears with verbs such as 争う, 議論する, 対立する, and 交渉する. Typical topics include territory, budgets, inheritance, copyright, and policy.","nuance":"The style is formal and somewhat stiff. It is very common in journalism and official writing, but can also be used for family conflicts. It highlights the issue’s multiple perspectives and lack of agreement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"原子力発電","r":"げんしりょくはつでん","m":"nuclear power"},{"t":"をめぐる","r":"をめぐる","m":"over"},{"t":"論争","r":"ろんそう","m":"controversy"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今も","r":"いまも","m":"still"},{"t":"続く","r":"つづく","m":"continues"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The controversy over nuclear power continues.","scene":"An article about the social energy debate"},{"tokens":[{"t":"著作権","r":"ちょさくけん","m":"copyright"},{"t":"をめぐる","r":"をめぐる","m":"over"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issues"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増えて","r":"ふえて","m":"increase"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Copyright-related issues are increasing.","scene":"Discussion of online copyright violations"},{"tokens":[{"t":"消費税増税","r":"しょうひぜいぞうぜい","m":"sales tax hike"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"与野党","r":"よやとう","m":"ruling and opposition"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"激しく","r":"はげしく","m":"fiercely"},{"t":"議論した","r":"ぎろんした","m":"debated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The ruling and opposition parties fiercely debated the sales tax hike.","scene":"A news program about tax policy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-014||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with について・に関して","sub":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる","patternReading":"をめぐって・をめぐる","coreMeaning":"Around ~ (debate/dispute)","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns can mean “about ~,” but they differ in conflict level and register. をめぐって implies debate or disagreement; について is neutral; に関して is formal and means “concerning.”","nuance":"Use について after verbs such as speaking, thinking, and studying. Use をめぐって after verbs expressing debate, dispute, or opposition. に関して is common in formal documents and emails.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"について","r":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話した","r":"はなした","m":"talked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We talked about the movie.","scene":"Friends discuss a movie they just watched"},{"tokens":[{"t":"遺産","r":"いさん","m":"inheritance"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"姉妹","r":"しまい","m":"sisters"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"争った","r":"あらそった","m":"fought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The two sisters fought over the inheritance.","scene":"Two sisters clash over dividing an inheritance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜について","point":"について: neutral topic, no dispute. をめぐって: conflict and opposing parties."},{"vs":"〜に関して","point":"に関して: formal “concerning,” without requiring conflict. をめぐって: emphasizes debate or disagreement."}]}},"n3-014||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる","patternReading":"をめぐって・をめぐる","coreMeaning":"Around ~ (debate/dispute)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse をめぐって with について. Remember: without debate or conflict, do not use をめぐって.","nuance":"The most common mistake is using をめぐって for harmless topics such as hobbies or movies.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"趣味","r":"しゅみ","m":"hobby"},{"t":"について","r":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Talk about hobbies.","scene":"A correct use of について"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 友達をめぐって話した → ✅ 友達について話した","❌ 映画をめぐって話した → ✅ 映画について話した","❌ 環境をめぐって話す（use it for debate） → ✅ 環境問題をめぐって議論する"],"compare":null}},"n3-014||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる","patternReading":"をめぐって・をめぐる","coreMeaning":"Around ~ (debate/dispute)","slug":"memory","definition":"The root 巡る (めぐる) means “to go around.” Picture several people standing in a circle, looking at issue N in the middle and arguing about it.","nuance":"For ordinary conversation, use について; when several people are arguing, use をめぐって. The image of a debate circle makes the pattern easy to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-014||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜をめぐって／をめぐる","patternReading":"をめぐって・をめぐる","coreMeaning":"Around ~ (debate/dispute)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases commonly appear in journalism and JLPT N3 materials: 領土をめぐって争う (dispute territory), 予算をめぐる議論 (budget debate), 相続をめぐって対立する (clash over inheritance), 政策をめぐる論争 (policy controversy), and 著作権をめぐる問題 (copyright issue). Learning them as chunks helps you use the pattern naturally.","nuance":"Distinguish their positions: をめぐって comes before a verb, while をめぐる comes before a noun. With nouns such as 議論, 対立, and 問題, use をめぐる. These phrases are common in formal writing and less common in casual speech.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"領土問題","r":"りょうどもんだい","m":"territorial issue"},{"t":"をめぐる","r":"をめぐる","m":"over"},{"t":"対立","r":"たいりつ","m":"conflict"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"続いている","r":"つづいている","m":"continues"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The conflict over the territorial issue is continuing.","scene":"A familiar phrase in journalism"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-015||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜に反して／に反する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に反して／に反する","patternReading":"にはんして／にはんする","coreMeaning":"Contrary to N, the actual result goes the opposite way","slug":"core","definition":"〜に反して describes an **actual result opposite** to a standard or expectation N. N is often 期待 (expectation), 予想 (prediction), 意思 (intention), ルール (rule), or 法律 (law). The speaker stresses that what happened **did not follow N**—“contrary to ~.”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a slightly formal tone. It does not stress anyone’s fault; it simply states an opposite result and is **more neutral** than に背いて. に反する + N is common in legal and regulatory texts.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"予想","r":"よそう","m":"prediction"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"政策","r":"せいさく","m":"policy"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"支持","r":"しじ","m":"support"},{"t":"された","r":"された","m":"was supported"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to predictions, the new policy was supported.","scene":"Discussing policy after an unexpected result"},{"tokens":[{"t":"医師","r":"いし","m":"doctor"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"忠告","r":"ちゅうこく","m":"advice"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"酒","r":"さけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やめなかった","r":"やめなかった","m":"did not quit"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to his doctor’s advice, he did not quit drinking.","scene":"Talking about a friend who ignored medical advice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-015||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & structure","sub":"〜に反して／に反する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に反して／に反する","patternReading":"にはんして／にはんする","coreMeaning":"Contrary to N, the actual result goes the opposite way","slug":"forms","definition":"Remember four forms: **に反して** connects to a following sentence describing a result; **に反する** modifies a following noun; **に反し** is a formal written contraction; **に反した** also modifies a noun with a past sense. All forms follow a **bare noun** + に.","nuance":"に反し is common in formal documents and speeches. に反する + N is normative and frequent in laws and rules: 法律に反する行為.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + に反して","rhs":"+ Result/following clause (S)","note":"Most common: contrary to N; the following clause states what actually happened"},{"lhs":"N + に反する","rhs":"+ N2","note":"Modifies a noun: an act or decision that violates N"},{"lhs":"N + に反し","rhs":"+ S","note":"Written contraction, without て"},{"lhs":"N + に反した","rhs":"+ N2","note":"Past modifier: an act that went against N"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ルール","m":"rule"},{"t":"に反する","r":"にはんする","m":"violate"},{"t":"行為","r":"こうい","m":"act"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"禁止","r":"きんし","m":"prohibited"},{"t":"されている","r":"されている","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Acts that violate the rules are prohibited.","scene":"Reading company rules"},{"tokens":[{"t":"意思","r":"いし","m":"intention"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"契約","r":"けいやく","m":"contract"},{"t":"させられた","r":"させられた","m":"was forced to sign"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was forced to sign a contract against my wishes.","scene":"Describing coercion at work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-015||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜に反して／に反する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に反して／に反する","patternReading":"にはんして／にはんする","coreMeaning":"Contrary to N, the actual result goes the opposite way","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main situations: **(1)** contrary to an expectation or prediction—the most common; **(2)** violating a rule or law; **(3)** against someone’s will or wishes. The following clause always states **something that happened**, not a command.","nuance":"If the following action is deliberate defiance, consider に背いて; に反して simply describes an objective contrast between expectation and reality.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"予想","r":"よそう","m":"prediction"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to expectations, I passed the exam.","scene":"Telling a friend about exam results"},{"tokens":[{"t":"校則","r":"こうそく","m":"school rules"},{"t":"に反した","r":"にはんした","m":"against"},{"t":"服装","r":"ふくそう","m":"clothing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"禁止","r":"きんし","m":"prohibited"},{"t":"されている","r":"されている","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Clothing that violates school rules is prohibited.","scene":"Reminding students about uniforms"},{"tokens":[{"t":"本人","r":"ほんにん","m":"the person"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意思","r":"いし","m":"will"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"against"},{"t":"働かされた","r":"はたらかされた","m":"was forced to work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was forced to work against my will.","scene":"Describing a high-pressure workplace"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-015||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with にもかかわらず / に対して / と違って","sub":"〜に反して／に反する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に反して／に反する","patternReading":"にはんして／にはんする","coreMeaning":"Contrary to N, the actual result goes the opposite way","slug":"compare","definition":"Three easily confused patterns: **にもかかわらず** (still do something despite difficult conditions), **に対して** (neutral parallel contrast), and **と違って** (simple difference). に反して requires N to be a **standard or expectation that is violated**.","nuance":"に反して means the result deviates from N; にもかかわらず highlights difficulty or persistence; に対して simply contrasts; と違って points out a difference.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"合意","r":"ごうい","m":"agreement"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"進めた","r":"すすめた","m":"proceeded with"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to the agreement, the company proceeded with the plan.","scene":"Hearing that a company broke its agreement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にもかかわらず","point":"に反して: N is an expectation/standard, and the result goes against it. にもかかわらず: N is an unfavorable condition, yet the event still occurs. 悪天候にもかかわらず試合をした ✅ / 悪天候に反して試合をした ❌"},{"vs":"〜に対して","point":"に対して: neutral parallel contrast, with no violation. に反して: B goes completely against standard A. 兄に対して弟は静かだ ✅ / 期待に反して弟は静かだ ✅ (if the expectation was that he would be noisy)"},{"vs":"〜と違って","point":"と違って: states a difference, not necessarily an opposition. に反して: goes in the opposite direction, usually against a standard. 兄と違って弟は静かだ (different) / 期待に反して弟は静かだ (contrary to expectations)"}]}},"n3-015||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜に反して／に反する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に反して／に反する","patternReading":"にはんして／にはんする","coreMeaning":"Contrary to N, the actual result goes the opposite way","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include **confusing it with にもかかわらず**, **choosing an N that is not a standard or expectation**, **forgetting する before a noun**, and **using に反した to connect clauses**.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, the trap often uses weather or health as N; choose にもかかわらず, not に反して.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"法律","r":"ほうりつ","m":"law"},{"t":"に反する","r":"にはんする","m":"violate"},{"t":"行為","r":"こうい","m":"act"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"罰せられる","r":"ばっせられる","m":"be punished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Acts that violate the law will be punished.","scene":"Quoting a legal provision"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 悪天候に反して、試合をした。 → ✅ 悪天候にもかかわらず、試合をした。 (悪天候 is a difficult condition, not an expectation)","❌ 規則に反行為 → ✅ 規則に反する行為 (する is required before a noun)","❌ 兄に反して弟は静かだ。 → ✅ 兄に対して弟は静かだ。 (no standard is being violated)","❌ 予想に反した、合格した。 → ✅ 予想に反して、合格した。 (に反した modifies a noun; it does not connect clauses)"],"compare":null}},"n3-015||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip: Go against N","sub":"〜に反して／に反する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に反して／に反する","patternReading":"にはんして／にはんする","coreMeaning":"Contrary to N, the actual result goes the opposite way","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: 反 means “against” or “opposite”—think of 反対 (opposition) and 反論 (counterargument). Picture **N as a directional sign**, while the actual result or action **goes against** the arrow.","nuance":"Do not use it for something merely unusual; there must be an expectation or rule that reality goes against.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-015||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜に反して／に反する · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に反して／に反する","patternReading":"にはんして／にはんする","coreMeaning":"Contrary to N, the actual result goes the opposite way","slug":"phrases","definition":"Frequent fixed phrases include **予想に反して**, **期待に反して**, **意思に反して**, **法律に反する**, and **規則に反する行為**. Memorizing them helps you recognize them quickly in reading questions.","nuance":"予想 and 期待 often pair with に反して for a sentence result; 法律 and 規則 often pair with に反する + N. Before a noun, に反して can change to に反する.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"期待","r":"きたい","m":"expectations"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"最悪","r":"さいあく","m":"worst"},{"t":"だった","r":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to expectations, the result was terrible.","scene":"Meeting to review business results"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-016||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜おかげで／おかげか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜おかげで／おかげか","patternReading":"おかげで／おかげか","coreMeaning":"Thanks to ~ (GOOD result, gratitude)","slug":"core","definition":"〜おかげで expresses a cause or person that **brings about a good result** for the speaker. The speaker feels **grateful** and regards that cause as fortunate. This pattern describes a **positive result**, the complete opposite of せいで (bad result and blame).","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a moderately formal tone. The key point is **gratitude**, not merely listing a cause. Japanese speakers often use it to **show humility**, giving credit to others or circumstances instead of taking credit themselves. For bad results, use せいで; use おかげで only when being **sarcastic**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"おかげで","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"しました","m":"did (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to my teacher, I passed.","scene":"Happily reporting a passing exam result and thanking the teacher"},{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"おかげで","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"quickly"},{"t":"治りました","r":"なおりました","m":"recovered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to the medicine, I recovered quickly.","scene":"Speaking to family after recovering"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-016||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜おかげで／おかげか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜おかげで／おかげか","patternReading":"おかげで／おかげか","coreMeaning":"Thanks to ~ (GOOD result, gratitude)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches to a **noun** or a **completed clause (Vた)**. After a noun, **の is required**: 先生のおかげで. With verbs, do not use the dictionary form; use **Vた** or **Vている** (emphasizing an ongoing state). There is also **おかげか**, with か expressing uncertainty: “I don't know if it was thanks to ~, but…”.","nuance":"The second clause is always a **good result** with a sense of gratitude. おかげか is softer and more modest than おかげで because the speaker does not firmly assert the cause. おかげで remains natural in formal writing.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + の","rhs":"おかげで + Good result","note":"の is required after a noun. E.g.: 友達のおかげで…"},{"lhs":"Vた / Vている","rhs":"おかげで + Good result","note":"Do not use the dictionary form. E.g.: 頑張ったおかげで…"},{"lhs":"Aい / Naな","rhs":"おかげで + Good result","note":"The adjective comes before おかげで. E.g.: 天気がよかったおかげで…"},{"lhs":"N + の + おかげか","rhs":"+ Good result","note":"〜か = “possibly thanks to”; the speaker is uncertain."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"おかげで","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"できた","m":"could"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to studying every day, I passed.","scene":"Describing a personal exam-prep strategy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"して","m":"doing"},{"t":"いる","m":"be doing"},{"t":"おかげか","m":"perhaps thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"体","r":"からだ","m":"body"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"healthy"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't know if it's thanks to exercising, but I feel healthy.","scene":"Telling a friend after exercising regularly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-016||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜おかげで／おかげか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜おかげで／おかげか","patternReading":"おかげで／おかげか","coreMeaning":"Thanks to ~ (GOOD result, gratitude)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are 4 main situations: ① a specific person or thing produces a good result; ② おかげか when the cause seems likely but is uncertain; ③ おかげさまで as a polite response when someone checks on you; ④ **sarcastic** use when the result is actually bad but the speaker deliberately says “thanks to.” **Choose おかげで when the speaker feels grateful.**","nuance":"おかげか is gentle and modest, often used in conversation. おかげさまで is a highly polite set phrase, almost a social reflex. Sarcastic おかげで requires clear intonation and context; otherwise listeners will understand it positively.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"おかげで","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"就職","r":"しゅうしょく","m":"get a job"},{"t":"できた","m":"could"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to my friends, I got a job.","scene":"Thanking friends who introduced me to a job"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"いる","m":"be doing"},{"t":"おかげか","m":"perhaps thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"肌","r":"はだ","m":"skin"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't know if it's thanks to eating vegetables every day, but my skin looks better.","scene":"Commenting on your skin in front of a mirror"},{"tokens":[{"t":"おかげさまで","m":"thanks to everyone"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"well"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to everyone, I'm doing well.","scene":"Responding when an acquaintance asks how you are"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-016||compare":{"title":"⚖️ おかげで vs せいで vs ために","sub":"〜おかげで／おかげか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜おかげで／おかげか","patternReading":"おかげで／おかげか","coreMeaning":"Thanks to ~ (GOOD result, gratitude)","slug":"compare","definition":"All three can be translated as “because of,” “due to,” or “thanks to,” but the **speaker's emotion is completely different**. おかげで = good result and gratitude. せいで = bad result and blame. ために (cause) = neutral and objective. Quick test: feeling **thankful**? Use おかげで. **Blaming** someone or something? Use せいで. Just explaining objectively? Use ために.","nuance":"おかげで clearly carries positive emotion and never merely states a cause. せいで usually conveys annoyance or resentment. ために (cause) is more formal and common in written notices.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"おかげで","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"being late"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"“Thanks to” him, I was late (sarcastic).","scene":"Sarcastically blaming a roommate who made you late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜せいで","point":"GOOD result → おかげで (gratitude); BAD result → せいで (blame). 雨のおかげで遅れた? WRONG → 雨のせいで遅れた."},{"vs":"〜ために（原因）","point":"ために is neutral/negative and explains a cause objectively; おかげで is positive, showing happiness and gratitude. For missing class because of illness, say 病気のために欠席した, not おかげで."},{"vs":"〜ばかりに","point":"ばかりに marks a BAD result caused by one single reason, with regret; おかげ is for GOOD results and gratitude. 金がないばかりに留学を諦めた."}]}},"n3-016||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜おかげで／おかげか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜おかげで／おかげか","patternReading":"おかげで／おかげか","coreMeaning":"Thanks to ~ (GOOD result, gratitude)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate “thanks to” too directly and use おかげ for negative situations as well, but Japanese requires a distinction: **good result → おかげで, bad result → せいで**. Two other form errors are forgetting **の** after a noun and using the **dictionary form** before おかげで.","nuance":"Using おかげで for a bad result is a serious meaning error. Omitting の or using Vた incorrectly are common grammar-test errors. Remember that おかげで behaves like a noun: a noun before it needs の, and a verb must take a modifying form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"おかげで","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"下がった","r":"さがった","m":"went down"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to the medicine, my fever went down.","scene":"Telling a doctor how you are after taking medicine"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨のおかげで遅れた。 → ✅ 雨のせいで遅れた。 (Use せいで for a bad result, except sarcastically)","❌ 先生おかげで合格した。 → ✅ 先生のおかげで合格した。 (A noun must be followed by の)","❌ 頑張るおかげで成功した。 → ✅ 頑張ったおかげで成功した。 (Use Vた, not the dictionary form)"],"compare":null}},"n3-016||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜おかげで／おかげか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜おかげで／おかげか","patternReading":"おかげで／おかげか","coreMeaning":"Thanks to ~ (GOOD result, gratitude)","slug":"memory","definition":"おかげ is written with the kanji **陰 (かげ) — shade, shelter**. Japanese culture views success as supported not only by the individual but also by help and protection from others. Remember the mantra: **GOOD → おかげで, BAD → せいで**.","nuance":"This mnemonic helps prevent confusion with せいで. “Shade” suggests safety and comfort → a positive result. Think of “せい” as pointing to the cause of a problem → blame and negativity.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-016||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜おかげで／おかげか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜おかげで／おかげか","patternReading":"おかげで／おかげか","coreMeaning":"Thanks to ~ (GOOD result, gratitude)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in conversation and on tests: **〜のおかげで助かった** (thanks to ~, I was saved), **〜のおかげで成功した** (thanks to ~, I succeeded), and **おかげさまで**—a polite reply when someone checks on you.","nuance":"おかげさまで is a set phrase and needs nothing after it in a short reply. The other phrases usually take a Vた verb or noun + の. To sound humble, Japanese speakers often use おかげさまで instead of saying “I did it myself.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"皆さん","r":"みなさん","m":"everyone"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"おかげで","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"プロジェクト","r":"ぷろじぇくと","m":"project"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"しました","m":"did (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to everyone, this project succeeded.","scene":"Giving thanks at the project closing event"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-017||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ことから · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことから","patternReading":"ことから","coreMeaning":"Because ~; based on the fact that ~ (reason/origin based on evidence)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ことから expresses a **reason or origin based on an observable fact or characteristic**. It can be translated as “because ~,” “from the fact that ~,” or “based on ~.” The first clause is an OBJECTIVE FACT; the second is a CONCLUSION, judgment, or name.","nuance":"Objective and formal, commonly used in writing, reports, and explanations. It is not used for personal emotional reasons. Compared with から/ので, it sounds stronger and clearly evidence-based.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"足跡","r":"あしあと","m":"footprints"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"exist"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"推測","r":"すいそく","m":"infer"},{"t":"される","r":"される","m":"is believed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because there are footprints, it can be inferred that the culprit came here.","scene":"Police discuss the crime scene during an investigation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"白い","r":"しろい","m":"white"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flowers"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"咲く","r":"さく","m":"bloom"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"木","r":"き","m":"tree"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"白梅","r":"はくばい","m":"white plum"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"呼ばれる","r":"よばれる","m":"is called"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because its white flowers bloom, this tree is called “white plum.”","scene":"A botanical garden guide introduces the tree’s name"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-017||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜ことから · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことから","patternReading":"ことから","coreMeaning":"Because ~; based on the fact that ~ (reason/origin based on evidence)","slug":"forms","definition":"The clause before ことから uses the **plain form**. With na-adjectives and nouns, **である** must be added to create the formal tone.","nuance":"Do not use the polite form before ことから. This is a written pattern commonly found in news, articles, and explanatory texts.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形 / た形 / ている形）","rhs":"+ ことから","note":"An action or state serving as the basis."},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"+ ことから","note":"A quality or characteristic serving as the reason."},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"+ である + ことから","note":"Use である instead of な."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"+ である + ことから","note":"A noun serving as the reason or origin."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"形","r":"かたち","m":"shape"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mount Fuji"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"似ている","r":"にている","m":"resemble"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"小富士","r":"こふじ","m":"Little Fuji"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"呼ばれている","r":"よばれている","m":"is called"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it resembles Mount Fuji, this mountain is called “Little Fuji.”","scene":"Reading a sightseeing guidebook"},{"tokens":[{"t":"桜","r":"さくら","m":"cherry blossoms"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"さくら公園","r":"さくらこうえん","m":"Sakura Park"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"呼ばれる","r":"よばれる","m":"is called"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it has many cherry trees, this park came to be called Sakura Park.","scene":"An article introducing a local cherry-blossom spot"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"is"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"割引","r":"わりびき","m":"discount"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"受けられる","r":"うけられる","m":"can receive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because he is a student, he can receive a discount.","scene":"A cinema employee confirms eligibility"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-017||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜ことから · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことから","patternReading":"ことから","coreMeaning":"Because ~; based on the fact that ~ (reason/origin based on evidence)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ことから has three main uses: **explaining a name or origin** (such as a place name or nickname), **drawing a conclusion from observed evidence**, and **explaining customs or culture**. The first clause is always a verifiable fact.","nuance":"This is a formal written pattern and is uncommon in everyday conversation. It often appears in travel guides, news, and academic writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"証拠","r":"しょうこ","m":"evidence"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見つかった","r":"みつかった","m":"was found"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"特定された","r":"とくていされた","m":"was identified"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because evidence was found, the culprit was identified.","scene":"A news report about a crime"},{"tokens":[{"t":"赤","r":"あか","m":"red"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"幸運","r":"こううん","m":"good luck"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"表す","r":"あらわす","m":"symbolize"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"結婚式","r":"けっこんしき","m":"wedding"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"赤","r":"あか","m":"red"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"使われる","r":"つかわれる","m":"is used"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because red symbolizes good luck in Japan, it is used at weddings.","scene":"An article explaining Japanese wedding customs"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"沈黙","r":"ちんもく","m":"silence"},{"t":"している","r":"している","m":"is being"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何か","r":"なにか","m":"something"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"隠している","r":"かくしている","m":"is hiding"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"推測","r":"すいそく","m":"infer"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his silence, we can infer that he is hiding something.","scene":"An employee analyzes the manager’s attitude in a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-017||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing similar patterns","sub":"〜ことから · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことから","patternReading":"ことから","coreMeaning":"Because ~; based on the fact that ~ (reason/origin based on evidence)","slug":"compare","definition":"〜ことから is often confused with other reason patterns. The key point: **ことから is used when the first clause is an OBJECTIVE FACT and the second is a CONCLUSION or NAME**, not an ordinary reason for an action.","nuance":"While から/ので can express subjective reasons, ことから is distinctly more objective and formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"足跡","r":"あしあと","m":"footprints"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"exist"},{"t":"ことから","r":"ことから","m":"because/from the fact"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"分かる","r":"わかる","m":"know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From the footprints, we know that someone came.","scene":"An investigator discusses the scene"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜から / 〜ので","point":"から/ので express ordinary cause and effect and can be subjective (疲れたから寝た). ことから emphasizes an observable fact/feature → conclusion or name."},{"vs":"〜ことで","point":"ことで = the means “by doing V” (毎日練習することで上達する); ことから = starting from a fact (花が多いことから名付けられた)."},{"vs":"〜ために（原因）","point":"ために often states a cause of a bad or neutral result (病気のために休んだ); ことから gives the basis for an inference or origin."},{"vs":"〜をきっかけに","point":"をきっかけに = an event that creates a TURNING POINT and change (引っ越しをきっかけに生活が変わった); ことから = an objective basis for a CONCLUSION (足跡があることから来たと分かる)."}]}},"n3-017||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ことから · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことから","patternReading":"ことから","coreMeaning":"Because ~; based on the fact that ~ (reason/origin based on evidence)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use ことから when the reason is a personal feeling or intention. Remember: **the second clause must be a CONCLUSION, JUDGMENT, or NAME**, not merely an action.","nuance":"This mistake makes the sentence unnatural and may be judged grammatically incorrect in formal writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By practicing every day, I will improve.","scene":"A correct example replacing ことから"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 嬉しいことから笑った。 → ✅ 嬉しかったから笑った。(Use から for an emotional reason)","❌ 毎日練習することから上手になる。 → ✅ 毎日練習することで上手になる。(Use ことで for a means)","❌ 雨が降ったことから、出かけた。 → ✅ 雨が降ったから、出かけた。(Use から for a simple action)","❌ 彼は学生ことから、割引がある。 → ✅ 彼は学生であることから、割引がある。(Add である after a noun)"],"compare":null}},"n3-017||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ことから · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことから","patternReading":"ことから","coreMeaning":"Because ~; based on the fact that ~ (reason/origin based on evidence)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember **ことから** as “starting from that fact.” Imagine seeing **evidence** in the first clause and then drawing a **conclusion or name** in the second.","nuance":"This tip helps distinguish it from ことで (means) and ordinary から (reason).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-017||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ことから · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことから","patternReading":"ことから","coreMeaning":"Because ~; based on the fact that ~ (reason/origin based on evidence)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases with ことから are common in journalism and academic writing to **explain the origin of a name** or **state the basis for an inference**.","nuance":"Memorizing these phrases helps you quickly understand explanatory passages, place names, and descriptions of phenomena.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"桜","r":"さくら","m":"cherry blossoms"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"ことから"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"桜町","r":"さくらまち","m":"Sakuramachi"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"呼ばれる","r":"よばれる","m":"is called"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it has many cherry blossoms, it is called Sakura-machi.","scene":"A passage introducing a place name"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-018||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ために（原因） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ために（原因）","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~, due to ~ (cause—usually negative/neutral)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ために（原因）expresses the **cause/reason** leading to a usually unwanted result. It is **objective and formal**, often used in notices and official documents. However, the same ために form can also mean **purpose** ('in order to'), so identify its meaning from the structure.","nuance":"Neutral and **objective**, without the clear blame of せいで or the personal softness of から/ので. Common in **written language**, official notices, and news reports. The result may be negative, but the speaker remains neutral.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"closed"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the typhoon, school was closed.","scene":"Hearing a notice about school closing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"寝坊","r":"ねぼう","m":"oversleeping"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗り遅れた","r":"のりおくれた","m":"missed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I overslept, I missed the train.","scene":"Explaining a late arrival to a colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-018||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & clues","sub":"〜ために（原因） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ために（原因）","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~, due to ~ (cause—usually negative/neutral)","slug":"forms","definition":"Recognize **two cause structures**: A) **N + の + ために**: the noun is the cause; B) **Vた + ために**: an event that occurred is the cause. Do not confuse these with **V辞書形 + ために**, which means purpose.","nuance":"The following clause usually describes an **unwanted event**: 中止になる, 遅刻する, 失敗する. Do not use it with commands, invitations, or strong volition.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"のために","note":"A noun showing the cause (disaster, illness, incident)"},{"lhs":"V（た）","rhs":"ために","note":"An action that occurred and caused a consequence"},{"lhs":"V（ない）","rhs":"ために","note":"Because something was not done → negative result"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ために","note":"⚠️ Purpose 'in order to,' not cause"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"休んだ","r":"やすんだ","m":"was absent"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of my illness, I missed the meeting.","scene":"Asking a manager to excuse an absence"},{"tokens":[{"t":"準備","r":"じゅんび","m":"preparation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"足りなかった","r":"たりなかった","m":"was insufficient"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because we were underprepared, we failed.","scene":"Reviewing a failed project"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-018||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜ために（原因） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ために（原因）","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~, due to ~ (cause—usually negative/neutral)","slug":"usage","definition":"ために（原因）is commonly used when an **objective cause** leads to an **unwanted result**. In formal written language, it can replace から/ので to maintain an objective tone.","nuance":"It sounds **formal and somewhat stiff**, fitting notices and news reports rather than emotional conversation. Typical results include 中止, 遅刻, 失敗, and 停止—not deliberate actions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"交通渋滞","r":"こうつうじゅうたい","m":"traffic jam"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"be late"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of a traffic jam, I was late for the meeting.","scene":"An employee apologizing for being late"},{"tokens":[{"t":"大雪","r":"おおゆき","m":"heavy snow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"営業","r":"えいぎょう","m":"business operations"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"休止","r":"きゅうし","m":"suspend"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the heavy snow, we are suspending operations today.","scene":"A storefront notice on a snowy day"},{"tokens":[{"t":"不注意","r":"ふちゅうい","m":"carelessness"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accident"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"起きた","r":"おきた","m":"occurred"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of carelessness, an accident occurred.","scene":"Reporting an accident caused by carelessness"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-018||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Comparing cause patterns","sub":"〜ために（原因） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ために（原因）","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~, due to ~ (cause—usually negative/neutral)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ために（原因）from other cause expressions by considering the **speaker's attitude** and the **result**.","nuance":"ために is **neutral and formal**; せいで assigns blame, おかげで expresses gratitude, and から/ので are more casual.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大雪","r":"おおゆき","m":"heavy snow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まった","r":"とまった","m":"stopped"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the heavy snow, the train stopped.","scene":"Seeing a notice about snow-related service suspension"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ので／〜から","point":"Casual and common in conversation; ために is formal and used in writing or notices."},{"vs":"〜せいで","point":"せいで clearly assigns blame and signals a bad result; ために is neutral and non-accusatory."},{"vs":"〜おかげで","point":"おかげで is used for good results and gratitude; ために is neutral → usually negative."},{"vs":"〜ことから","point":"ことから gives a basis for inference ('judging from ~'); ために shows a direct cause."},{"vs":"〜ために（目的）","point":"V辞書形+ために = purpose 'in order to'; Nの/Vた+ために = cause 'because of'."}]}},"n3-018||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common usage pitfalls","sub":"〜ために（原因） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ために（原因）","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~, due to ~ (cause—usually negative/neutral)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse the purpose and cause meanings of ために. Focus on the **form before ために** instead of guessing from context.","nuance":"A serious error is using cause ために for a **positive result**, which makes the sentence bookish and emotionally unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"canceled"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the typhoon, the match was canceled.","scene":"A notice postponing a soccer match"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 合格するために、うれしい。 (wrong: V辞書形+ために = purpose) → ✅ 合格したから、うれしい。","❌ 合格したために、うれしい。 (good result, subjective emotion) → ✅ 合格したおかげで／ので、うれしい。","⚠️ 彼のために is ambiguous: 'for his benefit' (purpose) or 'because of his fault' (cause). → Use context: good result = purpose; bad result = cause.","❌ 彼のために、成功した。 (good result with causal ために) → ✅ 彼のおかげで、成功した。"],"compare":null}},"n3-018||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips & mnemonic","sub":"〜ために（原因） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ために（原因）","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~, due to ~ (cause—usually negative/neutral)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick distinction: **V辞書形 + ために = in order to** (looking ahead); **Nの/Vた + ために = because of** (looking back).","nuance":"Mnemonic: \"If the following clause shows **volition** → PURPOSE; if it shows **no volition** → CAUSE.\"","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-018||phrases":{"title":"💬 Fixed phrases & collocations","sub":"〜ために（原因） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ために（原因）","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~, due to ~ (cause—usually negative/neutral)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases often appear in JLPT N3 questions and official notices. Mastering them helps you quickly identify ために（原因） in reading and listening tasks. They commonly combine with nouns for disasters, incidents, illnesses, or careless actions.","nuance":"N の ために appears frequently in reading passages; V た ために often appears in sentences describing consequences.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝坊","r":"ねぼう","m":"oversleeping"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"始発","r":"しはつ","m":"first train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗り遅れた","r":"のりおくれた","m":"missed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I overslept, I missed the first train.","scene":"Recounting a late morning"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-019||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜せいで／せいか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜せいで／せいか","patternReading":"せいで／せいか","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (bad result), blaming ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜せいで expresses a **cause leading to a BAD result**. The speaker blames that cause—it may be another person, circumstances, or themselves. 〜せいか is the **uncertain** version: 'I don't know if it's because of ~, but a bad result still occurred.' Translations: 'because of ~,' 'due to ~.'","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, but Japanese speakers often avoid it in formal texts because its blaming tone is clear. せいで strongly conveys **reproach/blame**; せいか is milder and more speculative. Never use it with a good result.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいで","r":"せいで","m":"because of ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"運動会","r":"うんどうかい","m":"sports day"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the rain, the sports day was canceled.","scene":"Outdoor event postponed"},{"tokens":[{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"oneself"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ミス","m":"mistake"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいで","r":"せいで","m":"because of ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チーム","m":"team"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"迷惑","r":"めいわく","m":"trouble"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"かけた","r":"かけた","m":"caused"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of my own mistake, I caused trouble for the whole team.","scene":"Employee blames themselves after ruining a project"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ストレス","m":"stress"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいか","r":"せいか","m":"perhaps because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食欲","r":"しょくよく","m":"appetite"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"does not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't know if it's because of stress, but I have no appetite.","scene":"Complaining about recent loss of appetite"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-019||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜せいで／せいか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜せいで／せいか","patternReading":"せいで／せいか","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (bad result), blaming ~","slug":"forms","definition":"Connection patterns: nouns must be followed by の; verbs use the た-form or ている-form, not the dictionary form. Other variants include **せいか** (speculation), **せいだ** (sentence-final), and **せいにする** (actively assigning blame).","nuance":"The following result is always **BAD** or disadvantageous and carries blame. せいか often describes an unusual phenomenon with no clear cause. せいにする emphasizes the act of blaming.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"の＋せいで＋[bad result]","note":"N is the culprit; の is required"},{"lhs":"Vた / Vている","rhs":"＋せいで＋[bad result]","note":"Do not use Vる / Vない with せいで"},{"lhs":"Nの / V普通形","rhs":"＋せいか＋[bad result]","note":"Add か to express uncertain speculation"},{"lhs":"〜文末","rhs":"せいだ","note":"States 'the main cause is ~'"},{"lhs":"〜","rhs":"せいにする","note":"Actively blame someone/something"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝不足","r":"ねぶそく","m":"lack of sleep"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいで","r":"せいで","m":"because of ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ミス","m":"mistake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of lack of sleep, I made a mistake at work.","scene":"Employee explaining a reporting error"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何でも","r":"なんでも","m":"anything"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいにする","r":"せいにする","m":"blame"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He blames other people for everything.","scene":"Complaining about a coworker who avoids responsibility"},{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"failed"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"準備","r":"じゅんび","m":"preparation"},{"t":"不足","r":"ふそく","m":"lack"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいだ","r":"せいだ","m":"is because of"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The failure was due to insufficient preparation.","scene":"Reviewing a failed presentation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-019||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜せいで／せいか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜せいで／せいか","patternReading":"せいで／せいか","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (bad result), blaming ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Common situations include blaming another person, circumstances, or yourself. **せいか** is used when the speaker is unsure of the cause but still implies mild blame. Japanese culture encourages accepting responsibility or referring to circumstances instead of directly pointing at someone.","nuance":"In formal writing (apologies and reports), Japanese speakers often use **ために** or refer to 'circumstances' to soften blame. In casual settings, せいで is freely used for playful blame or complaints.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいで","r":"せいで","m":"because of ~"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"late"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was late because of him.","scene":"Arriving late after waiting for someone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"oneself"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいで","r":"せいで","m":"because of ~"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"failed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It failed because of me.","scene":"Blaming yourself after ruining something"},{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"疲れて","r":"つかれて","m":"tired"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"せいか","r":"せいか","m":"perhaps because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"集中","r":"しゅうちゅう","m":"concentration"},{"t":"できない","r":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Perhaps because I've been tired lately, I can't concentrate.","scene":"Noticing your own lack of focus"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-019||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"〜せいで／せいか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜せいで／せいか","patternReading":"せいで／せいか","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (bad result), blaming ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare it with other cause patterns. The key differences are **the speaker's attitude** and **the type of result**: せいで is negative and blaming; おかげで is positive and grateful; ために is neutral; から・ので simply gives an ordinary reason.","nuance":"せいで has a much stronger **resentful/blaming** tone. せいか is milder and more questioning. Note: おかげで can be used sarcastically with a bad result, but せいで is never used with a good result.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいで","r":"せいで","m":"because of ~"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the rain, the match was canceled.","scene":"Discussing a match postponed by weather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"おかげで","point":"GOOD result → おかげで; BAD result → せいで. E.g.: 彼のおかげで助かった vs 彼のせいで怒られた."},{"vs":"ために（原因）","point":"ために states an objective, neutral cause without blame; せいで carries emotional reproach."},{"vs":"から／ので","point":"から・ので explain an ordinary reason without emotion; せいで clearly conveys resentment."},{"vs":"せいか","point":"せいで asserts the cause; せいか expresses doubt: 'I wonder if it is because of ~.'"}]}},"n3-019||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜せいで／せいか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜せいで／せいか","patternReading":"せいで／せいか","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (bad result), blaming ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors: using せいで for a good result, forgetting の after a noun, using the dictionary form before せいで, and overusing せいで in formal contexts.","nuance":"In formal apologies, Japanese speakers often use **ために** or refer to 'circumstances' to avoid direct blame. せいで can sound childish or unprofessional.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天候","r":"てんこう","m":"weather"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいで","r":"せいで","m":"because of ~"},{"t":"予定通り","r":"よていどおり","m":"as planned"},{"t":"進められません","r":"すすめられません","m":"cannot proceed"},{"t":"でした"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the weather, we could not proceed as planned.","scene":"Reporting a weather-affected schedule"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生のせいで合格した。 → ✅ 先生のおかげで合格した。","❌ 雨せいで、試合が中止になった。 → ✅ 雨のせいで、試合が中止になった。","❌ 食べるせいで太った。 → ✅ 食べすぎているせいで太った。","❌ 会議のせいで、予定が早まった。（結果が良い） → ✅ 会議のおかげで、予定が早まった。"],"compare":null}},"n3-019||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜せいで／せいか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜せいで／せいか","patternReading":"せいで／せいか","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (bad result), blaming ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory aid through kanji association: せい (所為) means 'fault' or 'the act that caused something.' Picture a finger **pointing directly** at someone or something—that is what receives the blame.","nuance":"Contrast it with おかげ (shade or protection): せい is like a sharp thorn, suggesting discomfort and negativity.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-019||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜せいで／せいか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜せいで／せいか","patternReading":"せいで／せいか","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (bad result), blaming ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases often appear in exams and daily life. **〜のせいにする** is common when discussing the habit of blaming others. **〜のせいで〜なくなった** describes a loss caused by a negative factor. **〜のせいか〜気がする** expresses doubt about a cause.","nuance":"These phrases appear frequently in everyday speech and JLPT reading passages. 〜のせいにする often carries criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝不足","r":"ねぶそく","m":"lack of sleep"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"せいか","r":"せいか","m":"perhaps because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"head"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"痛い","r":"いたい","m":"hurts"},{"t":"気","r":"き","m":"feeling"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Perhaps because of lack of sleep, I feel a headache coming on.","scene":"Guessing why you feel unwell"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-020||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning: MEANS → RESULT","sub":"〜ことで · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことで","patternReading":"ことで","coreMeaning":"By ~ / through ~ (a means of achieving a result)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ことで expresses the means or method for achieving a result. The core idea is using a specific action as a tool: do V → thereby achieve a result. It answers “How?”, unlike purpose or cause.","nuance":"Neutral and usable in both speech and writing. In written language and self-help books, ことで is common because it emphasizes the mechanism and value of the process. Compared with ことによって, ことで feels more familiar and less formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことで","r":"ことで","m":"by doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By practicing every day, your Japanese will improve.","scene":"A student asks how to study Japanese"},{"tokens":[{"t":"情報","r":"じょうほう","m":"information"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"共有","r":"きょうゆう","m":"share"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことで","r":"ことで","m":"by doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チーム","m":"team"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"効率","r":"こうりつ","m":"efficiency"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上がる","r":"あがる","m":"increase"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By sharing information, the team’s efficiency will increase.","scene":"A team discusses improving work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-020||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation: V + ことで","sub":"〜ことで · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことで","patternReading":"ことで","coreMeaning":"By ~ / through ~ (a means of achieving a result)","slug":"forms","definition":"There are three main forms: **V dictionary form + ことで** (a regular action or process), **Vた form + ことで** (a completed action → result), and **N + ということで** (an established reason or conclusion → agreement).","nuance":"The V dictionary form is the most common. Vた form emphasizes an action that has occurred. ということで is often used in announcements and meetings to finalize a matter.","structure":[{"lhs":"V dictionary form","rhs":"＋ ことで ＋ Result","note":"Regular action / process → means"},{"lhs":"Vた form","rhs":"＋ ことで ＋ Result","note":"Completed action → leads to a result"},{"lhs":"普通形 ＋ ということで","rhs":"＋ Decision / Conclusion","note":"Known reason → agreement"},{"lhs":"Vない form","rhs":"＋ ことで ＋ Result","note":"Negated action → means (uncommon)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"継続","r":"けいぞく","m":"persistence"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことで","r":"ことで","m":"by doing"},{"t":"成果","r":"せいか","m":"results"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"appear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By persevering, results will come.","scene":"Encouragement in a book or gym"},{"tokens":[{"t":"謝った","r":"あやまった","m":"apologized"},{"t":"ことで","r":"ことで","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"関係","r":"かんけい","m":"relationship"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"改善","r":"かいぜん","m":"improve"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I apologized, the relationship improved.","scene":"Talking about making up with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-020||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage: MEANS / CONCLUSION","sub":"〜ことで · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことで","patternReading":"ことで","coreMeaning":"By ~ / through ~ (a means of achieving a result)","slug":"usage","definition":"The most common use is **means**: V + ことで + result. In addition, **Vた + ことで** describes a completed action leading to a past result, while **ということで** finalizes a decision or conclusion based on known context.","nuance":"In conversation, ことで often appears in advice or explanations of **how to do something**. At work, ということで frequently appears at the end of emails or meetings to confirm agreement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"ずつ","m":"each time"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことで","r":"ことで","m":"by doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"自然と","r":"しぜんと","m":"naturally"},{"t":"身について","r":"みについて","m":"sink in"},{"t":"きます","m":"will come"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By practicing a little every day, it will naturally sink in.","scene":"A teacher advises a student how to study"},{"tokens":[{"t":"予算","r":"よさん","m":"budget"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"足りない","r":"たりない","m":"not enough"},{"t":"ということで","r":"ということで","m":"because of that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because the budget was insufficient, the plan was canceled.","scene":"A company announces a project cancellation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-020||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish: ことで vs ことから vs ために","sub":"〜ことで · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことで","patternReading":"ことで","coreMeaning":"By ~ / through ~ (a means of achieving a result)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most often confused are: **ことで** for means (how to do something), **ことから** for source or reason (because of a fact), and **ために** for purpose (in order to do something). Quick check: “How?” → ことで; “From what / why?” → ことから; “For what purpose?” → ために.","nuance":"ことで emphasizes the **action mechanism**. ことから emphasizes the **cause–conclusion relationship**. ために emphasizes the **intended goal**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"話し合う","r":"はなしあう","m":"discuss"},{"t":"ことで","r":"ことで","m":"by doing"},{"t":"解決策","r":"かいけつさく","m":"solution"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見つかった","r":"みつかった","m":"was found"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By discussing it, we found a solution.","scene":"Describing a successful team meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ことから","point":"ことで = means (HOW); ことから = source/reason (WHY). Example: 毎日練習することで上手くなる vs 花が多いことから名付けた"},{"vs":"〜ために（目的）","point":"ことで = method for achieving a result; ために = future purpose. Example: 練習することで上手くなる vs 合格するために練習する"},{"vs":"〜ということで","point":"ということで = a conclusion from known context, not a means. Example: 予算がないということで中止になった"}]}},"n3-020||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors: confusing ことで / ことから","sub":"〜ことで · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことで","patternReading":"ことで","coreMeaning":"By ~ / through ~ (a means of achieving a result)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A common error is using **ことで instead of ことから** when expressing a reason or source. Remember: ことで answers “How?”, while ことから answers “Why?” or “From what?”.","nuance":"ことで is not used with stative adjectives such as liking, disliking, or being skilled. It requires an **active action**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"overexertion"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"ことで","r":"ことで","m":"by not doing"},{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なれる","m":"can become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By avoiding overexertion, you can stay healthy.","scene":"Advice on staying healthy"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日練習することから上手くなる。 → ✅ 毎日練習することで上手くなる。 (Use ことで for a means, not ことから.)","❌ 花が多いことで名付けた。 → ✅ 花が多いことから名付けた。 (Use ことから for a name’s origin, not ことで.)","❌ 毎日練習したことで上手くなる。 → ✅ 毎日練習することで上手くなる。 (A general rule uses present tense in both clauses; Vた is fine if the result is also past, e.g. 謝ったことで改善した.)","❌ 日本語が好きなことで上手くなる。 → ✅ 毎日勉強することで上手くなる。 (ことで needs an active action, not a state.)"],"compare":null}},"n3-020||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip: PRESS BUTTON, GET RESULT","sub":"〜ことで · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことで","patternReading":"ことで","coreMeaning":"By ~ / through ~ (a means of achieving a result)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **V as a button** and ことで as the mechanism that transfers force: press the action → the result lights up. Remember: “By doing X → achieve Y.”","nuance":"This tip helps distinguish it from ことから (source), because ことで involves a **hand actively causing an effect**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-020||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases: ことで / のことで","sub":"〜ことで · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことで","patternReading":"ことで","coreMeaning":"By ~ / through ~ (a means of achieving a result)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in tests and daily life include **Vる + ことで + できる** (you can do something by doing V), **N + のことで相談/困る** (discuss or have trouble with N), and the office phrase **それではということで**, used to move on to the next item.","nuance":"These phrases often appear in JLPT N3 reading passages and workplace conversations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それでは","m":"then"},{"t":"ということで","r":"ということで","m":"to conclude"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ここまで","m":"this point"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"しましょう","m":"let’s make it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Then, let’s stop here for today.","scene":"The chair ends a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-021||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜だからといって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だからといって","patternReading":"だからといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜だからといって means: **A may be true, but A is not a valid reason to do B (which is wrong)**. This pattern rejects using A to justify action B. Translation: 'just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~'.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, often in criticism, arguments, and instruction. The speaker takes a **critical, firm** stance rather than describing things neutrally. The second clause is usually negative or critical.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何でも","r":"なんでも","m":"anything"},{"t":"買える","r":"かえる","m":"can buy"},{"t":"わけではない","m":"does not mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because you have money does not mean you can buy anything.","scene":"Friends claim money can buy everything"},{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"礼儀","r":"れいぎ","m":"courtesy"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れて","r":"わすれて","m":"forget"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いけない","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter how busy you are, you must not forget courtesy.","scene":"A boss reminds a late employee to be polite"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-021||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜だからといって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だからといって","patternReading":"だからといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach to a clause: **verbs / い-adjectives in plain form + からといって**, while **nouns / な-adjectives + だからといって**. Keep だ after nouns and な-adjectives. The second clause must be negative or critical.","nuance":"Do not use an absolute affirmative clause afterward. To express ordinary cause and effect, 'because A, B,' use だから, not だからといって.","structure":[{"lhs":"V / いA 普通形 + からといって","rhs":"Negative / critical after clause","note":"忙しい + からといって → 忙しいからといって"},{"lhs":"N + だからといって","rhs":"Negative / critical after clause","note":"Must include だ: 日本人だからといって"},{"lhs":"なA + だからといって","rhs":"Negative / critical after clause","note":"静か + だ + からといって → 静かだからといって"},{"lhs":"〜からといって","rhs":"〜とは限らない / 〜わけではない","note":"Rejecting a hasty conclusion"},{"lhs":"〜からといって","rhs":"〜てはいけない / 〜べきではない","note":"Criticizing / prohibiting an action"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本人","r":"にほんじん","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"だからといって","r":"だからといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"みんな","m":"everyone"},{"t":"寿司","r":"すし","m":"sushi"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"わけではない","m":"does not mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Being Japanese does not mean that everyone likes sushi.","scene":"Discussing cultural stereotypes"},{"tokens":[{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"だからといって","r":"だからといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"安全","r":"あんぜん","m":"safe"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"限らない","r":"かぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because it is quiet does not necessarily mean it is safe.","scene":"Looking for a rental and avoiding overconfidence"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-021||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜だからといって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だからといって","patternReading":"だからといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main uses: **① rejecting wrongful actions** (the reason is not enough to do something bad), **② rejecting hasty conclusions** (A does not imply B), and **③ rejecting stereotypes** (assigning a trait to a group).","nuance":"This is a pattern for critical reasoning. The speaker acknowledges A but blocks the A→B inference. It is often used to gently criticize a colleague, teach a child, or write social commentary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"成績","r":"せいせき","m":"grades"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"優秀","r":"ゆうしゅう","m":"excellent"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"人材","r":"じんざい","m":"talent"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"限らない","r":"かぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Good grades do not necessarily mean someone is outstanding talent.","scene":"Hiring interview; avoid judging by grades"},{"tokens":[{"t":"うまくいかない","m":"not go well"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"あきらめて","m":"give up"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いけない","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because things are not going well does not mean you should give up.","scene":"Encouraging a struggling colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-021||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜だからといって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だからといって","patternReading":"だからといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜だからといって with similar patterns to see the differences: **rejecting an excuse, hypothetical concession, unexpected reality, simple contrast, and ordinary cause and effect**.","nuance":"だからといって does more than contrast; it **refutes someone else's reasoning**. としても presents a hypothetical situation; のに expresses surprise at reality; けれど simply joins contrasting clauses; だから expresses cause and effect.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"だからといって","r":"だからといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"だらだら","m":"lazily"},{"t":"過ごして","r":"すごして","m":"spend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いけない","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because it is the weekend does not mean you can spend all day being lazy.","scene":"Reminding yourself over the weekend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜としても","point":"Hypothetical concession: 'even if A…', the second clause remains unchanged. 忙しいからといって遅れてはいけない ≠ 忙しいとしても連絡ぐらいできるはずだ."},{"vs":"〜のに","point":"Reality CONTRARY to expectation; B happens unexpectedly. 忙しいからといって手伝わなくていいわけではない ≠ 忙しいのに手伝いに来てくれた."},{"vs":"〜けれども","point":"Only contrasts two clauses; it does not refute an argument. 忙しいけれど来た ≠ 忙しいからといって遅れてはいけない."},{"vs":"〜だから","point":"Ordinary cause: 'because A, B.' 忙しいから来なかった ≠ 忙しいからといって来なくていいわけではない."}]}},"n3-021||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜だからといって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だからといって","patternReading":"だからといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes by applying the 'because A, so B' logic of English to Japanese. Remember: **the second clause with だからといって cannot be an absolute affirmative**. Do not drop だ after nouns or な-adjectives.","nuance":"The most common mistake is ❌ using an affirmative clause such as '幸せだ'. This pattern needs a negative or critical second clause to mean 'A is not a valid reason for B'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ","m":"cold"},{"t":"だからといって","r":"だからといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"授業","r":"じゅぎょう","m":"class"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"休んで","r":"やすんで","m":"skip"},{"t":"いい","m":"okay"},{"t":"わけではない","m":"does not mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because you have a cold does not mean you can freely skip class.","scene":"A mother warns a child not to use illness as an excuse"}],"pitfalls":["❌ お金があるからといって、幸せだ。 → ✅ お金があるからといって、幸せとは限らない。","❌ 日本人からといって、みんな寿司が好きだ。 → ✅ 日本人だからといって、みんな寿司が好きなわけではない。","❌ 忙しいから、礼儀を忘れていい。 → ✅ 忙しいからといって、礼儀を忘れてはいけない。","❌ 高いからといって、いいものだ。 → ✅ 高いからといって、いいものとは限らない。"],"compare":null}},"n3-021||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜だからといって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だからといって","patternReading":"だからといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Split the pattern into **だから (so/because) + といって (saying that)** → literally, 'saying because of that… [not necessarily]'. It blocks the use of reason A as an excuse for B.","nuance":"This is purely a memory aid, not an additional grammatical nuance. Picture one person giving a reason and the other replying: 'Yes, A is true, but A is not an excuse for B!'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-021||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜だからといって · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だからといって","patternReading":"だからといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean ~ / does not permit ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"Typical combinations common in tests and conversation: **〜とは限らない**, **〜わけではない**, and **〜てはいけない**. だからといって can also begin a sentence to refute a preceding idea.","nuance":"〜わけではない sounds natural in conversation; 〜とは限らない is slightly more formal. 〜てはいけない clearly expresses prohibition or criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"だからといって","r":"だからといって","m":"just because..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何でも","r":"なんでも","m":"anything"},{"t":"許される","r":"ゆるされる","m":"be forgiven"},{"t":"わけじゃない","m":"does not mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Being friends does not mean that anything is forgivable.","scene":"Discussing boundaries in friendship"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-022||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜のあまり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜のあまり","patternReading":"のあまり","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ing too much (extreme emotion) → an uncontrollable action","slug":"core","definition":"〜のあまり describes an emotion or state that becomes so intense that it spills over into an unintended action or reaction. It means “because of ~ing too much” or “due to an excess of ~.” The emotion is the cause, while the following clause is an uncontrollable result, such as crying, trembling, freezing, or losing sleep.","nuance":"This is a formal, written-style pattern often found in novels, newspapers, and speeches. In daily conversation, Japanese speakers more often use 〜すぎて. のあまり emphasizes the extreme intensity of an emotion and presents the reaction objectively: “I did not mean to; the emotion was too strong for me to control.” The result is usually negative or unusual, and it is not used for intentional, planned actions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"嬉しさ","r":"うれしさ","m":"joy"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"涙","r":"なみだ","m":"tears"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"come out"},{"t":"た"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so happy that tears came to my eyes.","scene":"Hearing you passed university after days of worry"},{"tokens":[{"t":"悲しみ","r":"かなしみ","m":"sadness"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"meals"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"取れなかった","r":"とれなかった","m":"could not eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so overcome with grief that I could not eat.","scene":"Losing a loved one and having no appetite"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-022||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜のあまり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜のあまり","patternReading":"のあまり","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ing too much (extreme emotion) → an uncontrollable action","slug":"forms","definition":"The most common structure is N + の + あまり, where N must be a noun expressing an emotion or extreme state, such as 喜び, 悲しみ, 緊張, 驚き, 心配, or 疲れ. With an い-adjective, nominalize it by adding さ (例: 嬉しい → 嬉しさ). There is also Vた + あまり（に）, though it is less common and more formal.","nuance":"The form 〜のあまりに, with に added, is also fully standard and slightly more formal. のあまり and のあまりに can be used interchangeably. With Vた, it emphasizes that the completed action was excessive and then produced an uncontrollable result.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (emotion)","rhs":"のあまり + V (unintentional)","note":"Nouns for extreme emotions or states: 喜び, 悲しみ, 緊張, 驚き, 心配, 疲れ"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"あまり（に） + V","note":"Less common, formal form"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aさ + のあまり","note":"Must nominalize with さ: 嬉しい → 嬉しさ"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"な + あまり","note":"Very rare; mainly literary"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"緊張","r":"きんちょう","m":"nervousness"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"声","r":"こえ","m":"voice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"震え","r":"ふるえ","m":"tremble"},{"t":"た"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so nervous that my voice trembled.","scene":"Speaking before a crowd for the first time"},{"tokens":[{"t":"驚き","r":"おどろき","m":"surprise"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"words"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"come out"},{"t":"なかった"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so surprised that I could not speak.","scene":"Meeting a friend thought missing for years"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-022||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜のあまり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜のあまり","patternReading":"のあまり","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ing too much (extreme emotion) → an uncontrollable action","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜のあまり when an intense emotion breaks through the limits of physical or mental control and creates an unusual result. Common nouns include 喜び (joy), 悲しみ (sadness), 驚き (surprise), 怒り (anger), 緊張 (nervousness), 心配 (worry), and 感動 (emotion).","nuance":"This pattern is mainly used in writing, especially narration or news reports, to describe extreme human reactions. In conversation it can sound literary or humorous unless the speaker wants a serious emphatic tone. The following clause is usually an unwanted action: 泣いてしまった, 言いすぎた, or 失神した.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"worry"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"眠れ","r":"ねむれ","m":"sleep"},{"t":"なかった"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so worried that I could not sleep.","scene":"Tossing and turning the night before an interview"},{"tokens":[{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"passing"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"喜び","r":"よろこび","m":"joy"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大声","r":"おおごえ","m":"loudly"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"叫んでしまった","r":"さけんでしまった","m":"ended up shouting"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so happy to pass that I shouted loudly.","scene":"Seeing your name on the successful candidates list"},{"tokens":[{"t":"疲れ","r":"つかれ","m":"exhaustion"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"立ったまま","r":"たったまま","m":"while standing"},{"t":"寝てしまった","r":"ねてしまった","m":"fell asleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so tired that I fell asleep standing up.","scene":"Dozing off while waiting for the subway after work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-022||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with すぎて・ばかりに・ほど","sub":"〜のあまり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜のあまり","patternReading":"のあまり","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ing too much (extreme emotion) → an uncontrollable action","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse 〜のあまり with 〜すぎて, 〜ばかりに, and 〜ほど. The differences involve nuance and the words they combine with: のあまり takes emotion nouns and emphasizes an extreme emotion causing loss of control; すぎて takes adjectives or verbs and is neutral and conversational; ばかりに expresses regret or blame; ほど expresses the degree of a quality.","nuance":"Formality ranking: のあまり is very formal and written; すぎて is neutral and common in speech; ばかりに is somewhat conversational but strongly regretful; ほど is neutral and flexible.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"嬉しさ","r":"うれしさ","m":"joy"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泣","r":"な","m":"cry"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so happy that I cried (emotion overflowing).","scene":"Recounting the moment you received an award"},{"tokens":[{"t":"嬉しすぎて","r":"うれしすぎて","m":"too happy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泣","r":"な","m":"cry"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so happy that I cried (casual speech).","scene":"A close friend sharing feelings at a café"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜すぎて","point":"すぎて works with all adjectives and verbs, is less formal, and is common in conversation; のあまり usually takes emotion nouns and rarely follows V directly, with a written style: 緊張しすぎて何も言えなかった (spoken) ↔ 緊張のあまり声が震えた (written)."},{"vs":"〜ばかりに","point":"ばかりに means “just because ~” and always carries regret or a bad result; のあまり only describes an excessive emotion and need not be negative: 寝坊したばかりに遅刻した (regret for oversleeping) ↔ 驚きのあまり言葉が出なかった (describing a reaction)."},{"vs":"〜ほど","point":"ほど expresses degree through comparison (“so tired you could die”) and does not imply a cause-and-effect relationship; のあまり always presents a cause leading to an uncontrollable reaction: 死ぬほど疲れた (degree only) ↔ 疲れのあまり立ったまま寝てしまった (so tired that you fell asleep standing)."}]}},"n3-022||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜のあまり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜のあまり","patternReading":"のあまり","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ing too much (extreme emotion) → an uncontrollable action","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes learners make with 〜のあまり. Mistake 1 is using it with a noun that is not an emotion; のあまり takes nouns expressing an extreme state. Mistake 2 is forgetting to nominalize an adjective. Mistake 3 is using an intentional action in the following clause; のあまり requires an unintended result. Mistake 4 is overusing it in speech, making the sentence sound bookish.","nuance":"Note the distinction: 〜のあまり does not simply attach to nouns such as 楽しさ (mild enjoyment) or 暑さ (heat) before an intentional action. It should express an emotion that causes a person to lose control. For mild emotions, use すぎて.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"感動","r":"かんどう","m":"emotion"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"涙","r":"なみだ","m":"tears"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"come out"},{"t":"た"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so moved that tears came to my eyes.","scene":"Watching a moving film in a theater"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 楽しいのあまり遅刻した → ✅ 楽しすぎて遅刻した (being happy does not cause an uncontrollable reaction)","❌ 嬉しいのあまり泣いた → ✅ 嬉しさのあまり泣いた (Aい must be nominalized with さ)","❌ 感動のあまり、プレゼントを買った (intentional action) → ✅ 感動のあまり、涙が出た","❌ Conversation: 緊張のあまり何も言えなかった → ✅ 緊張しすぎて何も言えなかった (more natural)"],"compare":null}},"n3-022||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"〜のあまり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜のあまり","patternReading":"のあまり","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ing too much (extreme emotion) → an uncontrollable action","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory image: a glass overflowing. あまり means the excess; when emotion rises above the rim, it spills out as an uncontrollable action. Mnemonic: Emotion spills → unusual action.","nuance":"This helps you remember the conditions: N must be a strong emotion, and the following clause must be an involuntary, unintended result. If the emotion is mild, this pattern is not a good fit; think of すぎて instead.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-022||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜のあまり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜のあまり","patternReading":"のあまり","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ing too much (extreme emotion) → an uncontrollable action","slug":"phrases","definition":"These typical emotion-noun phrases are almost fixed expressions with のあまり. Memorizing them helps you recognize the pattern quickly in reading questions and newspaper articles.","nuance":"All of these phrases have a written-style tone. In conversation, replace them with すぎて plus a verb or adjective with the same meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"喜び","r":"よろこび","m":"joy"},{"t":"のあまり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"踊り出した","r":"おどりだした","m":"started dancing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was so happy that I started dancing.","scene":"Shouting with joy after hearing your sister had a baby"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-023||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ばかりに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad outcome and deep regret","slug":"core","definition":"〜ばかりに expresses that **one single reason** led to a **negative outcome**, with the speaker feeling **deep regret**. It means *just because ~* or *simply because ~*. The nuance is: \"If A had not happened, B would not have happened.\"","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, often in stories involving regret. It is stronger and more personal than せいで. Do not use it for good results.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ついた","r":"ついた","m":"told"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"みんな","m":"everyone"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"信頼","r":"しんらい","m":"trust"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"失った","r":"うしなった","m":"lost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I lied, I lost everyone's trust.","scene":"Telling how one lie cost trust at work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"正直","r":"しょうじき","m":"honest"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"損","r":"そん","m":"loss"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I am too honest, I sometimes lose out.","scene":"A friend complains that honesty leads to being used"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-023||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"〜ばかりに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad outcome and deep regret","slug":"forms","definition":"These are the connection patterns for 〜ばかりに. **The following clause is always a bad or regrettable result.** The verb form **Vた** (a completed event) is most common. な-adjectives and nouns can also be used, which is an often-overlooked point.","nuance":"Do not use Vる for habits or future events. With な-adjectives and nouns, add な or である.","structure":[{"lhs":"V た","rhs":"V た + ばかりに + bad result","note":"completed action; speaker regrets it"},{"lhs":"A い","rhs":"A い + ばかりに + bad result","note":"a quality directly causes the result"},{"lhs":"A な","rhs":"A な + ばかりに / A な + である + ばかりに + bad result","note":"a な-adjective before a negative result"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + である + ばかりに + bad result","note":"a noun: status or attribute causes trouble"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"余計","r":"よけい","m":"unnecessary"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言った","r":"いった","m":"said"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"関係","r":"かんけい","m":"relationship"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"悪化","r":"あっか","m":"worsened"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I said something unnecessary, our relationship worsened.","scene":"Regretting an unnecessary remark during an argument"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"下手","r":"へた","m":"poor"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"誤解","r":"ごかい","m":"misunderstood"},{"t":"され"},{"t":"た"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because my Japanese was poor, I was misunderstood.","scene":"Explaining being misunderstood by coworkers due to weak Japanese"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-023||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜ばかりに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad outcome and deep regret","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to stress that **one single reason** caused a bad result. The following clause often describes a regret, such as losing an opportunity, failing, or being blamed. The speaker thinks: *If only I had not done A...*","nuance":"Somewhat formal; common in sincere apologies, memoirs, and lessons learned. The following clause often uses 〜てしまった or 〜される.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"長男","r":"ちょうなん","m":"eldest son"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多く","r":"おおく","m":"many"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibilities"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"負わされた","r":"おわされた","m":"was made to bear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I am the eldest son, I have to bear many responsibilities.","scene":"Complaining about pressure as the eldest child"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"trip"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行けなかった","r":"いけなかった","m":"could not go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I had no money, I could not go on the trip.","scene":"Sadly explaining missing a trip with friends due to poverty"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-023||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with せいで・のあまり・ばかりか","sub":"〜ばかりに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad outcome and deep regret","slug":"compare","definition":"Three patterns are especially easy to confuse. In short: **ばかりに = one cause that makes the speaker regret**, **せいで = blame directed at an external cause**, **のあまり = extreme emotion**, and **ばかりか = escalating addition**.","nuance":"They differ in degree of regret and who receives the blame. ばかりに tends toward deep self-blame.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"being late"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"機会","r":"きかい","m":"opportunity"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"逃した","r":"のがした","m":"missed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I was late, I missed the opportunity.","scene":"Blaming oneself for losing an important chance after oversleeping"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜せいで","point":"せいで blames an outside cause or circumstance and does not necessarily show self-blame. ばかりに points to one's own cause and deep regret. Example: 雨のせいで中止になった vs 遅刻したばかりに機会を逃した."},{"vs":"〜のあまり","point":"のあまり describes an extreme emotion leading to uncontrolled action. ばかりに describes one event causing a bad result, with regret. Example: 緊張のあまり声が震えた vs 緊張したばかりに失敗した."},{"vs":"〜ばかりか","point":"ばかりか adds an escalating item: 'not only A but also B.' ばかりに states one cause of a bad result. Example: 彼は英語ばかりか中国語も話せる vs 彼は失敗したばかりに自信を失った."}]}},"n3-023||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ばかりに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad outcome and deep regret","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using it with a good result, using it when there are multiple causes, or confusing it with ばかりか.","nuance":"Always check the following clause: it must be negative and regretful. There should be only one cause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"払った","r":"はらった","m":"paid"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"life"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"苦しく","r":"くるしく","m":"difficult"},{"t":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I paid a sum of money, life became difficult.","scene":"Explaining how debt made life a struggle"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 一生懸命勉強したばかりに合格した。 → ✅ 一生懸命勉強したおかげで合格した。","❌ 雨が降ったし、時間もなかったばかりに、行けなかった。 → ✅ 時間がなかったばかりに、行けなかった。","❌ 彼は英語ができるばかりに、性格もいい。 → ✅ 彼は英語ができるばかりか、性格もいい。"],"compare":null}},"n3-023||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ばかりに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad outcome and deep regret","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick reminder: ばかり = 'only'; ばかりに = 'just because of that'. Picture a small nail puncturing a tire: a tiny cause creates a major consequence worth regretting.","nuance":"Emphasize 'one and only' and 'regret'—that is the heart of this pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-023||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ばかりに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad outcome and deep regret","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases in exams and everyday life include **〜たばかりに〜てしまった**, **〜ないばかりに〜**, and **〜が下手なばかりに〜**.","nuance":"Learn these as chunks for faster, more natural use. The following clause is always negative.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"知らなかった","r":"しらなかった","m":"did not know"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I did not know, I ended up failing.","scene":"Regretting not gathering enough information beforehand"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-024||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜以上は／以上 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以上は／以上","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (duty from commitment)","slug":"core","definition":"〜以上は expresses that **once you have made a commitment, decision, or taken on a role**, the following clause states an unavoidable **duty or determination**. It marks a point of no return: once you pass it, you must move forward. The speaker clearly shows a sense of responsibility, so the following clause is usually obligatory.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, but it leans toward **formal statements**, workplaces, and speeches. It expresses **logical duty** arising from circumstances, without requiring strong emotion. Compared with からには, 以上は is **calmer and firmer**, emphasizing responsibility more than personal resolve. It often appears with 最後まで, 全力を尽くす, and 責任を取る.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"surely"},{"t":"守ら","r":"まもら","m":"keep"},{"t":"なければならない","r":"なければならない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you have made a promise, you must keep it.","scene":"Reminding someone about an important promise"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"引き受けた","r":"ひきうけた","m":"accepted"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"end"},{"t":"まで","m":"until"},{"t":"頑張ります","r":"がんばります","m":"do my best"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Now that I have accepted this job, I will do my best to the end.","scene":"An employee commits to a boss before a project"},{"tokens":[{"t":"教師","r":"きょうし","m":"teacher"},{"t":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生徒","r":"せいと","m":"students"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"誠実","r":"せいじつ","m":"honest"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should/must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As a teacher, one must be honest with one's students.","scene":"Discussing a teacher's professional responsibility"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-024||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜以上は／以上 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以上は／以上","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (duty from commitment)","slug":"forms","definition":"There are three main forms: **V 辞書形 + 以上は**, **V た + 以上は**, and **N / Na + である + 以上は**. The following clause expresses **duty or determination**. The form 以上 without は is also correct; は adds emphasis. The following clause often uses 〜なければならない, 〜べきだ, 〜てほしい, 〜つもりだ, or 〜はずだ.","nuance":"With **V 辞書形**, the commitment is made at the moment of speaking. With **V た**, the action has **already been completed**, making the responsibility clearer. With N/Na, **である is required**; do not use N + 以上 directly. This pattern is common in writing and formal statements, not casual conversation.","structure":[{"lhs":"V 辞書形","rhs":"以上は ＋ V（義務・決意）","note":"A commitment made now. Example: 約束する以上は…"},{"lhs":"V た","rhs":"以上は ＋ V（義務・決意）","note":"Already done → must continue. Example: 引き受けた以上は…"},{"lhs":"N / Na","rhs":"である ＋ 以上は ＋ V","note":"Role → responsibility. Example: 教師である以上は…"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強する","r":"べんきょうする","m":"study"},{"t":"つもりだ","r":"つもりだ","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Now that I have come to Japan, I intend to study Japanese.","scene":"A new international student discusses a study plan"},{"tokens":[{"t":"社会人","r":"しゃかいじん","m":"working adult"},{"t":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持つ","r":"もつ","m":"have"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should/must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As a working adult, one should take responsibility.","scene":"A worker advises a new graduate"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-024||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜以上は／以上 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以上は／以上","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (duty from commitment)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜以上は is used when the speaker emphasizes **responsibility arising from an event that has occurred or a role they hold**. Meaning ①: once you commit (accept a job, make a promise, or decide), you must act. Meaning ②: once you take on a role (teacher, parent, worker), you must behave accordingly. The following clause usually expresses duty.","nuance":"It appears more often in **formal email, speeches, and declarations** than in casual chat. The speaker sounds **serious and mature**. Do not use it for trivial matters or random actions without responsibility. Omitting は is correct, but は gives the duty more emphasis.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"participate"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全力","r":"ぜんりょく","m":"full effort"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出す","r":"だす","m":"give"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should/must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you enter the match, you must give it your all.","scene":"An athlete motivates themself before a match"},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parent"},{"t":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"考え","r":"かんがえ","m":"think about"},{"t":"なければならない","r":"なければならない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As parents, we must think about our children.","scene":"A father discusses family responsibility"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強する","r":"べんきょうする","m":"study"},{"t":"以上","r":"いじょう","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you study Japanese, you need to practice every day.","scene":"Discussing foreign-language study"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-024||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with からには・べきだ","sub":"〜以上は／以上 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以上は／以上","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (duty from commitment)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜以上は from other patterns expressing reasons or duty. **以上は** emphasizes **logical duty** arising from an established commitment or situation. **からには** also expresses duty, but emphasizes **personal determination and stronger emotion**. **ので/から** only state a reason, without responsibility. **べきだ** expresses general moral duty, not one tied to a specific commitment.","nuance":"In communication, 以上は often shows that the speaker is **mature and rational**. からには leans toward the will to follow through once a decision is made. べきだ is more like advice and is less binding. All can appear in writing, but 以上は and からには have a clearer responsibility nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participation"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"end"},{"t":"まで","m":"until"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"take"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since you joined, please take responsibility to the end.","scene":"A group leader reminds a member"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜からには","point":"以上は logical duty; からには determination + emotion"},{"vs":"〜ので／から","point":"以上は carries duty; ので/から only gives a reason"},{"vs":"〜べきだ","point":"べきだ is moral duty; 以上は duty from a commitment/role"}]}},"n3-024||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜以上は／以上 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以上は／以上","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (duty from commitment)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three main mistakes: **putting a question or request after it** (the following clause must express duty or determination); **confusing it with 以上に/以上 for comparison or quantity** (a completely different meaning); and **forgetting である after nouns or na-adjectives**. Remember: 以上は requires an obligatory following clause, not a choice.","nuance":"Mistake 1 breaks the responsibility meaning. Mistake 2 causes a grammar misunderstanding. Mistake 3 makes the structure incorrect. All commonly appear in JLPT N2 questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"surely"},{"t":"守ら","r":"まもら","m":"keep"},{"t":"なければならない","r":"なければならない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you have made a promise, you must keep it.","scene":"A model sentence showing correct usage"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 約束した以上は、行きますか？ → ✅ 約束した以上は、行かなければならない。","❌ 学生以上、勉強するべきだ → ✅ 学生である以上、勉強するべきだ。","❌ 雨が降った以上、濡れた → ✅ 雨が降ったので、濡れた。"],"compare":null}},"n3-024||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"〜以上は／以上 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以上は／以上","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (duty from commitment)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **a boat that has left the harbor**: once it has weighed anchor, it cannot turn back and must continue. 以上 literally means 'above / beyond'; once you pass that point, there is no way back. This pattern therefore carries the idea: 'once committed, you must follow through.'","nuance":"This memory tip helps you remember that the following clause must express duty, because there is no other choice.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-024||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜以上は／以上 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以上は／以上","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (duty from commitment)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases include **〜た以上は最後まで** (once you have ~, take it to the end), **〜である以上〜べきだ** (as a ~, one must), and **〜以上は全力を尽くす** (once you have ~, give it your all). They often appear in exams and speeches.","nuance":"These phrases express determination to fulfill one's responsibility, often in workplaces, sports, and study.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"引き受けた","r":"ひきうけた","m":"accepted"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once ~, must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全力","r":"ぜんりょく","m":"full effort"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"尽くします","r":"つくします","m":"give my all"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Now that I have accepted this job, I will give it my all.","scene":"An employee commits to a boss"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-025||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜さえ〜ば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ〜ば","patternReading":"さえ〜ば","coreMeaning":"Just ~ is enough (minimum condition)","slug":"core","definition":"〜さえ〜ば expresses **one minimum, sufficient condition**—having or not having X is enough to achieve the desired result. The speaker emphasizes that **nothing else is needed**. This is the central Japanese structure for 'just ~ is enough.'","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese, from everyday conversation to literature and song lyrics. It has a **decisive, reassuring** nuance: one specific factor is enough to make everything work. Compared with neutral 〜ば, さえ〜ば emphasizes being **minimal** and **sufficient**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"anything"},{"t":"でも","m":"also"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you have your health, you can do anything.","scene":"Discussing the most important condition for success"},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行ける","r":"いける","m":"can go"},{"t":"のに","m":"if only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only I had the time, I could go.","scene":"Regretting not having time to attend an event"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-025||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"〜さえ〜ば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ〜ば","patternReading":"さえ〜ば","coreMeaning":"Just ~ is enough (minimum condition)","slug":"forms","definition":"Four main forms are **N + さえ + Vば**, **Vます-stem + さえすれば**, **Aい + くさえあれば**, and the negative form **N + さえなければ**. You may also see **Vて + さえいれば**, emphasizing a continuing state.","nuance":"The conditional clause always ends in **ば**. The negative form **さえなければ** means 'if only X were absent' and is often used when X is the sole obstacle.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + さえ + Vば","note":"As long as there is N..."},{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"Vます-stem + さえすれば","note":"As long as one does V..."},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aく + さえあれば","note":"As long as the state is A..."},{"lhs":"N（negative）","rhs":"N + さえなければ","note":"As long as there is no N..."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"すれば","r":"すれば","m":"if doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"上手く","r":"うまく","m":"well"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you practice, you'll improve.","scene":"Encouraging a friend to practice diligently"},{"tokens":[{"t":"暖かく","r":"あたたかく","m":"warm"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこ","r":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"でも","m":"also"},{"t":"大丈夫","r":"だいじょうぶ","m":"fine"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as it's warm, anywhere is fine.","scene":"Discussing the condition needed for comfort"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-025||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜さえ〜ば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ〜ば","patternReading":"さえ〜ば","coreMeaning":"Just ~ is enough (minimum condition)","slug":"usage","definition":"Meaning 1: **one sufficient condition**—X is the key, and Y will certainly happen if X exists. Meaning 2: **negative**—'if only X were absent,' X being the sole obstacle.","nuance":"The following clause often uses **〜できる** (can), **〜いい** (that is enough), or **〜のに** (if only). With のに, the speaker clearly expresses **regret**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"anything"},{"t":"でも","m":"also"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you have money, you can do anything.","scene":"Discussing money's power at work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"降らなければ","r":"ふらなければ","m":"if it doesn't fall"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遠足","r":"えんそく","m":"field trip"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行けた","r":"いけた","m":"could have gone"},{"t":"のに","m":"if only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only it hadn't rained, we could have gone on the field trip.","scene":"Regretting that rain canceled the trip"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"いて","r":"いて","m":"be here"},{"t":"くれれば","r":"くれれば","m":"for me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"他","r":"ほか","m":"other"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"anything"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いらない","r":"いらない","m":"don't need"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you're by my side, I don't need anything else.","scene":"A romantic confession in a film"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-025||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜さえ〜ば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ〜ば","patternReading":"さえ〜ば","coreMeaning":"Just ~ is enough (minimum condition)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare easily confused patterns to choose the right nuance: **さえ〜ば** emphasizes one minimum sufficient condition; **ば** is simply conditional; **だけ** limits quantity; standalone **さえ** emphasizes 'even'; **ても** expresses concession.","nuance":"Use さえ〜ば when you want to assert that 'this ONE thing is enough' and nothing else is needed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"買える","r":"かえる","m":"can buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you have money, you can buy it.","scene":"Quick comparison with ordinary ば"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ば","point":"ば is neutral conditional; さえ〜ば emphasizes 'this minimum condition alone is enough.' お金があれば買える → if you have money, you can buy it; お金さえあれば買える → just money is enough, nothing else is needed."},{"vs":"〜だけ","point":"だけ limits the scope to 'only X'; さえ〜ば emphasizes that X is the minimum sufficient condition. これだけあれば → this amount is enough; これさえあれば → this exact thing alone is enough."},{"vs":"〜さえ (không có ば)","point":"Standalone さえ = 'even X' (an extreme case); さえ〜ば = 'if only X' (a condition). 水さえ飲めない → I can't even drink water; 水さえあれば大丈夫 → as long as there is water, it's fine."},{"vs":"〜ても","point":"ても = 'even if X' (the condition does not prevent the result); さえ〜ば = 'as long as X exists' (a sufficient condition). お金があっても無理 → even with money, it's impossible; お金さえあればできる → as long as you have money, you can do it."}]}},"n3-025||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜さえ〜ば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ〜ば","patternReading":"さえ〜ば","coreMeaning":"Just ~ is enough (minimum condition)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with さえ〜ば: forgetting ば, conjugating verbs incorrectly, using an illogical condition, or confusing it with standalone さえ.","nuance":"Remember: さえ and ば work together in this structure. Without ば, the meaning changes completely.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行ける","r":"いける","m":"can go"},{"t":"のに","m":"if only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as I have the time, I can go.","scene":"Correct sentence fixing the missing ば"}],"pitfalls":["❌ お金さえある、何でもできる → ✅ お金さえあれば、何でもできる","❌ 食べるさえすれば → ✅ 食べさえすれば (Vます-stem + さえすれば)","❌ 死んでさえいれば生きられる → ✅ 生きてさえいれば、何とかなる","❌ 水さえあれば飲めない → ✅ 水さえ飲めない = even water cannot be drunk (さえ without ば)"],"compare":null}},"n3-025||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜さえ〜ば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ〜ば","patternReading":"さえ〜ば","coreMeaning":"Just ~ is enough (minimum condition)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine さえ as **the one key** and ば as **the lock**. With this exact key, the door opens and things succeed. When you mean 'just ~ is enough,' think of さえ〜ば.","nuance":"This mnemonic helps you remember that さえ emphasizes the minimum element, while ば creates the condition. No complicated conditions are needed; one core factor is enough.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-025||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜さえ〜ば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜さえ〜ば","patternReading":"さえ〜ば","coreMeaning":"Just ~ is enough (minimum condition)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases often seen in the JLPT and everyday life: **N + さえあれば〜できる**, **Vます-stem + さえすれば**, **N + さえなければ**, and **Vて + さえいれば**.","nuance":"Memorize these phrases for quick recognition in listening and reading. In particular, **〜さえあれば〜できる** appears very often in test questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just need"},{"t":"いれば","r":"いれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happy"},{"t":"だ"}],"vi":"As long as I have you, I'm happy.","scene":"A confession or message to a loved one"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-026||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜からには／からは · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜からには／からは","patternReading":"からには／からは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (determination + duty)","slug":"core","definition":"〜からには expresses **strong determination or duty** arising from something that has happened or been decided. It can be translated as “once you have ~, you must ~.” This is not a simple reason, but the speaker’s **personal commitment**.","nuance":"Usable in both speech and writing, with a fairly formal tone. It conveys **stronger personal will** than 以上は. It often combines with 〜ぞ and 〜よ to show determined emotion. Do not use it for unintended events such as rain.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強する","r":"べんきょうする","m":"study"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"したい","r":"したい","m":"want to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I study Japanese, I want to pass.","scene":"A student sets a determined Japanese-learning goal"},{"tokens":[{"t":"やる","r":"やる","m":"do"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"決めた","r":"きめた","m":"decided to"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"end"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"頑張る","r":"がんばる","m":"do my best"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Now that I’ve decided to do it, I’ll see it through to the end!","scene":"Talking to oneself when starting a difficult project"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-026||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜からには／からは · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜からには／からは","patternReading":"からには／からは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (determination + duty)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern follows the **V dictionary form**, **Vた form**, or **N + である**. The following clause must express a **volitional action**: 〜たい, 〜つもりだ, 〜べきだ, 〜なければならない, and so on.","nuance":"〜からは is similar in meaning to 〜からには but is **less common** in modern Japanese, often appearing in firm declarations or a somewhat formal/classical style. 〜からには is common in both speech and writing and is much more frequent.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"からには + volitional V","note":"Decide to do → determination (例: 入るからには)"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"からには + 〜なければならない／べきだ","note":"Something happened → duty (例: 約束したからには)"},{"lhs":"N + である","rhs":"からには + 〜べきだ／〜なければならない","note":"Status → responsibility (例: リーダーであるからには)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"made"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"絶対に","r":"ぜったいに","m":"definitely"},{"t":"守って","r":"まもって","m":"keep"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you’ve made a promise, you must keep it.","scene":"Reminding someone to keep a promise"},{"tokens":[{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"take the field"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勝つ","r":"かつ","m":"win"},{"t":"つもり","r":"つもり","m":"intention"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"戦え","r":"たたかえ","m":"fight"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you take the field, fight with the intention of winning.","scene":"A coach speaking to an athlete"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-026||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜からには／からは · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜からには／からは","patternReading":"からには／からは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (determination + duty)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜からには is used to **declare determination** (1) or **emphasize duty** (2) arising from a situation. It can also **ask someone else** to fulfill an obligation (3), and is common in disciplined settings such as sports and the workplace (4, 5).","nuance":"Complete sentences often use final particles **ぞ／よ／ね** to add emotion. Do not use it for unintended objective results.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"リーダー","r":"りーだー","m":"team leader"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チーム","r":"ちーむ","m":"team"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"まとめ","r":"まとめ","m":"lead"},{"t":"なければならない","r":"なければならない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I’m the team leader, I must lead the whole team.","scene":"A team leader recognizing their responsibility"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"マスターしたい","r":"ますたーしたい","m":"want to master"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Now that I’ve come to Japan, I want to master Japanese.","scene":"An international student committing to language study"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-026||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing from 以上は・から","sub":"〜からには／からは · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜からには／からは","patternReading":"からには／からは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (determination + duty)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜からには from 〜以上は, 〜から, and 〜だからこそ—three patterns that are easy to confuse because they all express a “because → therefore” relationship.","nuance":"からには carries a **determined** tone, 以上は a **responsible** tone, から simply states a **cause**, and だからこそ **emphasizes a special reason**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"挑戦する","r":"ちょうせんする","m":"take on"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"end"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"頑張りたい","r":"がんばりたい","m":"want to persist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I take on this challenge, I want to persist to the end.","scene":"Determination when starting a challenge"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜以上は","point":"以上は emphasizes logical duty arising from a situation; からには emphasizes personal will. 例: 引き受けた以上は最後まで / やると決めたからには最後まで"},{"vs":"〜から","point":"から gives a neutral reason and works for objective events; からには is a commitment to act and is used when the first clause involves the speaker’s decision or role. 例: 電車が遅れたから遅刻した (reason) / 引き受けたからには最後までやる (commitment)"},{"vs":"〜だからこそ","point":"だからこそ emphasizes the reason leading to a result; からには emphasizes determination after making a decision. 例: 難しいからこそ挑戦する / 挑戦するからには勝つ"}]}},"n3-026||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜からには／からは · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜からには／からは","patternReading":"からには／からは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (determination + duty)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three main mistakes with this pattern: using an uncontrollable natural event in the following clause, confusing it with から, which merely states a reason, or missing its determined nuance compared with 以上は. Remember that the following clause of からには must always express a volitional action or duty, such as 〜たい, 〜べきだ, or 〜なければならない.","nuance":"Learners often mechanically apply a “because, so” meaning and use an uncontrollable event in the following clause, or miss the nuance that differs from 以上は.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"made"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"絶対に","r":"ぜったいに","m":"definitely"},{"t":"守って","r":"まもって","m":"keep"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you’ve made a promise, you must definitely keep it.","scene":"Reminding someone to keep a promise"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降ったからには傘を持つ。 → ✅ 雨が降っているから傘を持つ。 (the second clause must express will; rain is a cause, not a commitment)","❌ 電車が遅れたからには遅刻だ。 → ✅ 電車が遅れたから遅刻だ。 (the second clause is an obvious result; use から)","❌ 約束したからには破る。 → ✅ 約束したからには守る。 (the second clause must be a duty-related follow-up action)"],"compare":null}},"n3-026||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜からには／からは · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜からには／からは","patternReading":"からには／からは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (determination + duty)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine an actor who has already stepped onto the stage and must perform with all their might, unable to stop halfway. Likewise, からには conveys the will that “once you do it, you must see it through.” Quick memory aid: から (because) + には (for) = once you have ~, then for that matter you must...","nuance":"This memory tip draws on Japanese cultural ideas: the Bushido spirit and perseverance (根性) are highly valued in Japanese society.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-026||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜からには／からは · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜からには／からは","patternReading":"からには／からは","coreMeaning":"Once you have ~, you must ~ (determination + duty)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed expressions using からには appear frequently in conversation and on exams.","nuance":"The phrase やるからには全力で！ is a common rallying cry in sports and the workplace.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"やる","r":"やる","m":"do"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once ~"},{"t":"全力","r":"ぜんりょく","m":"full effort"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"If you’re going to do it, give it your all!","scene":"An encouraging sports slogan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-027||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると","patternReading":"としたら とすれば とすると","coreMeaning":"If we suppose ~ (hypothesis → inference)","slug":"core","definition":"The three forms 〜としたら／とすれば／とすると all express a hypothesis: the speaker sets up situation X (which may be unreal) and then infers result Y. The basic meaning comes from と + する: temporarily treating X as true and reasoning from there.","nuance":"としたら is the most versatile, often used in open questions such as “If we suppose that, what would you do?” とすれば emphasizes logical inference. とすると often leads to a discovery or new realization. All three sound more hypothetical than 〜たら and often occur with もし.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","m":"if"},{"t":"宝くじ","r":"たからくじ","m":"lottery"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"当たった","r":"あたった","m":"win"},{"t":"としたら","m":"if we suppose"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you won the lottery, what would you do?","scene":"Joking with a friend who bought a lottery ticket"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"truth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"say"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"とすれば","m":"if we suppose"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"深刻","r":"しんこく","m":"serious"},{"t":"だ"}],"vi":"If he is telling the truth, the problem is serious.","scene":"Considering someone's statement during an investigation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"5分","r":"ごふん","m":"5 minutes"},{"t":"遅れて","r":"おくれて","m":"late"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"とすると","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"間に合わない","r":"まにあわない","m":"won't make it"},{"t":"な"}],"vi":"If the train is five minutes late, we won't make it.","scene":"Checking the train information board at the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-027||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると","patternReading":"としたら とすれば とすると","coreMeaning":"If we suppose ~ (hypothesis → inference)","slug":"forms","definition":"All three forms follow a clause in the plain form (普通形). With nouns and な-adjectives, だ is required: だとしたら, だとすれば, だとすると. They cannot follow the ます form.","nuance":"In spoken Japanese, としたら is most common. とすれば sounds logical or analytical. とすると sounds like the speaker is realizing something.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"としたら／とすれば／とすると","note":"例: 行くとしたら / 行ったとしたら / 行かないとしたら"},{"lhs":"イA（普通形）","rhs":"としたら／とすれば／とすると","note":"例: 安いとしたら / 安かったとしたら"},{"lhs":"N / ナA","rhs":"だとしたら／だとすれば／だとすると","note":"だ is required; do not omit it"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"としたら","m":"if we suppose"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"よ"}],"vi":"If he comes, I'll let you know.","scene":"Arranging a group meeting with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"true"},{"t":"だとすれば","m":"if that is so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大変","r":"たいへん","m":"serious"},{"t":"だ"}],"vi":"If that story is true, we're in serious trouble.","scene":"Hearing a rumor spreading around the company"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-027||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると","patternReading":"としたら とすれば とすると","coreMeaning":"If we suppose ~ (hypothesis → inference)","slug":"usage","definition":"The three forms share a basic meaning but suit different contexts. としたら is common in conversation and open questions. とすれば emphasizes logical inference and suits investigations, analysis, and work discussions. とすると often appears when a hypothesis leads to a new situation or realization.","nuance":"In meetings, Japanese speakers may begin a polite rebuttal with そうだとしたら rather than directly rejecting the other person's point. Adding もし makes the hypothesis more explicit.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし"},{"t":"転職","r":"てんしょく","m":"change jobs"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"としたら","m":"if we suppose"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どんな"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"選び","r":"えらび","m":"choose"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you changed jobs, what kind of work would you choose?","scene":"Asking a friend over lunch"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ついて","r":"ついて","m":"tell"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"とすれば","m":"if we suppose"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"動機","r":"どうき","m":"motive"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"だろう"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If she is lying, what might her motive be?","scene":"Questioning a report in a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"証拠","r":"しょうこ","m":"evidence"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"本物","r":"ほんもの","m":"genuine"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"とすると","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"事件","r":"じけん","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"複雑","r":"ふくざつ","m":"complex"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる"}],"vi":"If this evidence is genuine, the case becomes more complicated.","scene":"A detective examining new evidence"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-027||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing たら／ば／としても","sub":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると","patternReading":"としたら とすれば とすると","coreMeaning":"If we suppose ~ (hypothesis → inference)","slug":"compare","definition":"These are patterns learners often confuse. たら is a versatile conditional used for temporal sequence and discovery as well. ば expresses a general condition that is consistently true. としても is concessive: even if X is true, Y still occurs. とすると is completely different from それとも.","nuance":"としたら presents a hypothesis and inference without asserting that it will happen. としても expresses concession and often appears with でも or やはり in the second clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ったら","r":"ふったら","m":"if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持つ","r":"もつ","m":"take"}],"vi":"If it rains tomorrow, I will take an umbrella.","scene":"Checking the weather forecast"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たら","point":"たら is much broader: condition, sequence, and discovery. としたら only sets up a hypothesis for inference. 例: 東京に着いたら、電話して (call when you arrive) → としたら is not a natural replacement."},{"vs":"〜ば","point":"ば expresses a general condition: 春になれば花が咲く. とすれば sets up a specific hypothesis for reasoning: 彼が犯人とすれば、証拠が必要だ."},{"vs":"〜としても","point":"としても is concessive: 行くとしても、遅れるよ (even if we go, we'll be late). としたら is hypothetical: 行くとしたら、早めに行く (if we go, we should go early)."},{"vs":"〜それとも","point":"それとも means “or” in a choice question: コーヒー、それとも紅茶? とすると means “if so” and is used for inference."}]}},"n3-027||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると","patternReading":"としたら とすれば とすると","coreMeaning":"If we suppose ~ (hypothesis → inference)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These three forms can substitute for one another in many contexts, but avoid using としたら for events expected to happen, and avoid confusing とすると with それとも. Choose the right form when you need logical reasoning or a realization.","nuance":"For a realistic condition, use たら or ば. For concession, use としても. In conversation, all three forms may be accepted interchangeably in some cases, but written Japanese requires attention to nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ったら","r":"ふったら","m":"if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持ちます","r":"もちます","m":"take"}],"vi":"If it rains tomorrow, I will take an umbrella.","scene":"Telling family before leaving home"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 明日雨が降るとしたら傘を持つ。 → ✅ 明日雨が降ったら傘を持つ。 (the event is realistically possible; たら is enough)","❌ 彼は行くとすると、それとも行かない？ → ✅ 彼は行くとしたら、いつ行く？ (それとも means 'or,' not a hypothesis)"],"compare":null}},"n3-027||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると","patternReading":"としたら とすれば とすると","coreMeaning":"If we suppose ~ (hypothesis → inference)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: としたら means “if we suppose,” とすれば means “if that is true, then...,” and とすると means “and if so, we realize that....” All three come from と + する: set up a hypothesis and continue reasoning.","nuance":"Use としたら for questions, とすれば for reasoning, and とすると for expressing a realization.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-027||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としたら／とすれば／とすると","patternReading":"としたら とすれば とすると","coreMeaning":"If we suppose ~ (hypothesis → inference)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed expressions often appear in exams and conversation: もし〜としたら emphasizes a hypothesis, 〜だとすれば reasons from an event, and 〜とすると infers a result or discovery. All mean “if we suppose X, then Y follows,” but とすると often leads to a new conclusion, while としたら often introduces a question such as “what would you do?” or a suggestion. としたら is most common in speech, especially in sentences like もし時間があるとしたら、旅行に行きたい. とすれば is common in writing and reports: この案が採用されるとすれば、コストが増える. とすると often marks a sudden realization: 彼が来ないとすると、私たちだけで始めよう. Mastering these expressions helps with natural conversation and JLPT reading comprehension.","nuance":"These expressions usually appear at the start of a sentence or after a hypothetical clause. The second clause often contains だろう, はずだ, かもしれない, or ことになる to express an inference.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"としたら","m":"if we suppose"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きたい","r":"いきたい","m":"want to go"}],"vi":"If I had time, I would like to travel.","scene":"Answering a boss during a break"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-028||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜としても · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としても","patternReading":"としても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, the result remains unchanged","slug":"core","definition":"〜としても expresses **hypothetical concession**: even if A were to happen, the result B would remain unchanged. The speaker emphasizes that their will, attitude, or judgment will not be shaken by the hypothetical situation. The preceding event is often something that has not happened or is unreal.","nuance":"Usable in both speech and writing, with a moderately formal tone. “Even if” shows that the speaker **does not regard A as reality**, but only presents it as a hypothetical situation to test their determination. Stronger and more assertive than 〜ても; close to 〜にしても, but with a clearer hypothetical emphasis.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"としても","r":"としても","m":"even if assumed"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"挑戦","r":"ちょうせん","m":"challenge"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"価値","r":"かち","m":"value"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ある","m":"exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I fail, it will still be worth trying.","scene":"Encouraging yourself before a major challenge"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"謝る","r":"あやまる","m":"apologize"},{"t":"としても","r":"としても","m":"even if assumed"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"許す","r":"ゆるす","m":"forgive"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if he apologizes, I still cannot forgive him.","scene":"Standing firm after being betrayed"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-028||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and connections","sub":"〜としても · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としても","patternReading":"としても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, the result remains unchanged","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the connection patterns for としても. The base form is **V（普通形）＋としても**; when the preceding element is a noun or な-adjective, **だ is required** → **だとしても**. An い-adjective connects directly without だ.","nuance":"With N/ナA, だ remains from だ（+ としても）; learners often forget it. Negative and past forms follow the same rule: じゃなかったとしても, 高かったとしても. たとえ/仮に before the structure strengthens the hypothetical concession.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"＋ としても","note":"Vた/ない are both possible; た-form = assuming it happened"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ だとしても","note":"だ is required; do not omit it"},{"lhs":"ナA","rhs":"＋ だとしても","note":"Example: 静かだとしても"},{"lhs":"イA","rhs":"＋ としても","note":"Add としても directly; no だ"},{"lhs":"たとえ／仮に","rhs":"＋ 〜としても","note":"Emphasizes the hypothetical; the result remains unchanged"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"としても","r":"としても","m":"even if assumed"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancel"},{"t":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, the match will not be canceled.","scene":"Deciding to play despite a bad weather forecast"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お金持ち","r":"おかねもち","m":"wealthy person"},{"t":"だとしても","r":"だとしても","m":"even if assumed to be"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happiness"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"限らない","r":"かぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if someone is rich, they are not necessarily happy.","scene":"Advising a friend not to chase money alone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-028||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜としても · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としても","patternReading":"としても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, the result remains unchanged","slug":"usage","definition":"Main use: set up a hypothesis and then assert that the result **still remains unchanged**. It is often used when the speaker wants to maintain a firm position: “even if that is so, nothing will change.” It combines with たとえ (no matter what) or 仮に (supposing) to emphasize the hypothetical nature.","nuance":"In Japanese communication, としても is a tool for **polite rebuttal**: instead of saying “you are wrong,” a speaker may say たとえそうだとしても… and then present an opposing view. The sentence therefore often sounds determined yet polite, especially in debates, negotiations, and literary writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"仮に","r":"かりに","m":"supposing"},{"t":"そう","m":"so"},{"t":"だとしても","r":"だとしても","m":"even if it is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"解決","r":"かいけつ","m":"solution"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ならない","m":"does not become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even supposing that is true, it would solve nothing.","scene":"Concluding a point during a workplace debate"},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"戻せる","r":"もどせる","m":"can turn back"},{"t":"としても","r":"としても","m":"even if assumed"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"後悔","r":"こうかい","m":"regret"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I could turn back time, I would not regret my choice.","scene":"Staying firm about your decision"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-028||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from ても・としたら","sub":"〜としても · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としても","patternReading":"としても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, the result remains unchanged","slug":"compare","definition":"Comparison of としても with easily confused patterns. The key point is: **としても = hypothetical concession** (the result remains unchanged), while **としたら = setting up a hypothesis for inference** (the result may differ).","nuance":"〜ても is neutral and works for both real and hypothetical situations; 〜にしても has the sense “even if it is any such case” and is more formal; 〜たって is the casual spoken form of 〜ても.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"だとしても","r":"だとしても","m":"even if assumed to be"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"impossible"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even a doctor would be unable to help.","scene":"Discussing an extremely difficult medical case"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜としたら","point":"としたら = set up a hypothesis to INFER a result that may change; としても = concession, so the result remains UNCHANGED. 医者としたら vs 医者だとしても."},{"vs":"〜ても","point":"ても applies to both real and hypothetical situations and is neutral; としても emphasizes the hypothesis and a firmer result."},{"vs":"〜にしても","point":"にしても means “even if it is any case of ~,” covering a broad range; としても assumes one clearly defined situation."}]}},"n3-028||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner pitfalls","sub":"〜としても · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としても","patternReading":"としても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, the result remains unchanged","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical errors with としても. They mainly result from loosely translating it as “even if” while forgetting its **hypothetical** condition, or confusing it with としたら and ても.","nuance":"The following clause of としても must not be a result that changes according to the hypothesis; it must be a fixed conclusion. Quick check: if removing “supposing” still leaves a natural sentence, ても may be enough and としても may not be needed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"反対","r":"はんたい","m":"opposition"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"exist"},{"t":"としても","r":"としても","m":"even if assumed"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やる","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if there is opposition, I will do it.","scene":"Asserting determination to pursue an idea"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降るとしても、試合が中止になる。（the result changes with the hypothesis） → ✅ 雨が降るとしても、試合は中止しない。","❌ 彼が医者だとしたら無理だ。（confusing “if he is” with “even if he is”） → ✅ 彼が医者だとしても無理だ。","❌ 雨が降っているとしても、出かけます。（it is actually raining → use ても） → ✅ 雨が降っていても、出かけます。"],"compare":null}},"n3-028||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜としても · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としても","patternReading":"としても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, the result remains unchanged","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: としても = とする (assume or regard as) + ても (even if). Therefore, it always means “even if we assume it.” Picture a wall standing still: no matter which way the wind (the hypothesis) blows, the wall (the result) does not fall.","nuance":"Combined with たとえ, it forms たとえ〜としても. When you see たとえ at the beginning of a sentence, expect the following clause to use ても/としても and end with a firm or unchanged conclusion.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-028||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜としても · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜としても","patternReading":"としても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, the result remains unchanged","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common collocations include たとえ〜としても, 仮に〜としても, そうだとしても, and N/ナA＋だとしても. In JLPT questions, a common trap is recognizing the accompanying words that signal “hypothetical + concession.”","nuance":"たとえ makes the hypothesis strong and natural; 仮に is more formal; そうだとしても is a polite way to rebut someone during a discussion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"そう","m":"so"},{"t":"だとしても","r":"だとしても","m":"even if it is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"別","r":"べつ","m":"different"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"観点","r":"かんてん","m":"viewpoint"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"考える","r":"かんがえる","m":"consider"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"また","m":"again"},{"t":"違う","r":"ちがう","m":"different"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if that is so, it looks different from another perspective.","scene":"A polite concession during a debate"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-029||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜にしては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にしては","patternReading":"にしては","coreMeaning":"Unexpected result by the standard of ~ (better/worse than expected)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にしては describes a result B that is **unexpected** when compared with a standard A. A is a status or experience used as a benchmark, and B may be **better** or **worse** than expected. It does not simply mean “although”; it means “for A, B is surprisingly like this.”","nuance":"Neutral in attitude, but always carries a hint of **surprise**. It works in both speech and writing. For praise, it gives an indirect compliment; for criticism, it softens the judgment. Avoid using it with sensitive categories such as “foreigners,” as it may sound stereotypical.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"新人","r":"しんじん","m":"newcomer"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"capable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a newcomer, he is surprisingly capable at work.","scene":"A new colleague handles a project well"},{"tokens":[{"t":"10年間","r":"じゅうねんかん","m":"for 10 years"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住んで","r":"すんで","m":"lived"},{"t":"いた","r":"いた","m":"was"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"下手","r":"へた","m":"poor"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For someone who lived in Japan for ten years, their Japanese is surprisingly poor.","scene":"Someone speaks Japanese hesitantly after living in Japan for ten years"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-029||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"〜にしては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にしては","patternReading":"にしては","coreMeaning":"Unexpected result by the standard of ~ (better/worse than expected)","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach 〜にしては to a noun, verb, or experience phrase to create standard A. General formula: A ＋ にしては ＋ unexpected result.","nuance":"Do not insert another particle between A and にしては. With nouns, use N＋にしては directly. With verbs, Vた／Vている before it is most natural. Nouns referring to people or experiences with a clear typical image are especially common.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N ＋ にしては ＋ [unexpected result]","note":"Most common. N must be something with a typical image: 子供, 新人, プロ, 外国人…"},{"lhs":"Vた／Vている","rhs":"Vた／Vている ＋ にしては ＋ [unexpected result]","note":"An action or experience as the standard: 初めて作った, 10年住んでいた, 3か月しか練習していない…"},{"lhs":"〜歳・〜年間","rhs":"［time/age］ ＋ にしては ＋ [unexpected result]","note":"Nouns marking age or experience: 3歳にしては, 10年間にしては…"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"字","r":"じ","m":"handwriting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手い","r":"うまい","m":"skillful"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a child, the handwriting is surprisingly good.","scene":"Looking at a child’s handwriting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"3か月","r":"さんかげつ","m":"three months"},{"t":"しか"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いない","r":"いない","m":"not yet"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skillful"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For only three months of practice, that is quite impressive.","scene":"A student plays a difficult piece after three months"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-029||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜にしては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にしては","patternReading":"にしては","coreMeaning":"Unexpected result by the standard of ~ (better/worse than expected)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are two directions of surprise: **above expectations** (A is usually weak, but B is good) and **below expectations** (A is usually strong, but B is poor). The same structure works for both; only the direction of the result changes.","nuance":"Be careful with 外国人にしては…: it may imply a low general standard for foreigners and sound like discriminatory praise. Usually use にしては when A has a broadly recognized standard, such as a child, newcomer, or expert.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"初めて","r":"はじめて","m":"first time"},{"t":"作った","r":"つくった","m":"made"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"おいしい","r":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a first attempt at cooking, the dish is delicious.","scene":"Tasting a friend’s first homemade dish"},{"tokens":[{"t":"大学院生","r":"だいがくいんせい","m":"graduate student"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"レポート","r":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"雑","r":"ざつ","m":"sloppy"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a graduate student, the report is far too sloppy.","scene":"A teacher finds a graduate student’s report careless"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-029||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with としては/にしても","sub":"〜にしては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にしては","patternReading":"にしては","coreMeaning":"Unexpected result by the standard of ~ (better/worse than expected)","slug":"compare","definition":"The patterns にしては/としては/にしても all attach to a standard A, but their viewpoints differ: にしては emphasizes **surprise**; としては evaluates **within a role**; にしても expresses **concession**, even if A is accepted. Compared with わりに, にしては uses a **typical image**, while わりに uses an **actual degree**.","nuance":"Remember: “I know A, but B is unexpected” = にしては; “judging within the role of A” = としては; “even if A, B still holds” = にしても. Compared with わりに, にしては often follows a person or occupation noun, while わりに follows 普通形 and compares relative degree.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"外国人","r":"がいこくじん","m":"foreigner"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a foreigner, their Japanese is surprisingly good.","scene":"A Japanese speaker talks with a fluent foreign Japanese speaker"},{"tokens":[{"t":"外国人","r":"がいこくじん","m":"foreigner"},{"t":"として"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"頑張って","r":"がんばって","m":"working hard"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As a foreigner, they are working hard.","scene":"Assessing a foreigner adapting to the local culture"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜としては (với tư cách A, trong giới hạn A)","point":"としては does not emphasize surprise; it evaluates within the role. 外国人としては上手だ = “skilled as a foreigner” (no surprise against expectations)."},{"vs":"〜にしても (dù là A đi nữa, vẫn B)","point":"にしても expresses concession or criticism: 外国人にしても無礼だ = “even for a foreigner, that is rude.” にしては instead describes a result contrary to expectations: 外国人にしては上手い."},{"vs":"〜わりに (tương đối với mức độ)","point":"わりに compares with the subject’s actual degree, while にしては compares with a status or typical image. 年のわりに若い = young for their age; 80歳にしては若い = young for someone who is 80."}]}},"n3-029||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜にしては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にしては","patternReading":"にしては","coreMeaning":"Unexpected result by the standard of ~ (better/worse than expected)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three main mistakes: **(1)** using にしては like としては and losing the sense of surprise; **(2)** attaching it to a subject without a typical image, such as 私; **(3)** using it when the result is ordinary and unsurprising.","nuance":"私にしては may sound odd because “I” has no shared typical standard. Using にしては for an ordinary result also sounds unnecessary. To evaluate relative actual degree, choose わりに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"新人","r":"しんじん","m":"newcomer"},{"t":"にしては","r":"にしては","m":"for a"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"capable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For a newcomer, he is surprisingly capable at work.","scene":"Commenting on a new graduate colleague"}],"pitfalls":["⚠️ にしては／としては are easy to confuse: 彼は学生にしては、よく勉強している implies B is SURPRISING, beyond expectations; 彼は学生としては、よく勉強している only evaluates him within his role, without surprise.","❌ 私にしては、背が高い。 → ✅ アジア人にしては、背が高い。 (私 has no shared typical standard; use a group with a clear standard.)","❌ 彼はプロにしては、まあまあだ。 → ✅ 彼はプロにしては、下手だ。 (An “ordinary” result creates no surprise; it must differ from the standard.)"],"compare":null}},"n3-029||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜にしては · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にしては","patternReading":"にしては","coreMeaning":"Unexpected result by the standard of ~ (better/worse than expected)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a ruler: A is the **benchmark mark**—the typical image associated with the status—and B is a **result that deviates** from it. If B is above the mark, it is praise; if below, criticism. Either way, the deviation causes surprise.","nuance":"This prevents confusion with としては: としては means “within the benchmark range,” while にしては means “deviating from the benchmark.” Remembering “I know A, but B surprises me” reminds you that surprise is required.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-030||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜くせに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜くせに","patternReading":"くせに","coreMeaning":"Even though ~ (critical, reproachful)","slug":"core","definition":"〜くせに expresses **contrast with criticism**: the speaker uses a trait or flaw X as a basis for criticizing the subject's contrary action Y. It always carries negative emotion—**resentment, sarcasm, or irritation**—so it is not used in neutral or formal situations.","nuance":"The tone is **very casual** and close to direct criticism. It usually refers to a third person, or is said directly to close friends. **Avoid it completely** for superiors or elders because it sounds rude. It is much stronger than のに: のに means 'although... but', while くせに implies 'you are no good, yet you...'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"not know"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"偉そう","r":"えらそう","m":"arrogant"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You don't know anything, so don't talk as if you do.","scene":"Yelling at a know-it-all roommate"},{"tokens":[{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"oneself"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"できない","r":"できない","m":"cannot do"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"other people"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"matters"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"とやかく","r":"とやかく","m":"meddle"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"criticize"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can't do it yourself, yet you keep criticizing others.","scene":"Complaining about a judgmental coworker"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生意気","r":"なまいき","m":"rude"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You're just a child, yet you talk so insolently.","scene":"Watching a niece talk back to an adult"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-030||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"〜くせに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜くせに","patternReading":"くせに","coreMeaning":"Even though ~ (critical, reproachful)","slug":"forms","definition":"The four main patterns are **V + くせに** (plain verb: 辞書形/ない/た), **Aい + くせに**, **Aな + な + くせに**, and **N + の + くせに**. After くせに comes a clause describing a contradictory action or state.","nuance":"With nouns, **の is required** (Nのくせに). With な-adjectives, keep な before くせに. There is **no special polite form**; you can make the following clause polite with です, but the pattern still sounds casual. The subjects of both clauses must match.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形/ない/た）","rhs":"くせに + Clause","note":"The first verb is plain form: た past or ない negative is also possible"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"くせに + Clause","note":"An い-adjective keeps い, e.g. 忙しいくせに"},{"lhs":"Aな + な","rhs":"くせに + Clause","note":"A な-adjective must keep な before くせに"},{"lhs":"N + の","rhs":"くせに + Clause","note":"A noun must take の, e.g. 子供のくせに"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"くれない","r":"くれない","m":"won't help me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You're busy, yet you won't help me.","scene":"Grumbling as a housemate sits around"},{"tokens":[{"t":"初心者","r":"しょしんしゃ","m":"beginner"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"なぜ","r":"なぜ","m":"why"},{"t":"そんなに","r":"そんなに","m":"so much"},{"t":"自信","r":"じしん","m":"confidence"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"do you"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"You're a beginner, so why are you so confident?","scene":"Surprised by a new graduate demanding high pay"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-030||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜くせに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜くせに","patternReading":"くせに","coreMeaning":"Even though ~ (critical, reproachful)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜くせに appears when **complaining about a third person**, reproaching a close friend directly, mocking someone who is incompetent yet acts superior, or criticizing a child or junior in a casual relationship.","nuance":"Do not use it in formal writing, letters, reports, or business emails. In anime and manga, characters often use it competitively or jokingly. Complaining about a boss with くせに is acceptable only privately among close friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"頼んで","r":"たのんで","m":"ask"},{"t":"おいた","r":"おいた","m":"did beforehand"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"すぐ","r":"すぐ","m":"immediately"},{"t":"忘れる","r":"わすれる","m":"forget"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You asked me to do it, yet you forgot right away.","scene":"Annoyed at a client who forgot advice"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの人","r":"あのひと","m":"that person"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"できない","r":"できない","m":"cannot do"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet they"},{"t":"文句","r":"もんく","m":"complaints"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"always"},{"t":"言ってる","r":"いってる","m":"say"}],"vi":"That person can't do it, yet all they do is complain.","scene":"Gossiping about a complaining coworker"},{"tokens":[{"t":"男","r":"おとこ","m":"man"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泣き虫","r":"なきむし","m":"crybaby"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You're a man, yet you're such a crybaby.","scene":"A girl teasing a boy about to cry"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-030||compare":{"title":"⚖️ くせに vs のに・にもかかわらず","sub":"〜くせに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜くせに","patternReading":"くせに","coreMeaning":"Even though ~ (critical, reproachful)","slug":"compare","definition":"くせに, のに, and にもかかわらず all express **逆接** (contrast), but differ in **emotion and style**. くせに = criticism; のに = neutral/frustrated; にもかかわらず = formal and neutral; ながら(も) = mild/formal contrast.","nuance":"Choose くせに to **reproach** someone. Choose のに for simple contrast or regret. Choose にもかかわらず in reports and essays. Choose ながら(も) for a slightly milder and more polite contrast.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"yet"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"tell"},{"t":"くれない","r":"くれない","m":"won't tell me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You know, yet you won't tell me.","scene":"Neutral, slightly regretful tone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"tell"},{"t":"くれない","r":"くれない","m":"won't tell me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You know perfectly well, yet you refuse to tell me!","scene":"Angrily reproaching someone who will not help"},{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"かかわらず","r":"かかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"failed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite making an effort, they still failed.","scene":"Formal sentence in a written report"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"のに","point":"のに = neutral contrast, usable for people or things. くせに = adds resentment and is for a person worth criticizing. Compare: 知っているのに教えない (states contrast), 知っているくせに教えない (reproach: knows but deliberately will not tell)."},{"vs":"にもかかわらず","point":"にもかかわらず = formal, neutral, used in writing. くせに = casual and negative, used in speech. They are not interchangeable."},{"vs":"ながら（も）","point":"ながら(も) = mild contrast without strong criticism, usable for people or things. くせに = direct criticism."}]}},"n3-030||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜くせに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜くせに","patternReading":"くせに","coreMeaning":"Even though ~ (critical, reproachful)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Classic errors include using くせに for yourself, using it with a superior, using it in formal contexts, and forgetting that **both clauses must have the same subject**.","nuance":"Because Japanese has a strong hierarchy, くせに toward a superior is **seriously disrespectful**. Use のに or remove the emotional judgment. When talking about yourself, it sounds odd because you criticize yourself with a pattern meant to criticize others.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大人","r":"おとな","m":"adult"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"口","r":"くち","m":"speech"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"真似","r":"まね","m":"imitate"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You're a child, yet you imitate the way adults speak.","scene":"Finding a child amusingly bossy"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私のお金がないくせに、高いものを買う。 → ✅ お金がないのに、高いものを買ってしまう。（Use のに when talking about yourself）","❌ 先生のくせに、間違える。 → ✅ 先生なのに、間違える。（Use のに for a superior）","❌ 彼はお金があるくせに、私は貧乏だ。 → ✅ 彼はお金があるくせに、払おうとしない。（Both clauses must have the same subject）","❌ レポートで「学生のくせに」と書く。 → ✅ 学生の身分でありながら、...（Use another pattern in formal writing）"],"compare":null}},"n3-030||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜くせに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜くせに","patternReading":"くせに","coreMeaning":"Even though ~ (critical, reproachful)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **くせ = 癖 = a bad habit or vice**. Thus, くせに suggests 'you have bad habit X, yet you do Y.' Picture a finger pointing at someone in contempt.","nuance":"This vivid mnemonic helps you remember that くせに always carries annoyance. Do not remember it as neutral 'although.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-030||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"〜くせに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜くせに","patternReading":"くせに","coreMeaning":"Even though ~ (critical, reproachful)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed expressions in tests and conversation include **知っているくせに**, **できないくせに**, **男のくせに**, **子供のくせに**, and **お前のくせに**. Learn these collocations as set phrases.","nuance":"These expressions are usually sarcastic or teasing. When you see one in a reading passage, recognize the speaker's negative emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"知ってる","r":"しってる","m":"know already"},{"t":"くせに","r":"くせに","m":"yet you"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"not know"},{"t":"ふり","r":"ふり","m":"pretend"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"してる","r":"してる","m":"be doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You know already, yet you pretend not to.","scene":"Teasing a classmate who plays dumb"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-031||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜にもかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~; despite ~ (formal)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にもかかわらず expresses **formal contrast**: although condition X, which should be an obstacle, exists, result Y still occurs, or vice versa. It is **objective and neutral**, without criticism or sarcasm. The speaker sees X and Y as **contradictory**, yet Y still happens.","nuance":"Usable in **polite speech** and **writing**, but especially common in formal writing: reports, news, and speeches. Compared with のに, it is **less emotional** and **more objective**; unlike くせに, it has **no blame**. The sense of “despite” is strong, almost “nothing can stop Y.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"悪天候","r":"あくてんこう","m":"bad weather"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大勢","r":"おおぜい","m":"many"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"集まった","r":"あつまった","m":"gathered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although the weather was bad, many people still gathered.","scene":"Reporting an outdoor event"},{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出なかった","r":"でなかった","m":"did not come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I tried hard, I still got no results.","scene":"Reflecting after a failure"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-031||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜にもかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~; despite ~ (formal)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches directly to a **noun**, **verb in plain form**, **i-adjective**, or **na-adjective / noun + である**. The result clause follows にもかかわらず. The **sentence tense** appears at the end; the preceding clause does not change tense.","nuance":"With nouns and na-adjectives, you can use **N + にもかかわらず** directly (悪天候にもかかわらず), or add **である** to emphasize “although it is…” (医者であるにもかかわらず). Both are formal; である makes the “status/nature” nuance clearer.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にもかかわらず","note":"悪天候・年齢・反対意見…"},{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"にもかかわらず","note":"た形・ない形・ている形…"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"にもかかわらず","note":"忙しい・難しい…"},{"lhs":"Naである / Nである","rhs":"にもかかわらず","note":"Use である with nouns/na-adjectives for “although it is…”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"くれた","m":"did for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he was busy, he still helped me.","scene":"Thanking a busy colleague for helping"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"である","m":"is"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"たばこ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"吸っている","r":"すっている","m":"is smoking"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he is a doctor, he still smokes.","scene":"Objectively noting a paradox"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-031||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜にもかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~; despite ~ (formal)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears most often in **corporate reports**, **speeches**, **academic papers**, and **news**. Japanese speakers choose にもかかわらず to present a contradiction **objectively and impartially**. It is standard language in academic and journalistic settings.","nuance":"Common implied attitudes include **admiration** (acting despite hardship), **regret** (failing despite effort), and **surprise** (an unexpected result). It is too formal for everyday conversation; use のに or けど instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"反対意見","r":"はんたいいけん","m":"opposing views"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"実施された","r":"じっしされた","m":"was implemented"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite the opposition, the plan was still implemented.","scene":"Speech about a policy decision"},{"tokens":[{"t":"医師","r":"いし","m":"doctor"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"忠告","r":"ちゅうこく","m":"advice"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"続けた","r":"つづけた","m":"continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite his doctor’s advice, he continued exercising.","scene":"Describing someone’s stubborn action"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-031||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing のに・にかかわらず","sub":"〜にもかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~; despite ~ (formal)","slug":"compare","definition":"The key point: **にもかかわらず** means formal contrast, while **にかかわらず** means “regardless of” (not dependent on X)—**a completely different meaning**, despite differing by only one も. のに and くせに can both mean “although,” but differ in register and attitude.","nuance":"The easiest pair to confuse is **にもかかわらず / にかかわらず**. Remember: に**も**かかわらず has も = “with or without it” → “although”; にかかわらず without も = “unrelated to it” → “regardless of.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"高齢","r":"こうれい","m":"advanced age"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"走っている","r":"はしっている","m":"runs"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he is elderly, he still runs every day.","scene":"Objective comment on an active older person"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜のに","point":"のに = CONVERSATION, showing regret or surprise; にもかかわらず = WRITING, objective and neutral. Both mean “although,” but their registers differ."},{"vs":"〜にかかわらず","point":"にかかわらず = “regardless of, independent of” (経験にかかわらず), not contrast; にもかかわらず = “although,” showing contrast. Easy to confuse because only も differs."},{"vs":"〜くせに","point":"くせに = CRITICAL or sarcastic toward someone; にもかかわらず = NEUTRAL and nonjudgmental, usable for things and situations."}]}},"n3-031||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜にもかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~; despite ~ (formal)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Two classic mistakes are **confusing it with にかかわらず** (regardless of) and **using it in casual conversation**. Also, do not use にもかかわらず to criticize someone as くせに does.","nuance":"にもかかわらず = “although” (contrast), while にかかわらず = “regardless of” (independence). They differ by only one も, but their meanings are completely different!","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"難しかった","r":"むずかしかった","m":"was difficult"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"合格した","r":"ごうかくした","m":"passed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although the exam was difficult, I still passed.","scene":"Proudly talking about an exam"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 経験にもかかわらず応募できる → ✅ 経験にかかわらず応募できる (regardless of experience)","❌ 雨にもかかわらず行った (to a friend) → ✅ 雨なのに/雨だけど行った","❌ Using にもかかわらず to criticize like くせに → ✅ にもかかわらず only states an objective contrast"],"compare":null}},"n3-031||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips & imagery","sub":"〜にもかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~; despite ~ (formal)","slug":"memory","definition":"Use a **visual mnemonic**: X is a barrier, and にもかかわらず is a train **breaking through the barrier** and moving on. かかわる means “to be involved/bound”; かかわらず means “not bound by X” → **despite X, Y is still achieved**.","nuance":"Mantra: “Despite X, which should stop it → Y still happens.” When reading a sentence, remember **X as an obstacle** and **Y continuing anyway**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-031||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜にもかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~; despite ~ (formal)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are phrases used **at the start of a sentence** or common noun combinations with にもかかわらず. In particular, **それにもかかわらず** means “nevertheless” and links sentences in formal writing.","nuance":"In exams and reading passages, N + にもかかわらず often appears with abstract nouns such as 悪天候・反対意見・年齢・経営環境.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降っていた","r":"ふっていた","m":"was falling"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"nevertheless"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"go out"},{"t":"かけた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was raining. Nevertheless, he still went out.","scene":"Telling about a daring friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-032||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜ながら（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ながら（も）","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Both ~ and / Even though ~, still (2 meanings)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ながら（も） has **two nearly opposite meanings**: (1) **simultaneous** — doing A and B at the same time; (2) **contrast** — even though A, B still happens. The contrast meaning often adds **も**, becoming **ながらも**, for emphasis. Although the connecting form is the same, listeners determine the meaning from the relationship between the two clauses.","nuance":"The simultaneous meaning is more formal than the て-form and is common in writing or polite speech. The contrast meaning sounds **refined and literary**, often expressing admiration or mild concession, without the strong criticism of くせに. Adding も makes the contrast more explicit.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聴き","r":"きき","m":"listen"},{"t":"ながら","r":"ながら","m":"while"},{"t":"歩く","r":"あるく","m":"walk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I walk while listening to music.","scene":"Walking home after work while listening to music to relax"},{"tokens":[{"t":"知り","r":"しり","m":"know"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"even though"},{"t":"黙って","r":"だまって","m":"remain silent"},{"t":"いた","r":"いた","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even though he knew, he remained silent.","scene":"He knew the truth but did not say anything"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-032||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ながら（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ながら（も）","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Both ~ and / Even though ~, still (2 meanings)","slug":"forms","definition":"The simultaneous meaning connects only with the **Vます-stem**. The contrast meaning connects with the **Vます-stem, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns**. With な-adjectives and nouns, **であり** is often added before ながら, or ながら may follow the noun directly, though this is less common.","nuance":"The simultaneous structure requires the **same subject** in both clauses, with both actions performed by **one person**. Contrast does not require the same subject, but if the subjects differ, のに or が is usually more natural. Adding も in contrast is optional but very common for emphasis.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"ながら + V2","note":"Simultaneous: do V while doing V2"},{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"ながら（も） + S","note":"Contrast: even though V…"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"ながら（も） + S","note":"Contrast: even though A…"},{"lhs":"Aな / N","rhs":"（であり）ながら（も） + S","note":"Contrast; literary"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"ながら","r":"ながら","m":"while"},{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We talk while eating.","scene":"Family members chat during a meal"},{"tokens":[{"t":"残念","r":"ざんねん","m":"unfortunate"},{"t":"ながら","r":"ながら","m":"regrettably"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行けません","r":"いけません","m":"cannot go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unfortunately, I cannot go.","scene":"Declining a coworker's party invitation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-032||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜ながら（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ながら（も）","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Both ~ and / Even though ~, still (2 meanings)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch highlights the **two main uses**. The simultaneous meaning describes two parallel actions by the same person. The contrast meaning is used when the clauses conflict, often showing **criticism versus praise or limitation versus strength**.","nuance":"残念ながら is a very common polite idiom placed at the beginning of a sentence to deliver bad news or soften a refusal. In conversation, ながらも often sounds **literary or formal**, but it can still be used for emphasis and emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"狭い","r":"せまい","m":"cramped"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"even though"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"居心地","r":"いごこち","m":"comfort"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Though small, it is a comfortable place.","scene":"Praising a friend's small rental room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"し","r":"し","m":"do"},{"t":"ながら","r":"ながら","m":"while"},{"t":"運転","r":"うんてん","m":"drive"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"act"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"危険","r":"きけん","m":"dangerous"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Driving while using a phone is dangerous.","scene":"Warning a friend using a phone while driving"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-032||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing のに・にもかかわらず・くせに","sub":"〜ながら（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ながら（も）","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Both ~ and / Even though ~, still (2 meanings)","slug":"compare","definition":"A quick comparison of ながら(も) with easily confused contrast patterns. The key point: **ながら(も) is mild, refined, and nonjudgmental**; のに carries strong emotion, にもかかわらず is formal and objective, and くせに is often reproachful.","nuance":"Learners often confuse these because all can mean “even though…”. Remember: do not use ながら(も) to complain. Use くせに when you want to reproach someone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"狭い","r":"せまい","m":"cramped"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"even though"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"きれい","r":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Though small, it is beautiful.","scene":"Admiring a small, nicely decorated room"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"のに","point":"のに carries subjective emotion, “it should have been… but”; ながら(も) is neutral and refined. Example: 知っていながら言わない / 知っているのに言わない."},{"vs":"にもかかわらず","point":"にもかかわらず is formal and objective, used in documents/reports; ながら(も) is refined and works in conversation. Example: 狭いながらも楽しい / 狭いにもかかわらず."},{"vs":"くせに","point":"くせに is forceful, critical, and resentful; ながら(も) is nonjudgmental and often positively admiring. Example: できないくせに偉そう / できないながらも頑張る."}]}},"n3-032||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ながら（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ながら（も）","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Both ~ and / Even though ~, still (2 meanings)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with ながら include confusing its two meanings, using different subjects in the clauses, or choosing actions that cannot happen simultaneously.","nuance":"Before writing, ask yourself: **Do A and B conflict?** If yes → contrast. If not → simultaneous. Do not blindly apply “both…and” to every sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聴き","r":"きき","m":"listen"},{"t":"ながら","r":"ながら","m":"while"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study while listening to music.","scene":"A student reviews lessons in a private room"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 泳ぎながら本を読む。 → ✅ 音楽を聴きながら本を読む。 (choose two actions that can happen together; avoid impossible combinations)","❌ 私が歩きながら、彼が話す。 → ✅ 私が歩きながら話す。 (the same person walks and talks)","❌ 狭いながらも、不便だ。 → ✅ 狭いながらも、居心地がいい。 (contrast needs tension: cramped ↔ comfortable; cramped ↔ inconvenient is not a contrast)"],"compare":null}},"n3-032||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ながら（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ながら（も）","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Both ~ and / Even though ~, still (2 meanings)","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick memory aid for the two meanings: **V+V → parallel**, **A/N + も → contrast**. Test the sentence by asking whether A and B can **happen at the same time in reality**; if yes, it is simultaneous; otherwise, it is contrastive.","nuance":"If you see も, it is **almost certainly contrastive**. If clause A is an adjective or noun, it is also contrastive. Remember: “V V in parallel – A/N in opposition.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-032||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ながら（も） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ながら（も）","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Both ~ and / Even though ~, still (2 meanings)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common fixed phrases with ながら(も) found in JLPT questions and everyday conversation. Mastering them helps you quickly identify the meaning of a sentence and use the pattern naturally, especially when politely refusing or offering modest praise.","nuance":"残念ながら is the most common idiom for a **polite refusal**; 知っていながら can carry mild reproach; 狭いながらも and 小さいながらも are used to praise something modest.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"残念","r":"ざんねん","m":"unfortunate"},{"t":"ながら","r":"ながら","m":"regrettably"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今回","r":"こんかい","m":"this time"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participate"},{"t":"できません","r":"できません","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unfortunately, I cannot participate this time.","scene":"Replying to an invitation to a class reunion"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-033||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜以来 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以来","patternReading":"いらい","coreMeaning":"From a past point, a state continues up to now","slug":"core","definition":"「〜以来」 describes a time or event in the past from which a state or habit has continued up to the present. It does not simply mean 'after X'; it emphasizes an unbroken timeline from X to now. The later clause must express an ongoing state (ずっと〜, 今でも〜) or a repeated habit; do not use it for an action that happened only once.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; in letters and news, it can sound formal or nostalgic. Compared with 〜てから, 〜以来 strongly suggests lasting continuity and attachment—Japanese speakers often use it when recalling important memories or milestones. Not used for the future.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"卒業","r":"そつぎょう","m":"graduation"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ずっと","m":"all along"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会って","r":"あって","m":"meet"},{"t":"いない","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have not seen him since graduating.","scene":"Recalling an old relationship after years apart"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accident"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"運転","r":"うんてん","m":"driving"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"怖く","r":"こわく","m":"afraid"},{"t":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since that accident, I have become afraid of driving.","scene":"Telling a friend about a fear after a traffic accident"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-033||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜以来 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以来","patternReading":"いらい","coreMeaning":"From a past point, a state continues up to now","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the two main patterns: N + 以来 and Vて + 以来. Both begin a clause, and the later clause describes an ongoing state or habit. The formal form stays the same; in correspondence, 〜以来、〜しております is common.","nuance":"Both forms can be used with present and past tense. With N + 以来, N should be a meaningful milestone such as graduation, marriage, joining a company, or an accident; avoid very recent points such as 'this morning.' With Vて + 以来, V is the action marking the starting point.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + 以来","note":"N is a time or event noun"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"Vて + 以来","note":"Action V is the starting point"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"している","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since coming to Japan, I study Japanese every day.","scene":"An international student discussing a daily study habit"},{"tokens":[{"t":"入社","r":"にゅうしゃ","m":"joining a company"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"being late"},{"t":"した","m":"have done"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have not been late even once since joining the company.","scene":"Discussing a disciplined work record in an interview"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-033||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜以来 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以来","patternReading":"いらい","coreMeaning":"From a past point, a state continues up to now","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch groups common meanings: an event or milestone such as graduation, an accident, or marriage, or an action such as coming to Japan or meeting someone, becomes the starting point; an emotional state or habit then continues to the present. It often combines with 〜ようになった to emphasize lasting change.","nuance":"Best for experiences with lasting significance. あの日以来 is emotional and can suggest fate or a deep bond. In news, 以来 highlights a lasting change after an event; in letters, ご無沙汰しております often appears alongside it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作る","r":"つくる","m":"make"},{"t":"ようになった","m":"started to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since getting married, I have started cooking.","scene":"A newlywed husband describing a lifestyle change"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"him"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れられない","r":"わすれられない","m":"cannot forget"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have not been able to forget him since that day.","scene":"Confiding after a fateful meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-033||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with てから / ごろから / 以降","sub":"〜以来 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以来","patternReading":"いらい","coreMeaning":"From a past point, a state continues up to now","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most often confused with 〜以来 are 〜てから, 〜ごろから, and 〜以降. A useful test: if adding 'ずっと' sounds natural, use 以来; if you are only stating event order, use てから; if the time point is vague or approximate, use ごろから; if you are simply marking a point without requiring continuation to now, use 以降.","nuance":"These forms are especially easy to confuse. 〜てから is neutral and does not require continuity. 〜以降 can mean 'from now on' or 'after 3 o'clock' and may point to the future. 〜以来 always looks back from the past to the present and is not used for the future.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"卒業","r":"そつぎょう","m":"graduation"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"いない","m":"have not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have not returned to school even once since graduating.","scene":"Discussing not returning to an old school for years"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てから","point":"てから only shows the order 'do A, then B'; B need not continue. 以来 requires B to continue from A to now. Example: 結婚してから料理を始めた (I started cooking after getting married) ↔ 結婚以来、ずっと料理している (I have cooked ever since marriage)."},{"vs":"〜ごろから","point":"ごろから marks a vague approximate starting point and does not stress continuity; 以来 stresses an unbroken timeline. Example: 子供のころから好きだ (I have liked it since childhood) ↔ 入社以来、一度も遅刻したことがない (I have never been late since joining the company)."},{"vs":"〜以降","point":"以降 marks an objective point and can refer to the future; 以来 always points 'up to now.' Example: 3時以降なら空いています (I am free after 3) ↔ 卒業以来、会っていません (I have not met them since graduation)."}]}},"n3-033||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜以来 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以来","patternReading":"いらい","coreMeaning":"From a past point, a state continues up to now","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make four major errors with 〜以来: using a one-time action in the later clause, using it for a future event, using a time point that is too recent such as 'this morning,' and using the dictionary form instead of the て-form before 以来. Fix: always ask, 'Can I naturally add ずっと?'","nuance":"These errors come from translating 'since' too literally. 〜以来 cannot describe an event that ended in the past or a future milestone. For a very recent point, use 〜から.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"卒業","r":"そつぎょう","m":"graduation"},{"t":"以来","r":"いらい","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ずっと","m":"all along"},{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have been busy ever since graduating.","scene":"A correct example with an ongoing state"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 卒業以来、昨日旅行した。 → ✅ 卒業以来、一度も旅行していない。 (The later clause must describe an ongoing state up to now)","❌ 今朝以来、ずっと忙しい。 → ✅ 今朝からずっと忙しい。 (The point is too recent; do not use 以来)","❌ 卒業以来、旅に行くつもりだ。 → ✅ 卒業以来、毎年旅に行っている。 (以来 is not used for the future)","❌ 結婚する以来、ずっと料理している。 → ✅ 結婚して以来、ずっと料理している。 (The V before 以来 must be in て-form, not dictionary form)"],"compare":null}},"n3-033||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜以来 · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜以来","patternReading":"いらい","coreMeaning":"From a past point, a state continues up to now","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember 〜以来, picture a river that starts at point A and flows continuously to the present. The word 以来 consists of 以 (take/from) and 来 (come/reach), meaning 'from that point onward.' If the river breaks—if there is no continuity—do not use 以来.","nuance":"This image is especially useful for avoiding confusion with てから: てから is like standing at A and then stepping to B; no continuous flow is required. 以来 is an uninterrupted current.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-034||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜度に／たびに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜度に／たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever A occurs → B also occurs (a regular repeated pattern)","slug":"core","definition":"〜たびに describes a **repeated pattern**: whenever A occurs, B occurs in turn. It means **whenever ~, every time ~, each time ~**. Its key feature is **regular repetition with no exceptions**: it is not a one-time event, but something that happens every time.\n\nB is often the speaker's **subjective reaction**—an emotion such as nervousness, joy, or longing, or a personal observation. Because it centers on the speaker, たびに often has an **emotional, nostalgic** tone, unlike the mechanical objectivity of と.","nuance":"Natural in both speech and writing; it is also common in Japanese poetry and music. It feels **familiar and steadily recurring**, like breathing—it does not emphasize change or irregularity. Compared with **〜ごとに** (each time unit/person), たびに connects to **each occurrence of an action/event**; compared with **〜と** (a natural rule), たびに leans toward **personal emotion**; compared with **〜ときに** (one occasion), たびに emphasizes that it happens **EVERY TIME**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会う","r":"あう","m":"meet"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"緊張","r":"きんちょう","m":"nervousness"},{"t":"してしまう"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I meet her, I get nervous.","scene":"Telling a friend about a girl I like"},{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look at"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"思い出す","r":"おもいだす","m":"remember"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every time I look at the photos, I think of my mother.","scene":"Looking through an old photo album alone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-034||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and connections","sub":"〜度に／たびに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜度に／たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever A occurs → B also occurs (a regular repeated pattern)","slug":"forms","definition":"There are **two main connection patterns**:\n1. **V辞書形 + たびに** — use the verb's dictionary form, NOT V-た: 行くたびに ×行ったたびに.\n2. **N + の + たびに** — insert の: 旅行のたびに ×旅行たびに.\n\nたびに can be written with the kanji **度(たび)**, with the same reading. 度 means “time” or “occasion,” so 度に means “on each occasion.” X must be a **repeatable event or action**; if X happens only once, this pattern cannot be used.","nuance":"Neutral in formality, so it works in casual conversation and writing. B is often the speaker's **emotion or observation**, rather than an unavoidable natural law.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"＋ たびに ＋ B","note":"行くたびに・会うたびに・食べるたびに..."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ の ＋ たびに ＋ B","note":"旅行のたびに・試験のたびに・季節のたびに"},{"lhs":"〜たびに","rhs":"＝ 〜度(たび)に","note":"Same meaning; written with kanji"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"発見","r":"はっけん","m":"discovery"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every time I go to Japan, I make a new discovery.","scene":"Telling a colleague about business trips"},{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"現地","r":"げんち","m":"local"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cuisine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"楽","r":"たの","m":"enjoyment"},{"t":"しむ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every time I travel, I enjoy the local cuisine.","scene":"Answering what you like to do while traveling"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-034||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜度に／たびに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜度に／たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever A occurs → B also occurs (a regular repeated pattern)","slug":"usage","definition":"Usage falls into two clear groups:\n① **V辞書形 + たびに**: whenever you perform action A, B occurs—会うたびに, 料理するたびに, 失敗するたびに.\n② **N + の + たびに**: on every occasion of N, each time N—旅行のたびに, 試験のたびに.\n\nIn real life, たびに often appears in **advertising and slogans** with a warm, familiar feeling (食べるたびに幸せになる), and in **song lyrics and poetry** to express recurring emotion, especially longing: 君に会うたびに.","nuance":"Do not use たびに for something that happens exactly once. Do not use it for a natural law such as “when spring comes, flowers bloom”—use と instead. To express **gradual change with each occurrence** (the more ~, the more ~), choose につれて / につれ, not たびに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会う","r":"あう","m":"meet"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"energy"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらう"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every time I meet him, I feel re-energized.","scene":"Talking with a close friend about an inspiring person"},{"tokens":[{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happiness"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Happiness with every bite.","scene":"Candy commercial slogan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-034||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from と / ときに / につれて","sub":"〜度に／たびに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜度に／たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever A occurs → B also occurs (a regular repeated pattern)","slug":"compare","definition":"Three patterns are often confused: **と（条件）**, **ときに**, and **につれて** can all be translated as “when ~,” but their meanings differ.\n\n**〜と** = an objective/natural rule or inevitable result: 春になると桜が咲く.\n**〜ときに** = a point in time; it does NOT emphasize repetition: 困ったときに手伝ってください.\n**〜につれて** = as A gradually increases, B gradually changes: 年をとるにつれて体が弱くなる.\n**〜たびに** = an event repeats each time, with B as the speaker's reaction or emotion: 会うたびに好きになる.","nuance":"Quick test: can you add \"毎回\"? → たびに works; ときに does not. Is it a natural or mechanical rule? → と. Is there gradual, proportional change? → につれて.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"桜","r":"さくら","m":"cherry blossoms"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"咲く","r":"さく","m":"bloom"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever spring arrives, the cherry blossoms bloom.","scene":"Natural-law example—use と, not たびに"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜と（条件）","point":"と expresses an objective/natural rule; たびに expresses a life pattern with emotion. 春になると桜が咲く (natural) ✅ / 彼に会うたびに笑顔になる (emotional) ✅"},{"vs":"〜ときに","point":"ときに marks ONE occasion and does not stress repetition; たびに stresses that it happens EVERY TIME. 困ったときに手伝う (one occasion) vs 困るたびに手伝う (helps every time)"},{"vs":"〜につれて","point":"につれて = gradual proportional change (the more ~, the more ~); たびに = the same result each time. 年をとるにつれて体が弱くなる (gradual change) vs 試験のたびに緊張する (nervous every time)"},{"vs":"〜ごとに","point":"ごとに attaches to UNITS (1時間ごと・10分ごと); たびに attaches to ACTIONS/EVENTS (行くたび・会うたび). 1時間ごとに休憩する vs 会うたびに話すことが変わる"}]}},"n3-034||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜度に／たびに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜度に／たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever A occurs → B also occurs (a regular repeated pattern)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The 3 most common errors are:\n1. **Omitting の** after a noun: 旅行たびに → **旅行のたびに**.\n2. **Using V-た before たびに**: 行ったたびに → **行くたびに**. The verb before たびに is always in dictionary form.\n3. **Using it with a non-repeatable event**: 生まれるたびに is WRONG because birth happens only once; use 旅行するたびに instead. Also, do not confuse the kanji **旅(たび)**, “trip,” with **度/たびに**, “each time.”","nuance":"When checking an error, ask: how many times can X happen? If only once, do not use たびに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"trip"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮る","r":"とる","m":"take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I take photos on every trip.","scene":"Correct form—remember の after the noun"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 旅行たびに写真を撮る。 → ✅ 旅行のたびに写真を撮る。","❌ 行ったたびに新しい発見がある。 → ✅ 行くたびに新しい発見がある。","❌ 生まれるたびに世界が変わる。 → ✅ 新しい土地を訪れるたびに世界が広がる。","❌ 彼に会う旅に、緊張してしまう。（I accidentally selected the kanji 旅[trip] instead of hiragana たびに） → ✅ 彼に会うたびに、緊張してしまう。（keep hiragana or write 度に; do not confuse it with 旅）"],"compare":null}},"n3-034||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜度に／たびに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜度に／たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever A occurs → B also occurs (a regular repeated pattern)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture **an old clock**: every time the hands complete a revolution, the chime rings—again and again, without missing once. That is たびに.\n\nIf you remember the kanji “度” meaning “time” or “occasion,” you have the key: 一度・二度・三度… count each occurrence → 度に = “every time.” The short mantra is: **“Each TIME X → Y also happens.”** The decisive memory cue is **ALWAYS**—not once, not occasionally.","nuance":"Compared with ときに (one occasion), たびに evokes a **steady loop**. Compared with ごとに (each time unit), たびに connects to **each occurrence of an action**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-034||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜度に／たびに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜度に／たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever A occurs → B also occurs (a regular repeated pattern)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently on tests and in daily life:\n- **会うたびに〜**: whenever I meet ~ — 会うたびに元気をもらう.\n- **Nのたびに〜**: on every occasion of ~ — 試験のたびに緊張する.\n- **〜たびに〜になる**: become ~ every time — 会うたびに好きになる.\n- **失敗するたびに〜**; **料理するたびに〜** — repeated action + repeated result.\nIn music and poetry, the motif **君に会うたびに** (whenever I meet you) is very common.","nuance":"These phrases are friendly and common in everyday conversation as well as song lyrics. When you see one in a reading passage, immediately expect a repeated event before たびに and a corresponding repeated result or emotion after it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"君","r":"きみ","m":"you"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会う","r":"あう","m":"meet"},{"t":"たびに"},{"t":"好","r":"す","m":"like"},{"t":"きに"},{"t":"なる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every time I meet you, I fall more in love with you.","scene":"Romantic lyric or confession text"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-035||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜たとたん（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たとたん（に）","patternReading":"たとたん","coreMeaning":"Just as ~, ~ happened immediately (unexpectedly)","slug":"core","definition":"〜たとたん（に）describes **B happening immediately** after **A has just ended**. The key point is the **unexpected element**: B is usually unplanned, sometimes like **Murphy's Law** (the moment you do something, it goes wrong). It is commonly used to **recount** an event that happened in the past.","nuance":"It has a **dramatic, unexpected** tone, much stronger than 〜たらすぐ. It can be used in everyday speech to tell a funny or unlucky story; formal writing often avoids it because it is too emphatic, using **〜した直後** or **〜すると同時に** instead. The subject of B is usually **unintentional** and uncontrollable (rain falls, someone dozes off, gets dizzy, or a machine breaks).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出た","r":"でた","m":"go out"},{"t":"とたんに","m":"immediately"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り始めた","r":"ふりはじめた","m":"started falling"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just as I stepped outside, it suddenly started raining.","scene":"You just leave home, and it starts raining; you tell your friends."},{"tokens":[{"t":"横になった","r":"よこになった","m":"lie down"},{"t":"とたん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"眠ってしまった","r":"ねむってしまった","m":"fell asleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The moment I lay down, I fell asleep.","scene":"You are exhausted; as soon as your head hits the pillow, you sleep."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-035||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜たとたん（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たとたん（に）","patternReading":"たとたん","coreMeaning":"Just as ~, ~ happened immediately (unexpectedly)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern must use a verb in the **た-form** (past/completed form). とたん is usually written in hiragana; it may also be written with the kanji 途端. The particle に **may be omitted** without changing the meaning.","nuance":"This is a **very common error**: do not use the dictionary form. Clause B must describe an **event that occurs**, not an intentional action.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（た形）","rhs":"とたん（に）","note":"Attach directly after the V-た form"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"とたん、","note":"Form without に, common in speech"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"途端に","note":"Kanji form, slightly formal"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けた","r":"あけた","m":"open"},{"t":"とたん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飛び出してきた","r":"とびだしてきた","m":"burst out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just as I opened the door, the dog burst out.","scene":"You open the door and your dog rushes outside."},{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んだ","r":"のんだ","m":"take"},{"t":"とたんに"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"楽になった","r":"らくになった","m":"felt better"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as I took the medicine, I felt better.","scene":"Your headache eases soon after you take medicine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-035||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜たとたん（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たとたん（に）","patternReading":"たとたん","coreMeaning":"Just as ~, ~ happened immediately (unexpectedly)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are two main uses. **1) A sudden event occurs** after an action or time boundary: as soon as A happens, B strikes (rain, a phone ringing, a dog jumping out). **2) A state changes instantly**: the body or a machine reacts immediately after an action (medicine makes you feel better, turning on the power makes a light burn out).","nuance":"In conversation, it is used to **recount** unlucky or coincidental situations naturally. In academic or report writing, it is less common because of its dramatic tone; use **〜した直後** instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"プレゼン","m":"presentation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"始まった","r":"はじまった","m":"started"},{"t":"とたん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"マイク","m":"microphone"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"壊れた","r":"こわれた","m":"broke"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just as the presentation started, the microphone broke.","scene":"Murphy’s Law: it breaks the moment you start."},{"tokens":[{"t":"立ち上がった","r":"たちあがった","m":"stand up"},{"t":"とたん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"めまい","m":"dizziness"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"した","m":"occurred"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as I stood up, I felt dizzy.","scene":"You stand too quickly after sitting for a long time and feel faint."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-035||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar “right after” patterns","sub":"〜たとたん（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たとたん（に）","patternReading":"たとたん","coreMeaning":"Just as ~, ~ happened immediately (unexpectedly)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch separates 〜たとたん（に）from other easily confused “right after” patterns. The key differences are **unexpectedness** and **degree of simultaneity**.","nuance":"〜たとたん stands out for unexpectedness: B is almost an immediate reaction beyond expectation. たらすぐ / てすぐ are neutral and B can be intentional, while 〜と tends to express a natural rule.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けた","r":"あけた","m":"open"},{"t":"とたん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飛び出してきた","r":"とびだしてきた","m":"burst out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just as I opened the door, the cat burst out.","scene":"The cat was waiting by the door and dashed out as soon as it opened."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たらすぐ","point":"たとたん emphasizes UNEXPECTEDNESS; たらすぐ only means “right after,” and B can be intentional (帰ったらすぐ電話する)."},{"vs":"〜てすぐ","point":"てすぐ is flexible and neutral, common in conversation; たとたん suggests something uncontrollable."},{"vs":"〜か〜ないかのうちに","point":"〜か〜ないか… describes X and Y as almost SIMULTANEOUS (Y begins before X is clearly finished); たとたん means X fully ends, then Y occurs."},{"vs":"〜と","point":"と expresses CAUSE AND EFFECT or a natural rule that can be predicted; たとたん emphasizes an UNEXPECTED event."}]}},"n3-035||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜たとたん（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たとたん（に）","patternReading":"たとたん","coreMeaning":"Just as ~, ~ happened immediately (unexpectedly)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These errors come from **applying the logic of another pattern** to たとたん. Remember: とたん requires **Vた**, and clause B is an **unexpected, involuntary event**, usually recounted in the **past**.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, the most common traps are clause B with **〜よう/〜つもり/〜ください** or a future tense. Think “recounting an event,” not “describing a plan.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"帰った","r":"かえった","m":"return"},{"t":"とたん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"眠ってしまった","r":"ねむってしまった","m":"fell asleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as I got home, I fell asleep.","scene":"Exhausted after work, you fall asleep upon getting home."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べるとたん、… → ✅ 食べたとたん、…","❌ 家に帰ったとたん、勉強しよう。 → ✅ 家に帰ったとたん、眠ってしまった。","❌ 家を出たとたん、雨が降る。 (present tense) → ✅ 家を出たとたん、雨が降り出した。","❌ 起きたとたん、走り出した (intentional) → ✅ 起きたとたん、めまいがした。"],"compare":null}},"n3-035||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜たとたん（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たとたん（に）","patternReading":"たとたん","coreMeaning":"Just as ~, ~ happened immediately (unexpectedly)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **途端（とたん）** uses 途 for “path” and 端 for “edge.” Its literal image is “right at the edge of the path”—cross the threshold and enter another state. Imagine **turning on a light switch**: your finger touches it, and the bulb lights immediately with no delay.","nuance":"When you see this word in a sentence, ask yourself: “What switched on right after this moment? Was it unexpected?” If yes, that is たとたん.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-035||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜たとたん（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たとたん（に）","patternReading":"たとたん","coreMeaning":"Just as ~, ~ happened immediately (unexpectedly)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common opening phrases in exams and daily life include **〜を開けたとたん**, **〜を出たとたん**, **〜が動き出したとたん**, and **〜が終わったとたん**. They function almost like “formulae” that lead into an unexpected second clause.","nuance":"These phrases often describe **opening/leaving/starting something and then an event occurring**—a very natural sentence type for telling an unlucky or coincidental story.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"着いた","r":"ついた","m":"arrive"},{"t":"とたん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ってきた","r":"ふってきた","m":"started raining"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as I arrived at school, it started raining.","scene":"You reach school and rain suddenly pours down; you tell a friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-036||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜うちに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜うちに","patternReading":"うちに","coreMeaning":"While A lasts / before the situation changes","slug":"core","definition":"〜うちに describes a period while state A still exists. Meaning ①: act on B before A changes or disappears; the speaker actively seizes the chance. Meaning ②: while A is happening, B occurs naturally and unexpectedly. The key idea is that A is a limited period and will not remain unchanged.","nuance":"Used in speech and writing. Meaning ① often suggests urgency and regret if the chance is missed; meaning ② often suggests surprise or spontaneity. Compared with 間に, うちに emphasizes that the state will end, clearly conveying “while there is still a chance.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"熱い","r":"あつい","m":"hot"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while hot"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please eat it while it is still hot.","scene":"A dish has just been served; urge the guest to eat it hot."},{"tokens":[{"t":"話して","r":"はなして","m":"talking"},{"t":"いる","m":"be"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while"},{"t":"泣いて","r":"ないて","m":"cry"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While talking, I suddenly started crying.","scene":"Sharing a sad story with a friend, tears come unexpectedly."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-036||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"〜うちに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜うちに","patternReading":"うちに","coreMeaning":"While A lasts / before the situation changes","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern uses **V辞書形 / Vない / Vている / Aい / Aな / Nの** + うちに. **Vない + うちに** means “before A occurs” and is very common. **Vている + うちに** often means “while A was happening, B unexpectedly occurred.”","nuance":"Do not use **Vた + うちに**. Clause A is an ongoing state; clause B may be a command or suggestion (seize the chance) or a natural result (unexpected event). It works in both formal and casual speech.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"うちに + B","note":"Act while action or state A continues"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"うちに + B","note":"Before A occurs (A has not occurred)"},{"lhs":"Vている","rhs":"うちに + B","note":"While A is happening, B occurs unexpectedly"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"うちに + B","note":"While still in state A (熱い・若い・明るい…)"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"なうちに + B","note":"While still A (元気な・暇な…)"},{"lhs":"Nの","rhs":"うちに + B","note":"While still N (子供・学生…)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忘れない","r":"わすれない","m":"not forget"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while"},{"t":"メモしよう","m":"let's note it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s write it down before we forget.","scene":"After hearing key information, decide to take notes immediately."},{"tokens":[{"t":"若い","r":"わかい","m":"young"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while still"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しなさい","m":"you should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Study while you are young.","scene":"An adult advises a young person to use the time to study."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-036||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜うちに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜うちに","patternReading":"うちに","coreMeaning":"While A lasts / before the situation changes","slug":"usage","definition":"Meaning ① is active and common in advice, invitations, and suggestions while an opportunity remains: 若いうちに, 熱いうちに, 忘れないうちに. Meaning ② is common in narratives when something unexpectedly happens during an ongoing activity.","nuance":"Meaning ① pairs well with **〜しなさい / 〜しよう / 〜てください / 〜べきだ**. Meaning ② pairs with **〜てしまう / 〜てきた / 〜ことになる**. In conversation, 熱いうちに immediately suggests “eat it while it’s hot.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"熱い","r":"あつい","m":"hot"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while hot"},{"t":"どうぞ","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please enjoy it while it is hot.","scene":"A server brings out a hot dish and invites the guest to eat."},{"tokens":[{"t":"運転","r":"うんてん","m":"driving"},{"t":"している","m":"be"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while"},{"t":"眠く","r":"ねむく","m":"sleepy"},{"t":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While driving, I suddenly became sleepy.","scene":"On a long drive, sleepiness comes on naturally."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-036||compare":{"title":"⚖️ うちに vs 間に vs 前に","sub":"〜うちに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜うちに","patternReading":"うちに","coreMeaning":"While A lasts / before the situation changes","slug":"compare","definition":"うちに・間に・前に all express time, but from different viewpoints. うちに = a window of opportunity, emphasizing that the state will change. 間に = a defined time span, neutrally. 前に = a time point, marking that one event occurs before another.","nuance":"Use うちに to emphasize making use of “while there is still time.” 間に focuses on arranging an event within a time frame; 前に simply expresses before-and-after order.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明るい","r":"あかるい","m":"light"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while still"},{"t":"帰ろう","r":"かえろう","m":"let's go home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s go home while it is still light.","scene":"At a friend’s house, it is getting dark, so suggest leaving."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜間に","point":"間に = a CLEAR, NEUTRAL time span; うちに = a continuing state, with a sense of SEIZING THE CHANCE. Ex: 留守の間に泥棒が入った (while away) / 明るいうちに帰ろう (while light → before dark)."},{"vs":"〜前に","point":"前に = BEFORE a specific time point; ないうちに = BEFORE an event occurs, emphasizing that the state has not changed. Ex: 忘れる前にメモする / 忘れないうちにメモする."},{"vs":"〜ながら","point":"ながら = two PARALLEL actions by the same subject, intentionally; うちに = A is the setting and B occurs, often unexpectedly. Ex: 音楽を聞きながら運転する / 運転しているうちに眠くなる."}]}},"n3-036||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜うちに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜うちに","patternReading":"うちに","coreMeaning":"While A lasts / before the situation changes","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include using Vた with うちに, confusing うちに with 間に, or using meaning ② for an intentional action.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, a common trap is **〜ないうちに**: understand it as “before it happens,” not “after it does not happen.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降らない","r":"ふらない","m":"not rain"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"before"},{"t":"帰ろう","r":"かえろう","m":"let's go home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s go home before it rains.","scene":"The sky is cloudy, so suggest leaving before the rain starts."}],"pitfalls":["❌ ~~忘れたうちにメモする~~ → ✅ 忘れないうちにメモする (Do not use Vた + うち for the “seize the chance” meaning).","❌ 授業のうちに寝た → ✅ 授業の間に寝た (Use 間に for a clear, neutral time span).","❌ 歩いているうちに、わざと転んだ → ✅ 歩いているうちに、うっかり転んだ (Meaning ② requires an unintended result)."],"compare":null}},"n3-036||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory trick: window of opportunity","sub":"〜うちに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜うちに","patternReading":"うちに","coreMeaning":"While A lasts / before the situation changes","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine うちに as **an open window of opportunity**. Meaning ①: the window is about to close, so act now. Meaning ②: the window is open and a gust of wind (B) unexpectedly blows in.","nuance":"The kanji 内 (うち) means “inside.” Mnemonic: **While A lasts → seize the chance / change naturally**. Remember 若いうちに, 熱いうちに, 忘れないうちに.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-036||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜うちに · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜うちに","patternReading":"うちに","coreMeaning":"While A lasts / before the situation changes","slug":"phrases","definition":"Phrases with うちに are very common in JLPT questions and everyday conversation. 〜ないうちに means “before ~” and is essential; 若いうちに appears in advice from older people; 今のうちに means “seize the chance now”; 元気なうちに means using the time while healthy; and 〜ているうちに describes something arising during an ongoing action. Mastering these fixed phrases builds natural listening and speaking reflexes.","nuance":"〜ないうちに is a favorite way to say “before ~”; 今のうちに urges someone to act “while there is still a chance.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while still"},{"t":"買って","r":"かって","m":"buy"},{"t":"おこう","m":"buy in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s buy it now while we still can.","scene":"The price is low, so suggest buying before the chance is gone."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-037||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜間に／間は · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜間に／間は","patternReading":"あいだに／あいだは","coreMeaning":"While ~: 間に = one-time event, 間は = continues throughout","slug":"core","definition":"〜間に／間は describes a time span during which a situation is ongoing. The difference lies in the particle: に marks one point within the span, so event B happens once; は emphasizes the whole span, so B continues throughout. Use this pattern to answer how B occurs while A is happening.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; it is not especially formal. 間は sounds definite and emphasizes “throughout that period,” while 間に is lighter and simply marks one event. In notices, は/に may be omitted and only 間 used.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"寝ている","r":"ねている","m":"is sleeping"},{"t":"間に","r":"あいだに","m":"while"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"掃除した","r":"そうじした","m":"cleaned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While my father was sleeping, I cleaned the room.","scene":"Father naps; I clean the house once"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"いる","m":"be in"},{"t":"間に","r":"あいだに","m":"while"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mt. Fuji"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"登りたい","r":"のぼりたい","m":"want to climb"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While I am still in Japan, I want to climb Mt. Fuji.","scene":"A student makes time to visit Mt. Fuji"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-037||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures and connecting forms","sub":"〜間に／間は · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜間に／間は","patternReading":"あいだに／あいだは","coreMeaning":"While ~: 間に = one-time event, 間は = continues throughout","slug":"forms","definition":"Clause A, the time span, connects with **V-ている**, **noun + の**, an **い-adjective**, or a **な-adjective + な**. B after **間に** is a one-time event; B after **間は** is a continuing action or state.","nuance":"This structure is also used in formal writing, for example: 在学している間に…, 会議の間は…. Do not use past-tense V-た for A because 間 refers to an ongoing span.","structure":[{"lhs":"V-ている","rhs":"間に + B (B occurs once)","note":"寝ている間に地震があった"},{"lhs":"V-ている","rhs":"間は + B (B continues)","note":"寝ている間は静かだ"},{"lhs":"N + の","rhs":"間に／間は","note":"夏休みの間に…"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"間に／間は","note":"明るい間に帰る"},{"lhs":"Aな + な","rhs":"間に／間は","note":"元気な間に…"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出かけている","r":"でかけている","m":"is out"},{"t":"間に","r":"あいだに","m":"while"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While my mother was out, I did my homework.","scene":"Mother is out; I finish my homework"},{"tokens":[{"t":"夏休み","r":"なつやすみ","m":"summer vacation"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"間は","r":"あいだは","m":"throughout"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"泳いだ","r":"およいだ","m":"swam"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I swam every day throughout summer vacation.","scene":"A sunny vacation with daily swimming"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-037||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage: に = once / は = throughout","sub":"〜間に／間は · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜間に／間は","patternReading":"あいだに／あいだは","coreMeaning":"While ~: 間に = one-time event, 間は = continues throughout","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch guides particle choice. If B is a **countable event that happens once** (an earthquake, call, cleaning, or reading) → use **間に**. If B is a **state or action continuing from start to finish of A** (concentrating, not playing games, staying quiet) → use **間は**.","nuance":"Quick test: if adding **ずっと** (throughout) to B still sounds natural, use 間は. If ずっと does not fit because B is only a point in time, use 間に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"授業","r":"じゅぎょう","m":"class"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"間に","r":"あいだに","m":"during"},{"t":"居眠り","r":"いねむり","m":"dozing"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I dozed off during class.","scene":"Dozing once during class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"授業","r":"じゅぎょう","m":"class"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"間は","r":"あいだは","m":"throughout"},{"t":"ずっと","m":"continuously"},{"t":"集中","r":"しゅうちゅう","m":"focus"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I stayed focused throughout the entire class.","scene":"A student listens seriously for the whole class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-037||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: うちに・ながら・ところに","sub":"〜間に／間は · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜間に／間は","patternReading":"あいだに／あいだは","coreMeaning":"While ~: 間に = one-time event, 間は = continues throughout","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most often confused with this one are **〜うちに**, **〜ながら**, and **〜ところに**. 間に simply describes a bounded time span.","nuance":"They differ in their view of time: うちに implies taking the chance; ながら requires the same subject and parallel actions; ところに captures an exact moment. 間に is more flexible in subject and duration.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"留守","r":"るす","m":"away from home"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"間に","r":"あいだに","m":"while"},{"t":"泥棒","r":"どろぼう","m":"thief"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"入った","r":"はいった","m":"broke in"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While I was away, a thief broke in.","scene":"You return to find the house burglarized"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜うちに","point":"うちに emphasizes SEIZING THE CHANCE to act before a state ends; 間に simply marks an objective span. Ex.: 明るいうちに帰ろう (go home before dark) / 明るい間に外で遊んだ (played outside while it was light)."},{"vs":"〜ながら","point":"ながら joins TWO parallel actions with the same subject; 間に allows B to occur within A, with different subjects possible. Ex.: 音楽を聞きながら歩く / 寝ている間に地震があった."},{"vs":"〜ところに","point":"ところに captures the exact MOMENT when B interrupts A; 間に marks a longer time span. Ex.: 出かけようとしているところに電話が来た / 留守の間に泥棒が入った."}]}},"n3-037||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner errors","sub":"〜間に／間は · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜間に／間は","patternReading":"あいだに／あいだは","coreMeaning":"While ~: 間に = one-time event, 間は = continues throughout","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse these because both can translate as “while.” Force yourself to ask: **Is B a one-time event or a process?**","nuance":"In tests, the trap is often は with a one-time event or に with an action containing ずっと.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"夏休み","r":"なつやすみ","m":"summer vacation"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"間に","r":"あいだに","m":"during"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"books"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"3冊","r":"さんさつ","m":"three books"},{"t":"読んだ","r":"よんだ","m":"read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I read three books during summer vacation.","scene":"Talking about summer reading"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 寝ている間は地震があった。 → ✅ 寝ている間に地震があった。 (an earthquake is a one-time event)","❌ 日本にいる間に勉強し続けた。 → ✅ 日本にいる間は勉強し続けた。 (studied continuously throughout)","❌ 夏休み間に本を読んだ。 → ✅ 夏休みの間に本を読んだ。 (a noun needs の)","❌ 食べた間に話しましょう。 → ✅ 食べている間に話しましょう。 (use V-ている for an ongoing span)"],"compare":null}},"n3-037||memory":{"title":"🎯 Visual memory tips","sub":"〜間に／間は · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜間に／間は","patternReading":"あいだに／あいだは","coreMeaning":"While ~: 間に = one-time event, 間は = continues throughout","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a time span as a line. **に** is like a dot placed on the line, marking one moment or event. **は** is like a pen sweeping across the entire line, emphasizing the whole span.","nuance":"In Japanese culture, 間 (MA) can also mean a meaningful pause or interval. This helps you remember that a time span is not empty; it has its own significance.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-037||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜間に／間は · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜間に／間は","patternReading":"あいだに／あいだは","coreMeaning":"While ~: 間に = one-time event, 間は = continues throughout","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases containing 間, such as 〜ている間に, 〜の間じゅう, and 長い間, frequently appear on the JLPT and in daily conversation. Mastering them helps you understand and use the pattern accurately in real contexts.","nuance":"長い間 is often used in thanks; 間じゅう emphasizes the entire time span.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"長い","r":"ながい","m":"long"},{"t":"間","r":"あいだ","m":"period"},{"t":"お世話になりました","r":"おせわになりました","m":"thank you for your help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thank you for all your help over such a long time.","scene":"Thanking a colleague when saying goodbye"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-038||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜たばかり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たばかり","patternReading":"たばかり","coreMeaning":"Just ~ (an action has just finished; it still feels fresh)","slug":"core","definition":"〜たばかり describes an action that has **just finished from the speaker’s subjective perspective**. Unlike たところ (objective, immediate timing), たばかり emphasizes the feeling that it is **still fresh**. The time span can range from minutes to months as long as the speaker feels it was not long ago.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. In conversation, たばかり often expresses pride (recently married), complaint (broke right after buying it), or modest explanation (just started working, so I am not used to it yet). Compared with たところ, たばかり is **softer and more subjective**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have only just arrived in Japan.","scene":"Answering whether you have been in Japan long"},{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just got married.","scene":"Telling a coworker about your wedding"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-038||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜たばかり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たばかり","patternReading":"たばかり","coreMeaning":"Just ~ (an action has just finished; it still feels fresh)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches **directly to the verb’s た-form**. It does not use the dictionary form or て-form. The two main positions are sentence-final (〜たばかりだ) and before a noun (〜たばかりの + N).","nuance":"Use 〜たばかりです in polite speech. To state a reason, use 〜たばかりなので. The form 〜たばかりの + N is common in advertising: 発売したばかりの新商品.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（た形）","rhs":"ばかりだ","note":"Sentence-final: just V"},{"lhs":"V（た形）","rhs":"ばかりの + N","note":"Modifier: N that was just V"},{"lhs":"V（た形）","rhs":"ばかりなので／ばかりから","note":"Reason: because just V, …"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今朝","r":"けさ","m":"this morning"},{"t":"起きた","r":"おきた","m":"woke up"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I only just woke up this morning.","scene":"Explaining your sleepy voice at lunchtime"},{"tokens":[{"t":"買った","r":"かった","m":"bought"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"スマホ","r":"すまほ","m":"smartphone"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"落としてしまった","r":"おとしてしまった","m":"dropped accidentally"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I dropped the phone I had just bought.","scene":"Telling a friend about your bad luck"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-038||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜たばかり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たばかり","patternReading":"たばかり","coreMeaning":"Just ~ (an action has just finished; it still feels fresh)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜たばかり appears in many communication settings: explaining why you are not used to something, emphasizing that an item is fresh, or politely declining because you just ate. The pattern 〜たばかりの + N is especially common when introducing products.","nuance":"There is no fixed time limit: “just” may mean five minutes or two weeks, depending on **the speaker’s feeling**. When used to decline an invitation, it sounds natural and polite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"入社","r":"にゅうしゃ","m":"joining company"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"慣れ","r":"なれ","m":"be used to"},{"t":"ていない","r":"ていない","m":"not yet"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just joined the company, so I am not used to things yet.","scene":"A new employee explains working slowly"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"ate"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"なので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おなか","r":"おなか","m":"stomach"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いっぱい","r":"いっぱい","m":"full"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just ate, so I am full.","scene":"Declining an offer of more food"},{"tokens":[{"t":"生まれた","r":"うまれた","m":"was born"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"赤ちゃん","r":"あかちゃん","m":"baby"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"柔らかい","r":"やわらかい","m":"soft"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A newborn baby is soft.","scene":"Talking about newborns"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-038||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with たところ / てばかり","sub":"〜たばかり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たばかり","patternReading":"たばかり","coreMeaning":"Just ~ (an action has just finished; it still feels fresh)","slug":"compare","definition":"The two patterns most often confused with たばかり (subjective “just”) are たところ (objective, immediate completion) and てばかりいる (doing nothing but, often critical).","nuance":"たばかり emphasizes the feeling that something is fresh; たところ emphasizes the exact immediate moment; てばかりいる has nothing to do with “just finished” and expresses criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"着いた","r":"ついた","m":"arrived"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just arrived. (It was an hour ago.)","scene":"Answering when you arrived; たばかり fits the fresh feeling"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"着いた","r":"ついた","m":"arrived"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"just now"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I arrived just now. (A few seconds ago.)","scene":"Answering a call right after entering the house"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たところ","point":"たところ = objectively “just finished at this moment”; たばかり = subjectively “still feels fresh,” with flexible timing."},{"vs":"〜てばかりいる","point":"てばかりいる = “do nothing but V” (critical), not “just did.” Example: 食べてばかりいる = keep doing nothing but eating."}]}},"n3-038||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜たばかり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たばかり","patternReading":"たばかり","coreMeaning":"Just ~ (an action has just finished; it still feels fresh)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often forget to conjugate into the た-form or confuse たばかり with たところ. たばかり is less natural for something that happened only seconds ago; use たところ in that situation.","nuance":"This pattern always requires the た-form; never use the dictionary form (食べるばかり is wrong). Also, ばかり after the て-form means “do nothing but” (食べてばかり), so keep the meanings separate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"さっき","r":"さっき","m":"earlier"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"ate"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just finished eating a little while ago.","scene":"Declining an invitation to eat; some time has passed"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べるばかりです → ✅ 食べたばかりです (use the た-form)","今着いたばかりです is still CORRECT even if it was 5 seconds ago; 今着いたところです sounds more natural when stressing the exact moment — both work, but the NUANCE differs.","❌ 食べてばかりいます (different meaning: do nothing but eat) → ✅ 食べたばかりです (just ate)"],"compare":null}},"n3-038||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips and imagery","sub":"〜たばかり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たばかり","patternReading":"たばかり","coreMeaning":"Just ~ (an action has just finished; it still feels fresh)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture something **fresh from the oven, still steaming**: たばかり carries the feeling that something is still fresh and intact. In this pattern, ばかり suggests “only just” or “still fresh.”","nuance":"Compared with たところ, which captures a precise moment like a snapshot, たばかり is like a cup of tea that is still warm—you do not need to know exactly how long ago it was, only that it still feels warm.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"買った","r":"かった","m":"bought"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a book I just bought.","scene":"Gently reinforcing the pattern"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-038||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases and collocations","sub":"〜たばかり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜たばかり","patternReading":"たばかり","coreMeaning":"Just ~ (an action has just finished; it still feels fresh)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in exams and everyday life. Learning them as chunks helps you respond faster when you encounter them.","nuance":"Most express a recent event or give an explanation; workplace phrases are especially useful for asking others for understanding.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"生まれた","r":"うまれた","m":"was born"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"just"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"赤ちゃん","r":"あかちゃん","m":"baby"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A baby that was just born.","scene":"Describing a baby in a hospital"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-039||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In progress / Just finished ~ (three action moments)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ところだ describes a specific moment in an action. The preceding verb form determines the meaning: V辞書形＋ところ = about to do, Vている＋ところ = in the middle of doing, and Vた＋ところ = just finished. Japanese speakers use this pattern to capture the exact current moment.","nuance":"This pattern describes a state right around the time of speaking, not habits or general truths. In conversation, dropping です sounds casual; adding です is polite and important at work. ちょうど / 今 / まさに strengthens the sense of “right now.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しているところ","r":"しているところ","m":"in the middle of doing"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m doing my homework right now.","scene":"Answering what you are doing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょうど","r":"ちょうど","m":"just"},{"t":"出かけるところ","r":"でかけるところ","m":"about to go out"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m just about to go out.","scene":"The phone rings as you prepare to leave"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰ってきたところ","r":"かえってきたところ","m":"just got back"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ve just gotten back.","scene":"Someone calls as you enter the house"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-039||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In progress / Just finished ~ (three action moments)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the three core structures and the past variant. The key is the **verb form**: 辞書形, ている, or た. Each form corresponds to a point on the action timeline.","nuance":"All three forms are natural in speech and writing. Use です with seniors, customers, and colleagues. ところだった recounts something that almost happened and often carries a sense of relief.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"+ ところだ","note":"About to do — action has not started"},{"lhs":"Vている","rhs":"+ ところだ","note":"In progress — midway"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"+ ところだ","note":"Just finished — completion has just occurred"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"+ ところだった","note":"Almost did it (but it did not happen)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今から","r":"いまから","m":"from now"},{"t":"食べるところ","r":"たべるところ","m":"about to eat"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m just about to eat.","scene":"Friends invite you out as you set the table"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べているところ","r":"たべているところ","m":"in the middle of eating"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m eating lunch.","scene":"A call comes during lunch"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べたところ","r":"たべたところ","m":"just finished eating"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ve just finished lunch.","scene":"Asked whether you have eaten"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-039||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In progress / Just finished ~ (three action moments)","slug":"usage","definition":"In daily life, 〜ところだ is often used to report your current status when asked what you are doing, to decline tactfully, or to confirm work progress. It also appears in business emails and messages as a polite way to say that something is being handled.","nuance":"With managers or customers, use ただ今 + 〜ているところです for a professional tone. Put ちょうど at the beginning to emphasize coincidence. Do not use this pattern for distant past events; たところだ means “just.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しているところ","r":"しているところ","m":"in a meeting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Sorry, I’m in a meeting right now.","scene":"You answer during a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょうど","r":"ちょうど","m":"just"},{"t":"食べ終わったところ","r":"たべおわったところ","m":"just finished eating"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ve just finished eating.","scene":"You are invited to eat after becoming full"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-039||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns contrasted","sub":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In progress / Just finished ~ (three action moments)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch untangles patterns that are easily confused around ところ. The most important distinction is たところ versus たばかり, along with ところで (changing the topic) versus ところだ.","nuance":"たところ emphasizes the **objective point of completion**; たばかり emphasizes the speaker’s **sense that it is recent**, even if some time has passed. たとたん emphasizes an **event immediately afterward**; ところで at the start of a sentence changes the topic.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"まとめているところ","r":"まとめているところ","m":"compiling"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m compiling the materials.","scene":"A colleague asks about your progress"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たばかり","point":"したところ = objective, exactly just finished; たばかり = subjective, emphasizes “just.” 例: 今帰ったところです (just arrived now) vs 今帰ったばかりです (just got back, perhaps minutes ago)."},{"vs":"〜たとたん（に）","point":"たところ only reports a state of recent completion; たとたん emphasizes that an event happened immediately afterward. 例: 家に着いたところです (just arrived home) vs 家に着いたとたん、雨が降り出した (as soon as I arrived, it started raining)."},{"vs":"ところで（chuyển đề tài）","point":"ところで at the start of a sentence is a discourse marker for changing the topic. It is unrelated to this pattern; ところだ identifies the action’s position."}]}},"n3-039||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In progress / Just finished ~ (three action moments)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes learners make with ところだ. Remember: the verb form determines the meaning; たところ never means “after”; and this pattern is not used for habits.","nuance":"These errors often come from translating word for word. When unsure, ask: What point is the action at? Which verb form matches that point?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗るところ","r":"のるところ","m":"about to board"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m just about to board the train.","scene":"Texting beside the train door"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 行かないところだ (to mean “about not going”) → ✅ 行くところだ, or use context to clarify.","❌ 食べたところです (mistakenly understood as “after eating”) → ✅ Means only “just finished eating,” not “after.”","❌ 毎日走っているところです (describing a habit) → ✅ 毎日走っています.","❌ いいところに来た (mistaken for this grammar pattern) → ✅ This idiom means “You came at just the right time.”"],"compare":null}},"n3-039||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In progress / Just finished ~ (three action moments)","slug":"memory","definition":"Do not memorize three separate formulas. Remember that ところ means **position**. Each verb form shows where you are in the action: **before** it (辞書形), **in the middle** (ている), or **just past the finish** (た). Mnemonic: “Before the gate — inside — just out the door.”","nuance":"The ところだった variant is easy to remember as standing at the gate but not entering, meaning “almost did.” ちょうど and 今 help identify “right now” and are common in real conversations.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-039||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"ところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In progress / Just finished ~ (three action moments)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases with ところ appear often on the JLPT and in workplace communication: **今〜ところです**, **ちょうど〜ところでした**, **ただ今、〜ているところです**, and **危うく〜ところだった**. Memorize them for faster responses.","nuance":"In work emails, ただ今 + 〜ているところです is the standard way to report that something is being handled. 危うく + ところだった describes narrowly avoiding trouble and often conveys relief.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ただ今","r":"ただいま","m":"right now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"confirmation"},{"t":"しているところ","r":"しているところ","m":"checking"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m checking it right now.","scene":"Replying to a work email"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-040||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In the middle of ~ / Just finished ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜ところだ does not have one fixed meaning by itself. It attaches to **three verb forms** to identify a **moment** in the action: V辞書形 = about to begin, Vている = in progress, and Vた = just completed. Japanese speakers use this pattern to report “what stage I am at now.”","nuance":"The nuance is **objective and time-specific**, without strong emotion. Combine it with 今・ちょうど・たった今 to pinpoint the moment. In the workplace, it sounds polite and professional.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰って","r":"かえって","m":"return"},{"t":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just got back.","scene":"You have just entered the house and someone asks if you arrived."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょうど","m":"right now"},{"t":"出かける","r":"でかける","m":"go out"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m just about to go out.","scene":"A friend calls while you are putting on your shoes."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-040||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In the middle of ~ / Just finished ~","slug":"forms","definition":"The three verb forms create three meanings. In addition, **V辞書形 + ところだった** adds the sense of “almost did it, but it did not happen,” while **Vた + ところで** is a concessive structure meaning “even if you finish it, it is useless.”","nuance":"This pattern contains a built-in time perspective: there is no complicated conjugation; changing だ → だった simply shifts the viewpoint to the past.","structure":[{"lhs":"V dictionary","rhs":"Vる + ところだ","note":"About to do (not started)"},{"lhs":"V ている","rhs":"Vている + ところだ","note":"In progress (midway)"},{"lhs":"V た","rhs":"Vた + ところだ","note":"Just finished (this moment)"},{"lhs":"V dictionary","rhs":"Vる + ところだった","note":"Almost did it, but it did not happen"},{"lhs":"V た","rhs":"Vた + ところで","note":"Even if you finish it..."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今から","r":"いまから","m":"from now"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m about to eat now.","scene":"Your mother asks whether you have eaten."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"している","m":"doing"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m in a meeting right now.","scene":"A coworker texts to ask about something."},{"tokens":[{"t":"たった今","r":"たったいま","m":"just now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"着いた","r":"ついた","m":"arrived"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just arrived.","scene":"Calling to say you have just arrived."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-040||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In the middle of ~ / Just finished ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when **the listener needs to know what stage you are at** in an action. To answer “What are you doing now?”, use Vているところ. To report that you are about to leave, use V辞書形+ところ. To report that you have just finished, use Vた+ところ.","nuance":"In the workplace, ただ今〜ところです is a **polite, professional** way to ask someone to wait without dismissing their request. 危うく〜ところだった often carries a feeling of **relief after a narrow escape**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"している","m":"doing"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m doing my homework.","scene":"A friend asks, “What are you doing?”"},{"tokens":[{"t":"危うく","r":"あやうく","m":"almost"},{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accident"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"起こす","r":"おこす","m":"cause"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I almost caused an accident.","scene":"You slam on the brakes before a truck."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ただ今","r":"ただいま","m":"currently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"check"},{"t":"している","m":"doing"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m checking it now.","scene":"Replying to a customer waiting for an answer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-040||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In the middle of ~ / Just finished ~","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch clears up the **most common confusions**: たところ vs たばかり, ている vs ているところ, and Vる+ところ vs つもり.","nuance":"たところ is **objective** and refers to a very short time span, often a few minutes. たばかり reflects the speaker’s **subjective feeling** of “recently,” even if the event happened quite a while ago.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰った","r":"かえった","m":"returned"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just got back.","scene":"A coworker asks whether you have gone home."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たばかり","point":"たところ = just finished moments ago, objectively; たばかり = “recently” as felt by the speaker, even if some time has passed."},{"vs":"〜ている","point":"ている simply describes an ongoing state; ているところ highlights the exact moment in the middle of the action."},{"vs":"〜つもり","point":"つもり is a prior intention; Vる+ところ is an action about to happen right now."}]}},"n3-040||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner errors & traps","sub":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In the middle of ~ / Just finished ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors mainly come from stretching the time too far beyond the “moment,” or using the wrong verb form.","nuance":"This pattern applies to **a narrow point in time**. It is unsuitable for next year’s plans, daily habits, or general future possibilities.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰って","r":"かえって","m":"return"},{"t":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just got back.","scene":"Telling a friend after arriving at your room."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 来年結婚するところだ → ✅ 来年結婚するつもりだ (distant future)","❌ 毎日勉強しているところだ → ✅ 毎日勉強している (habit)","❌ 今、電話しないところです → ✅ 今、電話するところです (Vない is not used)","❌ 食べたところだ when you mean “about to eat” → ✅ 食べるところだ"],"compare":null}},"n3-040||memory":{"title":"🎯 Visual memory tip","sub":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In the middle of ~ / Just finished ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine an action as a house: V辞書形 stands **in front of the door**, Vている is **inside the room**, and Vた has just **stepped out of the gate**. ところ is the **coordinate** where you are standing.","nuance":"This image helps you choose the form quickly: see the verb form and immediately picture its position.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-040||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","patternReading":"するところだ／しているところだ／したところだ","coreMeaning":"About to ~ / In the middle of ~ / Just finished ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear constantly in the JLPT and workplace emails. Memorize them for a quick, natural response.","nuance":"Phrases with 今/ちょうど/たった今/ただ今 all emphasize the timing “right now.” ちょうど additionally suggests a coincidental match.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ただ今","r":"ただいま","m":"currently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"check"},{"t":"している","m":"doing"},{"t":"ところ","r":"ところ","m":"moment"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m currently checking it.","scene":"Emailing a waiting customer about their inquiry."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-041||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜際（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜際（に）","patternReading":"さいに","coreMeaning":"When ~ / on the occasion of ~ (formal – written, notices)","slug":"core","definition":"〜際（に） describes the **time or occasion** for doing something, translated as 'when ~' or 'on the occasion of ~'. It is a **formal written expression**, often found in notices, instructions, regulations, and business emails. Unlike ordinary ときに, 際に highlights an **important or noteworthy occasion** and has an official tone.","nuance":"It has a **formal, objective** tone and is uncommon in everyday conversation. When you see or hear 際に, it usually signals an official document or regulated announcement. 際に without は/には usually reports an event; adding は or には highlights a procedure or condition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お"},{"t":"困り","r":"こまり","m":"difficulty"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際は","r":"さいは","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お"},{"t":"声がけ","r":"こえがけ","m":"call out"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you need help, please call out to us.","scene":"Notice on a train or in a building"},{"tokens":[{"t":"来日","r":"らいにち","m":"visit Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際に","r":"さいに","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"各地","r":"かくち","m":"various places"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"視察","r":"しさつ","m":"inspect"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When he visited Japan, he inspected various places.","scene":"Formal report sentence"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-041||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜際（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜際（に）","patternReading":"さいに","coreMeaning":"When ~ / on the occasion of ~ (formal – written, notices)","slug":"forms","definition":"There are three main connection patterns: **noun + の + 際（に）**, **dictionary-form verb + 際（に）**, and **た-form verb + 際（に）**. Adding は or には gives the sentence a **condition/rule** nuance: 'when this situation occurs, you must...'.","nuance":"With verbs, **V-辞書形** describes an action that will happen or is about to happen; **V-た** describes an action that already happened. In regulations, **際は/際には** ends the condition clause, followed by a polite command or instruction.","structure":[{"lhs":"N ＋ の","rhs":"＋ 際（に）","note":"Noun for an occasion or situation (例: 緊急の際)"},{"lhs":"V-辞書形","rhs":"＋ 際（に）","note":"When about to do ~ / an upcoming action (例: 利用する際)"},{"lhs":"V-た","rhs":"＋ 際（に）","note":"When having done ~ / a completed action (例: 来日した際)"},{"lhs":"〜際は / 際には","rhs":"＋ 指示・依頼","note":"Emphasizes a rule or condition (例: 際はご連絡ください)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お"},{"t":"降り","r":"おり","m":"get off"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際は","r":"さいは","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お"},{"t":"忘れ物","r":"わすれもの","m":"forgotten item"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"よう"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ご注意","r":"ごちゅうい","m":"take care"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When getting off, please be careful not to leave anything behind.","scene":"Announcement on a Japanese train"},{"tokens":[{"t":"申し込み","r":"もうしこみ","m":"application"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際に","r":"さいに","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"書類","r":"しょるい","m":"documents"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ご"},{"t":"提出","r":"ていしゅつ","m":"submit"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When applying, please submit the required documents.","scene":"Instructions on an application form"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-041||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜際（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜際（に）","patternReading":"さいに","coreMeaning":"When ~ / on the occasion of ~ (formal – written, notices)","slug":"usage","definition":"Meaning 1: used in **instructions, rules, and notices**—'When ~, do ~.' Meaning 2: used in **formal accounts** of past events—'When ~, did ~.' Meaning 3: in **business emails**, 際には can emphasize 'whenever necessary.' On signs, it is often shortened to **際は** or simply 際.","nuance":"This is **official language** with a distant but polite tone. Do not use it for ordinary activities such as eating or going to bed. In conversation, use ときに instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"緊急","r":"きんきゅう","m":"emergency"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際は","r":"さいは","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"非常口","r":"ひじょうぐち","m":"emergency exit"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"ご"},{"t":"退場","r":"たいじょう","m":"exit"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In an emergency, please exit through the emergency exit.","scene":"Building safety sign"},{"tokens":[{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際","r":"さい","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"パスポート"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"be sure to"},{"t":"ご"},{"t":"持参","r":"じさん","m":"bring"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When traveling abroad, be sure to bring your passport.","scene":"Formal travel leaflet advice"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お"},{"t":"問い合わせ","r":"といあわせ","m":"inquiry"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際には","r":"さいには","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お"},{"t":"気軽に","r":"きがるに","m":"feel free to"},{"t":"ご"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have any questions, please feel free to contact us.","scene":"Business email for customer questions"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-041||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from ときに・にあたって","sub":"〜際（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜際（に）","patternReading":"さいに","coreMeaning":"When ~ / on the occasion of ~ (formal – written, notices)","slug":"compare","definition":"All three can be translated as 'when ~', but their **registers** differ. 際に is used in **notices, instructions, and regulations**; ときに is used in **everyday conversation**; にあたって is used for **major occasions** such as graduation ceremonies or openings, often with preparation or speeches.","nuance":"Using the wrong register is a serious Japanese error. Use 際に in formal documents, ときに when speaking with friends, and にあたって for special occasions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"困った","r":"こまった","m":"run into trouble"},{"t":"とき"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you run into trouble, let me know.","scene":"Casual conversation between friends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"ときに","point":"ときに works in all contexts, especially everyday conversation; 際に is limited to formal documents and notices."},{"vs":"にあたって","point":"にあたって emphasizes a special or major occasion and often preparation or a speech; 際に simply marks a time or occasion and need not be momentous."},{"vs":"場合","point":"場合 emphasizes a hypothetical situation or condition; 際に emphasizes the time an actual event occurs or is about to occur."}]}},"n3-041||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜際（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜際（に）","patternReading":"さいに","coreMeaning":"When ~ / on the occasion of ~ (formal – written, notices)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The two most common mistakes are: (1) **using 際に in ordinary conversation**, which sounds stiff and overdone; and (2) **forgetting の after a noun**—because 際 is a noun, の is required after N.","nuance":"Also, do not confuse it with にあたって: にあたって highlights a special occasion and the preparation involved, while 際に highlights a procedure or rule.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"地震","r":"じしん","m":"earthquake"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際は","r":"さいは","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"慌てず","r":"あわてず","m":"without panicking"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行動","r":"こうどう","m":"act"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In an earthquake, stay calm and take action.","scene":"Safety guidance. Correct example showing standard usage."}],"pitfalls":["❌ ご飯を食べる際は箸を使う → ✅ ご飯を食べるときは箸を使う (際に is too formal for an everyday activity)","❌ 地震際 → ✅ 地震の際 (の is missing after the noun)","❌ 卒業の際に先生に感謝を伝えた → ✅ 卒業にあたって先生に感謝を伝えた (use にあたって instead of 際に for a special formal occasion)"],"compare":null}},"n3-041||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜際（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜際（に）","patternReading":"さいに","coreMeaning":"When ~ / on the occasion of ~ (formal – written, notices)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a **formal notice board**: 'When X happens, do Y.' 際(さい) is a Sino-Japanese word meaning 'time, occasion, or boundary.' When reading instructions, rules, or official notices, think of 際.","nuance":"This tip helps you avoid over-formality in conversation. Ask: 'Is this a sign, instruction sheet, or business email?' If yes, use 際に; if you are talking with friends, use ときに.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-041||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜際（に） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜際（に）","patternReading":"さいに","coreMeaning":"When ~ / on the occasion of ~ (formal – written, notices)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear constantly on signs, instructions, and official notices. Memorizing them helps you recognize formal writing and use each expression in the right context.","nuance":"These formulaic phrases often begin a conditional clause, followed by a polite command or instruction. In business emails, use them together with keigo.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ご"},{"t":"利用","r":"りよう","m":"use"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"際は","r":"さいは","m":"when (formal)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"説明書","r":"せつめいしょ","m":"instructions"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"よく"},{"t":"お"},{"t":"読み","r":"よみ","m":"read"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When using the product, please read the instructions carefully.","scene":"Product instruction sheet"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-042||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜にあたって／にあたり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にあたって／にあたり","patternReading":"にあたって／にあたり","coreMeaning":"On a major occasion, before undertaking something important","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜にあたって／にあたり refers to the time when one is about to enter an important or significant occasion, such as New Year’s, an opening ceremony, graduation, or marriage. The following clause is often a speech, thanks, or preparatory action. It means more than simply “when”: it suggests standing at the threshold of the occasion and preparing oneself.","nuance":"Formal and ceremonial. Used in formal speech and writing, such as invitations and company emails. Not used in everyday conversation. にあたり is more formal than にあたって and is common in written documents.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新年","r":"しんねん","m":"New Year"},{"t":"にあたり","r":"にあたり","m":"on the occasion"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご挨拶","r":"ごあいさつ","m":"greetings"},{"t":"申し上げます","r":"もうしあげます","m":"I offer"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of the New Year, I would like to offer my greetings.","scene":"New Year speech to the company"},{"tokens":[{"t":"卒業","r":"そつぎょう","m":"graduation"},{"t":"にあたり","r":"にあたり","m":"on the occasion"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teachers"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"感謝","r":"かんしゃ","m":"thanks"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of graduation, I would like to thank my teachers.","scene":"Graduation speech before the whole school"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-042||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"〜にあたって／にあたり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にあたって／にあたり","patternReading":"にあたって／にあたり","coreMeaning":"On a major occasion, before undertaking something important","slug":"forms","definition":"There are two main patterns: **a noun for a major occasion + にあたって** and **a dictionary-form verb + にあたって**. にあたり is the more formal variant and often appears in speeches or emails. The following clause should express preparation or a respectful statement.","nuance":"Do not use this structure with て-form or た-form verbs, or with adjectives. With nouns, the noun must refer to an important event, such as 開会, 新年, or 卒業.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（major occasion）","rhs":"+ にあたって / にあたり","note":"Combines with a noun for an important event: 開会, 卒業, 新年, 結婚…"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"+ にあたって / にあたり","note":"A major action about to begin; next clause shows preparation or speech."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"開会","r":"かいかい","m":"opening"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"on the occasion"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お願い","r":"おねがい","m":"request"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of the opening, I have a request.","scene":"Speech at an opening ceremony"},{"tokens":[{"t":"出発","r":"しゅっぱつ","m":"departure"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"check"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before departing, I checked my luggage.","scene":"Preparing for an important business trip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-042||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜にあたって／にあたり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にあたって／にあたり","patternReading":"にあたって／にあたり","coreMeaning":"On a major occasion, before undertaking something important","slug":"usage","definition":"〜にあたって is mainly used in three situations: ① **speeches** for ceremonies, openings, or New Year; ② **expressing thanks or apologies** at graduation, farewells, or transfers; ③ **preparing** for a major event such as marriage, going abroad, or starting a business.","nuance":"In formal speeches, the standard pattern is: **[occasion] にあたり + [greeting/thanks] + [wish/hope]**. This pattern is almost obligatory at Japanese opening ceremonies.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"赴任","r":"ふにん","m":"assignment"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"語学研修","r":"ごがくけんしゅう","m":"language training"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"受けた","r":"うけた","m":"attended"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before being assigned overseas, I attended language training.","scene":"Telling colleagues about preparation for an assignment"},{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"on the occasion"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新居","r":"しんきょ","m":"new home"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"購入","r":"こうにゅう","m":"purchase"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As we prepared for our marriage, we bought a new home.","scene":"Telling friends about wedding preparations"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-042||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜にあたって／にあたり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にあたって／にあたり","patternReading":"にあたって／にあたり","coreMeaning":"On a major occasion, before undertaking something important","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare にあたって with similar “when” patterns: 際に, ときに, and に際して. All can translate as “when,” but their contexts and nuances differ. にあたって combines a major occasion with preparation or emotion; 際に concerns procedures or rules; ときに is neutral; に際して often concerns administrative procedures.","nuance":"If the sentence could add 感謝します, にあたって is a good choice. For procedural instructions such as reading or submitting something, 際に or に際して is more likely.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"出発","r":"しゅっぱつ","m":"departure"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"check"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before departing, I check my luggage.","scene":"Preparing for an important trip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜際に","point":"際に = when something occurs according to a procedure or rule; にあたって = on a major occasion + preparation. E.g.: 使用の際に説明書を読む / 卒業にあたって挨拶する."},{"vs":"〜ときに","point":"ときに = neutral “when,” used in everyday conversation; にあたって is formal and limited to important occasions."},{"vs":"〜に際して","point":"Both are formal, but に際して leans toward procedures, while にあたって leans toward emotion and opening a ceremonial occasion."}]}},"n3-042||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜にあたって／にあたり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にあたって／にあたり","patternReading":"にあたって／にあたり","coreMeaning":"On a major occasion, before undertaking something important","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use にあたって for every event, including small tasks, or confuse it with ときに in conversation. Remember: it is formal and used when the speaker views the event as major and worth preparing for.","nuance":"Do not use にあたって in everyday conversation. Use ときに or 前に instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"on the occasion"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"両親","r":"りょうしん","m":"parents"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"挨拶","r":"あいさつ","m":"greet"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of my marriage, I greeted my parents.","scene":"Polite preparation on the wedding day"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ご飯を食べるにあたって → ✅ ご飯を食べる前に","❌ 散歩するにあたって、靴を履いた → ✅ 散歩する前に、靴を履いた","❌ 入学にあたって、友達ができた (the following clause is an ordinary result) → ✅ 入学にあたって、必要な書類を準備した"],"compare":null}},"n3-042||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜にあたって／にあたり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にあたって／にあたり","patternReading":"にあたって／にあたり","coreMeaning":"On a major occasion, before undertaking something important","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip for にあたって: the kanji 当 (あ) means “to hit” or “to land exactly on.” あたる means “to hit,” so にあたって means “right on the occasion of ~.” Imagine standing before a great threshold and preparing before stepping through.","nuance":"Picture an opening ceremony ribbon-cutting: it is a major occasion, and everyone prepares carefully beforehand. When you encounter an important event, think of にあたって.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-042||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜にあたって／にあたり · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にあたって／にあたり","patternReading":"にあたって／にあたり","coreMeaning":"On a major occasion, before undertaking something important","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases include **新年にあたり** (on the occasion of the New Year), **開会にあたって** (on the occasion of an opening), **卒業にあたり** (on the occasion of graduation), and **〜を始めるにあたって** (before starting ~). These are formulaic openings in formal language.","nuance":"In the JLPT or when reading formal texts, these phrases immediately suggest an important speech or announcement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新年","r":"しんねん","m":"New Year"},{"t":"にあたり","r":"にあたり","m":"on the occasion"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"目標","r":"もくひょう","m":"goals"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"立てた","r":"たてた","m":"set"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"At the start of the New Year, I set some goals.","scene":"New Year diary entry about goals"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-043||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜次第で／次第では · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜次第で／次第では","patternReading":"しだいで・しだいでは","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; the result changes according to N","slug":"core","definition":"〜次第で expresses that the result changes according to N: depending on which N is chosen, the situation develops in that direction. The speaker presents N as the deciding factor, like the steering wheel of the situation. 〜次第では adds は and emphasizes that, depending on the circumstances, a possibility may occur.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; it is neither especially formal nor casual. It stresses N as the key factor directly determining the result, whether N can be controlled (effort) or not (weather). Compared with によって, 次第で more clearly conveys decisive influence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"どうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"決まる","r":"きまる","m":"be decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whether you succeed depends on your effort.","scene":"Encouraging someone preparing for an exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"予算","r":"よさん","m":"budget"},{"t":"次第では","r":"しだいでは","m":"depending on the case"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変え","r":"かえ","m":"change"},{"t":"なければならない","r":"なければならない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Depending on the budget, we may have to change our travel plans.","scene":"Discussing a trip with a friend while worried about the budget"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-043||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜次第で／次第では · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜次第で／次第では","patternReading":"しだいで・しだいでは","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; the result changes according to N","slug":"forms","definition":"The central formula is N + 次第で, where N must be a noun or nominalized expression. Adding は to form 次第では opens a possibility, which may be good or bad. At the end of a sentence, use 次第だ／です to mean “it depends on N.” Remember: Vます + 次第 is a completely different structure meaning “as soon as.”","nuance":"In formal writing, 次第で is often used in announcements and decisions; 次第では commonly occurs with possibility expressions such as かもしれない, なければならない, and ことになる. Do not use 次第で after a verb or adjective.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"次第で","note":"The result changes depending on N."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"次第では","note":"Opens a possibility: depending on N, ~ may happen."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"次第だ／です","note":"Sentence ending: it depends on N."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"出発","r":"しゅっぱつ","m":"departure"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"決める","r":"きめる","m":"decide"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We will decide whether to leave depending on the weather.","scene":"Friends debating whether to go hiking"},{"tokens":[{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"雰囲気","r":"ふんいき","m":"atmosphere"},{"t":"次第では","r":"しだいでは","m":"depending on the case"},{"t":"入ら","r":"はいら","m":"enter"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"かもしれない","r":"かもしれない","m":"may"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Depending on the atmosphere, I may not go into the shop.","scene":"Hesitating outside an unfamiliar café"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-043||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜次第で／次第では · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜次第で／次第では","patternReading":"しだいで・しだいでは","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; the result changes according to N","slug":"usage","definition":"In practice, 次第で most often appears in three situations: (1) decisions—choosing an option based on N; (2) conditions or warnings—with 次第では, stating a possible negative outcome; and (3) philosophical statements—the result depends on oneself.","nuance":"With 次第では, the following clause is often an unwanted result or a situation requiring change, because は implies comparison or selection. With 次第で, the following result is neutral and variable. This helps learners choose correctly on exams.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"次第では","r":"しだいでは","m":"depending on the case"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけ","r":"でかけ","m":"go out"},{"t":"られない","r":"られない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"かもしれません","r":"かもしれません","m":"probably may"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Depending on tomorrow’s weather, I may not be able to go out.","scene":"Planning a weekend outing while worrying about rain"},{"tokens":[{"t":"給料","r":"きゅうりょう","m":"salary"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"選び","r":"えらび","m":"choose"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to choose a job depending on the salary.","scene":"Job hunting and discussing selection criteria with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-043||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from によって","sub":"〜次第で／次第では · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜次第で／次第では","patternReading":"しだいで・しだいでは","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; the result changes according to N","slug":"compare","definition":"To avoid confusion: 〜によって says that things differ depending on N (地域によって習慣が違う). 〜次第で says that N is the key factor deciding the result (努力次第で変わる). Vます + 次第 has a completely different meaning: immediately after V.","nuance":"If the following clause describes something differing or changing according to N, use によって. If it describes a decision or an action that will be taken accordingly, use 次第で. If 次第 follows a verb ます-stem, it means “as soon as”; do not translate it as “depending on.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"成績","r":"せいせき","m":"grades"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"クラス","r":"クラス","m":"class"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"決まる","r":"きまる","m":"be decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Your class will be decided according to your grades.","scene":"Discussing class placement based on academic results"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜によって","point":"によって: differences vary objectively by N (地域によって習慣が違う). 次第で: N is the decisive factor, with a more intentional nuance (努力次第で変わる)."},{"vs":"Vます + 次第","point":"Vます + 次第: immediately after V (着き次第連絡します). It does not mean “depending on”; distinguish it by the form before it."}]}},"n3-043||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜次第で／次第では · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜次第で／次第では","patternReading":"しだいで・しだいでは","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; the result changes according to N","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors include confusing the two meanings of 次第, using Vた before 次第, or using によって when a decisive nuance is needed. This branch gives wrong/right pairs for immediate correction.","nuance":"The most serious error is confusing N + 次第で with Vます + 次第: simply check whether the word before 次第 is a noun or a verb. Another error is using によって when N is the decisive factor, which weakens the intended nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"決め","r":"きめ","m":"decide"},{"t":"ます","r":"ます","m":"polite"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will decide depending on the result.","scene":"Replying to a customer after checking the results"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 決まった次第で… → ✅ 決まり次第、… (Vます + 次第 does not use Vた)","△ 努力によって成功が決まる (not wrong, but bland and objective) → More natural: 努力次第で成功が決まる (emphasizes that N itself decides the result)","❌ 天気次第では出かける → ✅ 天気次第では出かけられないかもしれない (次第では + possibility clause)"],"compare":null}},"n3-043||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜次第で／次第では · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜次第で／次第では","patternReading":"しだいで・しだいでは","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; the result changes according to N","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a balance: N sits on one pan, and the result moves in the direction of the heavier side. 次第 is written with 次 (next/order) + 第 (rank), suggesting the order or condition of N—in other words, dependence on N.","nuance":"The key tip: check the word type before 次第—a noun means “depending on,” while a verb ます-stem means “as soon as.” Mnemonic: noun = depends; verb = as soon.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-043||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜次第で／次第では · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜次第で／次第では","patternReading":"しだいで・しだいでは","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; the result changes according to N","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases appear often in exams and at work: 〜次第だ (it depends on ~), 〜次第では (depending on the case), and Vます+次第 (as soon as). Memorizing them helps with quick recognition.","nuance":"In business email, Vます+次第 is very polite (“I will contact you as soon as it is done”). 次第で/次第では can also be used with friends and coworkers in conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"準備","r":"じゅんび","m":"preparation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"でき次第","r":"できしだい","m":"as soon as ready"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"will do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will contact you as soon as the preparations are complete.","scene":"Speaking to a business partner by email or phone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-044||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜らしい（推量・根拠） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしい（推量・根拠）","patternReading":"らしい","coreMeaning":"Seems / apparently (indirect inference) + typical of, true to","slug":"core","definition":"らしい has TWO completely different meanings: **(1) Indirect inference** — “seems, apparently,” based on heard or read information or indirectly observed signs; **(2) “Typical of”** — describing a person or thing as showing the defining traits of its group. The meaning depends on where らしい appears in the sentence.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. For inference, the speaker did NOT directly witness the event, but relies on hearsay or outward signs, so certainty is moderate—lower than に違いない. The “typical of” meaning often praises or criticizes someone according to social-role expectations and is common in Japanese: 日本人らしい, 先生らしい.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"not come"},{"t":"らしい","m":"apparently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Apparently, she is not coming today.","scene":"A coworker mentioned that she took the day off."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本当に","r":"ほんとうに","m":"really"},{"t":"男らしい","r":"おとこらしい","m":"manly"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is truly a manly man.","scene":"Everyone praises him for taking responsibility."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-044||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & conjugation","sub":"〜らしい（推量・根拠） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしい（推量・根拠）","patternReading":"らしい","coreMeaning":"Seems / apparently (indirect inference) + typical of, true to","slug":"forms","definition":"Inference structure: **V/A + らしい** (with な-adjectives, use the **stem + らしい**, without だ); **N + らしい** can stand at the end of a sentence. “Typical of” structure: **N + らしい + N**—らしい modifies the following noun. Polite: **らしいです**; connective: **らしく**; negative: **らしくない**; past: **らしかった**.","nuance":"らしい is an adjective-like auxiliary, so it remains unchanged before です: らしいです. Use らしく to connect clauses: らしく、... (“seems to..., so...”). らしくない is common with the meaning “not typical of.”","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）/ Aい（普通形）","rhs":"+ らしい"},{"lhs":"Aな（語幹）","rhs":"+ らしい"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"+ らしい (sentence-end → inference)"},{"lhs":"N らしい + N","rhs":"(meaning 2: typical / true to type)"},{"lhs":"らしいです / らしく / らしくない","rhs":"polite / connective / negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"らしい","m":"apparently"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Apparently, it is going to rain.","scene":"Hearing the weather forecast on a phone."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供らしい","r":"こどもらしい","m":"childlike"},{"t":"笑顔","r":"えがお","m":"smile"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"可愛い","r":"かわいい","m":"cute"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That childlike smile is so cute.","scene":"Looking at a baby’s bright smile."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-044||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & meaning clues","sub":"〜らしい（推量・根拠） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしい（推量・根拠）","patternReading":"らしい","coreMeaning":"Seems / apparently (indirect inference) + typical of, true to","slug":"usage","definition":"Used in two major contexts. **(1) Inference:** at the end of a sentence, when you hear information, read it, or notice indirect signs such as noise or facial expressions. **(2) Typical of:** before a noun, commenting that someone or something represents a group or role. Quick rule: **sentence-end = inference**, **before a noun = typical of**.","nuance":"For inference, らしい often passes on hearsay while adding the speaker’s judgment—it is less dryly factual than そうだ（伝聞）. For “typical of,” it can praise or tease according to social-role expectations: 男らしい, 女らしい, プロらしい.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"騒がしい","r":"さわがしい","m":"noisy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"何か","r":"なにか","m":"something"},{"t":"あった","r":"あった","m":"happened"},{"t":"らしい","m":"apparently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is noisy outside; apparently, something happened.","scene":"You hear noise in the hallway while working."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"女性らしい","r":"じょせいらしい","m":"feminine"},{"t":"服装","r":"ふくそう","m":"outfit"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"している","m":"wearing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is wearing a feminine outfit.","scene":"Praising a female coworker’s outfit."},{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"らしく","m":"apparently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"嬉しそう","r":"うれしそう","m":"looks happy"},{"t":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Apparently she passed the exam; she looked happy.","scene":"Seeing a classmate smile after results are announced."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-044||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from そうだ・ようだ・っぽい","sub":"〜らしい（推量・根拠） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしい（推量・根拠）","patternReading":"らしい","coreMeaning":"Seems / apparently (indirect inference) + typical of, true to","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch untangles easily confused patterns. In general: **らしい = indirect information / typical of**; **そうだ（外観） = directly visible**; **そうだ（伝聞） = simply reporting**; **ようだ = subjective perception**; **っぽい = somewhat like, not fully typical**.","nuance":"Choose based on your information source: if you **hear/read** it → らしい; if you **see it directly** → そうだ/ようだ; if you **repeat someone’s exact report** → そうだ（伝聞）; if it is **only somewhat like ~** → っぽい.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰った","r":"かえった","m":"went home"},{"t":"らしい","m":"apparently"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Apparently, she has already gone home.","scene":"In a meeting, you see her empty seat and hear she had to leave early."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうだ（外観）","point":"そうだ = directly observed at the moment of speaking; らしい = based on hearsay or indirect signs. 空が暗い＝雨が降りそうだ, but 天気予報で＝雨が降るらしい."},{"vs":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","point":"そうだ（伝聞） simply reports hearsay: “I hear that...”; らしい adds the speaker’s inference from evidence. 田中さんは来ないそうだ (reported) vs 田中さんは来ないらしい (inferred because he is absent)."},{"vs":"〜ようだ","point":"ようだ = a subjective judgment from personal observation or sensation; らしい = an objective basis from outside information. 彼は疲れているようだ (he looks tired) vs 彼は疲れているらしい (heard/read)."},{"vs":"〜っぽい","point":"っぽい = “somewhat like,” lacking the full traits; meaning 2 of らしい = “truly typical.” 子供っぽい大人 vs 子供らしい子供."}]}},"n3-044||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜らしい（推量・根拠） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしい（推量・根拠）","patternReading":"らしい","coreMeaning":"Seems / apparently (indirect inference) + typical of, true to","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors include confusing the two meanings of らしい, using it while directly observing something, and confusing its information source with そうだ（伝聞）.","nuance":"Learners often translate らしい as “seems” and use it even when they are looking at something directly. Always ask: “Am I seeing this directly, or only hearing or inferring it indirectly?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"近く","r":"ちかく","m":"near"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できた","m":"opened"},{"t":"らしい","m":"apparently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Apparently, a new shop has opened near the station.","scene":"A coworker mentions a new shop near the station."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼女は女性らしい。 → To mean “she is feminine,” add a following noun: 女性らしい女性／女性らしい服装. At sentence-end, らしい means inference.","❌ Seeing snow fall directly: 雪が降っているらしい。 → ✅ 雪が降っているようだ／みたいだ. Use らしい only when you are NOT directly witnessing it.","❌ 元気だらしい → ✅ 元気らしい. らしい follows a noun/な-adjective stem DIRECTLY, unlike ようだ, which needs な: 元気なようだ."],"compare":null}},"n3-044||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory trick: “Radio” + position","sub":"〜らしい（推量・根拠） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしい（推量・根拠）","patternReading":"らしい","coreMeaning":"Seems / apparently (indirect inference) + typical of, true to","slug":"memory","definition":"Use an image and a short mnemonic. Imagine a **radio** in your head: every signal you hear—rumors, news, or signs—is a broadcast; らしい means “based on the signal received, it seems...” A radio does NOT let you see something directly, matching the indirect nature of meaning ①.","nuance":"For meaning ②, “typical of,” remember the position rule: **before N = typical; sentence-end = seems**. Before a noun, らしい modifies it; at the end, it expresses an inference.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-044||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜らしい（推量・根拠） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしい（推量・根拠）","patternReading":"らしい","coreMeaning":"Seems / apparently (indirect inference) + typical of, true to","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common らしい phrases in exams and daily life include **男らしい / 女らしい** (manly/feminine), **子供らしい** (childlike), **プロらしい** (professional), and **日本人らしい** (typically Japanese). In speech, **〜らしいですよ** often shares news with the speaker’s judgment.","nuance":"“Typical of” phrases are tied to **social roles**, reflecting the Japanese idea of 役割. Saying someone is acting “true to their role” is natural and often complimentary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本当に","r":"ほんとうに","m":"truly"},{"t":"プロらしい","m":"professional"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is truly professional work.","scene":"Praising a coworker’s presentation."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-045||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜そうだ（外観） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（外観）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"Look ~ / Be about to ~ (direct observation)","slug":"core","definition":"〜そうだ（外観）expresses an **immediate judgment** based on direct visual observation. The speaker sees a person, thing, or event before them and predicts that it “looks ~” or is “about to ~.” This describes an **outward impression** that has not been verified internally.","nuance":"The nuance is almost a natural reflex when a specific sign is visible. It works in both speech and writing, in casual and formal contexts. Do not use it for verified facts or deep reasoning. Compared with ようだ / らしい, そうだ（外観）is **visual and intuitive**, without requiring logical evidence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいし","m":"delicious"},{"t":"そう","m":"looks"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake looks delicious, doesn’t it?","scene":"Seeing the cake outside a bakery"},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り","r":"ふり","m":"fall"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looks like it’s about to rain.","scene":"Watching dark clouds gather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-045||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜そうだ（外観） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（外観）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"Look ~ / Be about to ~ (direct observation)","slug":"forms","definition":"This is the **formation rule** for そうだ（外観）. It differs completely from そうだ（伝聞）, which follows the plain form. For appearance: **use the Vます-stem, drop い from Aい, drop だ from Aな**, then add そう.","nuance":"All three word types connect to そう after their ending is removed. If you see **plain form + そうだ**, it is hearsay, not appearance. いい and ない have special forms that must be memorized.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"そうだ","note":"An action looks about to happen: 降りそうだ"},{"lhs":"Aい (drop い)","rhs":"そうだ","note":"おいしい → おいしそうだ"},{"lhs":"Aな (drop だ)","rhs":"そうだ","note":"元気だ → 元気そうだ"},{"lhs":"いい / ない","rhs":"よさそう / なさそう","note":"Irregular; not いそう / ないそう"},{"lhs":"〜そうな","rhs":"+ N","note":"おいしそうなケーキ"},{"lhs":"〜そうに","rhs":"+ V","note":"楽しそうに踊る"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難し","r":"むずかし","m":"difficult"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"looks"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This problem looks difficult.","scene":"Glancing at a practice test"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"healthy"},{"t":"そう"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"見える","r":"みえる","m":"look"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He looks healthy.","scene":"Seeing a friend after an illness"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"よさ","m":"good"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"looks"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book looks good.","scene":"Looking at the cover in a bookstore"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-045||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜そうだ（外観） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（外観）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"Look ~ / Be about to ~ (direct observation)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜そうだ（外観）has two main uses: (1) commenting on the **appearance** of a person or thing, including qualities and states; (2) predicting an **imminent action or event** with Vます-stem + そう. Both are based on direct observation.","nuance":"In Japanese restaurants, 「おいしそうですね！」 is a common compliment when seeing someone else’s food—**only before eating**. After eating, say 「おいしかった！」. In self-talk, な is often added: 「降りそうだな」.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おいし","m":"delicious"},{"t":"そう","m":"looks"},{"t":"！"},{"t":"食べてみたい","r":"たべてみたい","m":"want to try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake looks so good! I want to try it.","scene":"Seeing a cake in a bakery display"},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り","r":"ふり","m":"fall"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"about to"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持っていこう","r":"もっていこう","m":"let’s take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looks like it’s about to rain, so let’s take an umbrella.","scene":"Seeing gloomy skies before leaving"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-045||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with similar patterns","sub":"〜そうだ（外観） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（外観）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"Look ~ / Be about to ~ (direct observation)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare そうだ（外観）with other “seems” patterns to avoid misuse. **The key point**: そうだ（外観）is based only on **direct observation at the moment of speaking**, without requiring reasoning or a source of information.","nuance":"ようだ and みたい can also describe observations, but they carry a more subjective, inferential nuance. らしい is based on heard information or indirect evidence. そうだ（伝聞）is entirely different because it follows the plain form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"healthy"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"looks"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person looks healthy.","scene":"Seeing someone jogging in a park"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ようだ / 〜みたいだ","point":"ようだ / みたい is used when there is **a basis for inference** (logic or indirect signs); そうだ（外観）is an **immediate visible impression**."},{"vs":"〜らしい","point":"らしい is based on **heard information / outside evidence**; そうだ（外観）uses what the speaker **sees directly**, without a source."},{"vs":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","point":"Look at the **form immediately before そう**: plain form (おいしいそう) means “I hear”; a dropped ending (おいしそう) means “looks delicious.”"}]}},"n3-045||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜そうだ（外観） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（外観）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"Look ~ / Be about to ~ (direct observation)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistake is **confusing the two types of そうだ**. If **い remains before そう** with an い-adjective, it expresses hearsay, not appearance. Also remember that いい and ない are irregular.","nuance":"If you say 「おいしいそう」 when you mean “looks delicious,” it means “I hear it is delicious,” which can cause confusion. Build the reflex: see it → drop い/だ, add そう.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいし","m":"delicious"},{"t":"そう","m":"looks"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake looks delicious, doesn’t it?","scene":"Looking at the cake in front of a friend"}],"pitfalls":["❌ このケーキはおいしいそうですね。 → ✅ このケーキはおいしそうですね。 (keeping い changes it to hearsay)","❌ この本はいいそうだ。 → ✅ この本はよさそうだ。 (いい → よさ)","❌ 彼女は元気だそうだ。(to mean 'looks healthy') → ✅ 彼女は元気そうだ。","❌ 宿題がなそうだ。 → ✅ 宿題がなさそうだ。"],"compare":null}},"n3-045||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜そうだ（外観） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（外観）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"Look ~ / Be about to ~ (direct observation)","slug":"memory","definition":"The memory tips focus on **form**: the appearance pattern is “cut the ending + add そう.” To distinguish it from hearsay, check whether **い appears immediately before そう**.","nuance":"Imagine looking at something and blurting out, “It looks so …!”—that is そうだ（外観）. When repeating what you heard, use the plain form + そうだ.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-046||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜ほど（程度） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ほど（程度）","patternReading":"ほど","coreMeaning":"To the extent ~; the more ~, the more ~ (degree)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ほど expresses **degree** by using event A as a **measure** for statement B. When A is an extreme action or emotion, it means “to the extent ~.” With Vば + Vる, it means “the more ~, the more ~,” showing a proportional increase. ほど can also mean “about” and “not as ... as N.”","nuance":"ほど is more formal and emphatic than くらい. くらい is common in speech, but ほど is more natural when stressing a high degree or in writing. It normally needs a B clause to anchor the result measured by A.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"anything"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"いない","m":"not"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"to the extent"},{"t":"忙しかった","r":"いそがしかった","m":"was busy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Today I was so busy that I had not eaten anything since morning.","scene":"Telling a friend about an extremely hectic workday."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"泣きたい","r":"なきたい","m":"want to cry"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"to the extent"},{"t":"嬉しかった","r":"うれしかった","m":"was happy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She was so happy that she wanted to cry.","scene":"She learns she got into her dream company."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-046||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜ほど（程度） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ほど（程度）","patternReading":"ほど","coreMeaning":"To the extent ~; the more ~, the more ~ (degree)","slug":"forms","definition":"How ほど connects: for “about,” it follows a **quantity noun**. For “to the extent,” it follows the **dictionary form of a verb or adjective**. For “the more... the more,” use **Vば + Vる + ほど** or **Aければ + Aい + ほど**.","nuance":"Note: In “the more... the more,” the A clause repeats the same verb or adjective; the clause after ほど gives the result. The B clause often uses なる to express change.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (quantity) + ほど","rhs":"about / approximate meaning","note":"e.g. 1時間ほど"},{"lhs":"V辞書形 / Aい + ほど","rhs":"B","note":"to the extent that A, B"},{"lhs":"Aな + な + ほど","rhs":"B","note":"to the extent that A, B"},{"lhs":"Vば + V辞書形 + ほど","rhs":"B","note":"the more V, the more B"},{"lhs":"Aければ + Aい + ほど","rhs":"B","note":"the more A, the more B"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1時間","r":"いちじかん","m":"1 hour"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"about"},{"t":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It takes about one hour.","scene":"Answering a customer asking about delivery time."},{"tokens":[{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"すれば","m":"if do"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"the more"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The more you practice, the better you get.","scene":"Encouraging someone studying Japanese."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-046||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜ほど（程度） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ほど（程度）","patternReading":"ほど","coreMeaning":"To the extent ~; the more ~, the more ~ (degree)","slug":"usage","definition":"Four common uses of ほど: (1) **About**—after a quantity to estimate; (2) **To the extent**—using an extreme image to stress degree; (3) **The more... the more**—showing proportional increase; (4) **Negative superlative**—Nほど～ない = nothing/no one compares with N.","nuance":"Read the context: number + ほど → about; extreme adjective/action → to the extent; Vば + Vる → the more; N + ない → negative comparison. ほど is formal and slightly literary, and is common in announcements, essays, and news.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"10人","r":"じゅうにん","m":"10 people"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"about"},{"t":"来","r":"き","m":"come"},{"t":"ました","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"About ten people came yesterday.","scene":"Reporting meeting attendance."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as ... as"},{"t":"安全","r":"あんぜん","m":"safe"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"国","r":"くに","m":"country"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"there is no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No country is as safe as Japan.","scene":"Comparing safety across countries."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-046||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing くらい・まで・だけ","sub":"〜ほど（程度） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ほど（程度）","patternReading":"ほど","coreMeaning":"To the extent ~; the more ~, the more ~ (degree)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ほど from commonly confused patterns: **くらい/ぐらい** also expresses degree but is milder and more colloquial; **まで** indicates a specific endpoint or limit; **だけ** limits quantity and does not evaluate degree.","nuance":"ほど is more emphatic and formal than くらい. Use ほど for strong emotions meaning “to such an extent.” For a simple approximation, くらい sounds more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as ... as"},{"t":"感動","r":"かんどう","m":"moved"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"もの"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No film has moved me as much as that one.","scene":"Expressing an impression of a film."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜くらい/ぐらい","point":"くらい: milder, colloquial, conversational degree; ほど: stronger, formal, written degree. E.g. 10分くらい vs 10分ほど."},{"vs":"〜まで","point":"まで: marks a specific endpoint “as far as”; ほど: marks an open-ended degree “to the extent,” with no endpoint. E.g. 泣くまで vs 泣きたいほど."},{"vs":"〜だけ","point":"だけ: limits quantity without evaluation; ほど: indicates approximate amount or comparative degree. E.g. 3つだけ vs 3つほど."}]}},"n3-046||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜ほど（程度） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ほど（程度）","patternReading":"ほど","coreMeaning":"To the extent ~; the more ~, the more ~ (degree)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with ほど: (1) using the wrong ば-form in the “the more... the more” pattern; (2) using ほど for a mild degree, which sounds unnatural; (3) omitting the negative clause in “nothing compares with N.”","nuance":"In JLPT questions, traps often test the correct ば-form, so the verb in the first clause must be conjugated correctly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"考","r":"かんが","m":"think"},{"t":"えれば","m":"if think"},{"t":"考","r":"かんが","m":"think"},{"t":"える","m":"think"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"the more"},{"t":"分からなくなる","r":"わからなくなる","m":"become unclear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The more I think about it, the less I understand.","scene":"Explaining a complicated problem."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べる食べるほど → ✅ 食べれば食べるほど（the first verb must use the ば-form）","❌ ちょっと嬉しいほど → ✅ 泣きたいほど嬉しい（an extreme degree is needed）","❌ 彼ほど親切な人はいる（missing the negative）→ ✅ 彼ほど親切な人はいない（Nほど〜ない requires negation）"],"compare":null}},"n3-046||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜ほど（程度） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ほど（程度）","patternReading":"ほど","coreMeaning":"To the extent ~; the more ~, the more ~ (degree)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **程（ほど）** originally means “extent” or “measure.” Picture a **measuring scale**: A is the mark, and B is the result you read. If A is a number → “about”; if A is an extreme action → “to the extent”; if A repeats with ば → “the more”; if A is noun + ない → “nothing compares with N.”","nuance":"Remember this chant: “Number → about; extreme → to the extent; ば+る → the more; N+ない → above all.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-046||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases and patterns","sub":"〜ほど（程度） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ほど（程度）","patternReading":"ほど","coreMeaning":"To the extent ~; the more ~, the more ~ (degree)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases with ほど in exams and daily life: **〜ば〜ほど** (the more... the more), **〜ほど〜ない** (nothing compares with), **〜ほどではない** (not to the extent), **それほど〜ない** (not that much), and **死ぬほど〜** (extremely, to death).","nuance":"These phrases appear often in both speech and writing. Learning them helps you understand and use ほど naturally.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"死","r":"し","m":"die"},{"t":"ぬ"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"to the extent"},{"t":"疲","r":"つか","m":"tired"},{"t":"れた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am dead tired.","scene":"After a hard day at work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-047||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜ように（様態） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように（様態）","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"So that ~, like ~, or tell/ask someone to ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜ように has multiple meanings, but they all center on “in the form/state of ~.” It has three uses: (1) GOAL — do X to achieve state/ability Y; (2) SIMILE — like Y; (3) INDIRECT INSTRUCTION — tell/ask/convey that someone should do Y. To identify the meaning, look at the V BEFORE and AFTER ように.","nuance":"ように is used in both speech and writing. The goal meaning often takes a potential verb or Vない, focusing on the RESULT/STATE rather than direct intention. The instruction meaning with 言う/頼む/伝える is an INDIRECT, polite style—very common in Japanese, especially at work.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am studying so that I can speak Japanese.","scene":"Explaining to friends why you spend so much time studying."},{"tokens":[{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ように","m":"like"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happy"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am as happy as if it were a dream.","scene":"Exclaiming after a wonderful wedding day."},{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"ように","m":"to come early"},{"t":"伝えて","r":"つたえて","m":"tell"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please tell them to come early.","scene":"Asking a colleague to pass a message to a newcomer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-047||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ように（様態） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように（様態）","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"So that ~, like ~, or tell/ask someone to ~","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure of ように changes by meaning. For goals, use V plain/Vない + ように + an intentional V. For similes, use N + の + ように or まるで + ように. For instructions, use V plain/Vない + ように + 言う/頼む/伝える. For prayers, use Vます + ように at the end of a sentence.","nuance":"There is no special polite conjugation; simply make the verb in the following clause polite with ます/です. 〜ようにしている is a fixed phrase meaning “set a rule for yourself and maintain the habit.”","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形 / Vない","rhs":"ように + V","note":"Goal: so you can do something / so you do not do something"},{"lhs":"Nの / まるで","rhs":"ように","note":"Simile: like ~, just as if ~"},{"lhs":"V辞書形 / Vない","rhs":"ように + 言う / 頼む / 伝える","note":"Indirect instruction: tell/ask/convey that ~"},{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"ように","note":"Prayer: hope that ~ (at sentence end)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"字","r":"じ","m":"characters"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"読める","r":"よめる","m":"can read"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"眼鏡","r":"めがね","m":"glasses"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"かけた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I put on glasses so that I could read the characters.","scene":"Explaining why you wear glasses while working."},{"tokens":[{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ","m":"a cold"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ひかない"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"手洗い","r":"てあらい","m":"handwashing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wash my hands so that I do not catch a cold.","scene":"Talking to a child about staying clean."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-047||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜ように（様態） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように（様態）","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"So that ~, like ~, or tell/ask someone to ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Use ①: GOAL — the first clause describes a STATE/ABILITY (a potential verb, an intransitive verb, or Vない), and the next clause is an intentional ACTION. Use ②: SIMILE — combine it with まるで or Nの for comparison. Use ③: INDIRECT INSTRUCTION — the following verb conveys the message; at the end of a sentence with ます, it becomes a PRAYER/WISH.","nuance":"For the GOAL meaning, ように can replace ために when the first clause is not an intentional action. For the INSTRUCTION meaning, this is much more POLITE than a direct imperative—Japanese speakers often use it at work and in messages. Prayers are common for exams and health.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"日記","r":"にっき","m":"diary"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書く","r":"かく","m":"write"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"して","m":"try to"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying to write in my diary every day.","scene":"Telling a friend about a new habit."},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"students"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"言った","r":"いった","m":"told"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher told the students to be quiet.","scene":"Telling a parent what happened in class."},{"tokens":[{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"します","m":"will"},{"t":"ように","m":"I hope"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hope I pass!","scene":"Praying before an exam."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-047||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from ために / みたい / ようだ","sub":"〜ように（様態） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように（様態）","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"So that ~, like ~, or tell/ask someone to ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Patterns easily confused with ように include ために (intentional purpose), みたい (casual simile), ようになる (change of state), and ようにする (forming a habit). Distinguish them by their STRUCTURE and NUANCE.","nuance":"ために is more formal than ように and emphasizes an intentional PURPOSE; ように emphasizes the RESULT. みたい is casual and conversational; ように is neutral and polite. ようになる describes CHANGE over time, while goal ように does not emphasize change.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Study so that you can speak Japanese.","scene":"Talking about a study goal."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"speak"},{"t":"ために"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Study for the purpose of speaking Japanese.","scene":"Talking about the purpose of studying Japanese."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ために（目的）","point":"ために requires an INTENTIONAL V (日本へ行くために); ように is used with an ABILITY/STATE V (日本語が話せるように)."},{"vs":"みたいだ（N4）","point":"みたい is CASUAL SPOKEN language; ように is more formal and also works in writing."},{"vs":"〜ようになる","point":"ようになる = a CHANGE that results after a process; ように = a GOAL you are aiming for, which may not be achieved."},{"vs":"〜ようにする","point":"ようにする = set a personal rule and maintain it; with 言う/頼む, convey a request to SOMEONE ELSE."}]}},"n3-047||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ように（様態） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように（様態）","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"So that ~, like ~, or tell/ask someone to ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse ように with ために, use the wrong verb form before ように, or use ように as a direct imperative. Remember: in the goal meaning, the first clause expresses a STATE/ABILITY, and the next clause expresses an ACTION.","nuance":"The most common error is using ために with a potential verb: 話せるために → incorrect. Conversely, ように with an intentional V (e.g. 買うように) can sometimes be correct when it means “so as not to forget to buy it,” but it may be unclear—use it carefully.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる"},{"t":"ために"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"して","m":"be studying"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am studying to become a doctor.","scene":"Answering an interview question about your career goal."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本語が話せるために勉強する。 → ✅ 日本語が話せるように勉強する。","❌ 医者になるように勉強する。 (if you mean the purpose “to become a doctor”) → ✅ 医者になるために勉強する。","❌ 早く来るように！ (direct command to a close friend) → ✅ 早く来るように言った/伝えた。 (if conveying it) or 早く来て！ (if giving a direct command)","❌ まるで子供ように泣いた。 → ✅ まるで子供のように泣いた。"],"compare":null}},"n3-047||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"〜ように（様態） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように（様態）","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"So that ~, like ~, or tell/ask someone to ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ように as an arrow: the first clause describes the target STATE (Y), and the next clause is an action or statement directed toward that state. When you see ように, ask: “What is it pointing toward?” That is Y.","nuance":"Picture an arrow from a present action toward an ideal “form Y.” In the instruction meaning, that arrow is conveyed through someone’s words.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-047||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ように（様態） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように（様態）","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"So that ~, like ~, or tell/ask someone to ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases with ように appear often on tests and in daily life: 〜ようにする (set a rule and try), 〜ないように (negative goal), 〜ますように (prayer), 〜のように (simile), and 〜ように言う/頼む (indirectly convey a request).","nuance":"〜ようにしている means “I am trying to maintain” something—Japanese speakers use it often when discussing habits and goals. 〜ますように commonly accompanies prayers and sounds formal and reverent.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"して","m":"try to"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying to eat vegetables every day.","scene":"Talking with a friend about healthy eating."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-048||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ","patternReading":"まるでようだ／みたいだ","coreMeaning":"Just like ~; exactly like ~ (exaggerated comparison)","slug":"core","definition":"**まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ** is a very strong comparison pattern: A resembles B so closely that it feels “just like,” “exactly like,” or “no different from” B. The speaker **knows it is not literally true** and uses the comparison to exaggerate emotion and create an impression. ようだ is used in writing; みたいだ is used in conversation.","nuance":"The similarity feels nearly 100%. The listener understands it figuratively. It expresses surprise, admiration, or disbelief. Unlike an ordinary comparison, it is not used for something that is literally true.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ようだ","m":"like"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It feels just like a dream.","scene":"After an incredible trip"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"声","r":"こえ","m":"voice"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"天使","r":"てんし","m":"angel"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ようだ","m":"like"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Her voice is just like an angel's.","scene":"Praising a friend's singing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-048||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ","patternReading":"まるでようだ／みたいだ","coreMeaning":"Just like ~; exactly like ~ (exaggerated comparison)","slug":"forms","definition":"A summary of the pattern's **connection formulas**. Depending on the word type and level of formality, use **ようだ** (written/formal) or **みたいだ** (casual/spoken). まるで always comes before the comparison.","nuance":"Forms: まるで + N + の + ようだ; まるで + N + みたいだ; まるで + clause + ようだ/みたいだ. Before a noun, ようだ becomes のような and みたいだ becomes みたいな. Before a verb or adjective, ようだ becomes ように and みたいだ becomes みたいに. かのようだ is very formal and stresses that the situation is “not actually so.”","structure":[{"lhs":"まるで + N","rhs":"の + ようだ","note":"Formal, written style."},{"lhs":"まるで + N","rhs":"みたいだ","note":"Spoken, casual style."},{"lhs":"まるで + 普通形","rhs":"ようだ/みたいだ","note":"Clause comparison; Aな keeps な before ようだ (nouns still use の as above)."},{"lhs":"まるで + 普通形","rhs":"かのようだ","note":"Very formal; stresses that it is unreal."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"大人","r":"おとな","m":"adult"},{"t":"みたいな","m":"like"},{"t":"話し方","r":"はなしかた","m":"way of speaking"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The child talks just like an adult.","scene":"Surprised by a nephew's mature speech"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"not know"},{"t":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"振る舞った","r":"ふるまった","m":"acted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He acted as if he knew nothing.","scene":"Telling how a friend pretended not to know"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-048||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ","patternReading":"まるでようだ／みたいだ","coreMeaning":"Just like ~; exactly like ~ (exaggerated comparison)","slug":"usage","definition":"**まるで〜ようだ** makes a strongly exaggerated comparison (①), or compares an entire clause or situation (②). It also **adds expressive force** in descriptions and in everyday speech with みたいだ (③④).","nuance":"The listener understands that **reality is not actually so**; it is only the speaker's subjective impression. It works well for praise, complaints, and vivid storytelling. In formal writing, 〜かのようだ emphasizes being “contrary to reality” even more.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ような","m":"like"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The story is just like something from a movie.","scene":"Telling an unbelievable story"},{"tokens":[{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"雪","r":"ゆき","m":"snow"},{"t":"のように","m":"like"},{"t":"白い","r":"しろい","m":"white"},{"t":"肌","r":"はだ","m":"skin"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Her skin is white as snow.","scene":"Describing a model's beauty"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-048||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ","patternReading":"まるでようだ／みたいだ","coreMeaning":"Just like ~; exactly like ~ (exaggerated comparison)","slug":"compare","definition":"These are the main points learners confuse. The patterns are similar in meaning but differ in nuance and formality. The key contrasts are **ordinary vs. exaggerated**, **written vs. spoken**, and **purpose vs. comparison**.","nuance":"Choose **ようだ** for polite, written language and **みたいだ** for casual conversation. **かのようだ** is the most formal and stresses “not actually so.” **のような** is not inherently exaggerated. **ように** can express purpose (“so that”)—do not confuse it with comparison.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ような","m":"like"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is no different from my father.","scene":"Describing a strict teacher like a father"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜のような","point":"のような: ordinary comparison; まるで〜ようだ: exaggerated, “just like.” Example: 父のような人 (a person like my father) vs まるで父のような人 (exactly like my father)."},{"vs":"〜みたいだ","point":"みたいだ is casual and spoken; ようだ is formal and written."},{"vs":"〜かのようだ","point":"Very formal and stresses “not actually so”; まるで〜ようだ is milder."},{"vs":"〜ように (mục đích)","point":"ように (purpose): “in order to”; まるで〜ように: “just like.”"}]}},"n3-048||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ","patternReading":"まるでようだ／みたいだ","coreMeaning":"Just like ~; exactly like ~ (exaggerated comparison)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include **forgetting まるで**, **confusing まるで with ちょうど**, **using it for something literally true**, and **confusing it with ように for purpose**.","nuance":"Remember: **まるで** must combine with ようだ/みたいだ/かのようだ for an exaggerated comparison. **ちょうど** means “exactly” or “right on time,” not “exactly like.” If something is literally true, do not use まるで.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"ロボット","m":"robot"},{"t":"みたいに","m":"like"},{"t":"働く","r":"はたらく","m":"work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He works like a robot.","scene":"Complaining about a tireless coworker"}],"pitfalls":["❌ Forgetting まるで: 夢のようだ。 → ✅ まるで夢のようだ。 (just like a dream)","❌ Confusing it with ちょうど: ちょうど夢のようだ。 → ✅ まるで夢のようだ。","❌ Using it for something true: 彼は医者だ。→ 彼はまるで医者のようだ。 (wrong if he is really a doctor)","❌ Confusing it with ように for purpose: まるで覚えられるように、ノートに書く。 → ✅ 覚えられるように、ノートに書く。 (so that you remember)"],"compare":null}},"n3-048||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ","patternReading":"まるでようだ／みたいだ","coreMeaning":"Just like ~; exactly like ~ (exaggerated comparison)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory aid: **まるで** is like an ultra-sharp photocopier—it copies the original so exactly that the two are almost indistinguishable. The pattern therefore expresses similarity that feels **nearly absolute**.","nuance":"Also remember: ようだ is used for polite, written language; みたい is used in casual speech. Both imply that the speaker **knows it is not literally true** and is using a vivid comparison.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-048||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"まるで〜ようだ／みたいだ","patternReading":"まるでようだ／みたいだ","coreMeaning":"Just like ~; exactly like ~ (exaggerated comparison)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are **classic** まるで comparison phrases commonly found in conversation, literature, and tests. Memorizing them helps you learn the pattern faster.","nuance":"These phrases emphasize something unbelievable, breathtakingly beautiful, or unreal. Use ようだ in formal contexts and みたい in casual ones.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"魔法","r":"まほう","m":"magic"},{"t":"みたいだ","m":"like"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It feels just like magic.","scene":"Seeing an unbelievably beautiful sunset"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-049||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"かのように／かのような · N3","v2":{"pattern":"かのように／かのような","patternReading":"かのように","coreMeaning":"As if ~ (but in fact not)","slug":"core","definition":"〜かのように／かのような describes an action, appearance, or state that looks like X, but is actually not X. It highlights the contrast between appearance and reality, often suggesting pretense, acting, or an exaggerated comparison. This formal expression is similar to ようだ, but adds the implication that it is not really so.","nuance":"Formal and literary; rarely used in everyday conversation. It creates a dramatic or exaggerated effect, or suggests that someone is acting. The sense of pretense is especially clear when describing human behavior.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"know nothing"},{"t":"かのように","r":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"振る舞って","r":"ふるまって","m":"behaving"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He behaved as if he knew nothing.","scene":"Hiding the truth and pretending to be innocent."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"すべて","r":"すべて","m":"everything"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"順調","r":"じゅんちょう","m":"smooth"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"かのように","r":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"笑って","r":"わらって","m":"smiling"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She smiled as if everything were fine.","scene":"Hiding her troubles behind a bright smile."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-049||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"かのように／かのような · N3","v2":{"pattern":"かのように／かのような","patternReading":"かのように","coreMeaning":"As if ~ (but in fact not)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is a plain-form clause + かのように + a verb or adjective, or + かのような + a noun. With nouns and な-adjectives, である is required before かのように. まるで may be added to strengthen the comparison.","nuance":"A formal, literary form. The variant かのごとく is often used in classical-style writing and journalism.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"かのように + V","note":"as if ~"},{"lhs":"Aい（普通形）","rhs":"かのように + V"},{"lhs":"N + である","rhs":"かのように + V"},{"lhs":"Aな + である","rhs":"かのように + V"},{"lhs":"V（普通形）/ N + である","rhs":"かのような + N","note":"modifies a noun"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"かのように","r":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"苦しんだ","r":"くるしんだ","m":"suffered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He suffered as if he were sick.","scene":"He was not ill but acted as if he were in pain."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"なかった","r":"なかった","m":"did not exist"},{"t":"かのような","r":"かのような","m":"as if"},{"t":"顔","r":"かお","m":"expression"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"している"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She wears an expression as if nothing had happened.","scene":"Pretending to be normal after a scandal."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-049||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"かのように／かのような · N3","v2":{"pattern":"かのように／かのような","patternReading":"かのように","coreMeaning":"As if ~ (but in fact not)","slug":"usage","definition":"Three main uses: (1) describing pretended behavior—someone acts as if X, though reality differs; (2) modifying a noun to describe something with qualities like X; and (3) creating an exaggerated comparison in literature and journalism.","nuance":"In speech, Japanese speakers usually use みたいに or まるで〜ようだ for a similar meaning, avoiding this literary expression.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"初めて","r":"はじめて","m":"for the first time"},{"t":"会った","r":"あった","m":"met"},{"t":"かのように","r":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"話しかけた","r":"はなしかけた","m":"started talking"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He spoke to me as if we were meeting for the first time.","scene":"They knew each other, but he pretended they were strangers."},{"tokens":[{"t":"まるで","r":"まるで","m":"just like"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"かのような","r":"かのような","m":"as if"},{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"だった"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was an experience just like a dream.","scene":"Describing a wonderful trip."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-049||compare":{"title":"⚖️ かのように vs ようだ・みたい","sub":"かのように／かのような · N3","v2":{"pattern":"かのように／かのような","patternReading":"かのように","coreMeaning":"As if ~ (but in fact not)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare かのように with similar comparison patterns. The key point is that かのように describes a false appearance: it looks that way, but is not really so. Other patterns may be neutral and need not imply pretense.","nuance":"In speech, use まるで〜みたい when you mean “it looks as if” without suggesting pretense. Use かのように in writing when describing someone who is acting or putting on an appearance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"know nothing"},{"t":"かのように","r":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"振る舞った","r":"ふるまった","m":"acted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He acted as if he knew nothing.","scene":"He knew the facts but still pretended otherwise."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ようだ","point":"ようだ is neutral or inferential; かのように implies pretense and contrast. Example: 彼は怒っているようだ (he seems angry) vs 怒っていないかのように振る舞った (he acted as if he were not angry—though he was)."},{"vs":"まるで〜ようだ","point":"まるで〜ようだ makes a vivid comparison without stressing pretense; かのように is more literary and often describes deliberate behavior."},{"vs":"〜みたいだ","point":"みたい is casual and natural in speech; かのように is formal and literary, and is rarely used in conversation."}]}},"n3-049||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"かのように／かのような · N3","v2":{"pattern":"かのように／かのような","patternReading":"かのように","coreMeaning":"As if ~ (but in fact not)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with かのように: (1) using it in everyday conversation, where it sounds overly literary; (2) forgetting である after a noun or な-adjective; and (3) confusing it with ようだ, which expresses neutral inference or comparison without the implication of pretense.","nuance":"The most common mistake is using かのように with friends, where Japanese speakers would normally use みたいに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"know nothing"},{"t":"かのように","r":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"振る舞った","r":"ふるまった","m":"acted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He acted as if he knew nothing.","scene":"He knew the facts but still pretended otherwise."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は知らないかのように話した。(casual speech) → ✅ 彼は知らないみたいに話した。","❌ 先生かのように話す。 → ✅ 先生であるかのように話す。","❌ 彼は怒っているようだ (mistaken as “pretending”) → ✅ 怒っていないかのように振る舞う (this expresses pretense)"],"compare":null}},"n3-049||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"かのように／かのような · N3","v2":{"pattern":"かのように／かのような","patternReading":"かのように","coreMeaning":"As if ~ (but in fact not)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: かのように = “acting.” An actor behaves like a character but is not actually that character. Word breakdown: か (questioning “could it be?”) + のように (like) → “as if ~.”","nuance":"Picture someone playing another person: the outside looks identical, but everyone knows it is fake.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-049||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common literary phrases","sub":"かのように／かのような · N3","v2":{"pattern":"かのように／かのような","patternReading":"かのように","coreMeaning":"As if ~ (but in fact not)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in literature and journalism include 知らないかのように (as if unaware), 時が止まったかのように (as if time had stopped), 夢であるかのような (dreamlike), and 〜かのごとく (a classical variant with a similar meaning).","nuance":"These phrases commonly appear in descriptive writing, novels, and formal speeches.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まった","r":"とまった","m":"stopped"},{"t":"かのように","r":"かのように","m":"as if"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"だった"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was quiet as if time had stopped.","scene":"Describing an extremely still scene."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-050||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ていく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away, toward the future","slug":"core","definition":"〜ていく describes movement or change **away** from the speaker's viewpoint. In time, it points toward the **future**; in space, it means **moving farther away**. It expresses an ongoing progression and is often used for trends, development, or commitments.","nuance":"It presents events objectively as moving forward from the present. It works in both speech and writing. The speaker is confident about the present direction, but the result has not happened yet. Unlike 〜てくる (coming closer / reaching the present), 〜ていく feels **not yet happened and still to come**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"鳥","r":"とり","m":"bird"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遠く","r":"とおく","m":"far away"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"飛んで","r":"とんで","m":"fly"},{"t":"いった","r":"いった","m":"went away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The bird flew far away.","scene":"Watching a bird fly out of sight in a park"},{"tokens":[{"t":"気温","r":"きおん","m":"temperature"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"だんだん","r":"だんだん","m":"gradually"},{"t":"下がって","r":"さがって","m":"decrease"},{"t":"いく","r":"いく","m":"will continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The temperature will keep dropping.","scene":"Commenting on the coming change in weather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-050||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜ていく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away, toward the future","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **verb te-form + いく**. いく changes according to tense and politeness. Examples: 食べていく, 帰っていく, なっていく. Note the te-form sound changes (て/で): 泳ぐ → 泳いでいく, 読む → 読んでいく.","nuance":"In casual speech, **いく → く** is often shortened (食べてく). The negative **〜ていかない** is uncommon; use 〜ないでいく (leave without doing something) or 〜ないことにする instead. For the past, use **〜ていった** to describe movement or change that progressed away.","structure":[{"lhs":"動詞て形","rhs":"いく","note":"Present / future"},{"lhs":"動詞て形","rhs":"いった","note":"Past"},{"lhs":"動詞て形","rhs":"いきます","note":"Polite"},{"lhs":"動詞て形","rhs":"てく","note":"Colloquial"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","r":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"走って","r":"はしって","m":"run"},{"t":"いった","r":"いった","m":"went away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The bus drove away.","scene":"Seeing a bus pull away from the stop"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","m":"from now on"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"頑張って","r":"がんばって","m":"do one's best"},{"t":"く","r":"く","m":"continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will keep doing my best from now on.","scene":"Replying to encouragement from a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-050||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜ていく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away, toward the future","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main uses: **(1)** A change that gradually moves from the present into the future (気温が下がっていく); **(2)** movement away from the speaker's position (彼は走っていった); **(3)** the intention to keep doing something in the future (これからも勉強していく). In speeches, **〜ていきます** is also a common commitment formula.","nuance":"For change over time, it often appears with **だんだん, どんどん, このまま**. For commitments, use **〜ていきます** or **〜ていきたいと思います**. Do not use 〜ていく for a completed change; it points toward the future.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"気温","r":"きおん","m":"temperature"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"どんどん","r":"どんどん","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"下がって","r":"さがって","m":"decrease"},{"t":"いく","r":"いく","m":"will continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The temperature will keep falling.","scene":"A winter weather forecast"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"in front"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"走って","r":"はしって","m":"run"},{"t":"いった","r":"いった","m":"went away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A dog ran past me and away.","scene":"Seeing a dog run farther away while walking"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","m":"from now on"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"make"},{"t":"いく","r":"いく","m":"continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will continue to work hard from now on.","scene":"Giving an acceptance speech"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-050||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with other patterns","sub":"〜ていく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away, toward the future","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish the two pairs most often confused. **〜ていく** vs **〜てくる**: one moves away, the other comes closer. **〜つつある** emphasizes an ongoing process and sounds formal and written.","nuance":"〜ていく and 〜てくる cannot replace each other because their time directions are opposite. 〜つつある highlights change in progress objectively and formally, while 〜ていく has a clearer future direction and can feel more speaker-oriented.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社会","r":"しゃかい","m":"society"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"変わって","r":"かわって","m":"change"},{"t":"いく","r":"いく","m":"continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Society will continue to change.","scene":"Discussing future social trends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てくる","point":"〜てくる: past → present, moving closer to the speaker. 〜ていく: present → future, moving away. Example: 気温が下がってきた (has fallen up to now) vs 気温が下がっていく (will fall from now on)."},{"vs":"〜つつある","point":"〜つつある: a change currently in progress, formal (written). 〜ていく: future-oriented and usable in speech. Example: 世界は変わりつつある (the world is changing) vs 世界は変わっていく (the world will change)."}]}},"n3-050||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & pitfalls","sub":"〜ていく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away, toward the future","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse movement direction: things that **fall or come closer** use 〜てくる, not 〜ていく. Also, **ていく** points to the future, while an action in progress in the past requires **ていた**.","nuance":"Identify the viewpoint: if something moves away from you, use ていく; if it approaches you, use てくる. For a continuing past state, use ていた. Using ていく for every change reverses the direction.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"小鳥","r":"ことり","m":"small bird"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"空","r":"そら","m":"sky"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"飛んで","r":"とんで","m":"fly"},{"t":"いった","r":"いった","m":"went away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The small bird flew up into the sky and out of sight.","scene":"Watching a bird fly away outdoors"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降っていく → ✅ 雨が降ってくる (rain falls toward the speaker, so use てくる)","❌ 昨日、ずっと勉強していく → ✅ 昨日、ずっと勉強していた (use ていた for an ongoing past action)","❌ あの子は私の方へ走っていった → ✅ あの子は私の方へ走ってきた (running toward me = coming closer)","❌ 日本に来てから、好きになっていく → ✅ 日本に来てから、好きになってきた (from the past up to the present)"],"compare":null}},"n3-050||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"〜ていく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away, toward the future","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture someone **walking farther away**: いく means “go,” moving away from you. Since going away points toward the future, 〜ていく connects with things that have not happened yet. By contrast, くる means coming closer, like the past moving toward the present.","nuance":"Tip: if you can paraphrase the sentence as “will increasingly...,” use ていく; if it means “has gradually... up to now,” use てくる. Remember the time direction: **ていく = arrow →**, **てくる = arrow ←**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-050||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ていく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away, toward the future","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in tests and daily life: **これからも〜ていく** (continue from now on), **〜ていこう** (let's keep doing it), **だんだん〜ていく** (gradually become), and **このまま〜ていく** (continue as things are).","nuance":"〜ていこう expresses an invitation or volition and often ends a sentence. これからも〜ていく sounds committed and is common in speeches. このまま〜ていく often accompanies concern about a negative trend (このまま悪化していく).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","m":"from now on"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"頑張って","r":"がんばって","m":"do one's best"},{"t":"いく","r":"いく","m":"continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will keep doing my best.","scene":"Promising this after a study session"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-051||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ていく（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく（変化）","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away (present → future)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜ていく describes something moving **away** or gradually changing toward the **future** from the speaker's current viewpoint. It combines the て-form of a verb with いく (to go), creating the sense of \"continuing onward and away.\" It can describe space (a bird flying away, a vehicle driving off) or time (gradually, continuing).","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. It gives an objective view of an ongoing process. Compared with 〜てくる, 〜ていく emphasizes the future and distance, and may suggest progress, regret, or negativity depending on context. It is not used for fully completed actions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"気温","r":"きおん","m":"temperature"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"だんだん","m":"gradually"},{"t":"下がっていく","r":"さがっていく","m":"gradually fall"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The temperature is gradually getting lower.","scene":"Watching the temperature fall"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"言わず","r":"いわず","m":"without saying"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出ていった","r":"でていった","m":"left"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He left without saying a word.","scene":"Leaving angrily after an argument"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","m":"from now on"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強していく","r":"べんきょうしていく","m":"continue studying"},{"t":"つもり","m":"intention"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intend to continue studying Japanese.","scene":"Answering about future plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-051||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ていく（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく（変化）","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away (present → future)","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure is **Vて + いく**. いく conjugates like an ordinary verb: ていった (past), ていかない (negative), and ていきます (polite). In casual speech, い is often dropped, giving 〜てく.","nuance":"〜てく sounds casual and familiar, and is common with friends or family. 〜ていった is often used when recounting something that happened in the past.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いく","note":"Basic form; change or movement away"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いきます","note":"Polite; used with superiors or customers"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いった","note":"Past; went away / gradually changed"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いかない","note":"Present/future negative"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いこう","note":"Volitional; let's move toward the future together"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"走っていった","r":"はしっていった","m":"drove away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The bus drove away.","scene":"The bus pulls away while you wait"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","m":"from now on"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"頑張っていこう","r":"がんばっていこう","m":"let's keep trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's keep moving forward together.","scene":"Encouraging teammates before a project"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手に","r":"じょうずに","m":"well"},{"t":"なっていく","m":"gradually become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My Japanese will keep improving.","scene":"Discussing goals with a classmate"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-051||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜ていく（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく（変化）","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away (present → future)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ていく has three main meanings: **gradual change toward the future** (だんだん〜ていく), **movement away** (〜ていった), and **continuing or maintaining something** (これからも〜ていく). The phrase 〜ていきたい also often appears in speeches as a commitment.","nuance":"When discussing the future, this pattern often combines with adverbs of gradual change such as だんだん, どんどん, and 少しずつ. In the spatial sense, いく is commonly used for movement away from the speaker. 〜ていきたい sounds gentle and expresses a wish or determination directed toward the future.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"少子化","r":"しょうしか","m":"low birth rate"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"進んでいくだろう","r":"すすんでいくだろう","m":"will continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The declining birth rate will probably continue.","scene":"Discussing a social issue"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hand"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"振りながら","r":"ふりながら","m":"while waving"},{"t":"歩いていった","r":"あるいていった","m":"walked away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend walked away while waving.","scene":"Saying goodbye at a station"},{"tokens":[{"t":"環境問題","r":"かんきょうもんだい","m":"environmental issue"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"これから","m":"from now on"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"悪化していく","r":"あっかしていく","m":"continue worsening"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思う","r":"おもう","m":"think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think the environmental problem will continue to worsen.","scene":"A speech about the future"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-051||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"〜ていく（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく（変化）","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away (present → future)","slug":"compare","definition":"These are the patterns most often confused with 〜ていく: 〜てくる, 〜ていた, 〜つつある, and 〜ようになる. They differ in time direction or formality. ていく points to the future, てくる from past to present, ていた describes past continuation, つつある is formal written language, and ようになる emphasizes a change in result or state.","nuance":"When comparing them, always identify the **time direction** and the speaker's **viewpoint**. 〜ていく looks from the present toward the future, 〜てくる from the past to the present, 〜ていた describes a past state without direction, 〜つつある is formal and process-focused, and 〜ようになる describes a turning point into a new habit or ability.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増えていく","r":"ふえていく","m":"increase gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The problems will continue to increase.","scene":"Making a future prediction"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てくる","point":"Opposite direction: 〜ていく = away/future (増えていく); 〜てくる = nearer/past→present (増えてきた)."},{"vs":"〜ていた","point":"〜ていた means past continuation ('was doing'), with no future direction. E.g. 食べていた (was eating) ≠ 食べていった (ate while leaving / ate and went away)."},{"vs":"〜つつある","point":"〜つつある is formal, common in writing, and emphasizes an ongoing process; 〜ていく works in speech and writing and shows the future direction more clearly."},{"vs":"〜ようになる","point":"〜ようになる describes changing into a new state (a turning point), while 〜ていく describes a process that gradually continues."}]}},"n3-051||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ていく（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく（変化）","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away (present → future)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse the direction of movement with 〜ていく. The rule is: an action that involves going somewhere and then returning uses 〜てくる, not 〜ていく.","nuance":"〜ていく describes movement away from the viewpoint, so movement toward the speaker must use 〜てくる. Always identify the direction before choosing the pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"鳥","r":"とり","m":"bird"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遠くへ","r":"とおくへ","m":"far away"},{"t":"飛んでいった","r":"とんでいった","m":"flew away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The bird flew far away.","scene":"Watching a bird fly out of sight"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降っていく → ✅ 雨が降ってくる (rain falls toward the speaker's viewpoint, not away)","❌ 買っていく (if you mean 'buy and come back') → ✅ 買ってくる (use てくる for buying something and returning, not ていく)","❌ これから勉強していった → ✅ これから勉強していく (use future いく, not past いった)"],"compare":null}},"n3-051||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ていく（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく（変化）","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away (present → future)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember the direction of the verbs: **いく (to go)** pictures something moving away, while **くる (to come)** pictures something moving closer. From this, 〜ていく naturally means a process directed toward the future.","nuance":"This trick helps prevent confusion between ていく and てくる: ask yourself, \"Is the event moving away or coming closer?\"","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-051||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ていく（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ていく（変化）","patternReading":"ていく","coreMeaning":"Change or movement away (present → future)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases commonly appear with 〜ていく in tests and conversation: **だんだん〜ていく**, **これからも〜ていく**, **〜くなっていく**, and **〜が消えていく**.","nuance":"〜くなっていく is common with い-adjectives (暑くなっていく, 寒くなっていく). これからも〜ていく is a common commitment formula in speeches.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","m":"from now on"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いきます","m":"will continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will continue to work hard.","scene":"A commitment in a speech"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-052||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜つつある · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜つつある","patternReading":"つつある","coreMeaning":"Gradually changing / in the process of change (formal written style)","slug":"core","definition":"〜つつある describes **a gradual change that is currently underway** and not yet complete. Unlike 〜ている, which can describe a state, つつある emphasizes **the ongoing transition between two states**.","nuance":"It has a **formal** tone and is used almost exclusively in **written language**: news articles (新聞文体), academic papers, and reports. In everyday conversation, 〜ている / 〜てくる is much more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"地球","r":"ちきゅう","m":"Earth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"温暖化","r":"おんだんか","m":"global warming"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"進み","r":"すすみ","m":"progress"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Global warming is gradually progressing.","scene":"Opening of an environmental news article"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人口","r":"じんこう","m":"population"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"減り","r":"へり","m":"decrease"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Japan's population is gradually declining.","scene":"News report on population"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-052||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜つつある · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜つつある","patternReading":"つつある","coreMeaning":"Gradually changing / in the process of change (formal written style)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: take the verb's **ます form**, remove ます, and add **つつある**. Examples: 進む → 進みつつある; 変わる → 変わりつつある; 減る → 減りつつある. Use it only with **change verbs** (変化動詞), such as 進む・減る・回復する・変わる; do not use it with ordinary action verbs such as 食べる・走る or momentary verbs such as 着く・始まる.","nuance":"Never use つつある with **intentional actions**: ❌ 食べつつある. Learners often confuse this pattern because they assume つつある means “be doing” with any verb.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます stem","rhs":"つつある","note":"Remove ます: 進む → 進み + つつある"},{"lhs":"V（変化動詞）","rhs":"つつある","note":"Not for intentional actions / moments"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"状況","r":"じょうきょう","m":"situation"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"改善され","r":"かいぜんされ","m":"be improved"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The situation is gradually improving.","scene":"Company progress report"},{"tokens":[{"t":"経済","r":"けいざい","m":"economy"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"回復し","r":"かいふくし","m":"recover"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The economy is gradually recovering.","scene":"Sentence from an economic article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-052||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & contexts","sub":"〜つつある · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜つつある","patternReading":"つつある","coreMeaning":"Gradually changing / in the process of change (formal written style)","slug":"usage","definition":"In practice, つつある appears in expressions describing changes such as **environmental and social trends**（地球の温暖化・都市化・デジタル化）and **recovery or decline**（回復・悪化）. It is associated with **journalistic and academic writing**.","nuance":"Avoid つつある in everyday conversation. For example, ❌「体重が増えつつあるよ」 sounds overly academic; the natural version is ✅「体重が増えてきてるよ」.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"体力","r":"たいりょく","m":"physical strength"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"回復し","r":"かいふくし","m":"recover"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Her physical strength is gradually recovering.","scene":"Medical report on a patient"},{"tokens":[{"t":"都市化","r":"としか","m":"urbanization"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"進み","r":"すすみ","m":"progress"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"地域","r":"ちいき","m":"areas"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Many areas are undergoing urbanization.","scene":"Urban planning article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-052||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare nearby patterns","sub":"〜つつある · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜つつある","patternReading":"つつある","coreMeaning":"Gradually changing / in the process of change (formal written style)","slug":"compare","definition":"つつある・ている・ていく・てきた all describe change, but differ in **viewpoint** and **style**. つつ (without ある) is a completely different pattern.","nuance":"Use つつある in reports and academic papers; use ている in everyday speech; use ていく to emphasize a future direction.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"伝統文化","r":"でんとうぶんか","m":"traditional culture"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"消え","r":"きえ","m":"disappear"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Traditional culture is gradually disappearing.","scene":"Example in a cultural news article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ている","point":"〜ている: common in conversation; describes a present state or ongoing action. つつある: formal writing; emphasizes a gradual, unfinished transition."},{"vs":"〜ていく","point":"〜ていく: emphasizes change moving toward the future, usually neutral and familiar. つつある: describes an ongoing present process in a formal style."},{"vs":"〜てきた","point":"〜てきた: views accumulated change from the past to the present. つつある: focuses on a process that is still unfinished now."},{"vs":"〜つつ","point":"〜つつ (without ある): doing A while doing B, with two parallel actions. つつある: a gradual change in progress. Completely different."}]}},"n3-052||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜つつある · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜つつある","patternReading":"つつある","coreMeaning":"Gradually changing / in the process of change (formal written style)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Key mistakes with つつある: **do not use it for ordinary intentional actions**, **avoid it in conversation**, and **do not omit つつ**.","nuance":"Although つつある can translate as “be doing,” its meaning is “be in the middle of a change,” not “be doing an activity.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"業績","r":"ぎょうせき","m":"performance"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"悪化し","r":"あっかし","m":"worsen"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The company's performance is gradually declining.","scene":"Quarter-end financial report"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べつつある → ✅ 食べている（an intentional action, not a change）","❌ A: 最近どう？ B: 体重が増えつつあるよ → ✅ 体重が増えてきてるよ（〜てくる is usual in conversation）","❌ 増えつあある → ✅ 増えつつある（include both つつ, don't omit a sound）","❌ 歩きつつある（while walking ~?） → ✅ 歩きつつ話す（use つつ, not つつある, for parallel actions）"],"compare":null}},"n3-052||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜つつある · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜つつある","patternReading":"つつある","coreMeaning":"Gradually changing / in the process of change (formal written style)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: in classical writing, 〜つつ means “while”; adding ある (exist) gives “be in the state of ~.” Picture **a graph arrow gradually rising or falling**—it is midway, not yet at its destination.","nuance":"Remember that つつある always combines 'change' with 'written style.' For natural speech, choose 〜ている.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-052||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"〜つつある · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜つつある","patternReading":"つつある","coreMeaning":"Gradually changing / in the process of change (formal written style)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Remember these common つつある collocations in news writing: 進みつつある, 回復しつつある, 変わりつつある, 普及しつつある, and 悪化しつつある. These phrases are almost **collocations** in formal texts.","nuance":"It often combines with **intransitive change verbs** such as 進む, 回復する, 変わる, and 普及する. When you see it in reading, recognize the Vます stem + つつある form immediately.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"若者","r":"わかもの","m":"young people"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"伝統離れ","r":"でんとうばなれ","m":"turning from tradition"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"進み","r":"すすみ","m":"progress"},{"t":"つつある","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Young people's shift away from tradition is progressing.","scene":"Article about young people in Japan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-053||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜てくる（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくる（変化）","patternReading":"てくる","coreMeaning":"Change from the PAST to the PRESENT: “have gradually... / begin to...”","slug":"core","definition":"〜てくる describes a change that has developed from the past up to the present, with the speaker noticing the change at the time of speaking. It often means “have gradually...” or “begin to...,” as in the weather getting hotter, rain beginning to fall, or one’s Japanese improving. This is one of the two key meanings of てくる; the other is “go do something and come back.”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, especially when someone notices a change in everyday conversation. てきた looks back on a process from the past until now; てくる describes something currently progressing toward the present. Unlike ていく, which points toward the future, てくる points toward the present.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手に","r":"じょうずに","m":"well"},{"t":"なってきた","r":"なってきた","m":"have gradually improved"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My Japanese has gradually improved lately.","scene":"Telling a friend after studying hard"},{"tokens":[{"t":"空","r":"そら","m":"sky"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"暗く","r":"くらく","m":"dark"},{"t":"なってきた","r":"なってきた","m":"has gradually darkened"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The sky has gradually grown dark.","scene":"Looking out the window at dusk"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-053||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜てくる（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくる（変化）","patternReading":"てくる","coreMeaning":"Change from the PAST to the PRESENT: “have gradually... / begin to...”","slug":"forms","definition":"Form the pattern by putting the main verb in the te-form (Vて) and adding くる. Here, くる is an auxiliary verb: it does not mean “come” literally but shows the direction of change. Use てきた for the past, てくる for the nonpast, and てきます for polite speech.","nuance":"Choose the tense carefully: てきた views a change as having progressed up to the present, while てくる presents it as currently developing. Compared with ていく, てくる points toward the present.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くる","note":"Nonpast: “is gradually...” or “begins...”"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"きた","note":"Past: “has gradually...” (viewed from now)"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"きます","note":"Polite; used in formal conversation"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"こない","note":"Negative; less common"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ってきた","r":"ふってきた","m":"began to fall"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It has started to rain.","scene":"Noticing rain while walking outside"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","r":"これから","m":"from now"},{"t":"頑張って","r":"がんばって","m":"work hard"},{"t":"きます","r":"きます","m":"will do (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will work hard from now on.","scene":"Speaking at a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-053||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜てくる（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくる（変化）","patternReading":"てくる","coreMeaning":"Change from the PAST to the PRESENT: “have gradually... / begin to...”","slug":"usage","definition":"〜てくる has four main uses: (1) a gradual change reaching the present, (2) a phenomenon beginning, (3) looking back on a sustained effort, and (4) an emotion or psychological sensation welling up. All share the idea of moving toward the present.","nuance":"Very common in conversation. Use adverbs such as だんだん, 少しずつ, and 最近 for gradual change. In use (3), てきた often ends a sentence reviewing achievements. In use (4), common nouns include 涙, 眠気, and 怒り.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寒く","r":"さむく","m":"cold"},{"t":"なってきた","r":"なってきた","m":"has gradually become"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It has gradually gotten colder lately, hasn’t it?","scene":"Talking with a coworker as winter arrives"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"たち"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"grow"},{"t":"してきた","r":"してきた","m":"have gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The children have grown up gradually.","scene":"Looking at old photos of the children"},{"tokens":[{"t":"10年","r":"じゅうねん","m":"10 years"},{"t":"間","r":"かん","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ずっと","r":"ずっと","m":"all along"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"してきた","r":"してきた","m":"have made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have worked hard for ten straight years.","scene":"Speaking at a company anniversary"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-053||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with ていく・ようになる","sub":"〜てくる（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくる（変化）","patternReading":"てくる","coreMeaning":"Change from the PAST to the PRESENT: “have gradually... / begin to...”","slug":"compare","definition":"〜てくる is often confused with 〜ていく, 〜ようになる, 〜はじめる, and its own “go and come back” meaning. Comparing them helps you choose the pattern based on time direction and intended meaning.","nuance":"The pair てくる and ていく contrasts time direction: てくる looks from the past to the present, while ていく looks from the present to the future. ようになる focuses on the result of a change, not the process. はじめる marks only the starting point.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"暖かく","r":"あたたかく","m":"warm"},{"t":"なってきた","r":"なってきた","m":"has gradually become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The weather has gradually warmed up.","scene":"Feeling the weather as spring arrives"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ていく（変化）","point":"てくる: PAST → PRESENT (coming closer). ていく: PRESENT → FUTURE (moving farther away). Example: 暖かくなってきた (has warmed up by now) vs 暖かくなっていく (will gradually warm up later)."},{"vs":"〜ようになる","point":"ようになる emphasizes the RESULT of a state change (becoming able to do something). てくる emphasizes the PROCESS leading to the present. Example: 日本語が話せるようになった (became able to speak Japanese) vs 日本語が上手になってきた (has gradually improved)."},{"vs":"〜はじめる","point":"はじめる marks only the STARTING POINT; it does not imply gradual progress. てくる can mean “begin,” but connects the event with the speaker’s awareness at that moment. Example: 雨が降りはじめた (rain began) vs 雨が降ってきた (rain has started falling toward the present)."},{"vs":"〜てくる（動作）","point":"The same form has different meanings depending on the verb: a change/state verb + てくる = gradual change (暗くなる, 増える); an action verb + てくる = go do it and return (買う, 行く). Example: 眠くなってきた (change) vs パンを買ってきた (go buy bread and return)."}]}},"n3-053||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜てくる（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくる（変化）","patternReading":"てくる","coreMeaning":"Change from the PAST to the PRESENT: “have gradually... / begin to...”","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include using ていく for past-to-present changes, using てくる for the future, confusing gradual change with “go and come back,” and using てくる for facts without a felt process.","nuance":"These errors come from missing the time direction of くる (come) and いく (go). For past to present, use てきた/てくる; for the future, use ていく.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"去年","r":"きょねん","m":"last year"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"暖かく","r":"あたたかく","m":"warm"},{"t":"なってきた","r":"なってきた","m":"has gradually become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The weather has gradually warmed since last year.","scene":"Describing a weather change"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 去年から暖かくなっていく → ✅ 去年から暖かくなってきた (Because the time span is “from last year until now,” use てきた)","❌ これから日本語が上手になってくる → ✅ これから日本語が上手になっていく (これから indicates the future, so use ていく)","❌ Interpreting 買い物をしてくる as “shop gradually” → ✅ It only means “go shopping and come back” (an action); for gradual change, use なる/増える + てくる","❌ 毎日コーヒーを飲みてきた → ✅ 毎日コーヒーを飲んできた (注意: the verb’s te-form is incorrect. 飲む→飲んで, so use 飲んできた)","❌ 日本に住みていく (when meaning “have lived until now” → use 住んできた, not 住んでいく)","△ 頭がよくなる (not wrong, but states only the result) → to emphasize gradual change up to now, add てくる: 頭がよくなってきた"],"compare":null}},"n3-053||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip: the arrow comes","sub":"〜てくる（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくる（変化）","patternReading":"てくる","coreMeaning":"Change from the PAST to the PRESENT: “have gradually... / begin to...”","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember that くる (来る) basically means “come.” A change “comes” toward us, moving from the past to the present. Picture an arrow traveling from far away straight toward the speaker. When you see てくる, ask: “What is coming toward me now?”","nuance":"This tip helps distinguish てくる from ていく (go away). If the event feels as though it is coming closer, use てくる; if it is moving away, use ていく.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-053||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"〜てくる（変化） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくる（変化）","patternReading":"てくる","coreMeaning":"Change from the PAST to the PRESENT: “have gradually... / begin to...”","slug":"phrases","definition":"Several fixed expressions are common in exams and daily life: 〜くなってきた (has become...), 〜が見えてきた (has come into view), 〜が分かってきた (has gradually become clear), and 〜が増えてきた (has increased). Memorize them for quick recognition.","nuance":"These expressions often end a sentence and convey the speaker’s assessment of a change. In JLPT questions, “_____てきた” commonly appears with verbs such as なる, 見える, 分かる, and 増える.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手に","r":"じょうずに","m":"well"},{"t":"なってきた","r":"なってきた","m":"has gradually improved"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My Japanese has gradually improved lately.","scene":"Assessing progress after review"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-054||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ようになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようになる","patternReading":"ようになる","coreMeaning":"Become able to... / start to... (a change of state)","slug":"core","definition":"「ようになる」 describes a **change of state** from NOT having something → HAVING it, or from HAVING it → losing it. It highlights **the result of practice or natural change**, rather than an active decision. Thus, 「泳げるようになった」 implies that the person went through practice and can now swim.","nuance":"This is an **objective pattern describing a transition**—the speaker does not express willpower such as “I’ll try” at the moment of speaking. It works in both spoken and written Japanese, without being especially formal or casual. With V可能形, it shows **acquiring a new ability**, often used when discussing learning achievements.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泳げる","r":"およげる","m":"can swim"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"become able"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By practicing every day, I became able to swim.","scene":"Telling a friend after a swimming course"},{"tokens":[{"t":"甘い","r":"あまい","m":"sweet"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"things"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"食べられる","r":"たべられる","m":"can eat"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"become able"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I became able to eat sweet things.","scene":"Talking about a change in one’s taste"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-054||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ようになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようになる","patternReading":"ようになる","coreMeaning":"Become able to... / start to... (a change of state)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch organizes the joining patterns. **The pattern always follows the dictionary, ない, or potential form of a verb**, never Vます. **V可能形 + ようになる** is the most common form because it expresses “from unable → able.”","nuance":"**Vない形 + ようになる** describes stopping a habit completely, not simply not doing something today. The past form 「〜ようになった」 is commonly used to mark a milestone that has been reached.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ようになる","note":"start a new habit / state"},{"lhs":"V可能形","rhs":"ようになる","note":"become able to ~ (most common)"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"ようになる","note":"come to no longer do ~"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗れる","r":"のれる","m":"can ride"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"become able"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother became able to ride the train alone.","scene":"Proudly talking about her getting used to city travel"},{"tokens":[{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"night"},{"t":"遅く","r":"おそく","m":"late"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ゲーム","m":"games"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しない","r":"しない","m":"not play"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"come to"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came to stop playing games late at night.","scene":"Sharing a lifestyle change with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-054||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜ようになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようになる","patternReading":"ようになる","coreMeaning":"Become able to... / start to... (a change of state)","slug":"usage","definition":"「ようになる」 has three main uses: (1) **new ability**: unable before → able now; (2) **new habit/state**: starting to do something regularly; (3) **loss of a habit/state**: use Vない形.","nuance":"When discussing **foreign-language achievements**, Japanese speakers often use 「〜語が話せるようになりました」 to praise progress. Avoid the present form 「〜ようになる」 for future plans; use ようにする for plans and efforts.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"ジョギング","m":"jogging"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"come to"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I started jogging every morning.","scene":"Telling a coworker about a new habit"},{"tokens":[{"t":"辛い","r":"からい","m":"spicy"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"things"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"食べられる","r":"たべられる","m":"can eat"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"become able"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I became able to eat spicy food.","scene":"Answering a friend who asks if you can eat spicy food"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲まない","r":"のまない","m":"not drink"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"come to"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came to stop drinking alcohol.","scene":"Sharing good news with family after quitting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-054||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with similar patterns","sub":"〜ようになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようになる","patternReading":"ようになる","coreMeaning":"Become able to... / start to... (a change of state)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ようになる from commonly confused patterns: **ようにする**, **てくる**, and **ことになる**. The key question is: “Is this change **a natural result** or **a deliberate action**?”","nuance":"「ようになる」 differs from these patterns by emphasizing **the point reached after a process**, while 「てくる」 emphasizes **the process gradually moving forward**. 「ことになる」 refers to an objective decision or rule, not a change in the speaker’s ability or habits.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"しない","r":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"come to"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Recently, I stopped working overtime.","scene":"Talking about a change in the work environment"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ようにする","point":"ようになる = NATURAL RESULT; ようにする = deliberate EFFORT/RULE. Compare: 早起きするようになった (it became a habit) vs 早起きするようにしている (I’m trying to get up early)."},{"vs":"〜てくる（変化）","point":"てくる = a change gradually moving toward the present; ようになる = a result or milestone reached. Compare: 日本語が上手になってきた (I’m gradually improving) vs 日本語が話せるようになった (I became able to speak Japanese)."},{"vs":"〜ことになる","point":"ことになる = an objective decision/rule, often one-time; ようになる = a gradual, natural change. Compare: 来月から残業しないことになった (the company decided) vs 残業しないようになった (it naturally became a habit)."}]}},"n3-054||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ようになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようになる","patternReading":"ようになる","coreMeaning":"Become able to... / start to... (a change of state)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with ようになる include **using the wrong verb form**, **using it to express intention**, and **confusing it with ようにする**. The golden rule: ようになる is used only for a **natural change of state**.","nuance":"When you want to say “I’ll try to...,” never use ようになる. 「毎日勉強するようになりたい」 sounds like “I wish I would naturally become diligent” and is awkward. Use ようにしたい instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この頃","r":"このごろ","m":"these days"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"甘い","r":"あまい","m":"sweet"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"控える","r":"ひかえる","m":"cut back"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"come to"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"These days, I’ve started cutting back on sweets.","scene":"Replying when a friend notices fewer snacks"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べますようになった → ✅ 食べるようになった (Vます never comes before ようになる)","❌ 日本語を話すようになります (stating a plan) → ✅ 日本語を話すようにします (making an effort)","❌ 毎日勉強するようになりたい → ✅ 毎日勉強するようにしたい","❌ 急に太っているようになった (sudden change) → ✅ 最近太るようになった (requires a process)"],"compare":null}},"n3-054||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜ようになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようになる","patternReading":"ようになる","coreMeaning":"Become able to... / start to... (a change of state)","slug":"memory","definition":"A word-based memory aid: 「よう」 means form or state (様), while 「なる」 means to become. Together: **become a new state**.","nuance":"Picture a seed (the old state) growing into a tree (the new state)—not overnight, but over time. That is why ようになる is associated with a process.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-054||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ようになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようになる","patternReading":"ようになる","coreMeaning":"Become able to... / start to... (a change of state)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Very common phrases with ようになる include **Nが話せるようになる**, **Nが分かるようになる**, **Nが見えるようになる**, and **Vないようになる**. They appear often in JLPT tests and conversation.","nuance":"With perception verbs such as 分かる, 見える, and 聞こえる, ようになる naturally describes the brain or body reaching a new state of awareness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"やっと","r":"やっと","m":"finally"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"新聞","r":"しんぶん","m":"newspaper"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"読める","r":"よめる","m":"can read"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"become able"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I finally became able to read Japanese newspapers.","scene":"Sharing a Japanese-learning achievement online"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-055||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ようにする · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようにする","patternReading":"ようにする","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ / Set a personal rule (conscious action)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ようにする describes making a conscious effort to reach a state or build a habit. The subject actively influences their own behavior and sets a personal rule. **する** ('to do') shows the will to act—not merely a wish or plan, but real repeated action that becomes a habit.","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese. The form **〜ようにしている** is especially common for habits being maintained. It sounds **modest**—Japanese speakers often say 'I am trying to' rather than simply 'I do' when discussing personal effort. Do not use it for a one-time action (use ことにする).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"try to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I try to eat vegetables every day.","scene":"Asked about healthy eating habits"},{"tokens":[{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"at night"},{"t":"遅く","r":"おそく","m":"late"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"食べない","r":"たべない","m":"not eat"},{"t":"ようにしている","r":"ようにしている","m":"try to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I try not to eat late at night.","scene":"Reminding yourself after seeing late-night food"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-055||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜ようにする · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようにする","patternReading":"ようにする","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ / Set a personal rule (conscious action)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **V辞書形／Vない形 + ようにする**. **〜ようにしている** describes maintaining a habit. **〜ようにした** emphasizes having made a resolution. **〜ようにしてください** is a polite request: 'please try to do/not do ~'.","nuance":"Use the form according to the time frame: the present commonly uses **〜ようにしている** to emphasize continuation, while the past uses **〜ようにした** to emphasize the act of deciding. 〜ようにしてください sounds gentler and more polite than a direct command.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"+ ようにする","note":"try to do ~"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"+ ようにする","note":"try not to do ~"},{"lhs":"〜ようにしている","rhs":"(V辞書形/ない形＋)","note":"maintain a habit (present)"},{"lhs":"〜ようにした","rhs":"(V辞書形/ない形＋)","note":"made a resolution; emphasizes starting"},{"lhs":"〜ようにしてください","rhs":"(V辞書形/ない形＋)","note":"polite request: 'please try to ~'"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"なるべく","m":"as much as possible"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"wake up"},{"t":"ようにした","r":"ようにした","m":"tried to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I decided to try to wake up as early as possible.","scene":"Starting a new habit"},{"tokens":[{"t":"授業中","r":"じゅぎょうちゅう","m":"during class"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"携帯","r":"けいたい","m":"phone"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使わない","r":"つかわない","m":"not use"},{"t":"ようにしてください","r":"ようにしてください","m":"please try not to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please try not to use your phone during class.","scene":"A teacher reminding students"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-055||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜ようにする · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようにする","patternReading":"ようにする","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ / Set a personal rule (conscious action)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ようにする is used in **two main situations**: (1) **setting personal rules and maintaining habits** through conscious repeated action; and (2) **making a polite request or suggestion** with **〜ようにしてください**, which is gentler than a direct command. In addition, **〜ようにしています** is used to give a modest answer about personal effort.","nuance":"In conversation, **〜ようにしています** sounds more natural and polite than **〜しています** when discussing habits. It shows modesty rather than claiming perfect success. For requests, **〜ないようにしてください** is more polite than **〜ないでください**, suggesting 'please try not to...' rather than simply forbidding something.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","m":"for the sake of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"ストレッチ","m":"stretch"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ようにしている","r":"ようにしている","m":"try to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For my health, I try to stretch every morning.","scene":"Answering '健康のために何かしていますか'"},{"tokens":[{"t":"なるべく","m":"as much as possible"},{"t":"エレベーター","m":"elevator"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使わない","r":"つかわない","m":"not use"},{"t":"ようにしている","r":"ようにしている","m":"try to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I try to avoid using the elevator.","scene":"Talking about walking for health"},{"tokens":[{"t":"タバコ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"吸わない","r":"すわない","m":"not smoke"},{"t":"ようにしてください","r":"ようにしてください","m":"please try not to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please try not to smoke.","scene":"A no-smoking sign in a public place"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-055||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜ようにする · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようにする","patternReading":"ようにする","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ / Set a personal rule (conscious action)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish **〜ようにする** from similar patterns: **ようになる** describes a **natural change in result** over time; **ことにする** describes a **decision**, often a single event; **ために** describes the **purpose** of an action; and **つもりだ** describes an **intention** that may not yet be acted on. Core idea: **ようにする = deliberate repeated effort**.","nuance":"In conversation, **ようにしている** sounds more modest and practical than **つもりだ** (intention) or **ことにする** (decision). **ようになる**, meanwhile, focuses objectively on an achieved result.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ようにしている","r":"ようにしている","m":"try to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying to exercise every day.","scene":"Comparing with ようになる"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ようになった","r":"ようになった","m":"came to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I gradually made exercising every day into a habit.","scene":"Describing a completed change"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ようになる","point":"ようになる = a gradual, often natural change; ようにする = active effort. 毎日運動するようになった (it became a habit) vs 毎日運動するようにしている (I am trying to exercise daily)."},{"vs":"〜ことにする","point":"ことにする = a one-time decision; ようにする = repeated effort to form a habit. 明日から運動することにした (I decided) vs 毎日運動するようにする (I will try to maintain it)."},{"vs":"〜ために","point":"ために = purpose (in order to do something); ようにする = effort (trying to reach a state). 日本語が上手になるために勉強する (study to improve) vs 日本語が上手になるようにする (try to improve—emphasizing effort)."},{"vs":"〜つもりだ","point":"つもり = intention or plan; ようにする = conscious action. 毎日運動するつもりだ (I intend to exercise daily) vs 毎日運動するようにしている (I am trying to exercise daily)."}]}},"n3-055||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ようにする · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようにする","patternReading":"ようにする","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ / Set a personal rule (conscious action)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with **〜ようにする**. The key distinction is between **repeated effort** (ようにする), a **one-time decision** (ことにする), and a **natural result** (ようになる).","nuance":"Use **〜ようにした** when you want to emphasize 'I set a rule.' If the habit is still continuing now, use **〜ようにしている**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"at night"},{"t":"遅く","r":"おそく","m":"late"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"食べない","r":"たべない","m":"not eat"},{"t":"ようにしている","r":"ようにしている","m":"try to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I try not to eat late at night.","scene":"A standard sentence for introducing a habit"}],"pitfalls":["明日8時に来るようにする is not wrong, but for a simple one-time decision, 明日8時に来ることにしよう (ことにする) is usually more natural — ようにする fits when achieving it requires EFFORT, not merely a decision.","❌ 毎日勉強するようにした (if you still maintain it) → ✅ 毎日勉強するようにしている","❌ 日本語が上手になるためにしている → ✅ 日本語が上手になるようにしている (emphasizes effort, not purpose)"],"compare":null}},"n3-055||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜ようにする · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようにする","patternReading":"ようにする","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ / Set a personal rule (conscious action)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip for **〜ようにする**: split it into **ように** ('in the manner/state of') + **する** ('to do') = **'do what is needed to reach that state.'** Because it contains **する**, it involves active action. Picture a hand setting a goal on your schedule and repeating it every day.","nuance":"Mantra: **'Set a goal + take control'**—this helps you remember that ようにする is not merely an intention, but controlled action.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-055||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ようにする · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ようにする","patternReading":"ようにする","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ / Set a personal rule (conscious action)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions with **〜ようにする** in everyday conversation and on exams: **〜ようにしている** (currently maintaining), **〜ようにしてください** (polite request), **〜ないようにする** (try not to do), and **できるだけ〜ようにする** (try your best).","nuance":"These expressions appear frequently in polite conversation, especially when discussing health, work, and the environment.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れない","r":"わすれない","m":"not forget"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"玄関","r":"げんかん","m":"entrance"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"置く","r":"おく","m":"put"},{"t":"ようにした","r":"ようにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So I would not forget my wallet, I decided to leave it by the entrance.","scene":"Sharing a tip for remembering personal items"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-056||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜てばかりいる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てばかりいる","patternReading":"てばかりいる","coreMeaning":"Keep doing... (criticizing an excessive repeated habit)","slug":"core","definition":"〜てばかりいる describes an action that is repeated **too much**, almost **taking up all one's time**, which displeases the speaker. Its main meanings are **“keep…ing”** and **“do nothing but…”**—for example, 食べてばかりいる = keep eating (critical). The key point is not mere continuation, but a **negative evaluation** of the behavior.","nuance":"Used more in **everyday speech** than in writing. The speaker may be a parent, friend, boss, or anyone offering **advice or criticism**. It can express **annoyance or frustration**, but often in a **softened** way: instead of directly saying “you are hopeless,” it points out the repeated behavior.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"文句","r":"もんく","m":"complaints"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言ってばかりいる","r":"いってばかりいる","m":"keeps complaining"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He keeps complaining.","scene":"A coworker sighs as he complains nonstop."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ゲーム","r":"ゲーム","m":"video games"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"してばかりいないで","r":"してばかりいないで","m":"don't keep playing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"しなさい","r":"しなさい","m":"go study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Don't keep playing games; go study!","scene":"A parent reminds a child who is absorbed in games."},{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"lately"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"悩んでばかりいる","r":"なやんでばかりいる","m":"keep worrying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Lately, I keep worrying.","scene":"Telling a friend after a stressful stretch of days."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-056||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜てばかりいる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てばかりいる","patternReading":"てばかりいる","coreMeaning":"Keep doing... (criticizing an excessive repeated habit)","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure is **Vて-form + ばかり + いる**. Since いる is a stative verb, it can be conjugated for tense, negation, and politeness as usual. To emphasize the **sole object**, を may be omitted and **ばかり placed directly after the noun**: ゲームばかりしている.","nuance":"The negative form **〜てばかりいない** is uncommon; in advice, Japanese speakers usually use **〜てばかりいないで** (don't keep…). In writing or motivational contexts, **〜てばかりもいられない** means “can't keep…”","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"ばかりいる","note":"Affirmative: 食べてばかりいる (keep eating)"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"ばかりいない","note":"Negative: 食べてばかりいない (not keep eating)"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"ばかりいないで","note":"Advice/command: 食べてばかりいないで (don't keep eating)"},{"lhs":"N + ばかり","rhs":"+ V","note":"Object focus: ゲームばかりしている"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"ばかりもいられない","note":"Can't keep: 悲しんでばかりもいられない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べてばかりいる","r":"たべてばかりいる","m":"keep eating"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"太ります","r":"ふとります","m":"get fat"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you keep eating, you'll get fat.","scene":"Friends tease someone who keeps snacking."},{"tokens":[{"t":"悲しんでばかりもいられない","r":"かなしんでばかりもいられない","m":"can't stay sad"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"forward"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"進もう","r":"すすもう","m":"let's move on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't stay sad forever; let's move forward.","scene":"Encouraging oneself after a loss."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-056||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜てばかりいる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てばかりいる","patternReading":"てばかりいる","coreMeaning":"Keep doing... (criticizing an excessive repeated habit)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜てばかりいる is used when the speaker wants to stress that something happens **too often or excessively**, and **should not continue**. There are two typical situations: **(1) criticizing someone else's bad habit**, and **(2) criticizing or complaining about oneself.**","nuance":"Not used for positive behavior. If you praise someone for studying hard but say 勉強してばかりいる, it sounds **sarcastic or mocking**—unless you are deliberately joking. For advice, Japanese speakers often use **〜てばかりいないで** at the end of a clause, before stating what should be done.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","r":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見てばかりいないで","r":"みてばかりいないで","m":"don't keep watching"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"たまには"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出なさい","r":"でなさい","m":"go out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Don't keep watching TV; go outside sometimes.","scene":"A mother speaks to a child glued to the TV."},{"tokens":[{"t":"寝てばかりいる","r":"ねてばかりいる","m":"keep sleeping"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"体","r":"からだ","m":"body"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"よくない","r":"よくない","m":"isn't good"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you keep sleeping, it's bad for your health.","scene":"A friend warns someone who loves sleeping in."},{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failures"},{"t":"してばかりいる","r":"してばかりいる","m":"keep making"},{"t":"なあ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I keep failing.","scene":"Complaining after messing things up several times."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-056||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with ている・ばかりだ","sub":"〜てばかりいる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てばかりいる","patternReading":"てばかりいる","coreMeaning":"Keep doing... (criticizing an excessive repeated habit)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse てばかりいる with **～ている**, **～ばかりだ**, and **～たばかり**. The key distinction is: **～てばかりいる** means both continuous repetition and **criticism**, while **～ている** neutrally describes a state, **～ばかりだ** expresses a one-way trend, and **～たばかり** means something just finished.","nuance":"Strength of criticism: **てばかりいる** is clearly strong; **ばかりだ** is more neutral; **ている** carries no criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"文句","r":"もんく","m":"complaints"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言ってばかりいる","r":"いってばかりいる","m":"keeps saying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He keeps complaining. (critical)","scene":"A coworker is annoyed that he keeps complaining."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子","r":"こ","m":"child"},{"t":"ども"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"泣いている","r":"ないている","m":"is crying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The child is crying. (neutral description)","scene":"Seeing a baby crying in a park."},{"tokens":[{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"いるばかりだ","r":"いるばかりだ","m":"only stay home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On days off, I just stay home. (trend)","scene":"Answering what you do on weekends."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ている","point":"ている = ongoing action / neutral habit; てばかりいる = excessive repetition + criticism. 食(た)べている (eating) → not negative; 食(た)べてばかりいる (keeps eating) → critical."},{"vs":"〜ばかりだ","point":"ばかりだ = only / one-way trend, without stressing criticism. 食(た)べるばかりだ = all that remains is eating; 食(た)べてばかりいる = keeps eating (blameworthy)."},{"vs":"〜たばかり","point":"たばかり = just finished (time); てばかり = repeated continuously (habit). 食(た)べたばかり = just finished eating; 食(た)べてばかり = keep eating."}]}},"n3-056||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜てばかりいる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てばかりいる","patternReading":"てばかりいる","coreMeaning":"Keep doing... (criticizing an excessive repeated habit)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are classic mistakes learners make: using it with positive behavior, using it for a one-time action, and confusing it with **～たばかり**.","nuance":"Watch the context: if someone praises you for studying hard but says 勉強してばかりいる, it sounds **sarcastic or mocking**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"してばかりいる","r":"してばかりいる","m":"keep doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I keep exercising. (sounds critical or sarcastic—not natural praise)","scene":"Trying to praise someone, but it sounds odd to Japanese speakers."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日運動してばかりいる。 → ✅ 毎日運動している。 (For a good habit stated neutrally, use ている)","❌ さっき食べてばかりいる。 → ✅ さっき食べたばかりだ。 (One action just finished → use たばかり)","❌ 夢を見てばかりいる。 (one time) → ✅ 夢を見たばかりだ。 (just dreamed)","❌ 頑張ってばかりいる。 → ✅ 頑張っている。 (For positive effort worth praising, don't use てばかりいる)","❌ 彼女に会ってばかりいる (meaning just met) → ✅ 彼女に会ったばかりだ。"],"compare":null}},"n3-056||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜てばかりいる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てばかりいる","patternReading":"てばかりいる","coreMeaning":"Keep doing... (criticizing an excessive repeated habit)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember **ばかり** = only/nothing but, and **ている** = being in a state. Together: **being in a state of doing only X** → “keep doing X.” Picture a parent scolding: “You just keep playing!”","nuance":"This trick helps distinguish it from neutral ている: if you feel annoyed or critical, use てばかり.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-057||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜一方だ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Increasingly… (a one-way, irreversible trend)","slug":"core","definition":"「〜一方だ」 describes a trend that keeps continuing in one direction (increasing or decreasing), with no sign of stopping or reversing. The speaker observes that things 'just keep going that way,' often expressing concern or helplessness.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, but especially common in journalism and analysis. It sounds objective because it is based on data or observable events, yet may also express concern when the trend is negative. It is clearer and more decisive than 〜つつある or 〜ていく because it stresses that the trend does not reverse.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"物価","r":"ぶっか","m":"prices"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"上がる","r":"あがる","m":"rise"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Prices just keep rising.","scene":"Watching news about runaway prices"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"体力","r":"たいりょく","m":"stamina"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"落ちる","r":"おちる","m":"decline"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His stamina keeps declining.","scene":"Doctor discussing an elderly patient"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-057||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"〜一方だ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Increasingly… (a one-way, irreversible trend)","slug":"forms","definition":"The only structure is **V-辞書形 + 一方だ**. The verb must express change, such as 増える, 減る, 悪化する, or 進む. Do not use the ます, た, or ない form before 一方だ.","nuance":"Somewhat formal and common in writing. 〜一方である is highly formal and written, while 一方だ can also be used in conversation when complaining.","structure":[{"lhs":"動詞辞書形","rhs":"一方だ","note":"上がる一方だ／減る一方だ"},{"lhs":"動詞辞書形","rhs":"一方である","note":"Formal written form"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"借金","r":"しゃっきん","m":"debt"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増える","r":"ふえる","m":"increase"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The debt keeps increasing.","scene":"Complaining while looking at the debt ledger"},{"tokens":[{"t":"少子高齢化","r":"しょうしこうれいか","m":"aging population"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"進む","r":"すすむ","m":"progress"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The declining birth rate and population aging keep worsening.","scene":"Expert analysis on the news"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-057||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜一方だ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Increasingly… (a one-way, irreversible trend)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three typical contexts: (1) **Economics / society** — 少子化 and 格差; (2) **Personal states** — 太る and 借金; (3) **News** — often with data and warnings.","nuance":"In news reports, 一方だ often appears with statistics and has an analytical tone. In conversation, it is used when someone feels stuck. It is not normally used simply to praise something.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"格差","r":"かくさ","m":"inequality"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"広がる","r":"ひろがる","m":"widen"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Inequality keeps widening.","scene":"Debating income inequality"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ストレス","m":"stress"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"たまる","m":"build up"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The stress just keeps building up.","scene":"Office worker complaining"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-057||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions & common confusions","sub":"〜一方だ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Increasingly… (a one-way, irreversible trend)","slug":"compare","definition":"Four commonly confused patterns: 一方で (the other side), つつある (in progress), ていく (change toward the future), and ばかりだ (keeps happening, often negatively).","nuance":"To distinguish **一方だ vs 一方で**, check whether で is present and where it appears. 一方だ ends a sentence; 一方で connects to the following clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"物価","r":"ぶっか","m":"prices"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"上がる","r":"あがる","m":"rise"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"賃金","r":"ちんぎん","m":"wages"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"変わらない","r":"かわらない","m":"not change"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Prices keep rising, while wages stay the same.","scene":"Discussing the economy as prices rise"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜一方で","point":"一方だ = a one-way trend; 一方で = 'meanwhile / on the other hand,' connecting two contrasting clauses. Note で: with で, it means the other side."},{"vs":"〜つつある","point":"つつある emphasizes being in the process of changing and is neutral; 一方だ emphasizes continuing in one direction, often hard to stop. 増えつつある = gradually increasing; 増える一方だ = keeps increasing."},{"vs":"〜ていく","point":"ていく describes change moving toward the future in a neutral way; 一方だ stresses no reversal and may express concern. 増えていく = will keep increasing; 増える一方だ = just keeps increasing."},{"vs":"〜ばかりだ","point":"Both can mean 'keeps getting,' but ばかりだ is more clearly negative, while 一方だ is more neutral and can describe economic growth."}]}},"n3-057||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜一方だ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Increasingly… (a one-way, irreversible trend)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include confusing 一方で with 一方だ, using a non-change verb, and using the pattern in an unsuitable positive context.","nuance":"Although positive uses are possible, ばかりだ or つつある is often more natural for saying 'getting better and better.' Pay attention to the context.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"患者","r":"かんじゃ","m":"patients"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"数","r":"かず","m":"number"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"増える","r":"ふえる","m":"increase"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The number of patients keeps increasing.","scene":"Doctor worried about an overcrowded hospital"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は走る一方だ。 → ✅ 彼の体重は増える一方だ。 (走る is not a change verb)","❌ 天気が寒い一方だ。 (adjective attached directly) → ✅ 天気が寒くなる一方だ。 (change the adjective into a change verb before adding 一方だ)","❌ 物価が上がる一方だで、賃金が上がらない。 → ✅ 物価が上がる一方だ。一方で、賃金は上がらない。 (一方で connects contrasting clauses)"],"compare":null}},"n3-057||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜一方だ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Increasingly… (a one-way, irreversible trend)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember 一方だ: 一 (one) + 方 (direction) = 'one direction, no turning back.' Picture a train plunging downhill without brakes.","nuance":"Also remember: だ is an assertion at the end of a sentence → one direction; で connects onward → two sides in contrast.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-057||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜一方だ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Increasingly… (a one-way, irreversible trend)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with 一方だ in exams and daily life include 増える一方だ, 減る一方だ, 悪化する一方だ, and 進む一方だ.","nuance":"These phrases often describe worrying trends, such as 円安が進む一方だ and 観光客が減る一方だ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"収入","r":"しゅうにゅう","m":"income"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"減る","r":"へる","m":"decrease"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"increasingly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My income just keeps decreasing.","scene":"Employee complaining about pay"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-058||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜がちだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜がちだ","patternReading":"がちだ","coreMeaning":"Often tends to / is prone to (usually negative)","slug":"core","definition":"〜がちだ describes a real tendency that often occurs, usually something unwanted: often catching colds, being late, forgetting, and so on. The speaker uses it after observing or experiencing something repeatedly, not for a momentary guess. Unlike 〜やすい, which means something is easy or prone by nature, がちだ emphasizes that it actually happens often.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; it is slightly more formal than 〜やすい when discussing health. It has a negative or unfavorable nuance and is rarely used for praise. With になる/なっている, it describes gradually becoming prone to ~.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"being late"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"がちだ","m":"often tends to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is often late.","scene":"Commenting on a coworker who is often late"},{"tokens":[{"t":"冬","r":"ふゆ","m":"winter"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ","m":"a cold"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ひき","m":"catching"},{"t":"がちだ","m":"often tends to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"People often catch colds in winter.","scene":"Talking about health as the weather turns cold"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-058||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"〜がちだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜がちだ","patternReading":"がちだ","coreMeaning":"Often tends to / is prone to (usually negative)","slug":"forms","definition":"This is the connection pattern for がちだ: attach it to the **ます-stem** of a verb (remove ます) or to a **noun** to mean “often ~.” When modifying a noun, use がちな / がちの; when making an adverb, use がちに.","nuance":"It does not attach directly to adjectives (❌忙しいがちだ). Nouns are often illnesses, weather, or health states such as 病気 or 曇り. Both がちな and がちの are correct, but がちな is more natural in speech.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます (remove ます)","rhs":"＋ がちだ","note":"Often tends to do/be ~ (negative tendency)"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ がちだ","note":"Often tends to be ~; usually illness, weather, or health"},{"lhs":"〜がちな / がちの","rhs":"＋ N","note":"Modifies a noun: 病気がちな子供"},{"lhs":"〜がちに","rhs":"＋ V","note":"Adverbial form: 遠慮がちに意見を言う"},{"lhs":"Vます / N","rhs":"＋ がちになる / なっている","note":"Become prone to ~ / be gradually becoming prone to ~"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"がちな","m":"often prone to"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"overexertion"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しないで","m":"please do not"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please do not push a child who often gets sick too hard.","scene":"A doctor advising a parent about a frail child"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"lately"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"忘れ","r":"わすれ","m":"forget"},{"t":"がちに","m":"often tend to"},{"t":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Lately, he has become prone to forgetting things.","scene":"A family member worries about his increasing absent-mindedness"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-058||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜がちだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜がちだ","patternReading":"がちだ","coreMeaning":"Often tends to / is prone to (usually negative)","slug":"usage","definition":"The main situations are **health** (often sick or tired), **repeated actions** (often late, forgetful, or skipping meals), and **natural phenomena** (often cloudy). In medical writing or advice, doctors and pharmacists use it objectively.","nuance":"With になる, it emphasizes the process of becoming prone to ~. Add です/だった for politeness or the past. Do not use it for a one-time action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"忙しくて","r":"いそがしくて","m":"busy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"meals"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"抜き","r":"ぬき","m":"skip"},{"t":"がちに","m":"often tend to"},{"t":"なっている","m":"have become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have been skipping meals because work is so busy.","scene":"Sharing a busy lifestyle with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"高齢者","r":"こうれいしゃ","m":"older adults"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"骨粗しょう症","r":"こつそしょうしょう","m":"osteoporosis"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なり","m":"become"},{"t":"がち","m":"often prone to"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Older adults are prone to developing osteoporosis.","scene":"A doctor advising a patient at a clinic"},{"tokens":[{"t":"曇り","r":"くもり","m":"cloudy weather"},{"t":"がちの","m":"often cloudy"},{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"続いて","r":"つづいて","m":"continues"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The cloudy weather has been continuing.","scene":"Talking about gloomy weather all week"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-058||compare":{"title":"⚖️ がち vs やすい vs っぽい","sub":"〜がちだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜がちだ","patternReading":"がちだ","coreMeaning":"Often tends to / is prone to (usually negative)","slug":"compare","definition":"Three easily confused tendency patterns: **がちだ** = something actually happens often, usually negatively; **やすい** = something is easy or prone by nature, neutrally; **っぽい** = seems like or feels like, subjectively. ばかり exaggerates “nothing but,” unlike the milder がち.","nuance":"When choosing between がち and やすい, ask: is this an **observed tendency** (がち) or an **inherent property** (やすい)? っぽい is often used for personality or appearance and is more subjective.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一人暮らし","r":"ひとりぐらし","m":"living alone"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"と","m":"if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食生活","r":"しょくせいかつ","m":"diet"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"偏り","r":"かたより","m":"become unbalanced"},{"t":"がちだ","m":"often tends to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When living alone, your diet often becomes unbalanced.","scene":"Discussing eating habits among people who live alone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜やすい","point":"やすい = an inherent tendency (neutral); がちだ = an observed tendency (negative). E.g. 壊れやすい (easy to break—property) vs 壊れがちだ (often breaks—tendency)."},{"vs":"〜っぽい","point":"っぽい = a subjective impression of being “~” about personality; がちだ = an observed tendency. E.g. 忘れっぽい (forgetful by nature) vs 忘れがちだ (actually often forgets)."},{"vs":"〜ばかり","point":"ばかり = an exaggerated “nothing but ~,” suggesting excess; がちだ = “often ~” at a moderate, realistic level."}]}},"n3-058||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜がちだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜がちだ","patternReading":"がちだ","coreMeaning":"Often tends to / is prone to (usually negative)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use がちだ for good things, one-time actions, or forget な before a noun. Remember: がちだ means a **repeated negative tendency**.","nuance":"You may still see がち in formal writing, but it is not used for praise. To say “often succeeds,” use よく成功する, not がち.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"頃","r":"ころ","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"がちだった","m":"often was prone to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I often got sick when I was a child.","scene":"Recalling frequent illness in childhood"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 成功しがちだ (often succeeds—unnatural) → ✅ 失敗しがちだ (often fails). がちだ almost always describes something negative.","❌ 病気がち子供 → ✅ 病気がちな子供. Add な when modifying a noun.","❌ 昨日、病気がちだ (one time) → ✅ 子供の頃、病気がちだった (a long-term tendency).","❌ 忙しいがちだ (adjective + がち) → ✅ 忙しくて、忘れがちだ. がちだ does not attach directly to adjectives."],"compare":null}},"n3-058||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜がちだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜がちだ","patternReading":"がちだ","coreMeaning":"Often tends to / is prone to (usually negative)","slug":"memory","definition":"がち sounds a little like “gachi,” which can evoke repeatedly nodding. Picture someone who is often sick, often late, and often forgetful—that is がちだ.","nuance":"Mnemonic: “Often prone to ~, again and again” → remember its negative nuance. With a new word, ask: is it negative and repeated?","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-058||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜がちだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜がちだ","patternReading":"がちだ","coreMeaning":"Often tends to / is prone to (usually negative)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed がち phrases often appear on exams: 遅刻しがち, 忘れがち, 病気がち, 曇りがち, and 遠慮がちに. Memorize them for quick recognition.","nuance":"遠慮がちに is the adverbial form and often comes before verbs such as 言う、聞く、頼む. 病気がち can also modify a noun as 病気がちな.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"忘れ","r":"わすれ","m":"forget"},{"t":"がちな","m":"often prone to"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"しっかり","m":"carefully"},{"t":"メモ","m":"notes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取る","r":"とる","m":"take"},{"t":"ようにしています","m":"make a habit of"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because he often forgets things, I make sure to take careful notes.","scene":"Explaining how to cope with a forgetful coworker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-059||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜っぽい · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っぽい","patternReading":"っぽい","coreMeaning":"Seemingly / X-like / prone to X (subjective, mild comment)","slug":"core","definition":"〜っぽい is a suffix attached to a noun, a verb stem without ます, or an い-adjective stem to create an い-adjective meaning **seemingly / X-like / prone to X**. The speaker gives a **subjective impression** based on personal perception rather than objective fact. Compared with そう/みたい, っぽい often describes a **quality or tendency** of a person or thing.","nuance":"Casual and familiar, mainly used in **everyday conversation**; limit it in formal writing and academic papers. It can carry a **mildly critical** tone for personality (子供っぽい, 嘘っぽい), but can also be neutral (白っぽい).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供っぽい","r":"こどもっぽい","m":"childish"},{"t":"ところ","m":"aspects"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"has"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He has some childish aspects.","scene":"Commenting on a friend's personality to someone else."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"飽きっぽい","r":"あきっぽい","m":"easily bored"},{"t":"性格","r":"せいかく","m":"personality"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She has a tendency to get bored easily.","scene":"Describing someone who often abandons hobbies."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-059||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜っぽい · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っぽい","patternReading":"っぽい","coreMeaning":"Seemingly / X-like / prone to X (subjective, mild comment)","slug":"forms","definition":"Three main patterns: **N + っぽい** (N-like), **Vます stem + っぽい** (often does / easily becomes), and **Aい stem + っぽい** (somewhat or strongly A-like). Since it is an い-adjective, it conjugates normally: **っぽくない**, **っぽかった**, and **っぽくて**.","nuance":"The suffix attaches directly, without と/に. っぽい is inherently subjective, common in conversation, and less common in official documents.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N＋っぽい","note":"N-like / possessing N's qualities (childlike, adult-like, a color, etc.)"},{"lhs":"Vます stem","rhs":"Vます stem＋っぽい","note":"Prone to / easily ~ (personality: forgetful, easily bored, quick-tempered)"},{"lhs":"Aい stem","rhs":"Aい stem＋っぽい","note":"Somewhat / seemingly / strongly A-like (cheap-looking, whitish, etc.)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"スープ","m":"soup"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"水っぽい","r":"みずっぽい","m":"watery"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This soup is watery.","scene":"Tasting soup at a new restaurant."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"飽きっぽい","r":"あきっぽい","m":"easily bored"},{"t":"性格","r":"せいかく","m":"personality"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She has a tendency to get bored easily.","scene":"Telling a friend about someone who often changes jobs."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"素材","r":"そざい","m":"material"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安っぽく","r":"やすっぽく","m":"cheap-looking"},{"t":"見える","r":"みえる","m":"looks"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This material looks cheap.","scene":"Judging an item while shopping."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-059||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜っぽい · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っぽい","patternReading":"っぽい","coreMeaning":"Seemingly / X-like / prone to X (subjective, mild comment)","slug":"usage","definition":"Depending on the base word, っぽい covers several areas: **people's personality**, **color and appearance**, **material and taste**, and the feeling that something is **'kind of a lie'**—a mild suspicion. In manga and anime, っぽい is especially common in dialogue by younger characters.","nuance":"Do not use it in reports or formal letters. It sounds natural when commenting with friends or family; the judgment is usually **mild**, not harsh.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"lately"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"怒りっぽく","r":"おこりっぽく","m":"short-tempered"},{"t":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I've been more short-tempered lately.","scene":"Commenting about yourself while chatting with a friend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"白っぽい","r":"しろっぽい","m":"whitish"},{"t":"服","r":"ふく","m":"clothes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"着て","r":"きて","m":"wearing"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Someone is wearing whitish clothes.","scene":"Describing someone standing outside the door."},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"嘘っぽくない","r":"うそっぽくない","m":"doesn't sound true"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Doesn't that sound made up?","scene":"Hearing an unbelievable excuse from a friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-059||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Comparing 'seems' patterns","sub":"〜っぽい · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っぽい","patternReading":"っぽい","coreMeaning":"Seemingly / X-like / prone to X (subjective, mild comment)","slug":"compare","definition":"These four patterns are often confused because they can all be translated as 'seems': **っぽい** is a subjective impression of a quality; **らしい** means true to type; **みたい** makes a visual comparison; and **そう** describes an outward sign of something about to happen.","nuance":"らしい is often positive, while っぽい can be neutral or mildly negative. みたい and そう do not attach to personality descriptions in the same way as っぽい.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"男っぽい","r":"おとこっぽい","m":"masculine"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She seems masculine.","scene":"Commenting on a woman's appearance or way of speaking."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜らしい","point":"らしい = proper/positive; っぽい = somewhat like, possibly critical. 男らしい (properly manly) ≠ 男っぽい (man-like)."},{"vs":"〜みたい","point":"みたい = an exact comparison; っぽい = having some quality of X. 水みたい (like water) vs 水っぽい (watery)."},{"vs":"〜がちだ","point":"がち = an actual tendency in behavior; っぽい = an inherent personality trait. 忘れがち (often forgets) vs 忘れっぽい (forgetful by nature)."},{"vs":"〜そうだ","point":"そう = an outward sign that something may happen; っぽい = a subjective impression. 雨が降りそう (looks like rain) vs 嘘っぽい (sounds like a lie)."}]}},"n3-059||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜っぽい · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っぽい","patternReading":"っぽい","coreMeaning":"Seemingly / X-like / prone to X (subjective, mild comment)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three errors: using っぽい with abstract nouns, using it in formal writing, and confusing it with らしい. Remember: **っぽい = subjective impression**, best suited to colors, materials, personality, and personal tendencies.","nuance":"For a real-world tendency that occurs frequently, がちだ is more neutral; to say something is inherently easy to happen, use やすい.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"怒りっぽい","r":"おこりっぽい","m":"short-tempered"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"損","r":"そん","m":"loss"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"suffers"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Short-tempered people end up worse off.","scene":"Advising a friend not to lose their temper."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 成功っぽい → ✅ 成功しそうだ (looks likely to succeed); っぽい is rarely used with abstract nouns.","❌ Formal writing: このデザインは安っぽい → ✅ このデザインは高級感に欠ける.","❌ Saying 男っぽい to praise someone as 'properly manly' → ✅ 男らしい is the positive choice.","❌ Using っぽい for an objective tendency → ✅ がちだ / やすい is more neutral."],"compare":null}},"n3-059||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜っぽい · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っぽい","patternReading":"っぽい","coreMeaning":"Seemingly / X-like / prone to X (subjective, mild comment)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **っぽい** as a filter bag: dip a little X into something and the whole sentence takes on X's flavor: 赤っぽい = reddish, 子供っぽい = childish, 嘘っぽい = sounds like a lie.","nuance":"When your brain senses 'a hint of X' rather than 'the real X,' use っぽい instead of だ/である.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-059||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common words & phrases","sub":"〜っぽい · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っぽい","patternReading":"っぽい","coreMeaning":"Seemingly / X-like / prone to X (subjective, mild comment)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These っぽい words appear frequently in conversation and on JLPT questions. Other useful examples include **大人っぽい** (mature-looking), **白っぽい** (whitish), **黒っぽい** (dark-colored), and **安っぽい言い訳** (a cheap, unconvincing excuse).","nuance":"These combinations are almost vocabulary items, so learn them as complete chunks for faster recall.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"うちの","m":"my"},{"t":"上司","r":"じょうし","m":"boss"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"怒りっぽい","r":"おこりっぽい","m":"short-tempered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My boss has a terrible temper.","scene":"Complaining to a coworker about your boss."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-060||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning: 3 directions of favors","sub":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","patternReading":"てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","coreMeaning":"Receiving and giving favors in three directions","slug":"core","definition":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる form a trio for expressing who does what for whom, including the direction of the favor. Once you understand the arrow, you understand most of this grammar.","nuance":"This trio is extremely common in both speech and writing. The hardest point for English-speaking learners is distinguishing the viewpoint of receiving a favor, since English often uses the general expression “do something for.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸して","r":"かして","m":"lend"},{"t":"もらった","r":"もらった","m":"had a favor done"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I had my friend lend me a book.","scene":"A friend lends you the book you need."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸して","r":"かして","m":"lend"},{"t":"くれた","r":"くれた","m":"did for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend lent me a book.","scene":"Your friend offers to lend it without being asked."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸して","r":"かして","m":"lend"},{"t":"あげた","r":"あげた","m":"did for them"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I lent my friend a book.","scene":"Your friend needs a book, so you gladly lend it."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-060||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation patterns","sub":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","patternReading":"てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","coreMeaning":"Receiving and giving favors in three directions","slug":"forms","definition":"The te-form connects to one of the three verbs **もらう**, **あげる**, or **くれる**. Particles change according to the direction: **に** marks the doer with もらう; **に** marks the recipient with あげる; **が** marks the doer with くれる.","nuance":"Use the **past tense** when the action is complete, especially when thanking someone. In professional settings, replace もらう with **いただく** and くれる with **くださる** for greater politeness.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"もらう","note":"I receive a favor — doer + に: 友達に手伝ってもらう"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげる","note":"I do a favor — recipient + に: 友達に手伝ってあげる"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くれる","note":"Someone does me a favor — doer + が: 友達が手伝ってくれる"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただく","note":"Honorific of もらう — 先生に教えていただく"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くださる","note":"Honorific of くれる — 先生が教えてくださる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"診て","r":"みて","m":"examine"},{"t":"もらった","r":"もらった","m":"had examined"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I had the doctor examine me.","scene":"The doctor carefully examines you during a visit."},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"弁当","r":"べんとう","m":"boxed lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"くれた","r":"くれた","m":"made for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother made me a boxed lunch.","scene":"Your mother prepares a bento for school."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"運んで","r":"はこんで","m":"carry"},{"t":"あげた","r":"あげた","m":"helped"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I helped my friend carry the luggage.","scene":"Your friend has heavy bags, so you lend a hand."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-060||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and asking for favors","sub":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","patternReading":"てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","coreMeaning":"Receiving and giving favors in three directions","slug":"usage","definition":"This trio appears in requests, asking permission, thanking, and storytelling. Choose the form based on **the relationship** and **who acts or receives the benefit**.","nuance":"**〜てくれない？** is not negative in meaning; it is a soft request, with **rising intonation** at the end. **〜てもらえませんか** is more polite and works well with acquaintances and coworkers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらえませんか","r":"もらえませんか","m":"could you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you take me to the station?","scene":"You ask a coworker for a ride after work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"くれない","r":"くれない","m":"could you help"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Could you help me for a moment?","scene":"You ask your roommate to lift a table."},{"tokens":[{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"重そう","r":"おもそう","m":"looks heavy"},{"t":"だから","m":"so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"あげましょう","r":"あげましょう","m":"I’ll help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That luggage looks heavy. Let me carry it.","scene":"You see a stranger carrying a heavy suitcase."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-060||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrasts and common mix-ups","sub":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","patternReading":"てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","coreMeaning":"Receiving and giving favors in three directions","slug":"compare","definition":"All three describe someone doing something for me or someone on my side, but they differ in **viewpoint and particles**. There is also a cultural rule: Japanese speakers avoid making favors sound like acts of generosity from above.","nuance":"The hardest contrast is **もらう vs くれる**: for the same event, use もらう when you asked for it, and くれる when the other person volunteered. English often uses “did it for me” for both, so ask: “Who initiated it?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"くれた","r":"くれた","m":"helped"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend taught me.","scene":"You describe how your friend voluntarily helped you study."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てもらう","point":"The subject receives the favor, emphasizing that I benefited: 友達に教えてもらった."},{"vs":"〜てくれる","point":"The subject gives the favor, emphasizing the other person’s kindness: 友達が教えてくれた."},{"vs":"〜てあげる","point":"The subject gives the favor, with a “doing someone a favor” nuance; avoid it with superiors: 友達に教えてあげた."},{"vs":"〜てくれない？","point":"Rising intonation = soft request; falling intonation with no ? = real negative: 手伝ってくれない？ vs 誰も手伝ってくれない."}]}},"n3-060||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","patternReading":"てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","coreMeaning":"Receiving and giving favors in three directions","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three errors: using あげる with superiors, choosing に instead of が, and misunderstanding 〜てくれない？ as a negative. Distinguish the **negative form** from **actual negation**.","nuance":"Using あげる with a superior sounds **arrogant, as if granting a favor**, and is especially inappropriate. With superiors, use 〜ていただく or お〜します.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"いただきました","r":"いただきました","m":"received teaching"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My teacher taught me.","scene":"A student describes receiving guidance from a teacher."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生に教えてあげた → ✅ 先生に教えていただいた","❌ 弟に手伝ってくれた。 → ✅ 弟が手伝ってくれた。 (With てくれる, the doer takes が, not に)","⚠️ 誰も手伝ってくれない (true negative complaint) differs from 手伝ってくれない？ (request with rising intonation) — both are grammatical.","❌ 部長にコピーを取ってあげます → ✅ 部長、コピーをお取りします"],"compare":null}},"n3-060||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip: draw the arrows","sub":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","patternReading":"てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","coreMeaning":"Receiving and giving favors in three directions","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture an **arrow diagram**: あげる points outward, while もらう and くれる point toward you. Identify where the benefit goes, and the correct verb becomes clear.","nuance":"もらう and くれる both point inward, but the initiator differs: もらう = I ask, くれる = they volunteer. Mnemonic: “Out – Ask – Volunteer.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-060||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases and expressions","sub":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","patternReading":"てもらう・てあげる・てくれる","coreMeaning":"Receiving and giving favors in three directions","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases include **〜てもらえますか** (could you do it?), **〜てくれませんか** (please help), **〜ていただけますか** (very polite), **〜てくれてありがとう** (thanks for doing it), and **〜てもらえて助かりました** (your help saved me).","nuance":"When thanking someone, use the **past form**: てくれて + ありがとう, てもらって + よかった. Longer forms and more negative wording generally sound more polite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらえて","r":"もらえて","m":"had taken"},{"t":"助かりました","r":"たすかりました","m":"was a great help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was a lifesaver having you take me home.","scene":"You thank a friend for giving you a ride home."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-061||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる","patternReading":"られる・させる","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive ~ (passive); let/make ~ (causative)","slug":"core","definition":"**〜（ら）れる** is passive: the subject **is affected by** someone else. **〜（さ）せる** is causative: the subject **lets or makes** someone else act. The two patterns point in opposite directions but share the logic of 'who causes → who is affected'.","nuance":"Both are used in speech and writing. 〜（ら）れる is neutral: it may mean 'be favorably affected' or 'be bothered/negatively affected'. 〜（さ）せる shows authority—the causer actively directs another person; with てくれる/てください, it can instead express kind permission or a polite request.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"褒められました","r":"ほめられました","m":"was praised"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He was praised by his teacher.","scene":"Seeing a coworker happy after being praised"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"息子","r":"むすこ","m":"son"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"させる","r":"させる","m":"makes study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The mother makes her son study.","scene":"A father talks about how the mother teaches their child"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-061||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる","patternReading":"られる・させる","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive ~ (passive); let/make ~ (causative)","slug":"forms","definition":"Passive and causative conjugation depends on the verb group. Memorize the two endings: **〜れる** (passive) and **〜せる** (causative).","nuance":"General rules: **Group I** changes the う sound to the あ row, then adds れる/せる. **Group II** drops る, then adds られる/させる. **する→される/させる, 来る→こられる/こさせる.** Note that 食べられる overlaps with the potential form, so context is essential.","structure":[{"lhs":"Group I (五段)","rhs":"う段 → あ段 + れる/せる","note":"飲む→飲まれる・飲ませる; 押す→押される・押させる"},{"lhs":"Group II (一段)","rhs":"drop る + られる/させる","note":"食べる→食べられる・食べさせる; 見る→見られる・見させる"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"される / させる","note":"注意する→注意される・注意させる"},{"lhs":"来る","rhs":"来られる / 来させる","note":"Pronounced こられる・こさせる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"読まれて","r":"よまれて","m":"is read"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is read by many people.","scene":"Introducing a favorite book"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊ばせた","r":"あそばせた","m":"let play"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The mother let her child play in the park.","scene":"Recounting taking a child out to play"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-061||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる","patternReading":"られる・させる","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive ~ (passive); let/make ~ (causative)","slug":"usage","definition":"Distinguish these uses: direct passive, indirect passive (迷惑受け身), causative permission, and causative force. This helps you choose the correct particle, 「に / を」.","nuance":"Direct passive changes an active sentence: 'AがBをV' → 'BがAにV-られる'. Indirect passive adds an affected person as the subject: 'affected person が cause に V-られる'. With transitive verbs, causative uses 「A に B を V-させる」; with intransitive verbs, 「A を V-せる」. For a polite request for permission, use させてください.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"泥棒","r":"どろぼう","m":"thief"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"盗まれた","r":"ぬすまれた","m":"was stolen"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A thief stole my wallet.","scene":"Reporting a theft in an apartment"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"やらせて","r":"やらせて","m":"let me do it"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please let me do it.","scene":"Asking to take on an important task"},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"降られて","r":"ふられて","m":"was rained on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ずぶ濡れ","r":"ずぶぬれ","m":"soaked"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I got soaked in the rain.","scene":"It starts raining while you are walking home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-061||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる","patternReading":"られる・させる","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive ~ (passive); let/make ~ (causative)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse passive with てくれる/てもらう because all involve someone affecting someone else. The key distinction is that **passive** focuses on being affected, often without control, while **てくれる/てもらう** means someone does something for you, with gratitude or a request.","nuance":"Indirect passive (迷惑受け身) often implies annoyance and cannot be replaced by てくれる. Causative reverses passive: causative means affecting another person; passive means being affected.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"足","r":"あし","m":"foot"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"踏まれた","r":"ふまれた","m":"was stepped on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend stepped on my foot.","scene":"On a crowded train"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"助けて","r":"たすけて","m":"help"},{"t":"くれた","r":"くれた","m":"did for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend helped me.","scene":"When I was in trouble"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てもらう","point":"てもらう = ask someone to do something (you actively request it); passive = be affected unwillingly. Ex: 母に部屋を掃除してもらった (asked Mom to clean the room) vs 母に日記を読まれた (Mom read my diary)."},{"vs":"〜てくれる","point":"てくれる = someone does something for you (grateful, happy); passive = receive an action. Ex: 友達が助けてくれた (My friend helped me) vs 友達に足を踏まれた (My friend stepped on my foot)."},{"vs":"〜（さ）せる（使役）","point":"Causative reverses passive: causative = the subject allows/forces; passive = the subject is affected. Ex: 父は私に留学させた (Dad let me study abroad) vs 私は父に留学させられた (Dad forced me to study abroad)."}]}},"n3-061||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる","patternReading":"られる・させる","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive ~ (passive); let/make ~ (causative)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common errors. Check whether the subject receives the action, whether someone is making another person act, and whether the particle に/を is correct.","nuance":"English can omit an explicit passive marker in some contexts, but Japanese requires passive conjugation. With causative, intransitive verbs use を and transitive verbs use に. 迷惑受け身 still requires られる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"褒められた","r":"ほめられた","m":"was praised"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was praised by my teacher.","scene":"After a presentation"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生に褒めました。 → ✅ 先生に褒められました。","❌ 母は子供に公園で遊ばせた。 → ✅ 母は子供を公園で遊ばせた。","❌ 雨に降って、風邪をひいた。 → ✅ 雨に降られて、風邪をひいた。","❌ 子供に勉強しました。 → ✅ 子供に勉強させた。"],"compare":null}},"n3-061||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる","patternReading":"られる・させる","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive ~ (passive); let/make ~ (causative)","slug":"memory","definition":"Visual mnemonic: **れる** resembles 'receive'—receive an action. **せる** resembles 'set'—set someone to a task. Combining **せ+られる** means 'be set to do something' → be forced.","nuance":"Draw four arrows: active (私が読む) → causative (私が読ませる) → passive (私が読まれる) → causative-passive (私が読まされる). The diagram makes the relationships easy to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-061||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜（ら）れる・〜（さ）せる","patternReading":"られる・させる","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive ~ (passive); let/make ~ (causative)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear often in JLPT questions and everyday conversation. Memorizing them as chunks helps you respond quickly.","nuance":"Note that させてください is a polite request for permission, not strong causation. In casual speech, you may hear shortened forms such as 〜れちゃう and 〜せちゃう.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"また","m":"again"},{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"させられた","r":"させられた","m":"was forced to work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was forced to work overtime again.","scene":"Complaining to a friend about work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-062||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える","patternReading":"ようにいう・たのむ・つたえる","coreMeaning":"Indirectly convey: tell / ask / send someone a message to do ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える is an **indirect speech** pattern for requests, commands, or favors. Instead of quoting exact words like 「〜」と言う, use **V dictionary form/ない-form + ように + 言う/頼む/伝える** to express telling, asking, or messaging someone to do or not do something. It is common in daily conversation, workplaces, and writing.","nuance":"This pattern is **more polite and gentle** than a direct command. It avoids sounding imposing and helps preserve the listener's dignity. With the passive **言われた**, it sounds objective and mild: “I was told...” rather than “My boss told me...”. Politeness generally increases from 言う → 頼む → お願いする.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"生徒","r":"せいと","m":"student"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"静かに","r":"しずかに","m":"quietly"},{"t":"する","m":"keep"},{"t":"ように言った","r":"ようにいった","m":"told to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher told the students to keep quiet.","scene":"In a noisy class, the teacher asks everyone to be quiet."},{"tokens":[{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"overexertion"},{"t":"しない","m":"not to"},{"t":"ように言われた","r":"ようにいわれた","m":"was told"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The doctor told me not to overexert myself.","scene":"After the appointment, the doctor gives the patient advice."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-062||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える","patternReading":"ようにいう・たのむ・つたえる","coreMeaning":"Indirectly convey: tell / ask / send someone a message to do ~","slug":"forms","definition":"General formula: **A は B に [V dictionary form / Vない-form] + ように + 言う／頼む／伝える**. The verb before ように **is not conjugated**—it stays in dictionary or negative form with ない. The main verb after ように expresses the act of conveying: 言う (tell), 頼む (ask), 伝える (pass on), 注意する (warn), お願いする (politely ask).","nuance":"The verb after ように sets the level of formality: 言う is neutral, 頼む means ask a favor, and お願いする is very polite, especially with customers or superiors. In business email, **〜するようお願いいたします** is extremely respectful.","structure":[{"lhs":"V dictionary form + ように","rhs":"言う／頼む／伝える","note":"Tell/ask/pass on that someone should do ~"},{"lhs":"Vない + ように","rhs":"言う／頼む／伝える","note":"Tell/ask/pass on that someone must NOT do ~"},{"lhs":"〜ように + 言われる","rhs":"(passive)","note":"Be told to ~ — gentle and objective"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"ように言った","r":"ようにいった","m":"told to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I told them to come early.","scene":"Telling a coworker to arrive early for the meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participation"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ようにお願いしました","r":"ようにおねがいしました","m":"politely asked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I politely asked the department manager to attend the meeting.","scene":"An employee politely asks their manager to attend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-062||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & nuance","sub":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える","patternReading":"ようにいう・たのむ・つたえる","coreMeaning":"Indirectly convey: tell / ask / send someone a message to do ~","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern is used in three main situations: (1) **indirectly conveying a request or command** when the speaker has authority or is being neutral; (2) **asking a favor** when help is needed; and (3) **passing on a message** through an intermediary. It is also used with 注意する to warn or 勧める to give advice.","nuance":"In Japanese communication, people often **avoid direct commands**, especially with superiors or older people. 〜ように言う turns a command into a gentler request. For favors, choose 頼む or お願いする instead of 言う to show humility.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"帰る","r":"かえる","m":"return"},{"t":"ように言われた","r":"ようにいわれた","m":"was told"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother told me to come home early.","scene":"A mother tells her child to come home early."},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone call"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"ように伝えてください","r":"ようにつたえてください","m":"please tell"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please tell Mr. Tanaka to call.","scene":"Asking a coworker to pass on a message."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"秘密","r":"ひみつ","m":"secret"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"する","m":"keep"},{"t":"ように頼んだ","r":"ようにたのんだ","m":"asked to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked my friend to keep it a secret.","scene":"Sharing something private and asking a friend not to tell."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-062||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing from と言う","sub":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える","patternReading":"ようにいう・たのむ・つたえる","coreMeaning":"Indirectly convey: tell / ask / send someone a message to do ~","slug":"compare","definition":"This section distinguishes 〜ように言う from commonly confused patterns: **〜と言う** (direct quotation) and **〜てほしいと言う** (a personal wish).","nuance":"〜と言う puts the exact words inside quotation marks and can sound like a direct command. 〜ように言う reports the message indirectly and more politely. 〜てほしいと言う emphasizes the speaker's personal wish, while ように言う simply conveys a request.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"ように言った","r":"ようにいった","m":"told to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I told him to come.","scene":"Passing on a request instead of saying it directly."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜と言う","point":"と言う quotes the exact words (using 「」); ように言う conveys them indirectly without an exact quotation. Example: 「早く来い」と言った → 早く来るように言った."},{"vs":"〜てほしいと言う","point":"てほしいと言う expresses a personal wish (“I want you to ~”), while ように言う expresses an indirect request or command from someone, often passed on as a message."}]}},"n3-062||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える","patternReading":"ようにいう・たのむ・つたえる","coreMeaning":"Indirectly convey: tell / ask / send someone a message to do ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using the wrong main verb, confusing direct and indirect speech, and choosing a verb that does not fit the relationship.","nuance":"Remember: before ように, use only V dictionary form or Vない-form—not polite or past forms. The verb after ように must be a reporting or communication verb.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone call"},{"t":"しない","m":"not use"},{"t":"ように注意された","r":"ようにちゅういされた","m":"was warned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was warned not to use my phone on the train.","scene":"On a train, staff warn passengers not to make calls."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 静かにするように歩く。 → ✅ 静かにするように言う。 (歩く is not a reporting verb)","❌ 「早く来い」と言った。(when you want an indirect message) → ✅ 早く来るように言った。","❌ 来るように言う (when speaking to a boss without authority) → ✅ 来るようにお願いする。 (choose the verb for the relationship)"],"compare":null}},"n3-062||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える","patternReading":"ようにいう・たのむ・つたえる","coreMeaning":"Indirectly convey: tell / ask / send someone a message to do ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine the basic meaning of ように as “so that / in such a way.” Thus, 〜ように言う means “say it in a way that someone does ~.” Combined with a reporting verb, it creates the formula: **tell, ask, or message someone to do ~**.","nuance":"Imagine holding a note and passing it to someone: “Tell him to do Y, okay?” The more you use 伝える, the more it resembles forwarding a message.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-062||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ように言う／頼む／伝える","patternReading":"ようにいう・たのむ・つたえる","coreMeaning":"Indirectly convey: tell / ask / send someone a message to do ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common fixed phrases with 〜ように言う／頼む／伝える in JLPT questions and everyday conversation. Mastering them helps you express yourself smoothly and naturally and understand the speaker's meaning in real conversations.","nuance":"The passive 〜ように言われる is very common when reporting someone else's words. 〜ように伝えてください is a polite pattern for asking an intermediary to pass on a message.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"him"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"ように伝えておいてください","r":"ようにつたえておいてください","m":"please tell him"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please remember to tell him to get in touch.","scene":"Asking a coworker to pass on a message."},{"tokens":[{"t":"レポート"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"今日中","r":"きょうじゅう","m":"by today"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出す","r":"だす","m":"submit"},{"t":"ように言われた","r":"ようにいわれた","m":"was told"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was told to submit the report by the end of today.","scene":"A boss assigns work through a secretary."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-063||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ということだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ということだ","patternReading":"ということだ","coreMeaning":"It means / I hear that (rephrasing or relaying information)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ということだ has two meanings: (1) **relaying** information heard or read — “I hear that”; (2) **rephrasing or concluding** from context — “that means.” Both stem from “the fact is...,” so the listener senses that the speaker is either passing on information or explaining it.","nuance":"Used in **both speech and writing**, with a moderately formal tone. Adding です as ということです makes it more polite. The relaying meaning **does not imply guesswork**, unlike らしい. The rephrasing meaning often appears when confirming what someone has said in conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"not come"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"I hear that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear that he will not come.","scene":"An employee hears the manager relay news"},{"tokens":[{"t":"つまり","m":"in short"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"that means"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In short, it means we failed.","scene":"Drawing a conclusion from project results"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-063||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜ということだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ということだ","patternReading":"ということだ","coreMeaning":"It means / I hear that (rephrasing or relaying information)","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure is **普通形（plain form）+ ということだ**. With nouns and な-adjectives, keep **だ** before と: 学生だ → 学生だということだ. The polite form is **〜ということです**; the question form is 〜ということですか.","nuance":"This structure nominalizes an entire clause with こと, making it clearer and slightly more formal than そうだ. In casual speech, it may be shortened to って.","structure":[{"lhs":"V / Aい 普通形","rhs":"＋ ということだ","note":"行く・行った・行かない → 行くということだ"},{"lhs":"N / ナA","rhs":"だ ＋ ということだ","note":"学生だ / 元気だ → 学生だということだ"},{"lhs":"～普通体","rhs":"ということです","note":"Polite form, used with superiors/customers"},{"lhs":"～普通体","rhs":"ということですか","note":"Confirmation question “Does that mean...?”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"I hear that/means"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear that he is a student.","scene":"Answering about a friend’s occupation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"I hear that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear that the meeting was canceled.","scene":"A colleague relays news from the manager"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-063||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"〜ということだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ということだ","patternReading":"ということだ","coreMeaning":"It means / I hear that (rephrasing or relaying information)","slug":"usage","definition":"Function ① **relaying** is used when the speaker passes on information heard or read, such as reports or news. Function ② **rephrasing** is used to draw a conclusion from context, often with **つまり / 要するに**.","nuance":"In workplace conversation, **ということは...ですか？** is a polite way to confirm what the other person has just said, showing active listening. The relaying meaning often follows **〜によると** (according to...) or **部長から** (from the manager).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","r":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"よると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"経済","r":"けいざい","m":"economy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"回復","r":"かいふく","m":"recover"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"I hear that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear the economy is recovering.","scene":"Reading the morning news and telling a colleague"},{"tokens":[{"t":"つまり","m":"in short"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participate"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"ということです","r":"ということです","m":"that means"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So, does that mean you cannot participate?","scene":"Confirming after a friend says they are busy next week"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-063||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"〜ということだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ということだ","patternReading":"ということだ","coreMeaning":"It means / I hear that (rephrasing or relaying information)","slug":"compare","definition":"〜ということだ is often confused with 〜そうだ（伝聞）, 〜わけだ, and 〜とのことだ. Each has a different focus: そうだ only relays information, わけだ expresses a logical conclusion, and とのことだ is formal.","nuance":"ということだ can both relay information and rephrase it. そうだ and わけだ each serve a narrower role. とのことだ commonly appears in emails and company announcements.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"I hear that/means"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear that / So, tomorrow is a day off.","scene":"The manager announces a day off"},{"tokens":[{"t":"それで","m":"therefore"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"わけだ","r":"わけだ","m":"no wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So that is why we failed.","scene":"Understanding the cause after an explanation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","point":"そうだ only relays information; it does not mean “that means.” 明日は休みそうだ = “looks like it will be a day off” (様態) or “I hear it is a day off” (伝聞); 明日は休みということだ = “I hear that/so tomorrow is a day off.”"},{"vs":"〜わけだ","point":"わけだ gives a logical conclusion, “no wonder / naturally,” based on reasoning; ということだ rephrases or relays information. 成績がよかったわけだ = “no wonder they did well”; 成績がよかったということだ = “I hear they did well.”"},{"vs":"〜とのことだ","point":"とのことだ is commonly used in formal written language (emails and notices), while ということだ is more common and works in both speech and writing."}]}},"n3-063||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ということだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ということだ","patternReading":"ということだ","coreMeaning":"It means / I hear that (rephrasing or relaying information)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner mistakes: (1) failing to distinguish the two meanings without context; (2) omitting **だ** after nouns and な-adjectives; (3) confusing it with **〜という** (called); (4) using そうだ to mean “that means”—which is incorrect.","nuance":"Quick clues: **つまり / 要するに** → rephrasing; **〜によると / 〜から** → relaying information. In conversation, ということ may naturally be shortened.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"I hear that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear that they are a student.","scene":"Correcting a sentence that omitted だ"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 学生ということだ → ✅ 学生だということだ (keep だ after the noun)","❌ つまり、来ないそうだ → ✅ つまり、来ないということだ (そうだ does not rephrase)","❌ これは「さくら」ということだ → ✅ これは「さくら」という (this is a name, not a rephrasing)","❌ 彼は来ませんということだ → ✅ 彼は来ないということだ (use 普通形 before ということだ; only the final ending becomes polite with です)"],"compare":null}},"n3-063||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ということだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ということだ","patternReading":"ということだ","coreMeaning":"It means / I hear that (rephrasing or relaying information)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember the parts: **という** = “say/call,” **こと** = “fact,” and **だ** = “is.” Together: “the fact being said is...” → depending on context, this becomes “that means” or “I hear that.”","nuance":"When you see ということだ, ask yourself: “Is there an outside source?” → relaying; “Am I summarizing or restating something?” → rephrasing.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-063||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"〜ということだ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ということだ","patternReading":"ということだ","coreMeaning":"It means / I hear that (rephrasing or relaying information)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases in tests and daily life include: **つまり〜ということだ** (in short), **〜によると〜ということだ** (according to..., I hear that...), **そういうことだ** (that’s right), and **要するに〜ということだ** (to sum up).","nuance":"そういうことだ is a natural way to confirm agreement, without sounding overly formal. 要するに is slightly stronger than つまり as a summarizing expression.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そういう","r":"そういう","m":"like that"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"fact"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That’s right / That’s how it is.","scene":"Confirming when someone asks, “You mean...?”"},{"tokens":[{"t":"要するに","r":"ようするに","m":"in short"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"ということだ","r":"ということだ","m":"that means"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"To sum up, we do not have time.","scene":"Explaining why an invitation is declined"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-064||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜という · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜という","patternReading":"という","coreMeaning":"Called / named; identifies a noun or introduces content","slug":"core","definition":"〜という is a tool for **identifying and introducing content**. Its most familiar use attaches a name to something: 「X」というY = “a Y named X.” It can also place an entire clause before an abstract noun such as 話・知らせ・噂. In conversation, Japanese speakers often use 〜という言葉/概念 to introduce a new word politely.","nuance":"It is used in both speech and writing. Unlike と言う, the verb “to say,” という here is **unstressed** and has become a grammatical suffix for explanation. A proper name or clause can come before という, and a noun always follows it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"桜","r":"さくら","m":"cherry blossom"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flower"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買いました","r":"かいました","m":"bought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I bought a flower called Sakura.","scene":"Telling a friend about a flower you bought"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"named"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone call"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありました","r":"ありました","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A person named Tanaka called.","scene":"A receptionist reports a phone call"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"will not come"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"that"},{"t":"知らせ","r":"しらせ","m":"news"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"受けた","r":"うけた","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received news that he would not come.","scene":"Receiving an email about a schedule change"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-064||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜という · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜という","patternReading":"という","coreMeaning":"Called / named; identifies a noun or introduces content","slug":"forms","definition":"という does not change form. It connects **a proper name** or **a plain-form clause** to the noun that follows. Politeness does not change という; only the verb before it changes tense or form.","nuance":"When the following noun is concrete (人・花・料理), という **labels a name**. When it is abstract (話・知らせ・噂・点・条件), という introduces **the entire clause** as its content. This is essential for building complex sentences.","structure":[{"lhs":"Proper name (「X」)","rhs":"という + concrete N","note":"N named X"},{"lhs":"Plain-form clause (普通形)","rhs":"という + abstract N","note":"N with the content ~"},{"lhs":"〜という","rhs":"のは + N + です","note":"Definition: 〜というのは〜のことです"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"忖度","r":"そんたく","m":"sontaku"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"word"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意味","r":"いみ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"説明","r":"せつめい","m":"explain"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please explain the meaning of the word “sontaku.”","scene":"Asking a teacher about a word in an article"},{"tokens":[{"t":"全員","r":"ぜんいん","m":"everyone"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participation"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"that"},{"t":"条件","r":"じょうけん","m":"condition"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"決めた","r":"きめた","m":"decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We decided on the condition that everyone participate.","scene":"A partner states a condition before signing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-064||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜という · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜という","patternReading":"という","coreMeaning":"Called / named; identifies a noun or introduces content","slug":"usage","definition":"By situation, 〜という appears in three groups. Group 1 **introduces names** so the listener knows what something is called. Group 2 **introduces news or stories** by packaging a long clause as a noun. Group 3 appears in news and academic writing, where という attaches content to an abstract noun and keeps the sentence concise.","nuance":"In formal writing, という + N frequently appears in forms such as 「Xという動き」 and 「Xという点で」. When introducing a new word, adding 〜という言葉/表現 sounds **polite and friendly** rather than abrupt.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"もんじゃ焼き","r":"もんじゃやき","m":"monjayaki"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"食べ物","r":"たべもの","m":"food"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っていますか","r":"しっていますか","m":"do you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know the food called “monjayaki”?","scene":"Asking a Japanese friend about a local specialty"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"辞める","r":"やめる","m":"quit"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"that"},{"t":"噂","r":"うわさ","m":"rumor"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"広まっている","r":"ひろまっている","m":"is spreading"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A rumor that she will quit is spreading.","scene":"Hearing coworkers whisper at the office"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-064||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"〜という · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜という","patternReading":"という","coreMeaning":"Called / named; identifies a noun or introduces content","slug":"compare","definition":"The key distinction is this: **という** is an explanatory suffix, while **と言う** is the verb “to say.” If a name or clause comes before it and a noun follows, use という; for an act of speaking, use と言う. という also has the casual form って; というのは starts an explanation, while というのが acts as the subject.","nuance":"In speech, という is often reduced to って. For JLPT reading, especially, recognize という + N. If you see と言った or と言っている, it is the verb “to say,” not a naming suffix.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"that"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いた","r":"きいた","m":"heard"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I heard that he would come.","scene":"A coworker shares meeting information"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜と言う","point":"と言う is the verb “to say” and can take tense: と言った. という does not conjugate and always comes before a noun. Compare: 彼が来ると言った (he said he would come) vs 彼が来るという話 (a story that he would come)."},{"vs":"〜って","point":"って is a conversational shortening of という and と. という is more standard and works in writing. Compare: 田中って人 vs 田中という人."},{"vs":"〜というのは","point":"というのは begins a sentence and means “as for what is called…”: 「納豆」というのはどんな食べ物ですか. というのが acts as the subject: 彼が参加するというのが問題だ."},{"vs":"〜と呼ばれる","point":"と呼ばれる means “be called” and is often used for famous names; という simply presents a name or content."}]}},"n3-064||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜という · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜という","patternReading":"という","coreMeaning":"Called / named; identifies a noun or introduces content","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often forget **という** because English can say “a person named Lan” without an overt linker. In Japanese, という is required between the name and noun. Conversely, という may be wrongly used when reporting an act of speaking.","nuance":"On tests, these errors are usually about **syntactic function**, not spelling: if a noun follows, use という; if it ends the sentence or carries tense, use と言う.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"という","r":"という","m":"named"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会った","r":"あった","m":"met"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I met a person named Tanaka.","scene":"Talking about someone you just met"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 田中さん人に会った。 → ✅ 田中さんという人に会った。","❌ 彼は「来る」という。 → ✅ 彼は「来る」と言う。 (the verb “to say”)","❌ 彼が合格したというが知らせ… → ✅ 彼が合格したという知らせ…","❌ 彼が来るとという話 → ✅ 彼が来るという話"],"compare":null}},"n3-064||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜という · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜という","patternReading":"という","coreMeaning":"Called / named; identifies a noun or introduces content","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of という as a **sticker** or **name tag** attached to a noun. When you encounter a name or clause, attach it to the noun with という, and the listener immediately understands: “Oh, this is called…”","nuance":"This trick helps you remember the position: という always sits between two elements, like a bridge connecting a name or content to the main noun. When unsure, ask: “Am I labeling something, or reporting speech?”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-065||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜によると／によれば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜によると／によれば","patternReading":"によると・によれば","coreMeaning":"According to (a source), based on ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜によると／によれば means **according to (an information source)**. The speaker cites a specific source such as the news, TV, someone's words, or a document, then relays the information heard. This pattern only **cites a source**; it is not used for an independent assertion.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, especially in news and journalism. It has a modest tone: the speaker **does not take responsibility** for whether the information is true, attributing it to the source. によれば is more formal than によると.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天気予報","r":"てんきよほう","m":"weather forecast"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the weather forecast, I hear it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Hearing the weather forecast before going to work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新聞","r":"しんぶん","m":"newspaper"},{"t":"によれば","r":"によれば","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"倒産","r":"とうさん","m":"bankruptcy"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"らしい","r":"らしい","m":"apparently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the newspaper, that company apparently went bankrupt.","scene":"Relaying news read in the newspaper"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-065||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜によると／によれば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜によると／によれば","patternReading":"によると・によれば","coreMeaning":"According to (a source), based on ~","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure has two parts: **information source (noun) + によると/によれば**, followed by **information + そうだ/ということだ/らしい/とのことだ**. The source must be a noun referring to a person, document, or media source.","nuance":"によると is common in conversation; によれば is more common in writing, reports, and essays. The ending affects certainty: ということだ is highest, そうだ is neutral, and らしい is lower.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（information source）＋ によると","rhs":"〜そうだ","note":"I hear (neutral)"},{"lhs":"N（information source）＋ によれば","rhs":"〜ということだ","note":"I hear (high confidence)"},{"lhs":"N（information source）＋ によると/によれば","rhs":"〜らしい","note":"apparently (low confidence)"},{"lhs":"N（information source）＋ によると/によれば","rhs":"〜とのことだ","note":"formal, polite"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"words"},{"t":"によれば","r":"によれば","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難しかった","r":"むずかしかった","m":"was difficult"},{"t":"らしい","r":"らしい","m":"apparently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to him, the exam was apparently difficult.","scene":"Relaying what a friend said about the exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"報告書","r":"ほうこくしょ","m":"report"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"売上","r":"うりあげ","m":"sales"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増加","r":"ぞうか","m":"increase"},{"t":"している","m":"are increasing"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the report, I hear sales are increasing.","scene":"Citing a sales report in a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-065||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜によると／によれば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜によると／によれば","patternReading":"によると・によれば","coreMeaning":"According to (a source), based on ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to cite information from a **specific source**: news, newspapers, weather forecasts, experts, surveys, or someone else's words. The information clause usually ends with そうだ, らしい, or ということだ.","nuance":"In Japanese journalism, によると **identifies the source**, avoiding unsupported claims and reflecting a modest 謙虚 style. Do not use it for something the speaker knows firsthand or personally evaluates.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大きな","r":"おおきな","m":"large"},{"t":"地震","r":"じしん","m":"earthquake"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あった","m":"occurred"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear there was a large earthquake.","scene":"After watching an earthquake report"},{"tokens":[{"t":"専門家","r":"せんもんか","m":"expert"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"効果","r":"こうか","m":"effective"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"has"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to experts, I hear this medicine is effective.","scene":"Quoting an expert in a presentation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-065||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish: によると vs によって","sub":"〜によると／によれば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜によると／によれば","patternReading":"によると・によれば","coreMeaning":"According to (a source), based on ~","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes によると from commonly confused patterns, especially によって, which looks similar but has a completely different meaning. It also compares に基づいて to clarify the difference between citing a source and acting on a basis.","nuance":"によると = **information source**; によって = **cause/means/depending on**; に基づいて = **basis for action**. Replacing one with another can change the meaning or make the grammar incorrect.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"地震","r":"じしん","m":"earthquake"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あった","m":"occurred"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear there was an earthquake in Osaka.","scene":"Watching news about an earthquake in Osaka"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜によって","point":"によって = due to/depending on/by means of; によると = cites a source. Example: 人によって意見が違う (opinions differ by person), 人によると... (according to that person)."},{"vs":"〜に基づいて","point":"に基づいて = act based on grounds (事実に基づいて判断する); によると = relay information from a source (ニュースによると...)."},{"vs":"〜そうだ（伝聞）/〜らしい","point":"そうだ/らしい are sentence endings and cannot stand alone here; によると supplies the source. 天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうだ."}]}},"n3-065||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & fixes","sub":"〜によると／によれば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜によると／によれば","patternReading":"によると・によれば","coreMeaning":"According to (a source), based on ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with によると/によれば include **omitting the required ending**, making a definite assertion, or confusing it with によって.","nuance":"Learners often transfer the phrase “according to me” and use によると for personal opinions. This is wrong because によると cites an external source, not a personal view.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"10時","r":"じゅうじ","m":"10 o'clock"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to him, I hear the meeting starts at 10:00.","scene":"Correcting a student's sentence in class"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 天気予報によると、明日は雨だ。 → ✅ 天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうだ。","❌ ニュースによると、地震があった。 → ✅ ニュースによると、地震があったそうだ。","❌ 地震によると、家が壊れた。 → ✅ 地震によって、家が壊れた。","❌ 私によると、この計画はいい。 → ✅ 私の考えでは、この計画はいい。"],"compare":null}},"n3-065||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜によると／によれば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜によると／によれば","patternReading":"によると・によれば","coreMeaning":"According to (a source), based on ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: imagine a **signal-receiving antenna**. によると points to a source (newspaper, TV, or speaker); the signal received is the clause ending in そうだ/らしい. Since it is only a received signal, do not state it as certain.","nuance":"Short mnemonic: “The source speaks; we **relay**, not **assert**.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-065||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜によると／によれば · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜によると／によれば","patternReading":"によると・によれば","coreMeaning":"According to (a source), based on ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed combinations often appear in JLPT questions and news. Memorizing them helps you read and fill in answers faster.","nuance":"Common sources: 天気予報, ニュース, 新聞, 調査, 専門家, 〜の話, 噂. Common endings: そうだ, ということだ, らしい, とのことだ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"噂","r":"うわさ","m":"rumor"},{"t":"によれば","r":"によれば","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"辞める","r":"やめる","m":"quit"},{"t":"そうだ","r":"そうだ","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to rumors, I hear he will quit his company next month.","scene":"Telling a colleague some overheard news"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-066||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜そうだ（伝聞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"I hear... / Reporting information from an outside source","slug":"core","definition":"〜そうだ（伝聞）is used to **report** information the speaker heard from someone else, the media, or the news. It means “I hear...” The speaker **does not guarantee** that it is true, but simply passes on the information. Unlike 様態 (it looks/seems), 伝聞そうだ follows the **plain form** of verbs, adjectives, and nouns.","nuance":"This form is **neutral and objective**, without strong emotion. It is common in both daily conversation and writing. そうです is more polite than そうだ. In conversation, Japanese speakers often use らしい to soften certainty or avoid making a firm claim; そうだ suggests a clear information source.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"account"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"よると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"あの"},{"t":"レストラン"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to my friend, I hear that restaurant is really good.","scene":"Telling a coworker about a new restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"天気予報","r":"てんきよほう","m":"weather forecast"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"よると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"rain"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the weather forecast, I hear it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Checking the forecast before going to work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-066||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜そうだ（伝聞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"I hear... / Reporting information from an outside source","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches directly to the **plain form** (普通形). No further change is needed. Nouns and な-adjectives must have **だ** before そうだ. With い-adjectives, **keep the final い**—an important point that distinguishes it from 様態.","nuance":"The polite form is **そうです**, used with older people or in the workplace. そうだ can become **そうだった** when the reported time is in the past (different from changing the tense of the verb or adjective before そうだ); そうでしょ is not used because this reports objective information rather than making an inference.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"+ そうだ","note":"行くそうだ / 行ったそうだ / 行かないそうだ"},{"lhs":"いA","rhs":"+ そうだ","note":"楽しいそうだ (keep い)"},{"lhs":"なA","rhs":"+ だ + そうだ","note":"静かだそうだ"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"+ だ + そうだ","note":"医者だそうだ"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来年","r":"らいねん","m":"next year"},{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear he is getting married next year.","scene":"Repeating news from a mutual friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"面白い","r":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear this book is interesting.","scene":"Friends recommending a book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-066||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜そうだ（伝聞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"I hear... / Reporting information from an outside source","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when you want to **report** information you did not witness directly. It often combines with **〜によると / 〜の話では** to identify the source. At work, it is useful for relaying news from managers, clients, and others.","nuance":"Do not use it for something you **saw yourself**; that calls for 様態. It can report negative states: ないそうだ. In polite settings, そうです sounds considerably more formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"よると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その"},{"t":"地域","r":"ちいき","m":"area"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"洪水","r":"こうずい","m":"flooding"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あった","r":"あった","m":"occurred"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear that area had flooding.","scene":"Watching the morning news"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"転勤","r":"てんきん","m":"transfer"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear Tanaka is being transferred next month.","scene":"Talking at the office"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"not come"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear the company president is not coming today.","scene":"Employees talking before a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-066||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing そうだ vs らしい","sub":"〜そうだ（伝聞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"I hear... / Reporting information from an outside source","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish the three commonly confused patterns: **そうだ（伝聞）**, **そうだ（様態）**, and **らしい**. The key points are the **form before the pattern** and the **degree of certainty**.","nuance":"そうだ（伝聞）comes from hearing → plain form. そうだ（様態）comes from seeing → ます-stem / drop い / drop だ. らしい combines hearsay and inference, with lower certainty.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"rain"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear it will rain. (from someone else or a forecast)","scene":"Comparing with 雨が降りそうだ (it looks like rain from dark clouds)"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうだ（様態）","point":"伝聞 follows the plain form; 様態 follows the ます-stem / adjective stem. 雨が降るそうだ (I hear it will rain) vs 雨が降りそうだ (it looks like rain)."},{"vs":"〜らしい","point":"そうだ reports information from a clear source and is more certain; らしい leans toward inference or hearsay and is useful when speaking cautiously."},{"vs":"〜ということだ","point":"Both mean “I hear,” but ということだ is formal and common in writing or announcements; そうだ is natural in everyday conversation."}]}},"n3-066||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜そうだ（伝聞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"I hear... / Reporting information from an outside source","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are classic learner errors with そうだ（伝聞）. Remember: **nouns and な-adjectives need だ**, **い-adjectives keep い**, and negatives are formed on the verb or adjective before そうだ (not by negating そうだ).","nuance":"The most serious error is confusing 伝聞 with 様態, which reverses the meaning. Always check the form immediately before そうだ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"面白い","r":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear that movie is interesting.","scene":"Friends inviting you to see a movie"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は学生そうです。 → ✅ 彼は学生だそうです。","❌ あの店はおいしそうです。（want to say “I hear it is good”） → ✅ あの店はおいしいそうです。","❌ 明日は雨が降るそうではない。（want to say “I hear it will NOT rain”） → ✅ 明日は雨が降らないそうだ。（negate the verb/adjective before そうだ, not そうだ）","❌ 彼女は元気そうだ。（want to say “I hear she is well”） → ✅ 彼女は元気だそうだ。"],"compare":null}},"n3-066||memory":{"title":"🎯 Ear-eye memory trick","sub":"〜そうだ（伝聞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"I hear... / Reporting information from an outside source","slug":"memory","definition":"The quickest memory aid: **ears hear** (伝聞) and use the full **plain form**; **eyes see** (様態) and use a **shortened form** (ます-stem / drop い / drop だ).","nuance":"Simply look at the **form before そうだ** to tell whether it reports information or describes an appearance.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-066||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"〜そうだ（伝聞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","patternReading":"そうだ","coreMeaning":"I hear... / Reporting information from an outside source","slug":"phrases","definition":"These common combinations make sentences sound **natural in Japanese**: use によると to identify the source, の話では to cite someone’s account, or と聞いた to emphasize that you heard it.","nuance":"In a work email, 〜とのことです has the same meaning but is more formal; そうだよ is casual and suited to friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"account"},{"t":"では"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安くて","r":"やすくて","m":"cheap and"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to Tanaka, I hear the new shop is cheap and good.","scene":"A coworker talking about a newly opened shop"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-067||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜とのことだ／とのこと · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とのことだ／とのこと","patternReading":"とのことだ","coreMeaning":"Reportedly; formal indirect information","slug":"core","definition":"〜とのことだ relays information heard or received from someone else, often through email, documents, or an intermediary. The speaker is only passing on the information and does not claim responsibility for its content. It is clearly more formal than そうだ and common in business and reports.","nuance":"Usable in speech and writing, but it has a written, businesslike tone. With friends, そうだ sounds more natural. とのことです is more polite; とのことでございます is extremely honorific for customers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中様","r":"たなかさま","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"ご欠席","r":"ごけっせき","m":"absence (hon.)"},{"t":"とのことです","r":"とのことです","m":"reportedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Tanaka is absent today, according to the notice.","scene":"A secretary reports a customer's schedule to a manager."},{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department head"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"メール","m":"email"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"とのことです","r":"とのことです","m":"reportedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the department head's email, tomorrow's meeting was canceled.","scene":"Reading a company email and reporting it to a colleague."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-067||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and connecting forms","sub":"〜とのことだ／とのこと · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とのことだ／とのこと","patternReading":"とのことだ","coreMeaning":"Reportedly; formal indirect information","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is **普通形 + とのこと**; add **だ/です** when it ends a sentence. 普通形 includes the plain form of verbs and adjectives, and noun + だ. **N + とのこと** is also possible. **とのことです** is polite, while **とのこと** without だ is common in emails and memos.","nuance":"Formality generally increases as follows: そうだ, ということだ, とのことだ, とのことです, とのことでございます. Standard business emails often use とのことです or とのことでございます.","structure":[{"lhs":"動詞・イ形・ナ形辞書形／た形","rhs":"+ とのことだ／です","note":"Plain form before とのこと"},{"lhs":"N（＋だ）","rhs":"+ とのことだ／です","note":"だ may be omitted after nouns"},{"lhs":"普通形","rhs":"+ とのこと","note":"Bare form in emails/memos"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"によりますと","r":"によりますと","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"参加者","r":"さんかしゃ","m":"participants"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"30名","r":"さんじゅうめい","m":"30 people"},{"t":"とのことです","r":"とのことです","m":"reportedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the materials, there are 30 participants.","scene":"Reporting the number of registrants at a meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"off"},{"t":"とのこと","r":"とのこと","m":"according to the notice"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tomorrow is a day off, according to the notice.","scene":"Writing a quick memo for a colleague."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-067||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜とのことだ／とのこと · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とのことだ／とのこと","patternReading":"とのことだ","coreMeaning":"Reportedly; formal indirect information","slug":"usage","definition":"Use it to relay received information in workplace settings such as emails, reports, and messages from colleagues or customers. The source of the information is often highlighted.","nuance":"It sounds too bookish and distant in casual conversations with friends. With superiors or customers, use **とのことです** or **とのことでございます**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"鈴木さん","r":"すずきさん","m":"Mr. Suzuki"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"遅れる","r":"おくれる","m":"be late"},{"t":"とのことです","r":"とのことです","m":"reportedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Suzuki is reportedly going to be a little late.","scene":"A colleague relays Mr. Suzuki's schedule."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"ご来店","r":"ごらいてん","m":"visit the store"},{"t":"されない","r":"されない","m":"not (hon.)"},{"t":"とのことです","r":"とのことです","m":"reportedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The customer is reportedly not coming to the store today.","scene":"A receptionist reports to the manager."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-067||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with そうだ／ということだ","sub":"〜とのことだ／とのこと · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とのことだ／とのこと","patternReading":"とのことだ","coreMeaning":"Reportedly; formal indirect information","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare these reporting patterns: **そうだ** for casual speech, **ということだ** for general interpretation or reporting, **とのことだ** for a clear source and formal relay, and **と言った** for a direct quote.","nuance":"Choose based on formality and whether there is a specific source. For business emails or messages passed through an intermediary, とのこと is usually the most natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来られない","r":"こられない","m":"cannot come"},{"t":"とのことです","r":"とのことです","m":"reportedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to Mr. Tanaka, he cannot come today.","scene":"A third party relays Mr. Tanaka's message."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","point":"そうだ is common in casual speech; とのことだ is formal and suited to emails or intermediaries. Example: 友達は来ないそうだ (friend) vs 田中様は来られないとのことです (business)."},{"vs":"〜ということだ","point":"ということだ focuses on general interpretation or reporting, without requiring a clear source; とのことだ highlights a specific source and sounds more formal."},{"vs":"〜と言った","point":"と言った directly reports someone's words without making them more polite; とのこと reports them indirectly and politely through an intermediary."}]}},"n3-067||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜とのことだ／とのこと · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とのことだ／とのこと","patternReading":"とのことだ","coreMeaning":"Reportedly; formal indirect information","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use とのこと too formally with friends or forget です in formal emails. Choose the ending based on the audience and communication channel.","nuance":"With friends or family, use そうだ or って. With customers or business partners, avoid bare だ and use です/でございます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"課長","r":"かちょう","m":"section chief"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"午後","r":"ごご","m":"afternoon"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"外出","r":"がいしゅつ","m":"go out"},{"t":"とのことです","r":"とのことです","m":"reportedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The section chief is reportedly going out this afternoon.","scene":"A secretary reports the section chief's absence."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 友達は来ないとのこと。 → ✅ 友達は来ないそうだ。 (そうだ is usual in casual speech)","❌ メールで: 会議は中止とのこと。 → ✅ 会議は中止とのことです。 (formal writing needs です)","❌ 本日は休みとのことだ。 → ✅ 本日は休みとのことでございます。 (use でございます with VIP customers)"],"compare":null}},"n3-067||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"〜とのことだ／とのこと · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とのことだ／とのこと","patternReading":"とのことだ","coreMeaning":"Reportedly; formal indirect information","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a **business memo** saying: “According to X's email → Y is reportedly true.” との can suggest “with,” and こと means “matter,” so とのこと means “the fact that.”","nuance":"No example is needed. When you see a business email or an indirect message, think of とのことです.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-067||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common workplace phrases","sub":"〜とのことだ／とのこと · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とのことだ／とのこと","patternReading":"とのことだ","coreMeaning":"Reportedly; formal indirect information","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases frequently appear in business emails: **〜様より、〜とのことでございます**, **〜とのことです**, and **〜とのことでした**.","nuance":"Learn the full frame: [Source] + によると / より + [Information] + とのことです.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"山田様","r":"やまださま","m":"Mr. Yamada"},{"t":"より","m":"from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"14時","r":"じゅうよじ","m":"2 p.m."},{"t":"から"},{"t":"とのことでございます","r":"とのことでございます","m":"reportedly (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to Mr. Yamada, the meeting starts at 2 p.m.","scene":"A formal customer email quoting Mr. Yamada."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-068||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜べきだ／べきではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜べきだ／べきではない","patternReading":"べきだ／べきではない","coreMeaning":"Should/must on moral or duty grounds; should not","slug":"core","definition":"〜べきだ expresses what one **ought to do** according to morality, responsibility, or social standards the speaker believes are right. It is not a legally binding rule, but the speaker’s **conscience speaking**. By saying べきだ, the speaker shows a fairly strong personal belief: “That is how people ought to behave.”","nuance":"This pattern works in both speech and writing, but in conversation it can sound rigid or judgmental. With a superior or someone you have just met, it may feel imposing. Its strength falls between: たほうがいい (gentle advice) → べきだ (moral duty) → なければならない (obligation).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"守る","r":"まもる","m":"keep"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Promises should be kept.","scene":"Reminding a friend about keeping one’s word"},{"tokens":[{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく","r":"つく","m":"tell"},{"t":"べきではない","r":"べきではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not lie.","scene":"Teaching young children about morality"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-068||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜べきだ／べきではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜べきだ／べきではない","patternReading":"べきだ／べきではない","coreMeaning":"Should/must on moral or duty grounds; should not","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches to the **dictionary form of a verb (辞書形)**, not the ます-form or te-form. The negative form is べきではない, while the past form べきだった expresses regret. With する, both すべきだ and するべきだ are correct, but **すべきだ** is more common in writing.","nuance":"べきだった = “should have done it” → regret about not doing it. べきではなかった = “should not have done it” → regret about doing it. These forms are often confused: べきだった is not the negative of べきだ.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"べきだ","note":"should / ought to — on moral grounds"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"べきではない","note":"should not — opposing an action"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"すべきだ／するべきだ","note":"すべきだ is more formal and concise"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"べきだった","note":"should have — regret"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"べきではなかった","note":"should not have — regret"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"したら","r":"したら","m":"if one has"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"謝る","r":"あやまる","m":"apologize"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you made a mistake, you should apologize.","scene":"Talking about principles of conduct"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"すべきだ","r":"すべきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should stay quiet on the train.","scene":"Reminding visitors about Japanese train culture"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-068||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜べきだ／べきではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜べきだ／べきではない","patternReading":"べきだ／べきではない","coreMeaning":"Should/must on moral or duty grounds; should not","slug":"usage","definition":"〜べきだ is commonly used in three broad situations: **(1) stating what is obvious or morally right**, **(2) warning against an action**, and **(3) criticizing or demanding responsibility in speeches and debates**. It is not a pattern for gentle advice such as “eat vegetables to stay healthy”; it expresses a principle.","nuance":"In news reporting, べきだ can sound strongly critical, as in “The government should explain itself more clearly.” In daily life, use care: saying “〜べきだ” to a superior sounds like lecturing them and is very impolite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ため","r":"ため","m":"for the sake"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"もっと","r":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"すべきだ","r":"すべきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思う","r":"おもう","m":"think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think we should exercise more for our health.","scene":"Discussing healthy habits in an interview"},{"tokens":[{"t":"個人","r":"こじん","m":"personal"},{"t":"情報","r":"じょうほう","m":"information"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"他人","r":"たにん","m":"others"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"教える","r":"おしえる","m":"reveal"},{"t":"べきではない","r":"べきではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not reveal personal information to others.","scene":"Discussing information security"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-068||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜べきだ／べきではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜べきだ／べきではない","patternReading":"べきだ／べきではない","coreMeaning":"Should/must on moral or duty grounds; should not","slug":"compare","definition":"〜べきだ is most often confused with **〜なければならない** (obligation), **〜ほうがいい** (gentle advice), and **〜ものだ** (general truth). The key point: べきだ concerns **morality and conscience**, not rules or ordinary advice.","nuance":"On the obligation scale: たほうがいい is subjective and friendly; べきだ is principled and can sound judgmental; なければならない is objective and mandatory—everyone must do it, and failing to do so is wrong.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"中","r":"じゅう","m":"within"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"返す","r":"かえす","m":"return"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book should be returned today.","scene":"Discussing a library book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜なければならない","point":"べきだ = MORAL duty / common sense, based on the speaker’s judgment — 「税金を払うべきだ」. なければならない = mandatory under a LAW/rule — 「税金を払わなければならない」."},{"vs":"〜ほうがいい","point":"ほうがいい = gentle advice focused on the listener’s benefit — 「早く寝たほうがいい」. べきだ = stronger, standard-based advice — 「早く寝るべきだ」."},{"vs":"〜ものだ","point":"ものだ = an obvious truth / general rule — 「人間はいつか死ぬものだ」. べきだ = what one ought to do morally — 「約束は守るべきだ」."},{"vs":"〜たほうがよかった","point":"たほうがよかった = mild regret: another choice would have been better — 「もっと早く寝たほうがよかった」. べきだった = heavier regret tied to DUTY/morality — 「もっと早く行動するべきだった」."}]}},"n3-068||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner pitfalls","sub":"〜べきだ／べきではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜べきだ／べきではない","patternReading":"べきだ／べきではない","coreMeaning":"Should/must on moral or duty grounds; should not","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use べきだ too casually as ordinary “should” in daily advice. In reality, べきだ is **strong** and carries moral or judgmental overtones. For small matters such as eating vegetables or going to bed early, use たほうがいい.","nuance":"With a superior, avoid べきだ because it sounds like a moral command. Also remember that the negative form is べきではない, not べきない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"べきだった","r":"べきだった","m":"should have"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"even though"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"なぜ","r":"なぜ","m":"why"},{"t":"来なかった","r":"こなかった","m":"did not come"},{"t":"ん","r":"ん","m":"(explanation)"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should have come. Why didn’t you?","scene":"Mildly reproaching a friend who missed an appointment"}],"pitfalls":["野菜を食べるべきだ is grammatical but sounds HEAVY and judgmental for a small matter like eating vegetables — use 野菜を食べたほうがいい (gentle advice) instead of the moral force of べきだ.","❌ 先生は休むべきだ → RUDE → ✅ 先生はお休みになったほうがいいです","❌ 税金を払うべきだ (if you mean legally required) → ✅ 税金を払わなければならない — use なければならない for laws","❌ 来るべきなかった → ✅ 来るべきではなかった — “should not have come”","❌ べきない → ✅ べきではない — the negative must include ではない"],"compare":null}},"n3-068||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜べきだ／べきではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜べきだ／べきではない","patternReading":"べきだ／べきではない","coreMeaning":"Should/must on moral or duty grounds; should not","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine a notebook titled **“Things one ought to do”**: keep promises, apologize after mistakes, and help people in trouble. Whenever you want to stress “that is what one ought to do,” connect it with べきだ.","nuance":"This memory tip is only an image to aid recall, not a grammar rule. Combine it with real examples and comparisons of nuance for lasting retention.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-068||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜べきだ／べきではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜べきだ／べきではない","patternReading":"べきだ／べきではない","coreMeaning":"Should/must on moral or duty grounds; should not","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in JLPT questions, news, and speeches: **すべきだ**, **べきではない**, **べきだった**, and **べき行動**.","nuance":"すべきだ is the shortened form of するべきだ and is widely used in formal writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"傷つける","r":"きずつける","m":"hurt"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"べきではない","r":"べきではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not say things that hurt others.","scene":"Discussing workplace communication principles"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-069||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜にほかならない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Precisely / Nothing but ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にほかならない is a **strong assertion** that X is precisely Y, and nothing else. The speaker gives a **definitive conclusion**, with complete certainty. This formal written pattern often appears in essays and speeches.","nuance":"Formal and analytical; **not used in everyday conversation**. Unlike に違いない (inference), にほかならない **asserts a fact** rather than making a judgment. The polite form にほかなりません can be used in speeches.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His success is precisely the result of his hard work.","scene":"An essay analyzing someone's success."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"愛情","r":"あいじょう","m":"love"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is nothing but love.","scene":"Analyzing the nature of a work of art."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-069||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜にほかならない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Precisely / Nothing but ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simple: add にほかならない after a **noun** or a clause in the **plain form (普通形)**. To stress a reason, use **〜のは〜にほかならない**. The polite form is にほかなりません, used in formal speeches.","nuance":"There are no complex conjugation changes; negative and past forms are rare because this pattern focuses on asserting an essential nature in the present. In practice, にほかならない is a fixed phrase, though it has a polite form. Written usage commonly uses the present tense.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にほかならない","note":"Example: これは愛にほかならない"},{"lhs":"〜のは","rhs":"N／Plain form＋にほかならない","note":"Stresses the reason: ~ is precisely because of ~"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The reason he passed is nothing but his daily effort.","scene":"An essay explaining the reason for success."},{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"原因","r":"げんいん","m":"cause"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"準備不足","r":"じゅんびぶそく","m":"lack of preparation"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The cause of the failure was nothing but inadequate preparation.","scene":"A conclusion in an analysis."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-069||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"〜にほかならない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Precisely / Nothing but ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use it to **conclude or explain** the essential cause of something, or to **assert its true nature**. It always appears at the end of a sentence, not at the end of a subordinate clause. It is common in analytical writing, commentary, and speeches.","nuance":"It is **formal and academic**, and is not used in everyday conversation. Compared with こそ, which also emphasizes “precisely,” にほかならない gives a **more decisive conclusion** and often accompanies reasoning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"根本原因","r":"こんぽんげんいん","m":"root cause"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"教育制度","r":"きょういくせいど","m":"education system"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The root cause of this problem is nothing but the education system.","scene":"An essay on education."},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","r":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"差別","r":"さべつ","m":"discrimination"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is nothing but discrimination.","scene":"Analyzing a social behavior."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"皆","r":"みな","m":"everyone"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"にほかなりません","r":"にほかなりません","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This success is entirely thanks to everyone's efforts.","scene":"A formal opening or closing speech."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-069||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜にほかならない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Precisely / Nothing but ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"compare","definition":"These three patterns may sound similar but are **quite different**: にほかならない = “precisely,” にすぎない = “merely,” and に違いない = “must be.” Distinguishing them prevents mistakes in reading and essay writing.","nuance":"にほかならない **formally asserts identity or nature**; に違いない expresses a **certain inference**; にすぎない **downplays the degree**; こそ **emphasizes the preceding word** and usually appears mid-sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これは","r":"これは","m":"this is"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is nothing but the result of hard work.","scene":"An assertion in an essay."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜に違いない","point":"にほかならない = asserts a fact that cannot be otherwise; に違いない = a confident inference based on evidence. Example: これは努力の結果にほかならない (is precisely) vs 彼は医者に違いない (must be a doctor)."},{"vs":"〜にすぎない","point":"にほかならない = asserts “precisely,” elevating the essential nature; にすぎない = “merely,” downplaying the degree. Example: これは努力の結果にほかならない (is precisely the result) vs これは偶然にすぎない (is merely coincidence)."},{"vs":"〜こそ","point":"こそ = emphasizes the preceding word, usually mid-sentence (努力こそが大事だ); にほかならない = concludes “nothing but,” appears at the end, and is more formal."}]}},"n3-069||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜にほかならない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Precisely / Nothing but ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often **use にほかならない in everyday conversation** or **confuse it with an inference**. In reality, this pattern is mainly for **formal writing** and expresses an **absolute assertion**.","nuance":"It sounds stiff when used casually. Remember: にほかならない = a **conclusion**, not a guess. To say “must be” in daily life, use に違いない or はずだ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あれ","r":"あれ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ケーキ","r":"けーき","m":"cake"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is nothing but cake.","scene":"Used too formally in a chat with friends."}],"pitfalls":["❌ あれはケーキにほかならない (to a friend) → ✅ あれはケーキだ","❌ 彼は医者にほかならない (when only guessing) → ✅ 彼は医者に違いない","❌ これはただの偶然にほかならない (downplaying) → ✅ これはただの偶然にすぎない"],"compare":null}},"n3-069||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜にほかならない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Precisely / Nothing but ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember it through the parts: **ほか** (他) means “other” or “outside,” and **ならない** means “not become” or “not be.” Together: “it is not something else” → **it is precisely that**. Imagine every other possibility eliminated, leaving one answer.","nuance":"This tip helps distinguish it from にすぎない: “nothing else” emphasizes essential nature, while “merely” downplays it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-069||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜にほかならない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Precisely / Nothing but ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"phrases","definition":"In tests and speeches, common fixed phrases include **〜のは〜にほかならない** (stressing a reason), **〜の結果にほかならない** (a result), and **〜にほかなりません** (polite). Memorizing them helps with quick recognition.","nuance":"These phrases are formal, so use them in writing or speeches. In conversation, replace them with だ or からだ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The reason he became good at Japanese is nothing but his daily effort.","scene":"A sentence in an essay."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-070||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜に違いない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference, about 95% certain)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に違いない expresses the speaker’s near-absolute certainty (~95%) about a judgment based on observable signs or evidence. It is not a verified fact, but the speaker believes it cannot be otherwise. 違い means “difference”; に違いない means “there is no other possibility,” or “must be.”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. It has a strong tone, close to an assertion. Compared with はずだ (a logical expectation), に違いない expresses more subjective and emotional certainty. In conversation, it is common with friends and close colleagues; the polite form に違いありません is used in formal writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He must be the culprit.","scene":"You see him secretly hiding something"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本人","r":"にほんじん","m":"Japanese person"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person must be Japanese.","scene":"You observe their bowing and clothing style"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-070||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜に違いない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference, about 95% certain)","slug":"forms","definition":"With nouns and な-adjectives, **である** may come before に違いない for a more formal tone. With verbs and い-adjectives, use the **plain form**, including negative and past forms. The polite form is **に違いありません**.","nuance":"The structure is simple and requires no complex changes. Using である makes the sentence firmer and more formal, so it suits written language.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に違いない","note":"Example: 医者に違いない"},{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"に違いない","note":"Examples: 行くに違いない / 行ったに違いない"},{"lhs":"Aい（普通形）","rhs":"に違いない","note":"Example: 安いに違いない"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"であるに違いない","note":"Example: 静かであるに違いない"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"であるに違いない","note":"Example: 先生であるに違いない（formal）"},{"lhs":"普通形 + に違いありません","rhs":"polite form","note":"Example: ご不便をおかけしたに違いありません"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He must be coming.","scene":"You say this after seeing him pack his luggage"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She must already know.","scene":"She smiles when the topic comes up"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-070||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜に違いない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference, about 95% certain)","slug":"usage","definition":"① Inferring a person or thing: based on evidence about its traits. ② Inferring a situation: based on an observable state. ③ In detective fiction, detectives often use it to draw a conclusion. ④ The polite form に違いありません is used in formal emails and documents.","nuance":"Do not use it for a vague feeling alone; there should be supporting evidence. It can combine with きっと for emphasis: きっと〜に違いない. The most formal form is に違いありません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"electric light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついて","r":"ついて","m":"lit"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"誰か","r":"だれか","m":"someone"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light is on, so someone must be inside.","scene":"You see a friend’s light on at night"},{"tokens":[{"t":"顔","r":"かお","m":"face"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"赤い","r":"あかい","m":"red"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Their face is red, so they must have a fever.","scene":"Your son looks flushed and tired"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-070||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with other patterns","sub":"〜に違いない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference, about 95% certain)","slug":"compare","definition":"These inference patterns are often confused. The key distinction: **に違いない** is a roughly 95%-certain inference, **はずだ** is a logical expectation, **だろう** is a soft guess, **かもしれない** expresses a lower possibility, **らしい** is based on hearsay, and **にほかならない** makes a 100% assertion.","nuance":"Choosing the wrong certainty level in conversation can cause misunderstanding. に違いない shows high confidence and nearly sounds like an assertion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He must be coming.","scene":"You see that he planned ahead and bought a ticket"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"はずだ","point":"はずだ = LOGICAL inference (70–80%), based on rules or schedules; に違いない = 95% CERTAINTY from evidence. 例: 彼は来るはずだ (according to the schedule) vs 彼は来るに違いない (I’m sure)."},{"vs":"だろう","point":"だろう = a soft personal guess (50–70%); に違いない = an almost absolute assertion. 例: 明日は雨だろう vs 明日は雨に違いない (the clouds are very dark)."},{"vs":"かもしれない","point":"かもしれない = 50% or less, uncertain; に違いない = 95%, almost certain. 例: 彼は来るかもしれない vs 彼は来るに違いない."},{"vs":"らしい","point":"らしい = an INDIRECT inference or hearsay, without firm assertion; に違いない = certainty based on direct evidence. 例: 彼は来るらしい (I hear he’s coming) vs 彼は来るに違いない (I saw his train ticket)."},{"vs":"にほかならない","point":"にほかならない = a 100% factual assertion, not an inference; に違いない = a 95%-certain inference. 例: 失敗の原因は準備不足にほかならない (the fact) vs 彼が犯人に違いない (the inference)."}]}},"n3-070||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜に違いない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference, about 95% certain)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with 〜に違いない include using it without clear evidence, forgetting である before に違いない after a noun or な-adjective, and confusing it with にほかならない. Remember that に違いない is a subjective inference supported by evidence, not an absolute assertion. Without specific signs, use だろう or かもしれない. Recognizing these errors helps avoid both grammar and nuance problems.","nuance":"Distinguish the basis for the inference clearly. If there is no evidence, do not use に違いない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He must be the culprit.","scene":"A standard sentence used with many clues"}],"pitfalls":["❌ Using に違いない for a vague feeling → ✅ You need clear signs or reasons; otherwise use だろう or かもしれない.","❌ 静かなに違いない → ✅ 静かであるに違いない (である is needed after な-adjectives and nouns).","❌ Confusing に違いない with にほかならない → ✅ にほかならない is 100% fact; に違いない is a ~95% inference."],"compare":null}},"n3-070||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜に違いない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference, about 95% certain)","slug":"memory","definition":"Visualize the pattern to remember it: after analyzing clues, **Detective Conan** often concludes: 「これは凶器に違いない！」. 違う means “wrong” or “different,” and 違いない means “cannot be wrong” → “must be.” Associate it with a red check mark ✓ meaning correct.","nuance":"This memory aid reinforces both the high certainty and the inferential nature of the pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-070||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜に違いない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference, about 95% certain)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common combinations include **きっと** + に違いない for emphasis meaning “surely,” **絶対** + に違いない for “absolutely,” and **〜た** + に違いない for a confident inference about the past.","nuance":"きっと and 絶対 increase the force of the assertion. The past form 〜たに違いない is used when inferring that something happened.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"きっと"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is sure to succeed.","scene":"Encouraging a friend who keeps working hard"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-071||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜はずだ／はずがない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はずだ／はずがない","patternReading":"はずだ／はずがない","coreMeaning":"Should be (well-founded expectation); cannot be (strong negation)","slug":"core","definition":"〜はずだ expresses the speaker's **well-founded expectation** that something will, is, or did happen, based on reasons such as a schedule, information, or a rule. 〜はずがない is a strong negative form: it **cannot possibly** be so because logic rules out that possibility. 〜はずだった conveys disappointment when reality differed from expectations.","nuance":"This is not a vague feeling, but an inference based on **logic or common sense**. Confidence is about 80%, lower than 〜に違いない. It is used in both speech and writing and is not overly formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"3分後","r":"さんぷんご","m":"in 3 minutes"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"はずです","r":"はずです","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This bus should arrive in three minutes.","scene":"Waiting for a bus while checking the schedule"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"はずがない","r":"はずがない","m":"cannot possibly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no way she would do such a thing.","scene":"Completely denying a rumor about a close friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-071||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"〜はずだ／はずがない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はずだ／はずがない","patternReading":"はずだ／はずがない","coreMeaning":"Should be (well-founded expectation); cannot be (strong negation)","slug":"forms","definition":"Verbs and adjectives come before はず in the **plain form**; nouns and ナA require の/な. はずがない replaces だ with がない; はずだった expresses the past.","nuance":"Pay attention to **N + の + はず**—learners often omit の. With ナ-adjectives, **な** is required.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"はずだ／はずがない","note":"行くはずだ・行かないはずだ・行ったはずだ"},{"lhs":"イA（普通形）","rhs":"はずだ／はずがない","note":"高いはずだ・安いはずがない"},{"lhs":"ナA語幹 + な","rhs":"はずだ／はずがない","note":"静かなはずだ"},{"lhs":"N + の","rhs":"はずだ／はずがない","note":"学生のはずだ — never say 学生はずだ"},{"lhs":"普通形（＋の）","rhs":"はずだった","note":"合格するはずだった／医者のはずだった"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"はずだ","r":"はずだ","m":"should be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He should still be a student.","scene":"Seeing him carry a schoolbag and guessing he is still a student"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"はずだ","r":"はずだ","m":"should be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There should be an exam today.","scene":"Remembering the exam listed on the calendar"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-071||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜はずだ／はずがない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はずだ／はずがない","patternReading":"はずだ／はずがない","coreMeaning":"Should be (well-founded expectation); cannot be (strong negation)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch distinguishes three situations: はずだ expresses a present or future expectation based on reasons; はずがない completely rejects a possibility; はずだった describes a past expectation that did not come true.","nuance":"はずだった often appears with のに to reveal **regret or disappointment**. 「そんなはずない！」 is a very natural reaction to unbelievable news.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"call"},{"t":"した","m":"called"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"はずだ","r":"はずだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She should know because I called her.","scene":"Telling a coworker there is no need to report it again"},{"tokens":[{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"はずだった","r":"はずだった","m":"was supposed to"},{"t":"のに","m":"but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"落ちて","r":"おちて","m":"failed"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was supposed to pass, but I failed.","scene":"Telling friends about failing the exam"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-071||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrasts","sub":"〜はずだ／はずがない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はずだ／はずがない","patternReading":"はずだ／はずがない","coreMeaning":"Should be (well-founded expectation); cannot be (strong negation)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch compares はず with easily confused patterns. The key differences concern **logic versus feeling, obligation versus expectation, and information source**.","nuance":"Learners often confuse はず and べき because both can be translated as 'should.' Remember: はず = what logic says is true; べき = what one ought to do.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"はず","r":"はず","m":"expectation"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That can't be true.","scene":"Objecting when someone says something unreasonable"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜に違いない","point":"はずだ is based on logic or a schedule (~80%); に違いない is based on a strong feeling of certainty (~95%). Example: 彼は来るはずだ — The schedule says he will come; 彼は来るに違いない — He is certain to come."},{"vs":"〜べきだ","point":"はずだ = the realistic expectation 'should'; べきだ = the moral obligation 'ought to.' Example: 彼は来るはずだ — He should come; 彼は来るべきだ — He ought to come."},{"vs":"〜わけがない","point":"はずがない rejects an EXPECTATION; わけがない rejects a REASON. Example: 彼が来るはずがない — He cannot possibly come; 彼が来るわけがない — There is no reason he would come."},{"vs":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","point":"はずだ is a personal inference from evidence; そうだ（伝聞） simply reports what was heard from someone else. Example: 明日は休みのはずだ — Tomorrow should be a day off; 明日は休みだそうだ — I hear tomorrow is a day off."}]}},"n3-071||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜はずだ／はずがない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はずだ／はずがない","patternReading":"はずだ／はずがない","coreMeaning":"Should be (well-founded expectation); cannot be (strong negation)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes when using はず. Remember: **nouns require の**, **はず is not for encouragement**, and it must be distinguished from べき and わけがない.","nuance":"はず expresses logic, not reassurance. To encourage someone, use きっと〜よ instead of はず.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく","m":"tell"},{"t":"はずがない","r":"はずがない","m":"cannot possibly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no way he would lie.","scene":"Defending a friend who is being suspected"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 医者はずだ。 → ✅ 医者のはずだ。 (Nouns require の)","❌ 彼は来るべきだ (when you mean 'should arrive') → ✅ 彼は来るはずだ; べきだ means 'ought to'","❌ 私はできるはずだ (self-encouragement) → ✅ 今の実力なら、できるはずだ (evidence-based); encouragement usually uses きっとできるよ","❌ 彼が来るわけがない (when you mean 'cannot be expected to come') → ✅ 彼が来るはずがない; わけがない emphasizes the reason"],"compare":null}},"n3-071||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜はずだ／はずがない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はずだ／はずがない","patternReading":"はずだ／はずがない","coreMeaning":"Should be (well-founded expectation); cannot be (strong negation)","slug":"memory","definition":"The character 筈 (はず) originally meant 'natural expectation' or 'common sense.' Imagine a ruler used to measure whether something fits common sense.","nuance":"This image helps you remember the core: はず is a tool of LOGIC, not emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-071||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜はずだ／はずがない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はずだ／はずがない","patternReading":"はずだ／はずがない","coreMeaning":"Should be (well-founded expectation); cannot be (strong negation)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently on exams and in conversation. Learn them as set expressions, especially 「そんなはずはない」.","nuance":"「そんなはずはない」 is a common reaction to surprising news, equivalent to 'No way!'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"10年","r":"じゅうねん","m":"10 years"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"のだから","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"うまい","m":"skilled"},{"t":"はずだ","r":"はずだ","m":"should be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After practicing for ten years, you should be good.","scene":"Explaining a friend's impressive skill"},{"tokens":[{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"はず","r":"はず","m":"expectation"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That cannot be true.","scene":"Rejecting something unbelievable"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-072||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけだ・わけがない・わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Meaning/reason — no wonder, impossible, must do","slug":"core","definition":"〜わけ comes from the noun 訳 (reason, grounds). Its three main meanings are: **わけだ** = draw a conclusion, “so that's why”; **わけがない** = completely deny something, “impossible”; **わけにはいかない** = cannot do something because of social or moral reasons. To identify the meaning, look at what follows わけ: だ, がない, or にはいかない.","nuance":"All three work in both spoken and written Japanese. わけだ often carries an “ah, I see” feeling. わけがない is emphatic and may show emotion. わけにはいかない expresses social responsibility and is often used about oneself.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"わけだ","r":"わけだ","m":"no wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He practices every day, so no wonder he is good.","scene":"Explaining why he is good after learning he practices often"},{"tokens":[{"t":"こんな"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can do"},{"t":"わけがない","r":"わけがない","m":"impossible"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A child could never solve a problem this difficult.","scene":"Doubting a child's ability"},{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"のだ"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行か","r":"いか","m":"go"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"must go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I made a promise, so I have to go.","scene":"Keeping a promise to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-072||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけだ・わけがない・わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Meaning/reason — no wonder, impossible, must do","slug":"forms","definition":"Use a plain-form clause before わけ. **With nouns and な-adjectives**, use な to connect: N/Aな + な + わけ. **わけにはいかない** only combines with verbs: dictionary form means you cannot do something, while ない-form means you cannot not do it (double negative → must do it).","nuance":"For polite speech, add です/ます at the end: 上手なわけですね. In formal writing, わけにはまいりません (humble form) may appear, but it is not required for the test.","structure":[{"lhs":"V・Aい 普通形","rhs":"わけだ／わけがない","note":"Verb plain form; i-adjective plain form (暑いわけだ...)"},{"lhs":"N・なA","rhs":"な + わけだ／わけがない","note":"な connects to だ; do not use だ"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"わけにはいかない","note":"Cannot do it for moral/social reasons"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"わけにはいかない","note":"Cannot NOT do it = must do it"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"知識","r":"ちしき","m":"knowledge"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"わけだ","r":"わけだ","m":"no wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since she is a doctor, no wonder she knows about medicine.","scene":"Hearing someone explain the reason"},{"tokens":[{"t":"こんなに"},{"t":"騒がしい","r":"さわがしい","m":"noisy"},{"t":"のに"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"わけがない","r":"わけがない","m":"impossible"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"With it this noisy, it can't possibly be quiet.","scene":"Objecting when someone says the room is quiet"},{"tokens":[{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"休む","r":"やすむ","m":"take off"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot morally"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have responsibilities, so I can't take the day off.","scene":"Explaining why you cannot miss work despite being tired"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-072||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけだ・わけがない・わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Meaning/reason — no wonder, impossible, must do","slug":"usage","definition":"**わけだ** uses a preceding fact as evidence and draws an obvious conclusion. **わけがない** strongly denies a possibility and rules it out completely. **わけにはいかない** is used when conscience, ethics, or society does not allow an action; with a negative verb, it means the action is required.","nuance":"わけだ has an explanatory “so that's how it is” tone; use です when speaking politely to a superior. わけがない can sound strong, so use care with older people. わけにはいかない is often used about oneself and shows a sense of group responsibility.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"１０年","r":"じゅうねん","m":"ten years"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住んで","r":"すんで","m":"live"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"わけだ","r":"わけだ","m":"no wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He lived in Japan for ten years, so no wonder his Japanese is good.","scene":"Explaining why a coworker's Japanese is good"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく"},{"t":"わけがない","r":"わけがない","m":"impossible"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no way he would lie.","scene":"Trusting a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"辞める","r":"やめる","m":"quit"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot morally"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't quit my job.","scene":"Considering resignation because of responsibilities"},{"tokens":[{"t":"試験前","r":"しけんまえ","m":"before an exam"},{"t":"なので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遊んで","r":"あそんで","m":"play"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot morally"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"With the exam coming up, I can't keep playing around.","scene":"Reminding yourself to study instead of play"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-072||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns contrasted","sub":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけだ・わけがない・わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Meaning/reason — no wonder, impossible, must do","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick distinction: **わけがない** differs from **はずがない** because はず expresses subjective expectation, while わけ expresses objective grounds, though they can sometimes substitute for each other. **わけにはいかない** has an ethical/social nuance, while **なければならない** expresses an objective obligation, **てはいけない** is an external prohibition, and **ざるを得ない** means being forced with no alternative.","nuance":"With superiors, なければならない can sound more objective; わけにはいかない may feel heavy. ざるを得ない is formal and often written.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"知ら","r":"しら","m":"know"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"わけがない","r":"わけがない","m":"impossible"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no way he doesn't know.","scene":"Rejecting the possibility that he is unaware"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜はずがない","point":"はずがない denies an expectation; わけがない denies a reason-based possibility. Often interchangeable: 知らないはずがない ≈ 知らないわけがない."},{"vs":"〜なければならない","point":"なければならない = duty/rule; わけにはいかない = moral/social restraint. 学校に行かなければならない (rule) vs 約束を破るわけにはいかない (morals)."},{"vs":"〜てはいけない","point":"てはいけない = an external rule or ban; わけにはいかない = conscience does not allow it. ここに入ってはいけない vs 一人で帰らせるわけにはいかない."},{"vs":"〜ざるを得ない","point":"ざるを得ない = forced with no alternative; わけにはいかない = duty/morals. 辞めざるを得ない (forced) vs 辞めるわけにはいかない (cannot due to responsibility)."}]}},"n3-072||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけだ・わけがない・わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Meaning/reason — no wonder, impossible, must do","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse the three forms because they all contain わけ. Remember: **わけだ** = conclusion, **わけがない** = reject a possibility, **わけにはいかない** = cannot act for moral reasons. Also, nouns and な-adjectives require な, and わけにはいかない is not used for ability.","nuance":"To distinguish わけがない from わけにはいかない, ask: “Is it impossible for something to happen—or can I not do it because of social reasons?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"休む","r":"やすむ","m":"take off"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot morally"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He has responsibilities, so he cannot take the day off.","scene":"Talking about a coworker who cannot miss work"}],"pitfalls":["❌ Mixing the 3 forms: 食べるわけだ (no wonder they eat) ≠ 食べるわけがない (impossible to eat) ≠ 食べるわけにはいかない (cannot eat for moral reasons)","❌ Forgetting な after Aな/N: 静かわけがない → ✅ 静かなわけがない","❌ Confusing わけがない and わけにはいかない: 休むわけがない (will not take off) ≠ 休むわけにはいかない (cannot take off due to responsibility)","❌ Using わけにはいかない for ability: 泳げないわけにはいかない ❌ → to say “cannot swim,” use 泳げるわけがない ✅"],"compare":null}},"n3-072||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけだ・わけがない・わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Meaning/reason — no wonder, impossible, must do","slug":"memory","definition":"The basic meaning of 訳 (わけ) is “reason” or “grounds.” Picture it this way: **わけだ** = “there is a reason” → no wonder; **わけがない** = “there is no reason” → impossible; **わけにはいかない** = “cannot follow that reasoning” → blocked by morals. Change what follows わけ, and the meaning changes.","nuance":"A simple memory hook is “reason.” だ confirms the reason, がない denies it, and にはいかない means you cannot follow it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-072||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜わけだ／わけがない／わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけだ・わけがない・わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Meaning/reason — no wonder, impossible, must do","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases include **道理で〜わけだ** (no wonder), **〜ないわけにはいかない** (cannot not do; must do), **どういうわけか** (for some reason), and **わけない** (easy; nothing difficult). Memorize them for natural usage.","nuance":"道理で〜わけだ is often used when you suddenly discover the cause. どういうわけか is very common in everyday conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"道理で","r":"どうりで","m":"no wonder"},{"t":"寒い","r":"さむい","m":"cold"},{"t":"わけだ","r":"わけだ","m":"no wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No wonder it's cold.","scene":"Seeing the window open and realizing why it is cold"},{"tokens":[{"t":"どういう"},{"t":"わけか"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やる気","r":"やるき","m":"motivation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出ない","r":"でない","m":"doesn't arise"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For some reason, I don't feel motivated today.","scene":"Sighing because you do not want to work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-073||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ものの · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledging then gently refuting, with mild regret)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ものの connects clauses by **acknowledging** the first clause and then **gently refuting it** in the second. It means “although…, but…,” with the second clause often **falling short of expectations** or **contrary to plans**, plus mild regret or quiet disappointment. This is a **formal written** pattern, unlike the blunt complaint of のに. The speaker **accepts reality** rather than loudly complaining.","nuance":"The tone is **objective and calm**: the speaker acknowledges that something was done or is true, but the result is incomplete. Compared with のに, ものの is **less emotional and more formal**; compared with けれど, it **more strongly highlights unmet expectations**. It often appears in **emails, reports, and essays**, and sometimes in conversation when a formal tone is appropriate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"3年","r":"さんねん","m":"3 years"},{"t":"勉強した","r":"べんきょうした","m":"studied"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"会話","r":"かいわ","m":"conversation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I have studied Japanese for three years, conversation is still difficult.","scene":"Assessing ability after three years of study"},{"tokens":[{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持っていった","r":"もっていった","m":"took along"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"一度も","r":"いちども","m":"not once"},{"t":"使わなかった","r":"つかわなかった","m":"did not use"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I took an umbrella, I did not use it even once.","scene":"Sunny and dry after carrying an umbrella all day"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-073||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜ものの · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledging then gently refuting, with mild regret)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch organizes the connecting forms of ものの. The most common is **Vた + ものの**, used when something was done but the result was unsatisfactory. With **nouns / な-adjectives**, use **である** or **だった** before ものの; avoid conversational “なものの” in standard written Japanese.","nuance":"This is a **formal** structure, usually following the plain form (普通形). The clause before ものの may be **affirmative or negative**, but it must contrast with what is expected from that clause.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ものの","note":"Did ~, but... (most common)"},{"lhs":"V普通形","rhs":"ものの","note":"Example: 行くとは言ったものの"},{"lhs":"Aい普通形","rhs":"ものの","note":"Example: 高いものの、品質がいい"},{"lhs":"N / Aな","rhs":"である・だった + ものの","note":"Do not use なものの in standard writing"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"品質","r":"ひんしつ","m":"quality"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is expensive, the quality is good.","scene":"Judging an expensive but worthwhile product"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"is"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"働いている","r":"はたらいている","m":"is working"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he is a student, he also works.","scene":"Introducing a friend who studies and works"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-073||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜ものの · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledging then gently refuting, with mild regret)","slug":"usage","definition":"The uses of ものの center on **two main situations**: (1) an action was performed but the result is **insufficient**; (2) something is true, but the result is **contrary to expectations**. In both cases, ものの focuses on the **contrast between effort and result**.","nuance":"The tone is **restrained and objective**, not loud or emotional. For strong dissatisfaction, use のに. For neutral contrast, use けれど. ものの suits **written language and reports**, or reflective speech to an older person or business partner.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"パソコン","r":"ぱそこん","m":"computer"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買った","r":"かった","m":"bought"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"使い方","r":"つかいかた","m":"how to use"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わからない","r":"わからない","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I bought a new computer, I do not know how to use it.","scene":"Just bought a laptop but cannot set it up"},{"tokens":[{"t":"大学","r":"だいがく","m":"university"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格した","r":"ごうかくした","m":"passed"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学費","r":"がくひ","m":"tuition"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"払えない","r":"はらえない","m":"cannot afford"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I was accepted to university, I cannot afford the tuition.","scene":"Facing hardship after receiving an acceptance letter"},{"tokens":[{"t":"申し込んだ","r":"もうしこんだ","m":"registered"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"結局","r":"けっきょく","m":"in the end"},{"t":"キャンセル","r":"きゃんせる","m":"cancel"},{"t":"してしまった","r":"してしまった","m":"ended up doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I registered, I ended up canceling.","scene":"Canceling a course after becoming busy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-073||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions: のに・けど・にもかかわらず","sub":"〜ものの · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledging then gently refuting, with mild regret)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ものの with easily confused “although” patterns: **のに**, **けれど**(も), **にもかかわらず**, and **とはいえ**. The key point is that ものの is both **formal** and **mildly regretful**, unlike the blunt のに and the neutral けれど.","nuance":"On a contrast scale: **けれど** is neutral, **のに** strongly expresses complaint, **ものの** is formal with quiet regret, and **にもかかわらず** is formal and emphasizes “despite.” とはいえ focuses more on **shifting the argument** than on describing a completed action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"転職した","r":"てんしょくした","m":"changed jobs"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"給料","r":"きゅうりょう","m":"salary"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","r":"あまり","m":"not much"},{"t":"変わらなかった","r":"かわらなかった","m":"did not change"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I changed jobs, my salary barely changed.","scene":"Mildly regretting a new job"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜のに","point":"のに = clear complaint/dissatisfaction in conversation; ものの = mild regret in writing. 勉強したのに失敗した (frustrated) vs 勉強したものの、失敗した (calm report)."},{"vs":"〜けれど(も)","point":"けれど = neutral contrast, without regret; ものの = stresses that the second clause defies expectations and is unfortunate."},{"vs":"〜にもかかわらず","point":"にもかかわらず = more formal and stresses “despite an obstacle”; ものの = milder, simply acknowledging then gently refuting."},{"vs":"〜とはいえ","point":"とはいえ = “even so,” shifting the argument; it is not tightly tied to a completed action, unlike ものの."}]}},"n3-073||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ものの · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledging then gently refuting, with mild regret)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using ものの in casual speech, making the second clause positive and expected, or forgetting である with nouns and な-adjectives.","nuance":"ものの is not grammatically wrong in conversation, but it sounds **stiff and bookish**. Japanese speakers usually choose けど/のに in everyday speech. A positive second clause can make the sentence meaningless because the required contrast disappears.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"留学した","r":"りゅうがくした","m":"studied abroad"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手く","r":"うまく","m":"well"},{"t":"話せない","r":"はなせない","m":"cannot speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I studied abroad, I still cannot speak Japanese well.","scene":"Reflecting after returning from studying abroad"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ご飯を食べたものの、まだお腹が空いている。 (with friends) → ✅ ご飯を食べたけど、まだお腹が空いている。","❌ 彼は学生なものの、働いている。 → ✅ 彼は学生であるものの、働いている。","❌ 試験に合格したものの、嬉しい。 → ✅ 試験に合格したものの、学費が払えない。 (the second clause must defy expectations)","❌ 私の趣味は写真を撮るものだ。 (confused with ものだ) → ✅ 写真を撮ったものの、うまく撮れなかった。"],"compare":null}},"n3-073||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜ものの · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledging then gently refuting, with mild regret)","slug":"memory","definition":"A visual memory aid: separate もの (= thing) + の (possession/explanation). “Although that thing is so…” → remember that the second clause contradicts the first. Imagine preparing carefully for a presentation but still running into trouble: “prepared ものの, still failed.”","nuance":"Do not remember ものの as a neutral “but”; link it to **a contrast with mild regret**. Mnemonic: “DID ~ YET ~”—“YET” means “but” and echoes the sound of の (no), helping you recall **ものの**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-073||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜ものの · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledging then gently refuting, with mild regret)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases with ものの in tests and business writing include **一応〜ものの** (did it for now, but) and **〜とはいうものの** (although that is what was said, but). In business emails, ご要望に応えたいとは思うものの… is used for a **polite refusal**.","nuance":"一応〜ものの expresses **humility**: “I did it, but it is still not good enough,” which fits Japanese workplace culture. とはいうものの concedes a point and then **shifts to the main point**, like “that said, however.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一応","r":"いちおう","m":"for now"},{"t":"完成した","r":"かんせいした","m":"completed"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"改善","r":"かいぜん","m":"improvement"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"余地","r":"よち","m":"room"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is complete for now, there is still room for improvement.","scene":"Humbly reporting project progress"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-074||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ざるを得ない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must reluctantly do — no choice (external pressure)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ざるを得ない describes a situation where someone must do something reluctantly because there is no other choice. The speaker is forced by external circumstances, not acting voluntarily. It is formal, carries a sense of reluctance, and often appears in writing and official statements.","nuance":"Register: formal/written; used in reports, speeches, and official notices, and less often in casual conversation. The pressure is very strong—there is no alternative. It often conveys annoyance, resignation, or dissatisfaction. In polite speech, it can become **〜ざるを得ません**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"上司","r":"じょうし","m":"boss"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"命令","r":"めいれい","m":"order"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"従わ","r":"したがわ","m":"obey"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it is my boss's order, I have no choice but to obey.","scene":"An employee complains to a coworker about an unpleasant order"},{"tokens":[{"t":"証拠","r":"しょうこ","m":"evidence"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"認め","r":"みとめ","m":"admit"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since there is evidence, I have no choice but to admit it.","scene":"A director is forced to admit a mistake in a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-074||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ざるを得ない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must reluctantly do — no choice (external pressure)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: **V-ない form, drop ない** + **ざるを得ない**. For group 1 (五段) and group 2 (一段) verbs, use the stem: 行く→行かざる, 食べる→食べざる. **する** changes to **せざる**, and **来る** changes to **こざる**—these are required exceptions. The negative form of **ざる** is **ない**, and **得ない** means “cannot”; together they mean “cannot not do.” Add **だった** for the past: ざるを得なかった.","nuance":"Formal, written style. Not used in casual conversation. The polite form is **ざるを得ません**. The past form **ざるを得なかった** is very common in narration.","structure":[{"lhs":"V-ない (drop ない)","rhs":"ざるを得ない","note":"Example: 行かざる, 食べざる"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"せざるを得ない","note":"Note: せ, not し"},{"lhs":"来る","rhs":"こざるを得ない","note":"Note: こ, not き"},{"lhs":"V-ざるを得ない","rhs":"→ ざるを得なかった","note":"Past: had to reluctantly"},{"lhs":"V-ざるを得ない","rhs":"→ ざるを得ません","note":"Polite"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まった","r":"とまった","m":"stopped"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歩か","r":"あるか","m":"walk"},{"t":"ざるを得なかった","r":"ざるをえなかった","m":"had to reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train stopped, so I had no choice but to walk.","scene":"Recounting being late because the train broke down"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ以上","r":"これいじょう","m":"any longer"},{"t":"待てない","r":"まてない","m":"cannot wait"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰ら","r":"かえら","m":"go home"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't wait any longer, so I have no choice but to go home.","scene":"Texting a friend because it is already too late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-074||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜ざるを得ない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must reluctantly do — no choice (external pressure)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when **circumstances force the action** and there is no other way. Common situations include objective causes such as rain or a broken train, difficult decisions such as selling assets or laying off staff, and admitting something you do not want to admit because there is evidence. It can refer to both the past and present.","nuance":"Emphasizes **reluctance** and **external pressure**. Do not use it for something you do voluntarily or enjoyably. In writing, it is often used to present a difficult decision politely.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"急用","r":"きゅうよう","m":"urgent matter"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できた","m":"came up"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"パーティー","m":"party"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"欠席","r":"けっせき","m":"be absent"},{"t":"せざるを得なかった","r":"せざるをえなかった","m":"had to reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"An urgent matter came up, so I had no choice but to miss the party.","scene":"Calling to apologize for missing a party"},{"tokens":[{"t":"借金","r":"しゃっきん","m":"debt"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"返す","r":"かえす","m":"repay"},{"t":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"precious"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"絵","r":"え","m":"painting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"売ら","r":"うら","m":"sell"},{"t":"ざるを得なかった","r":"ざるをえなかった","m":"had to reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"To repay my debt, I had no choice but to sell a precious painting.","scene":"Sharing that they had to sell a family heirloom"},{"tokens":[{"t":"予算","r":"よさん","m":"budget"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"足りない","r":"たりない","m":"be insufficient"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変更","r":"へんこう","m":"change"},{"t":"せざるを得ない","r":"せざるをえない","m":"must reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The budget is insufficient, so we have no choice but to change the plan.","scene":"An announcement at a company meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-074||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜ざるを得ない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must reluctantly do — no choice (external pressure)","slug":"compare","definition":"These patterns are often confused: **〜ざるを得ない**, **〜しかない**, **〜なければならない**, and **〜ないわけにはいかない**. The difference lies in the **source of pressure**: ざるを得ない = forced by circumstances, しかない = only option left, なければならない = rule or duty, ないわけにはいかない = moral or social responsibility.","nuance":"ざるを得ない is the most formal and emphasizes reluctance. しかない is conversational and less emotional. なければならない is neutral. ないわけにはいかない expresses a moral or social obligation—something you cannot avoid doing because it would be improper not to.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"帰ら","r":"かえら","m":"go home"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have no choice but to go home (I don't want to, but I must).","scene":"Wanting to stay but unable to"},{"tokens":[{"t":"帰る","r":"かえる","m":"go home"},{"t":"しかない","m":"only option"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is nothing to do but go home.","scene":"Calmly accepting reality"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜しかない","point":"しかない = there is only one way; it is less reluctant and works in speech. ざるを得ない = there is no choice, formal, and strongly reluctant. Example: 帰るしかない (only option is to go home) vs 帰らざるを得ない (must go home reluctantly)."},{"vs":"〜なければならない","point":"なければならない = a neutral duty or rule. ざるを得ない = forced by circumstances and emotionally reluctant. Example: 提出しなければならない (must submit due to a rule) vs 提出せざるを得ない (must submit despite not wanting to)."},{"vs":"〜ないわけにはいかない","point":"ないわけにはいかない = must do something because of morality or social responsibility. ざるを得ない = forced by objective circumstances. Example: 助けないわけにはいかない (must help out of conscience) vs 助けざるを得ない (forced to help)."}]}},"n3-074||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ざるを得ない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must reluctantly do — no choice (external pressure)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with 〜ざるを得ない include conjugating **する** incorrectly, using the pattern in overly casual contexts, or forgetting to change the verb to the negative form. Master the exceptions and check the level of formality.","nuance":"Incorrect verb conjugation is the most serious mistake and makes the sentence completely wrong. Used among friends, it sounds very stiff because it is overly formal. Use 〜しかない instead when appropriate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"上司","r":"じょうし","m":"boss"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"命令","r":"めいれい","m":"order"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"従わ","r":"したがわ","m":"obey"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have no choice but to obey my boss's order.","scene":"A properly formal sentence"}],"pitfalls":["❌ するざるを得ない → ✅ せざるを得ない (する changes to せ)","❌ 来る→きざるを得ない → ✅ 来る→こざるを得ない (来る changes to こ)","❌ お弁当を買わざるを得なかった (said to friends) → ✅ お弁当を買うしかなかった","❌ 大好きなケーキを食べざるを得ない (you enjoy it and choose it freely) → ✅ ざるを得ない is ONLY for genuine reluctance—say 食べたい for something enjoyable and voluntary"],"compare":null}},"n3-074||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜ざるを得ない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must reluctantly do — no choice (external pressure)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **ざる** is an old form of **ない** (not), and **得ない** means “cannot.” Together they mean **cannot not do** → must do. Imagine being backed into a corner with no way out, forced to go along with it.","nuance":"Do not worry about a Vietnamese sound association. Just remember the fixed phrase “ざるを得ない” = “must reluctantly”; do not overanalyze it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-074||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ざるを得ない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must reluctantly do — no choice (external pressure)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in exams and everyday materials: **〜せざるを得ない** (must do), **認めざるを得ない** (must admit), **変更せざるを得ない** (must change), and **〜ざるを得なかった** (had to do).","nuance":"認めざるを得ない is extremely common in debates and seminars, with a polite, tactful tone. 変更せざるを得ない is often used at work when announcing a change to a schedule or plan.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その点","r":"そのてん","m":"that point"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"認め","r":"みとめ","m":"admit"},{"t":"ざるを得ません","r":"ざるをえません","m":"must reluctantly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regarding that point, I have to admit it.","scene":"Politely agreeing in part during a seminar"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-075||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ことにする／ことになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにする／ことになる","patternReading":"ことにする／ことになる","coreMeaning":"ACTIVE choice / OBJECTIVE decision","slug":"core","definition":"〜ことにする describes a decision the speaker makes **personally** after considering the options. 〜ことになる describes a decision or arrangement that comes **from outside** (a company, circumstances, or a group). This pattern does not describe an event that has already happened; it describes a **chosen course of action**.","nuance":"ことにする is active and personal, often used as ことにした after a decision has been made. ことになる is objective and avoids saying directly “I decided,” so it sounds **more polite and modest** in announcements. The decision is fairly certain, stronger than つもりだ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ため","m":"for the sake of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"禁煙","r":"きんえん","m":"quit smoking"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことにした","r":"ことにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For my health, I decided to quit smoking.","scene":"Someone quits smoking after years of doing it."},{"tokens":[{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"転勤","r":"てんきん","m":"transfer"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことになった","r":"ことになった","m":"was decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting next month, I will be transferred to Osaka.","scene":"Receiving a notice from the company."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-075||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ことにする／ことになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにする／ことになる","patternReading":"ことにする／ことになる","coreMeaning":"ACTIVE choice / OBJECTIVE decision","slug":"forms","definition":"Both patterns attach **こと** after **V辞書形** or **Vない形**. The verb itself does not change tense; only する/なる changes.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. ことにしました / ことになりました sound more formal and are common at work and in announcements.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ことにする","note":"Decide to do something"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"ことにする","note":"Decide not to do something"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ことになる","note":"Be decided / become the case"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"ことになる","note":"Be decided not to do something"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことにした","r":"ことにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I decided to start exercising tomorrow.","scene":"Telling yourself what you will do."},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"企画","r":"きかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancel"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことになった","r":"ことになった","m":"was decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was decided at the meeting that the plan would be canceled.","scene":"Reporting the meeting’s outcome."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-075||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜ことにする／ことになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにする／ことになる","patternReading":"ことにする／ことになる","coreMeaning":"ACTIVE choice / OBJECTIVE decision","slug":"usage","definition":"Use ことにした for your own decisions, such as dieting, studying, or changing a habit. Use ことになった for something brought about by a company, organization, or circumstances, or when **announcing personal news** modestly.","nuance":"ことになりました is common in emails and internal announcements because it makes things sound like a shared arrangement rather than a personal imposition. ことにした sounds somewhat declarative and is less common in formal contexts.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"話し合い","r":"はなしあい","m":"discussion"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プロジェクト"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancel"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことになった","r":"ことになった","m":"was decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As a result of the discussion, it was decided to cancel the project.","scene":"Reporting in a meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"部署","r":"ぶしょ","m":"department"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働く","r":"はたらく","m":"work"},{"t":"ことになりました","r":"ことになりました","m":"was arranged"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting next month, I will be working in a new department.","scene":"An employee tells coworkers the news."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-075||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"〜ことにする／ことになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにする／ことになる","patternReading":"ことにする／ことになる","coreMeaning":"ACTIVE choice / OBJECTIVE decision","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ことにする (one-time decision), ことになる (decision made by others), ことにしている (intentional habit), and ことになっている (rule currently in effect).","nuance":"ことにしている and ことになっている both describe **ongoing states**, while ことにする/ことになる describe a change or decision. つもり is an uncertain intention. ようにする focuses on **repeated effort**, while ことにする only finalizes a decision.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"留学","r":"りゅうがく","m":"study abroad"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことにした","r":"ことにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"転勤","r":"てんきん","m":"transfer"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことになった","r":"ことになった","m":"was transferred"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She decided to study abroad. I, on the other hand, was transferred.","scene":"Two people discuss upcoming changes."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ことにしている","point":"ことにする=one-time decision; ことにしている=maintain as a habit. 毎朝走ることにした (decided) / 毎朝走ることにしている (keep doing it)"},{"vs":"〜ことになっている","point":"ことになった=just decided; ことになっている=existing rule/schedule. 会議は10時からになった (just set) / 会議は10時からになっている (scheduled beforehand)"},{"vs":"〜つもりだ","point":"つもり=an intention that may change; ことにする=a decision already made, with stronger commitment."},{"vs":"〜ようにする","point":"ようにする=make an effort repeatedly; ことにする=choose an option, without focusing on effort."}]}},"n3-075||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ことにする／ことになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにする／ことになる","patternReading":"ことにする／ことになる","coreMeaning":"ACTIVE choice / OBJECTIVE decision","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse する and なる because both can be translated as “decide.” Remember: **I decide → する**, **circumstances decide → なる**.","nuance":"Confusing する and なる does not make the sentence ungrammatical, but it completely changes the meaning and can cause misunderstandings at work: 辞めることにした = I quit; 辞めることになった = I was made to quit.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"wake up"},{"t":"ことにした","r":"ことにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I decided to wake up early in the morning.","scene":"Talking about a personal decision."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日運動することになった。 (when you mean you decided) → ✅ 毎日運動することにした。","❌ 結婚することにしました。 (sounds boastful in a happy announcement) → ✅ 結婚することになりました。","❌ お酒をやめないことになった。 (again confusing する/なる, this time in the negative) → ✅ お酒をやめないことにしました。","❌ 会議は9時からことになった。 (extra こと — 9時から is not a verb clause) → ✅ 会議は9時からになった。"],"compare":null}},"n3-075||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ことにする／ことになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにする／ことになる","patternReading":"ことにする／ことになる","coreMeaning":"ACTIVE choice / OBJECTIVE decision","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of する as an action caused by your own hands, and なる as a state that forms on its own. Thus “I decide” = ことにする, while “it becomes a decision from outside” = ことになる.","nuance":"Japanese speakers often use ことになる to **avoid** directly asserting “I decided,” so it sounds softer and more polite.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-075||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ことにする／ことになる · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにする／ことになる","patternReading":"ことにする／ことになる","coreMeaning":"ACTIVE choice / OBJECTIVE decision","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in JLPT questions and workplace Japanese. Memorize them with examples for quick recognition.","nuance":"ことになっている refers to a rule or schedule currently in effect, not a decision that was just made.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"午後","r":"ごご","m":"afternoon"},{"t":"2時","r":"にじ","m":"2 o’clock"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"行う","r":"おこなう","m":"hold"},{"t":"ことになっています","r":"ことになっています","m":"is scheduled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The meeting is scheduled to start at 2 p.m.","scene":"Looking at an established schedule."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-076||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ことにしている · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにしている","patternReading":"ことにしている","coreMeaning":"Setting a personal rule and consciously maintaining the habit","slug":"core","definition":"～ことにしている expresses a personal rule that the speaker has set and follows regularly. The main structure is V辞書形 / Vない形 ＋ ことにしている. Unlike ことにする (just deciding), this pattern adds 〜ている = continuing, so it describes a decision still being maintained now. In short: “I made this rule for myself, and it is still in effect.”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a moderately polite tone. It shows conscious will and a touch of pride in self-discipline. Compared with ようにしている, ことにしている is stronger: it is a “rule,” while ようにしている is an “effort.” Use ようにしています when speaking modestly to a doctor; use ことにしている when asserting your own rule.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"6時","r":"ろくじ","m":"6 o'clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"wake up"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I make it a rule to get up at 6 every morning.","scene":"Answering a coworker about waking early"},{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"go to bed"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"before"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"スマホ"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ない","r":"みない","m":"not look at"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I make it a rule not to look at my phone before bed.","scene":"Telling a friend about better sleep habits"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-076||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"〜ことにしている · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにしている","patternReading":"ことにしている","coreMeaning":"Setting a personal rule and consciously maintaining the habit","slug":"forms","definition":"There are two main forms: **V辞書形 ＋ ことにしている** (make it a rule to do) and **Vない ＋ ことにしている** (make it a rule not to do). Do not attach it directly to a noun; to use N, turn it into a verb phrase (e.g. 野菜 → 野菜を食べる). The past form **〜ことにしていた** means “used to maintain the rule” and often implies that it has stopped in the present. The polite form is **〜ことにしています**.","nuance":"For politeness, add ます at the end: ～ことにしています. For a decision that has been made but whose continuation is unclear, use ～ことにした. Never use ～ことにしている for an externally imposed rule; use ～ことになっている instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"＋ ことにしている","note":"Set a rule to ALWAYS do"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"＋ ことにしている","note":"Set a rule NOT to do"},{"lhs":"V辞書形/ない","rhs":"＋ ことにしていました（polite past）","note":"Used to maintain the rule"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"飲まない","r":"のまない","m":"not drink"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I make it a rule not to drink alcohol.","scene":"Refusing alcohol at a company party"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"単語","r":"たんご","m":"vocabulary"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"always"},{"t":"ノート"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"書く","r":"かく","m":"write"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I always write new words in my notebook.","scene":"Introducing my Japanese study method"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-076||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜ことにしている · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにしている","patternReading":"ことにしている","coreMeaning":"Setting a personal rule and consciously maintaining the habit","slug":"usage","definition":"Typical situations: ① **Health** — eating, sleeping, and exercising. ② **Study / work** — taking notes and replying to email on time. ③ **Self-restriction** — 〜ないことにしている is common for not checking SNS, not badmouthing people, or not drinking. ④ **Schedules** — deciding what to make time for each week or day.","nuance":"Do not use it for something imposed from outside. Because it implies initiative, use 〜ことになっている for company rules or laws. In conversation, ことにしている can sound firm and individualistic; ようにしている sounds softer and more modest.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ため","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For my health, I make it a rule to eat vegetables every day.","scene":"Answering a friend about my diet"},{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"メール","m":"email"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"24時間","r":"にじゅうよじかん","m":"24 hours"},{"t":"以内","r":"いない","m":"within"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"返信","r":"へんしん","m":"reply"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I make it a rule to reply to work emails within 24 hours.","scene":"Telling my supervisor how I work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-076||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Patterns often confused","sub":"〜ことにしている · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにしている","patternReading":"ことにしている","coreMeaning":"Setting a personal rule and consciously maintaining the habit","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare the patterns learners most often confuse: **〜ようにしている** emphasizes effort, which may be inconsistent; **〜ことになっている** refers to an external rule; **〜ことにする** is a one-time decision, without saying it is maintained; and **〜ている** neutrally describes a fact or habit. **〜ことにしている** combines an active decision with regular maintenance.","nuance":"When choosing a pattern, ask: Who made the rule? The speaker → ことにしている. A company or law → ことになっている. Is the speaker making an effort? → ようにしている. Is deliberate intention emphasized? → ことにしている. Is it simply a fact? → ている.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"走る","r":"はしる","m":"run"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"}],"vi":"I make it a rule to run every morning.","scene":"Asserting an established routine"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ようにしている","point":"ようにしている = making an effort, possibly unsuccessfully. ことにしている = setting and maintaining a rule. Example: 毎朝走るようにしている (I try to run) vs 毎朝走ることにしている (I make running my rule)."},{"vs":"〜ことになっている","point":"ことになっている = an external rule (company, law). ことにしている = a rule set by oneself. Example: 制服を着ることになっている (the school requires a uniform) vs 自分で制服を着ることにしている (I choose to wear one)."},{"vs":"〜ことにする","point":"ことにする = a decision just made (the starting point). ことにしている = that decision is being maintained. Example: 毎日走ることにした (I just decided to run daily) vs 毎日走ることにしている (I maintain that routine)."},{"vs":"〜ている","point":"ている neutrally describes a current habit, without showing intention. ことにしている adds “I set this rule earlier,” with a self-directed nuance. Example: 毎朝6時に起きている (fact) vs 毎朝6時に起きることにしている (self-imposed discipline)."}]}},"n3-076||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ことにしている · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにしている","patternReading":"ことにしている","coreMeaning":"Setting a personal rule and consciously maintaining the habit","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors: (1) Using **〜ことにした** for a habit that is still being maintained — without ~ている, it is wrong for this meaning. (2) Using **〜ことにしている** for a company or school rule — use **〜ことになっている**. (3) Using it for a natural change such as “the sky gradually getting dark” — this pattern requires deliberate action. (4) Forgetting the negative form **〜ないことにしている**, which is extremely common.","nuance":"Learners often translate “I always…” directly as いつも〜ている, but when emphasizing a self-imposed rule, use ことにしている. Do not add 毎日 redundantly when it is unnecessary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"走る","r":"はしる","m":"run"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I make it a rule to run every morning. (Correct—ongoing habit)","scene":"Correcting a learner's sentence"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎朝走ることにした。 → ✅ 毎朝走ることにしている。 (For an ongoing habit, use ている)","❌ この会社は9時から働くことにしている。 → ✅ この会社は9時から働くことになっている。 (Use ことになっている for company rules)","❌ 最近、日本語が上手になることにしている。 → ✅ 日本語が上手になるようにしている。 (Use ようにしている for natural change or effort, not ことにしている)"],"compare":null}},"n3-076||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"〜ことにしている · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにしている","patternReading":"ことにしている","coreMeaning":"Setting a personal rule and consciously maintaining the habit","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory aid: **ことにしている** = **ことにする** (decide) + **〜ている** (continuing). It means: “I decided once, and that decision is still in effect.” Imagine a notebook listing your personal rules, with a ✓ beside each one you are following.","nuance":"Speaking tip: when introducing your habits, add ことにしています to sound reliable. In a job interview, saying 毎日日本語を勉強することにしています sounds more disciplined than simply saying 勉強しています.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-076||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ことにしている · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ことにしている","patternReading":"ことにしている","coreMeaning":"Setting a personal rule and consciously maintaining the habit","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in tests and daily life: **毎日〜ことにしている** (I make it a rule to…), **〜ないことにしている** (a rule not to…), **健康のために〜ことにしている** (I do this for my health…), **ストレスをためないために〜ことにしている** (I do this to avoid stress…), and **〜には〜ことにしている** (for this matter, I usually…).","nuance":"These phrases often appear in N3 reading passages and conversations about habits. They show that the speaker is conscientious and reliable.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人の","r":"ひとの","m":"other people's"},{"t":"悪口","r":"わるぐち","m":"badmouthing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"言わない","r":"いわない","m":"not say"},{"t":"ことにしている","r":"ことにしている","m":"personal rule"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I make it a rule not to speak badly about other people.","scene":"Sharing a personal value with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-077||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜ものだ／ものではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものだ／ものではない","patternReading":"ものだ／ものではない","coreMeaning":"Common sense / inevitability; nostalgia; should not do (ものではない)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ものだ expresses a general rule or common truth of life: “that is how things naturally are.” It also recalls past habits (「～たものだ」), while the negative form ものではない means “should not do ~” according to common moral standards. This pattern often appears in advice and reflective speech.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a somewhat philosophical, experienced tone. たものだ recalls past habits warmly, often in conversations with close friends or older people telling stories. ものではない is gentler than てはいけない and presents a general moral principle.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人間","r":"にんげん","m":"human beings"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"誰でも","r":"だれでも","m":"everyone"},{"t":"間違える","r":"まちがえる","m":"make mistakes"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that is natural"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everyone makes mistakes; that is only natural.","scene":"Comforting a friend after a mistake"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"childhood"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"頃","r":"ころ","m":"time"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"often"},{"t":"川","r":"かわ","m":"river"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"泳いだ","r":"およいだ","m":"swam"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"used to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I was little, I used to swim in the river a lot.","scene":"Telling a friend about childhood"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-077||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & forms","sub":"〜ものだ／ものではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものだ／ものではない","patternReading":"ものだ／ものではない","coreMeaning":"Common sense / inevitability; nostalgia; should not do (ものではない)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern combines with the **dictionary form** (Vる), adjectives, and the **past form** (Vた). ものだ follows Vる for common truths and Vた for nostalgia. ものではない follows Vる to advise against an action. Note that な-adjectives require な before ものだ.","nuance":"Moderately formal and used in both writing and speech. たものだ often appears with the adverb よく (often).","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ものだ","note":"Common truth; natural consequence"},{"lhs":"V-た","rhs":"ものだ","note":"Nostalgic past habit"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ものではない","note":"Should not do (moral principle)"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"ものだ","note":"い-adjective"},{"lhs":"Aな + な","rhs":"ものだ","note":"な-adjective requires な"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"初めて","r":"はじめて","m":"first"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"緊張","r":"きんちょう","m":"feel nervous"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that is natural"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is natural to feel nervous during your first experience.","scene":"Encouraging someone starting a new job"},{"tokens":[{"t":"若い","r":"わかい","m":"young"},{"t":"頃","r":"ころ","m":"days"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"夜遅く","r":"よるおそく","m":"late at night"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"used to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I was young, I used to study until late at night.","scene":"An older employee telling a younger colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-077||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜ものだ／ものではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものだ／ものではない","patternReading":"ものだ／ものではない","coreMeaning":"Common sense / inevitability; nostalgia; should not do (ものではない)","slug":"usage","definition":"**Use 1**: State an obvious truth or general rule (Vる+ものだ). **Use 2**: Recall a past habit (Vた+ものだ), often with よく. **Use 3**: Use ものではない to advise that something should not be done, based on a general moral principle. **Use 4**: Combine it with よく to express surprise or admiration.","nuance":"With the common-truth meaning, the speaker shares a life lesson or generalization. With nostalgia, the tone is warm and wistful. ものではない is gentler than てはいけない but still carries a teaching or admonishing tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"過ぎる","r":"すぎる","m":"pass"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"早い","r":"はやい","m":"quick"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"naturally does"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only natural that time passes quickly.","scene":"Telling a friend how fast the year went by"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昔","r":"むかし","m":"long ago"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"よく"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"ここ"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊んだ","r":"あそんだ","m":"played"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"used to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I used to play here with my friends a lot.","scene":"Remembering the past while passing the old schoolyard"},{"tokens":[{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"傷つける","r":"きずつける","m":"hurt"},{"t":"ような"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"ものではない","r":"ものではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not say things that hurt other people.","scene":"A mother advising her child after a cruel remark"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-077||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜ものだ／ものではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものだ／ものではない","patternReading":"ものだ／ものではない","coreMeaning":"Common sense / inevitability; nostalgia; should not do (ものではない)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare these similar patterns to avoid confusion. ものだ expresses a **common truth**, べきだ a **personal duty**, わけだ a **logical conclusion**, ことだ **specific advice**, and ものがある a **subjective impression**.","nuance":"べきだ is subjective and can sound imposing. ものだ is objective and philosophical. わけだ is used when there is concrete evidence. ことだ commonly gives direct advice about a specific action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"冬","r":"ふゆ","m":"winter"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寒い","r":"さむい","m":"cold"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"naturally is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Winter is naturally cold.","scene":"Answering a child who asks why winter is cold"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜べきだ","point":"べきだ: “should do” based on personal duty or responsibility. ものだ: “that is how things are” as a general rule. Ex: 努力するべきだ (you should work hard) vs 努力すれば夢は叶うものだ (if you work hard, dreams come true – a general truth)."},{"vs":"〜わけだ","point":"わけだ: a logical conclusion from specific facts, “no wonder.” ものだ: a natural rule that needs no specific event. Ex: 時間が過ぎたわけだ (no wonder time passed) vs 時間は過ぎるものだ (time naturally passes)."},{"vs":"〜ことだ","point":"ことだ: direct, specific advice for the listener. ものだ: a general principle. Ex: 早く寝ることだ (you should go to bed early) vs 人間は早く起きるものだ (people should get up early – a general norm)."},{"vs":"〜ものがある","point":"ものがある: “there is something noteworthy,” expressing an impression. ものだ: “that is how it naturally is.” Ex: この映画には感動させるものがある (this film has something moving) vs 映画は面白いものだ (films are naturally interesting)."}]}},"n3-077||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ものだ／ものではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものだ／ものではない","patternReading":"ものだ／ものではない","coreMeaning":"Common sense / inevitability; nostalgia; should not do (ものではない)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often misuse ものだ for personal habits or forget な with な-adjectives. Remember: ものだ is for **general truths**, not isolated personal actions.","nuance":"Using it for a personal habit makes the sentence sound unnatural because Japanese speakers use ものだ for common truths, not an individual's private routine.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人間","r":"にんげん","m":"human beings"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"get up"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"naturally do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"People naturally get up early in the morning.","scene":"Discussing a general rule, not one person's habit"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は早く起きるものだ。 → ✅ 人間は朝早く起きるものだ。 (ものだ is for general truths, not personal habits)","❌ 都会は静かものだ。 → ✅ 都会は静かなものだ。 (Remember: Aな + な + ものだ)","❌ 学生の頃、よく勉強した。 → ✅ 学生の頃、よく勉強したものだ。 (Add ものだ to clearly express nostalgia)","❌ 嘘をつくものだ。 → ✅ 嘘をつくものではない。 (The negative form means 'should not lie'; the positive form changes the meaning)"],"compare":null}},"n3-077||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜ものだ／ものではない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ものだ／ものではない","patternReading":"ものだ／ものではない","coreMeaning":"Common sense / inevitability; nostalgia; should not do (ものではない)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember もの as “thing.” ものだ means “it is naturally this way in life.” Picture an old man nodding as he reflects on a rule of life.","nuance":"This mnemonic separates the three meanings: V-る+ものだ is a common truth, V-た+ものだ is nostalgia, and V-る+ものではない advises against an action.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-078||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜だけでなく／のみならず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だけでなく／のみならず","patternReading":"だけでなく／のみならず","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also ~ (expanding scope)","slug":"core","definition":"〜だけでなく means **not only A but also B**, emphasizing expansion to a second object or attribute. A is known, while B is added to make the judgment broader and more complete. **〜のみならず** is the formal equivalent.","nuance":"Neutral and additive. Unlike ばかりか, だけでなく does not stress surprise; it simply adds information. The second clause often uses **も** or **さえ**. In formal writing such as journalism or résumés, のみならず sounds professional.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"mind"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"smart"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"性格","r":"せいかく","m":"personality"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is not only smart but also kind-natured.","scene":"Praising a well-rounded friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大人","r":"おとな","m":"adults"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ゲーム","m":"game"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"楽しんでいる","r":"たのしんでいる","m":"are enjoying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Not only children but adults are enjoying this game too.","scene":"Discussing a popular game"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-078||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜だけでなく／のみならず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だけでなく／のみならず","patternReading":"だけでなく／のみならず","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also ~ (expanding scope)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **noun / plain form + だけでなく**, with **も** or **さえ** in the second clause. **〜のみならず** attaches directly after a noun or clause. **だけではなく** (with は) is slightly more polite and common in moderately formal writing.","nuance":"With na-adjectives, use **なだけでなく**; with nouns, use **N＋だけでなく**. Tense does not change the pattern; past time appears in the later verb. だけでなくて is also acceptable.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋だけでなく、Bも","note":"After a noun, commonly used with も"},{"lhs":"普通形 (V/Aい/Aな/N)","rhs":"＋だけでなく、Bも","note":"Aな → なだけでなく; N → Nだけでなく"},{"lhs":"N/普通形","rhs":"＋のみならず、Bも","note":"Formal written style"},{"lhs":"〜だけでなく（て）","rhs":"＋も","note":"Stronger emphasis; だけでなくて also works"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"頭痛","r":"ずつう","m":"headache"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"効く","r":"きく","m":"works for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This medicine works not only for headaches but also for fever.","scene":"Explaining medicine at a pharmacy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"優しい","r":"やさしい","m":"kind"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"できる","m":"is capable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is not only kind but also capable at work.","scene":"Evaluating a female colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-078||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜だけでなく／のみならず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だけでなく／のみならず","patternReading":"だけでなく／のみならず","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also ~ (expanding scope)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main uses: ① expanding abilities or qualities: A is good and B is good too; ② expanding the range of people: A (a smaller group) → B (a larger group); ③ expanding the field of impact from A to B. In every case, B is an **additional expansion** in the same direction as A.","nuance":"Useful in advertising, product descriptions, and positive evaluations. のみならず suits reports, presentations, and résumés. It is not normally used for criticism or negation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新製品","r":"しんせいひん","m":"new product"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"品質","r":"ひんしつ","m":"quality"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"high"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The new product is not only affordable but also high-quality.","scene":"Advertising a company product"},{"tokens":[{"t":"SNS","m":"social media"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"個人","r":"こじん","m":"individuals"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"企業","r":"きぎょう","m":"businesses"},{"t":"にとって","m":"for"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"重要","r":"じゅうよう","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ツール","m":"tool"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Social media is important not only to individuals but also to businesses.","scene":"Discussing social media's impact"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-078||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Easily confused patterns","sub":"〜だけでなく／のみならず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だけでなく／のみならず","patternReading":"だけでなく／のみならず","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also ~ (expanding scope)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare these patterns: **〜だけでなく** (most common and neutral), **〜ばかりか** (emphasizes a surprising or extreme B), and **〜のみならず** (formal and written). Also, **〜に加えて** means neutral “in addition,” while **〜はもちろん** means “A goes without saying,” which is different from “not only.”","nuance":"Choose だけでなく for neutral addition, ばかりか for a surprising B, and のみならず in formal writing. だけではなく is a slightly more polite variant with the same meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"中国語","r":"ちゅうごくご","m":"Chinese"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He can speak not only English but Chinese too.","scene":"Introducing a friend's language skills"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ばかりか","point":"ばかりか makes B more surprising or extreme; だけでなく simply adds information. E.g. 彼は遅刻するばかりか、嘘までついた (He not only was late but even lied)."},{"vs":"〜に加えて","point":"に加えて means neutral “in addition”; it lacks the “not only” scope contrast that だけでなく creates."},{"vs":"〜はもちろん","point":"はもちろん means “A goes without saying, and B too”; だけでなく means “go beyond A and add B.”"},{"vs":"〜のみならず","point":"Only the register differs: のみならず is more formal and written; its meaning is identical to だけでなく."}]}},"n3-078||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜だけでなく／のみならず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だけでなく／のみならず","patternReading":"だけでなく／のみならず","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also ~ (expanding scope)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with だけでなく include omitting **も/さえ** in the second clause, pairing opposite-valued clauses, using **のみならず** in casual conversation, or making the second clause negative.","nuance":"Japanese listeners expect **も/さえ** in the second clause; without it, the sentence can sound unfinished. A and B should point in the same direction, both positive or both negative.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"お店","r":"おみせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ","m":"taste"},{"t":"だけでなく","r":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is not only tasty but also inexpensive.","scene":"Recommending a good restaurant"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 英語だけでなく、中国語を話す。 → ✅ 英語だけでなく、中国語も話す。","❌ このレストランは安いだけでなく、まずい。 → ✅ Same direction: 安いだけでなく、おいしい。","❌ 彼女は優しいだけでなく、仕事もできない。 → ✅ Positive second clause: 優しいだけでなく、仕事もできる。","❌ 日本語のみならず、英語を勉強している。 → ✅ のみならず still needs も: 日本語のみならず、英語も勉強している。"],"compare":null}},"n3-078||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜だけでなく／のみならず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だけでなく／のみならず","patternReading":"だけでなく／のみならず","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also ~ (expanding scope)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick tip: **だけ = only**, **でなく = not**, together meaning “not only.” Picture the scope expanding from A to include B. Mnemonic: “Not only ~ but ALSO ~.”","nuance":"Formality rises as: だけでなく → ばかりか → のみならず. Remember: ばかりか makes B a shock; のみならず sounds bookish.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-078||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"〜だけでなく／のみならず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜だけでなく／のみならず","patternReading":"だけでなく／のみならず","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also ~ (expanding scope)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases in exams and daily life include: **〜だけでなく、〜も** / **〜だけでなく（て）、〜さえ** / **〜のみならず、〜も** / the variant **〜だけではなく**.","nuance":"In résumés, use: 国内のみならず、海外でも…; in restaurants: 味だけでなく、値段も….","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issue"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"みならず","r":"みならず","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"世界中","r":"せかいじゅう","m":"worldwide"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"でもある","r":"でもある","m":"is also"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This issue is not only Japan's but a problem worldwide as well.","scene":"Discussing a global issue"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-079||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく","patternReading":"ばかりか・ばかりでなく","coreMeaning":"Not only X, but even Y (Y is more surprising/extreme)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく means **“not only X, but even Y,”** where Y is always **more surprising, extreme, or unexpected** than X. The pattern conveys the speaker’s emotion and emphasizes escalation rather than neutral listing. It works for both positive and negative situations.","nuance":"Both ばかりか and ばかりでなく are used in speech and writing, but **ばかりか** expresses stronger surprise, while **ばかりでなく** is more neutral and closer to だけでなく. In conversation, ばかりか sounds more “shocking” and often pairs with **まで／さえ** in the second clause: *AばかりかBまで*. Do not use it when Y is merely ordinary additional information.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"遅刻する","r":"ちこくする","m":"be late"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"ついた","r":"ついた","m":"told"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He was not only late, but also told a lie.","scene":"Hearing about a coworker who was late and made excuses"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"美しい","r":"うつくしい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"smart"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is not only beautiful, but also intelligent.","scene":"Praising a female friend in front of friends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-079||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく","patternReading":"ばかりか・ばかりでなく","coreMeaning":"Not only X, but even Y (Y is more surprising/extreme)","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: **plain form + ばかりか／ばかりでなく**. Nouns connect directly: **N + ばかりか**. With na-adjectives, use **な + ばかりか**: 静かなばかりか. **ばかりでなく** has the same meaning and is more common in conversation. The second clause often combines with **まで／さえ** to emphasize escalation.","nuance":"This pattern sits **between spoken and written styles**. ばかりか is slightly formal and emphatic; ばかりでなく sounds natural and is closer to だけでなく. With nouns, no extra particle is needed: 英語ばかりか, 大雨ばかりか.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（plain form）","rhs":"ばかりか／ばかりでなく","note":"Vる・Vた・Vない are all possible: 遅刻するばかりか、雨が降るばかりでなく"},{"lhs":"i-adj（plain form）","rhs":"ばかりか／ばかりでなく","note":"美しいばかりか、軽いばかりか"},{"lhs":"na-adj + な","rhs":"ばかりか／ばかりでなく","note":"静かなばかりか、きれいなばかりか"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"ばかりか／ばかりでなく","note":"大雨ばかりか、英語ばかりでなく"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"ばかりでなく","r":"ばかりでなく","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雷","r":"かみなり","m":"thunder"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"鳴った","r":"なった","m":"rumbled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was not only raining; there was thunder too.","scene":"Describing unusual rain"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"失った","r":"うしなった","m":"lost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He not only had no money, but also lost his job.","scene":"Talking about someone facing compounded difficulties"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-079||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく","patternReading":"ばかりか・ばかりでなく","coreMeaning":"Not only X, but even Y (Y is more surprising/extreme)","slug":"usage","definition":"Remember: **Y must be surprisingly “extra,”** not ordinary information. Two main patterns are **negative escalation** (late → lie → run away) and **positive escalation** (pretty → smart; light → great battery). In conversation, ばかりでなく is more common; ばかりか feels stronger and often appears in commentary or criticism to stress severity.","nuance":"Do not use this when Y is ordinary or predictable. ばかりか usually appears in **emotional situations**—surprise, criticism, or exceptional praise. For polite, formal listing, だけでなく or のみならず is more suitable.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しない","r":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"同僚","r":"どうりょう","m":"coworkers"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"邪魔","r":"じゃま","m":"interference"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He not only fails to work, but even gets in his coworkers’ way.","scene":"An employee is criticized in a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"スマホ","r":"スマホ","m":"smartphone"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"軽い","r":"かるい","m":"light"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"バッテリー","r":"バッテリー","m":"battery"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"持ち","r":"もち","m":"life"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"格段","r":"かくだん","m":"far"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"良くなった","r":"よくなった","m":"improved"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The new smartphone is not only lighter; its battery life is also much better.","scene":"Telling a friend about a new phone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-079||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing similar patterns","sub":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく","patternReading":"ばかりか・ばかりでなく","coreMeaning":"Not only X, but even Y (Y is more surprising/extreme)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch compares 〜ばかりか with easily confused patterns. The key difference lies in **emotional nuance and the direction of addition**.","nuance":"In formal writing, のみならず often replaces ばかりでなく. In everyday conversation, だけでなく is more common because it is neutral and does not suggest exaggeration.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ピアノ","r":"ピアノ","m":"piano"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"弾ける","r":"ひける","m":"can play"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プロ並み","r":"プロなみ","m":"professional-level"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"歌","r":"うた","m":"singing"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"上手い","r":"うまい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She can not only play piano, but also sing at a professional level.","scene":"Describing a talented friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜だけでなく","point":"だけでなく: Y is ordinary added information. ばかりか: Y must be more surprising or extreme. Example: 英語だけでなく日本語も話せる (ordinary) vs 英語が話せるばかりか、翻訳もできる (impressive)"},{"vs":"〜に加えて","point":"に加えて: neutral accumulation, often used for listing facts. ばかりか: emphasizes surprising superiority. Example: 資格に加えて経験もある (listing) vs 美しいばかりか頭もいい (surprising)"},{"vs":"〜どころか","point":"どころか: denies A and presents opposite or stronger B. ばかりか: A is true and B adds an even stronger point. Example: お金がないどころか、借金もある (no money, also in debt) vs お金がないばかりか、仕事も失った (no money, also lost job)"},{"vs":"〜のみならず","point":"のみならず: formal, written, and less emotional. ばかりか: works in speech and writing and has an exclamatory tone. Example: 国内のみならず海外でも (report) vs 遅刻するばかりか、嘘までつく (complaint)"}]}},"n3-079||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく","patternReading":"ばかりか・ばかりでなく","coreMeaning":"Not only X, but even Y (Y is more surprising/extreme)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A common mistake for learners is using ばかりか when Y is merely ordinary added information, or using it when A and B contradict each other. Always remember: **Y must be surprising and stronger than X**.","nuance":"When the sentence expresses contrast (“far from A, actually B”), use どころか. When you are simply adding information, だけでなく sounds much more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大雨","r":"おおあめ","m":"heavy rain"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"洪水","r":"こうずい","m":"flood"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"起きた","r":"おきた","m":"occurred"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There was not only heavy rain, but even flooding.","scene":"Discussing disaster after heavy rain"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 英語ばかりか日本語も話せる → ✅ 英語だけでなく日本語も話せる (speaking both is ordinary, not surprising)","❌ お金がないばかりか、貯金もある → ✅ お金がないどころか、貯金もある (A “has no money” is denied; in fact, there is money)","❌ 頭がいいばかりか、靴もきれいだ → ✅ 頭がいいばかりか、スポーツもできる (B should be another valuable quality)","❌ 彼は優しいばかりか、給料も安い (A good, B bad: wrong direction) → ✅ 彼は優しいばかりか、仕事も丁寧だ (same positive direction)"],"compare":null}},"n3-079||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく","patternReading":"ばかりか・ばかりでなく","coreMeaning":"Not only X, but even Y (Y is more surprising/extreme)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **an escalating staircase**: A is the starting step, while B is much higher and makes you exclaim, “Wow!” ばかりか feels like **“not only that—there’s even more!”**.","nuance":"Unlike the dry だけでなく, ばかりか has emphatic rhythm and sounds like you are recounting something extraordinary. When remembering this pattern, always ask: **Is B really surprising?**","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-079||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜ばかりか／ばかりでなく","patternReading":"ばかりか・ばかりでなく","coreMeaning":"Not only X, but even Y (Y is more surprising/extreme)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common collocations in tests and daily life include **〜たばかりか、〜も**, **〜ばかりか、〜まで／さえ**, and **〜ばかりでなく、〜も**. These combinations help you recognize the pattern quickly.","nuance":"When the second clause contains まで or さえ, ばかりか is likely being used to emphasize escalation. ばかりでなく often pairs with も in the second clause: 英語ばかりでなく、フランス語も.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"遅刻する","r":"ちこくする","m":"be late"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議中","r":"かいぎちゅう","m":"during the meeting"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"even did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He not only arrived late, but even slept during the meeting.","scene":"Criticizing an irresponsible coworker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-080||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜にかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~ (condition has no effect)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にかかわらず means **regardless of any condition**: the result or decision **does not change**. The speaker emphasizes that the stated condition **has no effect** on what follows. It often appears with **opposing pairs** (ある/なし, 男女) or yes/no expressions.","nuance":"Formal and objective, mainly used in **written language**: notices, rules, and recruitment ads. It is uncommon in daily conversation; in casual speech, Japanese speakers use **〜ても**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"性別","r":"せいべつ","m":"gender"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"でも"},{"t":"応募","r":"おうぼ","m":"apply"},{"t":"できます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of gender, anyone can apply.","scene":"Company recruitment notice"},{"tokens":[{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"イベント","m":"event"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"開催","r":"かいさい","m":"hold"},{"t":"します","m":"will"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of the weather, the event will be held.","scene":"Staff announcing an outdoor event"},{"tokens":[{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"有無","r":"うむ","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご連絡","r":"ごれんらく","m":"contact us"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whether you have experience or not, please contact us.","scene":"Recruitment advertisement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-080||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"〜にかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~ (condition has no effect)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern follows a **noun** or an interrogative clause using **かどうか**. With a simple noun, that noun should suggest an **opposing pair** or combine with **〜の有無** to mean “whether or not.”","nuance":"The following clause is usually an **assertion** expressing an unchanged decision or action. Avoid using it for weak personal intentions.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にかかわらず","note":"condition noun, often an opposing pair: 男女, 年齢, 天候"},{"lhs":"V辞書形 + かどうか","rhs":"にかかわらず","note":"regardless of whether someone does V"},{"lhs":"Nの有無","rhs":"にかかわらず","note":"regardless of whether N exists"},{"lhs":"N1/N2 (opposing pair)","rhs":"にかかわらず","note":"regardless of whether it is N1 or N2"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participate"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whether you attend or not, please contact us.","scene":"Class meeting notice"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"食前","r":"しょくぜん","m":"before meals"},{"t":"・"},{"t":"食後","r":"しょくご","m":"after meals"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"服用","r":"ふくよう","m":"take"},{"t":"できます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This medicine can be taken before or after meals.","scene":"Instructions on a medicine leaflet"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-080||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜にかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~ (condition has no effect)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears in many **formal situations**: social conditions such as nationality, age, and gender; specific conditions such as results and participation; or timing such as before/after meals. The following clause is an action or decision applied **equally**.","nuance":"Formal and objective, commonly used in **notices, rules, and recruitment**. Do not use it in casual conversation with friends; use **〜ても** instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"国籍","r":"こくせき","m":"nationality"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"すべて","m":"all"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"平等","r":"びょうどう","m":"equal"},{"t":"です","m":"are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of nationality, everyone is equal.","scene":"Human rights article"},{"tokens":[{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"end"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"頑張る","r":"がんばる","m":"keep trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of the result, I will keep trying to the end.","scene":"Self-talk before an exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"年齢","r":"ねんれい","m":"age"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participate"},{"t":"できます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of age, everyone can participate.","scene":"Fitness class notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-080||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with にもかかわらず & を問わず","sub":"〜にかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~ (condition has no effect)","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick distinction: **にかかわらず** = regardless; the condition is unimportant. **にもかかわらず** = although; the condition contrasts with the result. **を問わず** = without distinction, often with listed opposing nouns.","nuance":"All three are formal, while **〜ても** is more casual. **を問わず** sounds more like a classification, often following opposing nouns such as 男女, 年齢, and 国籍.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"有無","r":"うむ","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"採用","r":"さいよう","m":"hire"},{"t":"します","m":"will"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of whether you have experience, we will hire you.","scene":"Recruitment notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にもかかわらず","point":"〜にかかわらず = “regardless” (the condition is unimportant); 〜にもかかわらず = “although” (there is an unexpected contrast). Example: 天気にかかわらず開催する (hold it regardless of weather) vs 雨にもかかわらず開催した (held it although it rained)."},{"vs":"〜を問わず","point":"〜を問わず is close in meaning to にかかわらず but usually follows specific opposing or category nouns (男女・年齢・国籍). にかかわらず is more flexible and can follow V + かどうか (whether or not)."},{"vs":"〜ても","point":"〜ても is conversational and casual; にかかわらず is formal written language. Example: 雨でも行く (I’ll go even if it rains) vs 天候にかかわらず行う."}]}},"n3-080||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes & traps","sub":"〜にかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~ (condition has no effect)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include **confusing it with にもかかわらず**, **using a simple noun without an opposing pair**, **confusing it with にかかわる**, and **overusing it in conversation**.","nuance":"These mistakes can change the meaning or make the context unnatural. When you see “although,” remember the **も**; for “regardless,” there is no も.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天候","r":"てんこう","m":"weather"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"行います","r":"おこないます","m":"hold"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of the weather, the match will be held.","scene":"Match schedule notice"}],"pitfalls":["❌ Confusing にかかわらず with にもかかわらず → ✅ 〜にもかかわらず has も and marks a CONTRAST; 〜にかかわらず has no も and says the condition has NO EFFECT.","❌ Using a simple noun (e.g. 病気にかかわらず) → ✅ Use an opposing pair or yes/no form: 病気の有無にかかわらず.","❌ Confusing にかかわらず (regardless) with にかかわる (related to) → ✅ にかかわる is a verb meaning “related to / important to” (命にかかわる問題); にかかわらず is the grammar pattern “regardless.”","❌ Using it with friends: 明日の天気にかかわらず行く → ✅ In conversation, use 雨でも行くよ or 雨だろうと行くよ."],"compare":null}},"n3-080||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜にかかわらず · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~ (condition has no effect)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **かかわる** means “be related to / be bound by.” **かかわらず** is its negative form, meaning “**not related to / not bound by** the condition.” Think of **rolling dice**: whether you roll a 1 or a 6, you still move forward—the result does not change.","nuance":"This helps distinguish it from にもかかわらず: も adds a contrastive “also/even,” while the form without も simply expresses an unconditional result.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-081||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜どころか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only is X untrue, but Y is the complete opposite","slug":"core","definition":"〜どころか completely denies what comes first (X), then presents a contrary or more extreme reality (Y). It means “not only is X untrue, but Y.” The speaker rejects the expectation of X and emphasizes the striking reality of Y.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. It has a strong tone, often showing surprise, criticism, or praise. Y must go in the opposite direction from X—better or worse are both possible. It is not used to list things in the same direction.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ダイエット","m":"dieting"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not only not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"逆に","r":"ぎゃくに","m":"instead"},{"t":"太って","r":"ふとって","m":"gain weight"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I not only failed to lose weight, but actually gained weight.","scene":"A friend complains after gaining weight during a week of dieting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"親切","r":"しんせつ","m":"kind"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not only not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"冷たい","r":"つめたい","m":"cold"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She was not kind at all; she was actually a cold person.","scene":"Describing a new coworker who contradicted the first impression"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-081||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"〜どころか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only is X untrue, but Y is the complete opposite","slug":"forms","definition":"〜どころか attaches directly to a noun, a plain-form verb, or an い-adjective/な-adjective. No extra conjugation is needed. The first part, X, is completely denied; the second part, Y, is an opposite reality or exceeds expectations.","nuance":"This is a neutral structure—not especially formal or casual. It can be used in writing, journalism, and conversation.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 英語どころか (let alone English)"},{"lhs":"Vる / Vない","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 泳ぐどころか (let alone swimming)"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 暑いどころか (far from hot)"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 簡単どころか (far from easy)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"簡単","r":"かんたん","m":"easy"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not only not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is not easy at all; it is very difficult.","scene":"Answering about a new assignment rumored to be easy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"休む","r":"やすむ","m":"take a break"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not only not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増えた","r":"ふえた","m":"increased"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Far from getting a break, I ended up with even more work.","scene":"An employee complains during the lunch break"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-081||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜どころか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only is X untrue, but Y is the complete opposite","slug":"usage","definition":"〜どころか has two main uses: (1) rejecting an expectation—X is not true, and even Y, often a lower standard, is not true; (2) reversing the situation—not only is X untrue, but Y is the opposite. むしろ can emphasize the reversal. どころではない is a related form meaning “there is no time for ~.”","nuance":"In conversation, どころか often counters a claim or comparison. With むしろ, the sentence means “strictly speaking” and makes the contrast clearer. どころではない is associated with urgent or busy situations that leave no room for X.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"テスト","m":"test"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"100点","r":"ひゃくてん","m":"100 points"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not only not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"合格点","r":"ごうかくてん","m":"passing score"},{"t":"さえ","m":"even"},{"t":"取れなかった","r":"とれなかった","m":"could not get"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Forget 100 points—I could not even get a passing score.","scene":"Telling someone about a disastrous test result"},{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しくて","r":"いそがしくて","m":"being busy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"どころではない","r":"どころではない","m":"no time for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am too busy; this is no time for a trip.","scene":"Turning down an invitation for a weekend outing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-081||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜どころか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only is X untrue, but Y is the complete opposite","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes どころか from similar patterns with related meanings. Each comparison highlights one key difference.","nuance":"These comparisons help prevent errors in reading and conversation. Focus on two axes: same direction (ばかりか) versus opposite direction (どころか), and complete denial versus an unexpected result (のに).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"謝る","r":"あやまる","m":"apologize"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not only not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"開き直って","r":"ひらきなおって","m":"be defiant"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He not only failed to apologize, but became defiant instead.","scene":"Describing someone who acted defiantly after making a mistake"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ばかりか","point":"ばかりか: Y goes in the same direction and adds information. どころか: Y goes in the opposite direction and denies X. Example: 休むどころか働いた (I did not rest; I worked) vs 休むばかりか文句も言った (not only rested but also complained)."},{"vs":"〜のに","point":"のに: shows an unexpected result without denying the preceding clause. どころか: completely denies the preceding clause. Example: 勉強したのに落ちた (I studied but failed) vs 合格どころか不合格だった (far from passing, I failed)."},{"vs":"〜どころではない","point":"どころではない: means “there is no time for X” because circumstances are busy or urgent. どころか: denies X and replaces it with opposite Y. Example: 遊ぶどころではない (no time to play) vs 遊ぶどころか勉強している (not playing but studying)."},{"vs":"〜むしろ","point":"むしろ: lightly rejects one option and suggests another, “rather.” どころか: absolutely denies X. Example: 暑いというより、むしろ寒い (rather than hot, it is cold) vs 暑いどころか寒い (far from hot, it is cold)."}]}},"n3-081||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜どころか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only is X untrue, but Y is the complete opposite","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A common mistake is using どころか to mean “not only X but also Y” when Y goes in the same direction as X or is simply higher. Make sure Y is opposite or clearly lower.","nuance":"If Y is more positive than X, Japanese can still use this pattern when there is a contrast: X is the expectation, while Y is the better reality. Example: まずまずどころか、すばらしい (far from merely okay, it is excellent).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not to mention"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ひらがな","m":"hiragana"},{"t":"も","m":"even"},{"t":"読めない","r":"よめない","m":"cannot read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cannot even read hiragana, let alone kanji.","scene":"A beginner assesses their Japanese ability"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 英語どころか日本語も話せる → ✅ 英語どころか、日本語も話せない (I cannot speak Japanese either, let alone English). Y must be opposite/lower.","❌ ひらがなどころか、漢字も読めない → ❌ Awkward because kanji is more advanced than hiragana. → ✅ 漢字どころか、ひらがなも読めない.","❌ 彼は優しいどころか、親切だ → ❌ Wrong because the meanings point in the same direction. → ✅ 彼は親切どころか、冷たい人だ.","❌ お金があるどころか、たくさんある → ❌ Wrong because the meanings point in the same direction. → ✅ お金があるどころか、借金だらけだ."],"compare":null}},"n3-081||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜どころか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only is X untrue, but Y is the complete opposite","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: imagine どころか as “forget reaching level X; reality is much worse or better.” どころ (所) means “place” or “level,” so the pattern means “don’t even talk about the level or issue of X.”","nuance":"This tip is based on the original meaning of どころ (place, level). Remembering “forget X” helps you look for an opposite or lower Y and avoid using two items in the same direction. Picture a descending scale: X is too high to reach, and even lower Y is impossible, or reality has reversed completely.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-081||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜どころか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only is X untrue, but Y is the complete opposite","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions in exams and conversation: (1) 〜どころか〜も: emphasizes extreme denial; (2) それどころか: sentence connector meaning “on the contrary”; (3) 〜どころではない: no time for ~; (4) AどころかむしろB: a clear reversal.","nuance":"それどころか often begins a sentence and connects to the previous idea to add an opposite fact. These expressions are worth memorizing for quick recognition in reading passages.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"not only not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝て","r":"ねて","m":"sleep"},{"t":"ばかり","m":"only"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He not only fails to study; he does nothing but sleep.","scene":"A teacher complains about a lazy student"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-082||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜にすぎない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にすぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"It is only ~, nothing more (downplaying, modesty)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にすぎない means **'it is only ~, nothing more.'** The speaker deliberately **downplays** the importance of something and stresses that it **does not exceed** the stated limit. This is a subjective evaluation, not an objective fact.","nuance":"Formal and objective; used in writing and polite speech. Often expresses **modesty**, lowering oneself to avoid boasting. Compared with neutral だけだ, にすぎない carries the sense **'don't regard it as more than that.'**","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I; my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意見","r":"いけん","m":"opinion"},{"t":"にすぎません","r":"にすぎません","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is only my opinion.","scene":"Modestly sharing a personal view in a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he; him"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is still only a child.","scene":"Mentioning a younger brother; do not expect maturity"},{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"第一歩","r":"だいいっぽ","m":"first step"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Failure is only the first step toward success.","scene":"Encouraging someone after a failed experiment"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-082||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜にすぎない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にすぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"It is only ~, nothing more (downplaying, modesty)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: a **noun** or a **plain-form clause** comes before にすぎない. With a な-adjective, keep な when it modifies the following noun. The polite form is にすぎません, and the past form is にすぎなかった.","nuance":"In polite speech, change only the ending: にすぎない → にすぎません. The preceding clause stays in plain form; do not add です／ます. The past form にすぎなかった emphasizes 'it was only… back then.'","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋にすぎない","note":"Example: 意見にすぎない"},{"lhs":"V / いA 普通形","rhs":"＋にすぎない","note":"Example: 子供が遊んでいるにすぎない"},{"lhs":"なA な ＋ N","rhs":"＋にすぎない","note":"Example: 彼はただの学生にすぎない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"のところ","r":"のところ","m":"at present"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"仮説","r":"かせつ","m":"hypothesis"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For now, it is only a hypothesis.","scene":"In a scientific presentation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he; him"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"words"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"冗談","r":"じょうだん","m":"joke"},{"t":"にすぎなかった","r":"にすぎなかった","m":"was only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His words were only a joke.","scene":"Looking back; it turned out he was not serious"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-082||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜にすぎない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にすぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"It is only ~, nothing more (downplaying, modesty)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are two main uses: (1) **modestly downplaying** oneself or one's product; and (2) **limiting the scope** of something, stressing that it is only a small part, not the whole. In academic contexts, it can say 'this is only temporary data/stage.'","nuance":"The tone is **objective and formal**. Avoid it in very casual conversations among friends. When referring to someone else, careless use may sound **condescending or dismissive**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I; me"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"普通","r":"ふつう","m":"ordinary"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"サラリーマン","r":"サラリーマン","m":"office worker"},{"t":"にすぎません","r":"にすぎません","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am only an ordinary office worker.","scene":"Introducing oneself modestly at a gathering"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"参考","r":"さんこう","m":"reference"},{"t":"にすぎません","r":"にすぎません","m":"only"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご覧","r":"ごらん","m":"look"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is only for reference, but please take a look.","scene":"Introducing material to a client"},{"tokens":[{"t":"数字","r":"すうじ","m":"numbers"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あくまで","r":"あくまで","m":"merely"},{"t":"参考","r":"さんこう","m":"reference"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"内容","r":"ないよう","m":"content"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The numbers are merely for reference. What matters is the content.","scene":"Discussing a sales report"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-082||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from だけ・しかない","sub":"〜にすぎない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にすぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"It is only ~, nothing more (downplaying, modesty)","slug":"compare","definition":"All can relate to 'only,' but their nuance differs: **にすぎない** downplays and sounds objective and formal; **だけだ** is neutral and common in conversation; **しかない** emphasizes scarcity or lack. **にほかならない** is the opposite: 'is precisely' (strongly affirming).","nuance":"For 'there is only 100 yen': 100円にすぎない downplays its value, while 100円しかない complains about the shortage. にすぎない is more formal; しかない often conveys regret.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"偶然","r":"ぐうぜん","m":"coincidence"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is only a coincidence.","scene":"Responding to claims that success came from repeated luck"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜だけだ","point":"だけだ is neutral 'only' and does not downplay. 1000円だけ (only 1,000 yen, stating a fact) vs 1000円にすぎない (a mere 1,000 yen, evaluating it as little)."},{"vs":"〜しかない","point":"しかない conveys LACK and regret. 100円しかない (there is only 100 yen; complaining it is little) vs 100円にすぎない (it is merely 100 yen; downplaying its value without emotion)."},{"vs":"〜にほかならない","point":"The emphasis is opposite: にすぎない = downplaying; にほかならない = strong assertion and elevation. これは偶然にすぎない (only chance) ↔ これは努力の結果にほかならない (is precisely the result of effort)."},{"vs":"〜すぎる","point":"すぎる means 'too ~' (eating too much). にすぎない means 'does not exceed the limit' → 'is only.' Completely different."}]}},"n3-082||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜にすぎない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にすぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"It is only ~, nothing more (downplaying, modesty)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: (1) using にすぎない to **praise** someone as 'he is only a genius,' which sounds dismissive; (2) **adding です** before にすぎない: '〜ですにすぎない' is wrong; (3) **confusing it with 〜すぎる** ('too much') or using にすぎない for shortage situations that require しかない.","nuance":"Remember: にすぎない **downplays**. Used with praise, '〜にすぎない' can sound like criticism. For a positive achievement, use a pattern such as にほかならない or simply praise it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he; him"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"プロ","r":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is merely an ordinary professional.","scene":"Giving an objective assessment; do not idolize him."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は天才にすぎない → ✅ 彼は天才だ / 彼は天才に違いない / (do not use にすぎない for praise)","❌ これは私の意見ですにすぎない → ✅ これは私の意見にすぎない / にすぎません","❌ 100円にすぎない (when you mean 'there is only 100 yen; not enough') → ✅ 100円しかない — しかない expresses lack.","❌ 食べすぎない (do not eat too much) → にすぎない is completely different: これは冗談にすぎない (it is only a joke)."],"compare":null}},"n3-082||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"〜にすぎない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にすぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"It is only ~, nothing more (downplaying, modesty)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **すぎる** means 'too much.' Add the negative **ない**: にすぎない = **'does not exceed'** → 'it is only that.' Picture a ruler whose endpoint is fixed at X and cannot go higher, implying 'nothing more.'","nuance":"Also remember that にすぎない often combines with **あくまで** for emphasis: あくまで参考にすぎない = 'it is merely for reference.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-082||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜にすぎない · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜にすぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"It is only ~, nothing more (downplaying, modesty)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases include **ほんの〜にすぎない** (only a small ~), **単なる〜にすぎない** (merely ~), and **〜にすぎない** combined with あくまで/ただ/ほんの to increase the sense of modesty.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, common forms include **あくまで〜にすぎない**, **〜の一環にすぎない** (only one part), and **〜の結果にすぎない** (only the result of ~).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それは","r":"それは","m":"that is"},{"t":"単なる","r":"たんなる","m":"mere"},{"t":"噂","r":"うわさ","m":"rumor"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is merely a rumor.","scene":"Reassuring someone about a false rumor"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これは","r":"これは","m":"this is"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"一部","r":"いちぶ","m":"part"},{"t":"にすぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is only one part of the plan.","scene":"Explaining the project to a manager"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-083||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜はともかく（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はともかく（として）","patternReading":"はともかくとして","coreMeaning":"Set ~ aside and focus on what matters more","slug":"core","definition":"〜はともかく（として） temporarily sets issue X aside and shifts attention to more important Y. It means: “Without discussing X for now, the main point is Y.” This pattern does not deny X; it simply leaves X outside the scope of the current discussion.","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese, including workplace and everyday conversation. Its gentle, tactful tone acknowledges what the other person said while shifting to the main point. The “set aside” nuance is slightly clearer than 〜はさておき, while はさておき is more formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"デザイン","m":"design"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Set the price aside—the design is beautiful.","scene":"Reviewing a product: it is expensive, but the design is worth it."},{"tokens":[{"t":"できる","m":"be able"},{"t":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"やって","m":"try doing"},{"t":"みよう","m":"let's try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whether we can do it or not, set that aside and give it a try.","scene":"Friends are unsure they can do it, so they decide to try first."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-083||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & connecting forms","sub":"〜はともかく（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はともかく（として）","patternReading":"はともかくとして","coreMeaning":"Set ~ aside and focus on what matters more","slug":"forms","definition":"Follows a **noun** or an **indirect question** (〜か / 〜かどうか). **N + はともかく** is most common; **N + はともかくとして** is a more formal variant, with “として” softening the transition. Do not attach it directly to a verb or adjective in dictionary form.","nuance":"Adding として makes the sentence slightly more buffered and polite, often appearing in presentations and emails. はともかく is neither overly formal nor casual, so it works comfortably with friends and coworkers.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"はともかく","note":"Most common form"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"はともかくとして","note":"More formal and softer"},{"lhs":"V普通形 + か / かどうか","rhs":"はともかく","note":"Indirect-question form"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"費用","r":"ひよう","m":"cost"},{"t":"はともかくとして","r":"はともかくとして","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まず","m":"first"},{"t":"アイデア","m":"idea"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"考え","r":"かんがえ","m":"think about"},{"t":"よう","m":"let's"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's set the cost aside and think about the idea first.","scene":"Setting cost aside in a meeting to discuss the idea first."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"participate"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whether he is going or not, set that aside—I am going.","scene":"Asked whether he will go; the speaker says they will go anyway."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-083||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜はともかく（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はともかく（として）","patternReading":"はともかくとして","coreMeaning":"Set ~ aside and focus on what matters more","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to set aside an issue—often one that is complex, difficult, or unnecessary to discuss now—and move to the main issue. It often concedes one point while focusing on something more important, or changes the topic tactfully without rejecting the other person.","nuance":"It has a polite, tactful tone and fits Japanese communication well: it does not reject another person's point, but puts it aside temporarily. It can be used with coworkers, customers, in emails, and in presentations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"完璧","r":"かんぺき","m":"perfect"},{"t":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まず","m":"first"},{"t":"始めて","r":"はじめて","m":"start"},{"t":"みよう","m":"let's try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without worrying about whether it is perfect, let's start first.","scene":"Starting a risky project despite unresolved concerns."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お世辞","r":"おせじ","m":"polite praise"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"率直","r":"そっちょく","m":"frank"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"say"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Set the polite praise aside and tell me frankly.","scene":"A boss asks an employee to speak honestly."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-083||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜はともかく（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はともかく（として）","patternReading":"はともかくとして","coreMeaning":"Set ~ aside and focus on what matters more","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch prevents confusion between 〜はともかく and patterns with similar forms or meanings. Key contrast: はともかく = **set aside**, にかかわらず = **regardless**, をはじめ = **use as a representative**, として = **in the capacity of**.","nuance":"The easiest pattern to confuse is 〜にかかわらず, since both may be loosely translated as “regardless of.” In reality, はともかく means setting X aside, while にかかわらず means regardless of X.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"冗談","r":"じょうだん","m":"jokes"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本題","r":"ほんだい","m":"main topic"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入ろう","r":"はいろう","m":"let's get into"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's put the jokes aside and get to the main topic.","scene":"Returning to the main topic after joking around."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜はさておき","point":"Both mean “set aside,” but 〜はさておき is more formal and written; 〜はともかく works comfortably in both speech and writing."},{"vs":"〜にかかわらず","point":"〜にかかわらず = “regardless of,” not “set aside.” 費用はともかく = set the cost aside; 費用にかかわらず = regardless of the cost."},{"vs":"〜をはじめ（として）","point":"〜をはじめ = “with ~ as a representative/starting example” for listing; it does not mean setting something aside."},{"vs":"〜として","point":"〜として = “in the capacity of.” として in はともかくとして only makes the transition more formal and does not have that meaning."}]}},"n3-083||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜はともかく（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はともかく（として）","patternReading":"はともかくとして","coreMeaning":"Set ~ aside and focus on what matters more","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with 〜はともかく and their corrections. The most frequent errors are attaching it directly to a verb and using it to deny X.","nuance":"Because it means “set aside,” do not use it to reject or negate X. If Y is not a more important focus than X, the sentence may sound unclear or unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"質","r":"しつ","m":"quality"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"悪すぎる","r":"わるすぎる","m":"too poor"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Set the price aside; the quality is far too poor.","scene":"Reviewing an item with an acceptable price but terrible quality."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べるはともかく、まずやってみよう。 → ✅ できるかどうかはともかく、まずやってみよう。","❌ 好きはともかく、大嫌いだ。 → ✅ 好きかどうかはともかく、一度話を聞いてほしい。","❌ 値段はともかく、安い。 → ✅ 値段はともかく、質がいい。"],"compare":null}},"n3-083||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜はともかく（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はともかく（として）","patternReading":"はともかくとして","coreMeaning":"Set ~ aside and focus on what matters more","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick way to remember 〜はともかく through an image and a mnemonic. **ともかく** alone means “anyway.” When は follows a noun, picture it as: “As for that, anyway—put it aside for now.”","nuance":"Imagine a meeting table: X is pushed aside to make room for the more important Y in the center. The clearer the image of that pushing motion, the easier the pattern is to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-083||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜はともかく（として） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜はともかく（として）","patternReading":"はともかくとして","coreMeaning":"Set ~ aside and focus on what matters more","slug":"phrases","definition":"Memorize these common phrases with 〜はともかく: **それはともかく** (“anyway, putting that aside”), **〜かどうかはともかく** (“whether or not, that aside”), and **〜はともかくとして** (the more formal variant).","nuance":"These phrases appear frequently on the JLPT and in topic-shifting conversation. 「それはともかく」 is the most natural way to switch topics suddenly in a workplace setting.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"はともかく","r":"はともかく","m":"set aside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"予定","r":"よてい","m":"schedule"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"決めよう","r":"きめよう","m":"let's decide"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Anyway, let's set that aside and decide today's schedule.","scene":"Moving from a side topic to the main plan in a meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-084||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜かな／かしら · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜かな／かしら","patternReading":"かな／かしら","coreMeaning":"I wonder / I wonder if... (self-questioning or soft questions)","slug":"core","definition":"かな/かしら are sentence-final particles used to **wonder to oneself**, express **uncertainty**, or ask someone a **soft question**. The speaker is unsure and thinking aloud. かな is used by all genders; かしら has a feminine, elegant nuance.","nuance":"This is **conversational** and is not used in formal writing. When asking someone, かな/かしら makes the question softer and less pressuring. かしら is less common today and is often associated with elegant middle-aged women or female manga characters.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"晴れる","r":"はれる","m":"be sunny"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder if it will be sunny tomorrow.","scene":"Muttering while looking at the cloudy sky"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"be okay"},{"t":"かしら","r":"かしら","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder if this is okay.","scene":"A female employee reviews her presentation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-084||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"〜かな／かしら · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜かな／かしら","patternReading":"かな／かしら","coreMeaning":"I wonder / I wonder if... (self-questioning or soft questions)","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: **plain form** + かな/かしら. With nouns and ナ-adjectives, **だ is usually omitted** because it sounds more natural: 雨かな (not 雨だかな). With verbs in the ない-form, **Vない + かな** adds a wishful meaning.","nuance":"Do not use です/ます before かな/かしら. 「〜だかな」 exists but sounds masculine or regional and is uncommon. For the JLPT, dropping だ is the safe choice.","structure":[{"lhs":"V / イadj (普通形)","rhs":"＋ かな／かしら","note":"行くかな・高いかしら"},{"lhs":"N / ナadj (stem)","rhs":"＋ かな／かしら (drop だ)","note":"雨かな・静かかしら"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"＋ かな","note":"早く来ないかな — I wish they would come soon"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder if he has arrived already.","scene":"Waiting at a café and checking the clock"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"かしら","r":"かしら","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder if this room is quiet.","scene":"A female student views an apartment"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-084||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜かな／かしら · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜かな／かしら","patternReading":"かな／かしら","coreMeaning":"I wonder / I wonder if... (self-questioning or soft questions)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main meanings: **(1) self-questioning** while thinking; **(2) softly asking someone** for a favor or permission; and **(3) Vない + かな** to express a wish. かしら works similarly but sounds feminine and gentle.","nuance":"With another person, かな turns a question into a **soft request**: 「手伝ってもらえるかな？」 sounds gentler than 「手伝ってくれる？」. かしら feels refined and feminine; men usually avoid it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いる","r":"いる","m":"be doing"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder what he is doing now.","scene":"Sitting alone and thinking about him"},{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","r":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"もらえる","r":"もらえる","m":"can you do for me"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, could you help me a little?","scene":"Hesitantly asking a busy colleague"},{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"soon"},{"t":"夏休み","r":"なつやすみ","m":"summer vacation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"not come"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wish"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wish summer vacation would come soon.","scene":"A student longs for vacation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-084||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with か/だろうか/んでしょ","sub":"〜かな／かしら · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜かな／かしら","patternReading":"かな／かしら","coreMeaning":"I wonder / I wonder if... (self-questioning or soft questions)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps distinguish かな from other question patterns. Each has a different focus: か forms **ordinary questions**, だろうか expresses **formal written reasoning**, っけ is used when **trying to remember**, and んでしょ asks for **confirmation**.","nuance":"かな leans toward talking to oneself, while んでしょ is directed at the listener and expects a response. だろうか sounds sincere but dry and formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"か","r":"か","m":"question marker"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Are you leaving? (direct question)","scene":"Asking directly and expecting a clear answer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder if I should go today.","scene":"Wondering alone without needing an answer"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜か","point":"〜か is a direct question that expects an answer; 〜かな is self-questioning and softens the question."},{"vs":"〜だろうか","point":"〜だろうか is formal written language used for reasoning; 〜かな is conversational and casual."},{"vs":"〜っけ","point":"〜っけ is used to recall something already known; 〜かな is used when unsure and wondering."},{"vs":"〜んでしょ?","point":"〜んでしょ? seeks confirmation from the listener; 〜かな is self-questioning and does not require a response."}]}},"n3-084||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜かな／かしら · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜かな／かしら","patternReading":"かな／かしら","coreMeaning":"I wonder / I wonder if... (self-questioning or soft questions)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Four common mistakes are using かしら as a man, unnecessarily keeping だ, using these forms in formal settings, and using か where かな would sound more natural.","nuance":"かな is conversational—use でしょうか in work emails or formal writing. かしら is no longer strictly gendered, but a man using it with strangers or older people may sound unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"true"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder if this is true.","scene":"A man wonders aloud using かな"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は来るかしら。 (a man speaking) → ✅ 彼は来るかな。 (かな sounds more natural for a man)","❌ 雨だかな。 → ✅ 雨かな。 (drop だ before かな)","❌ 会議が終わるかな。 (in a written report) → ✅ 会議が終わるでしょうか。 (use でしょうか in formal writing)","❌ 手伝ってくれるか？ → ✅ 手伝ってくれるかな。 (for a softer request)"],"compare":null}},"n3-084||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜かな／かしら · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜かな／かしら","patternReading":"かな／かしら","coreMeaning":"I wonder / I wonder if... (self-questioning or soft questions)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine the question mark inside a **thought bubble**. The な sound stretches gently, like a soft mutter. かしら sounds like “ka shira,” giving it a feminine, graceful feel.","nuance":"When you see 〜ないかな, do not translate it as “not...?”; it expresses a **wish** for something to happen. For example, 来ないかな = I wish they would come.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-084||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜かな／かしら · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜かな／かしら","patternReading":"かな／かしら","coreMeaning":"I wonder / I wonder if... (self-questioning or soft questions)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases include どうかな used independently; Vない+かな for a “wish”; 〜てくれないかな for a polite request; and そうかしら as a mildly surprised response.","nuance":"These phrases appear in the JLPT and everyday conversation. そうかしら sounds feminine and polite; men can say そうかな instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"どう","r":"どう","m":"how"},{"t":"かな","r":"かな","m":"I wonder"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wonder how this is.","scene":"Muttering over a difficult problem"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-085||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞）","patternReading":"よね／ね／よ","coreMeaning":"ね: right? · よ: you know · よね: right?","slug":"core","definition":"〜ね・〜よ・〜よね are **sentence-final particles** (終助詞). They do not change the clause's meaning; they add the **speaker's attitude**. ね = sharing and empathy when both people know; よ = announcing something the listener does not know; よね = asserting while seeking confirmation. Roughly: ね means “right?”, よ means “you know,” and よね means “right?”","nuance":"Used often in **spoken language**, messages, and casual conversation. ね and よね feel **friendly and soft**; よ sounds **confident**, and can sometimes seem pushy with superiors. With a superior, use “ですね” or “〜ではないでしょうか” instead of “ですよ” for greater politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"ね","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This film is interesting, right?","scene":"Sharing your reaction while watching a film with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"よ","m":"you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tomorrow is a day off, you know.","scene":"Reminding a classmate about the holiday"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"よね","m":"right?"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Tomorrow is a day off, right?","scene":"Remembering the holiday but still wanting confirmation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-085||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & conjugation","sub":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞）","patternReading":"よね／ね／よ","coreMeaning":"ね: right? · よ: you know · よね: right?","slug":"forms","definition":"All three particles come **at the end of a sentence**, after verbs, adjectives, or nouns in plain or polite form. After a noun or a な-adjective in casual speech, use **だ**: 休み**だ**よ, きれい**だ**ね. The polite equivalents are **ですね / ですよ / ですよね**.","nuance":"With friends, omitting the final particle can sound dry and mechanical. In formal speech, ですね is very common in social conversation: いい天気ですね. Be especially careful with ですよ to a superior, as it can sound pushy.","structure":[{"lhs":"V / Aい（普通形）","rhs":"＋ ね","note":"Sharing and seeking agreement"},{"lhs":"V / Aい（普通形）","rhs":"＋ よ","note":"Announcing something the listener does not know"},{"lhs":"N / なA ＋ だ","rhs":"＋ よね","note":"Assertion + request for confirmation"},{"lhs":"〜です","rhs":"＋ ね／よ／よね","note":"Polite forms: ですね・ですよ・ですよね"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"暑い","r":"あつい","m":"hot"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It's hot today, isn't it?","scene":"Making small talk with a coworker in an elevator"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"よ","m":"you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The exam is tomorrow, you know.","scene":"Reminding a classmate about the exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"よね","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This film is interesting, don't you think?","scene":"Watching a film and hoping your friend agrees"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-085||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞）","patternReading":"よね／ね／よ","coreMeaning":"ね: right? · よ: you know · よね: right?","slug":"usage","definition":"The choice depends on **who knows the information**. ね: both people know it, so the aim is empathy or agreement. よ: only the speaker knows it and needs to transfer it to the listener. よね: the speaker believes it is true but wants confirmation, often with a question mark or rising intonation.","nuance":"ね is also a **social lubricant**: いい天気ですね. After a warning, よ adds force: 危ないよ! Young people often use よね: 難しいよね — stating an opinion while inviting the listener to share it. ねえ is a drawn-out exclamation that expresses more emotion than ね, but is not used with superiors.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"月曜日","r":"げつようび","m":"Monday"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"よね","m":"right?"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Today is Monday, right?","scene":"Waking up groggy and asking your mother"},{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"落とし","r":"おとし","m":"drop"},{"t":"た","m":"did"},{"t":"よ","m":"you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You dropped your wallet!","scene":"Finding a friend's wallet in the hallway and calling out"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"ね","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She's beautiful, isn't she?","scene":"Sharing your reaction to a photo with someone nearby"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-085||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞）","patternReading":"よね／ね／よ","coreMeaning":"ね: right? · よ: you know · よね: right?","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish よね / ね / よ from similar patterns to choose the right nuance. The key points: **よね** = confirmation, **でしょう** = polite guess, **じゃないか** = strong emotion or surprise, **なあ** = private exclamation, **っけ** = trying to recall something forgotten.","nuance":"In polite conversation, “〜ですよね” and “〜でしょう” can be close, but よね shows that the speaker **has grounds and wants confirmation**, while でしょう allows the speaker to be **uncertain**. じゃないか is stronger and can feel pressuring or argumentative in the wrong context.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"よね","m":"right?"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"There is a meeting tomorrow, right?","scene":"Checking the meeting schedule with a coworker before leaving"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"でしょう／でしょ","point":"でしょう is a polite guess, and the speaker is uncertain; よね confirms something the speaker is almost sure about."},{"vs":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","point":"じゃないか emphasizes emotion and may pressure the listener; よね asks for confirmation gently, without forcing them."},{"vs":"〜なあ","point":"なあ is an exclamation to oneself and does not need agreement; ね addresses the listener to seek shared feeling."},{"vs":"〜っけ","point":"っけ is used when trying to recall something forgotten; よね is not forgetfulness, only a request for confirmation."}]}},"n3-085||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes & traps","sub":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞）","patternReading":"よね／ね／よ","coreMeaning":"ね: right? · よ: you know · よね: right?","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are classic learner mistakes: (1) using よ with a superior can sound commanding or pushy; (2) omitting the final particle can make Japanese sound blunt and emotionless; (3) confusing よ and ね in the same situation.","nuance":"Japanese speakers are highly sensitive to **imposing information**. Saying それは違いますよ to a boss is strongly discouraged; use 〜ではないでしょうか or そうですね instead. Reserve よ for friends, family, or relaxed situations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そう","m":"so/right"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That's right, isn't it?","scene":"Agreeing when your boss says, “The weather is nice today.”"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生、それは間違いだよ。 → ✅ 先生、それは間違いではないでしょうか。","❌ 会議は3時だ。（missing a final particle; sounds dry） → ✅ 会議は3時だよ。（announcing it to a friend）","❌ 今日は月曜日だね。（the listener does not know what day it is） → ✅ 今日は月曜日だよ。","❌ そうですよ！（to a superior; sounds pushy） → ✅ そうですね。／そうかもしれませんね。"],"compare":null}},"n3-085||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞）","patternReading":"よね／ね／よ","coreMeaning":"ね: right? · よ: you know · よね: right?","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture it this way: **ね** = two people standing together and looking in the same direction → sharing. **よ** = an arrow shot from me to you → announcing. **よね** = the arrow goes out and comes back → confirmation. **ねえ** = a lengthened sound, like lengthened emotion.","nuance":"Quick review mantra: **ね is “right?”, よ is “you know,” and よね is “right?”** Also remember: do not use よ like a “command” in front of your boss.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-085||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜よね／ね／よ（終助詞）","patternReading":"よね／ね／よ","coreMeaning":"ね: right? · よ: you know · よね: right?","slug":"phrases","definition":"These **fixed phrases** appear frequently in conversation and on exams. **そうですね** is a conversational phrase for agreement or hesitation; **そうですよね** seeks confirmation and is more familiar than **ですね**; **だよね** is used with friends and family.","nuance":"そうですね can also serve as a **filler** when you do not yet know how to answer. そうですよね sounds friendly and natural. だよね is very casual and should be used only with peers or close acquaintances.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そう","m":"so/right"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That's right, isn't it?","scene":"Agreeing when a coworker comments on the pleasant weather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-086||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量）","patternReading":"でしょう／でしょ","coreMeaning":"Probably ~ / Right? (polite guess / confirmation)","slug":"core","definition":"〜でしょう has two main functions: (1) a polite guess—“probably...,” based on indirect information; and (2) confirmation—“right?” or “I told you so!”, when the speaker believes their statement is true. Confirmation usually uses でしょ with rising intonation at the end. A guess shows about 70% certainty.","nuance":"This is the polite form of だろう, used in both speech and writing. For confirmation, でしょ sounds familiar and can sometimes feel slightly pushy (“I told you so!”). With superiors, avoid direct confirmation with でしょ; use 〜ですよね instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"でしょう","r":"でしょう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It will probably rain tomorrow.","scene":"Hearing the evening weather forecast."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"言った","r":"いった","m":"said"},{"t":"通り","r":"とおり","m":"as said"},{"t":"でしょ","r":"でしょ","m":"right"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"I told you so, right!","scene":"Someone ignored your advice, and your prediction came true."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-086||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量）","patternReading":"でしょう／でしょ","coreMeaning":"Probably ~ / Right? (polite guess / confirmation)","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: plain form + でしょう. With nouns and na-adjectives, do not add だ: 学生でしょう, きれいでしょう. でしょう is the polite form of だろう; でしょ is its shortened conversational form. For a higher level of politeness, use でしょうか.","nuance":"In speech, でしょ is common with friends and family. でしょうか is a polite, tentative question used at work to reduce pressure on the listener. Do not use でしょ with superiors in formal situations.","structure":[{"lhs":"V plain form","rhs":"でしょう・でしょ","note":"例: 行くでしょう"},{"lhs":"イA plain form","rhs":"でしょう・でしょ","note":"例: 高いでしょう"},{"lhs":"ナA stem / N","rhs":"でしょう・でしょ (no だ)","note":"例: きれいでしょう、学生でしょう"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"きっと","m":"surely"},{"t":"大丈夫","r":"だいじょうぶ","m":"fine"},{"t":"でしょう","r":"でしょう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tomorrow will surely be fine.","scene":"Reassuring a friend before an exam."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"いい","m":"good/okay"},{"t":"でしょ","r":"でしょ","m":"okay, right"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"This is okay, right?","scene":"Asking a friend about your chosen answer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-086||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量）","patternReading":"でしょう／でしょ","coreMeaning":"Probably ~ / Right? (polite guess / confirmation)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are two main uses: ① a polite guess based on indirect information, such as a weather forecast or situation; ② confirmation, when the speaker believes they are right and wants the listener to agree. It can also express empathy for the other person’s condition.","nuance":"For a guess, intonation falls at the end. For confirmation, it rises; でしょ can then sound familiar or mildly critical (“I told you so!”). Empathy uses でしょう with a gentle, comforting tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"晴れる","r":"はれる","m":"be sunny"},{"t":"でしょう","r":"でしょう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It will probably be sunny tomorrow.","scene":"Watching the weather forecast."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ほら","m":"see"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"やっぱり","m":"as expected"},{"t":"遅れた","r":"おくれた","m":"was late"},{"t":"でしょ","r":"でしょ","m":"right"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"See? You were late, just as I said!","scene":"A friend ignored your warning and was late."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-086||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare","sub":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量）","patternReading":"でしょう／でしょ","coreMeaning":"Probably ~ / Right? (polite guess / confirmation)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare でしょう with commonly confused forms: だろう (casual), かもしれない (about 50% possibility), はず (logical inference, about 80%), じゃない？ (casual confirmation), and ね (shared feeling, not a guess).","nuance":"The key point is that でしょう has two meanings determined by intonation: falling means a guess, rising means confirmation. Replacing it with だろう makes the sentence less polite. ね shares a feeling, while でしょう expresses the speaker’s own judgment.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"きっと","m":"surely"},{"t":"喜ぶ","r":"よろこぶ","m":"be happy"},{"t":"でしょう","r":"でしょう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She will probably be happy too.","scene":"Discussing a friend’s reaction to good news."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"だろう","point":"だろう is CASUAL; でしょう is more POLITE, with the same guessing meaning. 明日は晴れるだろう vs 明日は晴れるでしょう."},{"vs":"かもしれない","point":"かもしれない = 50%, “may”; でしょう = about 70%, “probably”."},{"vs":"はず","point":"はず = clear LOGICAL BASIS; でしょう = a softer prediction based on impression."},{"vs":"じゃない？","point":"じゃない？ = CASUAL confirmation, not formal; でしょう = more polite."},{"vs":"ね","point":"ね = SHARED feeling; でしょう = the speaker’s GUESS. 疲れたね vs 疲れたでしょう."}]}},"n3-086||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量）","patternReading":"でしょう／でしょ","coreMeaning":"Probably ~ / Right? (polite guess / confirmation)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using でしょ with a superior, confusing でしょう with だろう in formal contexts, forgetting that intonation changes the meaning, and using でしょう to seek confirmation about yourself, which can sound conceited.","nuance":"The intonation contrast matters: でしょう↘ is a guess, while でしょ↗ is confirmation. With a boss, replace でしょ with 〜ですよね or 〜でしょうか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"言った","r":"いった","m":"said"},{"t":"でしょ","r":"でしょ","m":"right"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"I told you so, right?","scene":"Reminding someone that you warned them."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生、わかるでしょ？ → ✅ 先生、おわかりですよね","❌ 明日は晴れるだろう (with a client) → ✅ 明日は晴れるでしょう","❌ 私はすごいでしょう？ → ✅ 私、すごいと思いませんか？","❌ 言ったでしょう。 (sounds like a neutral guess, not “I told you so”) → ✅ 言ったでしょ！ (rising でしょ gives the intended casual confirmation)"],"compare":null}},"n3-086||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"でしょ／でしょう（確認・推量）","patternReading":"でしょう／でしょ","coreMeaning":"Probably ~ / Right? (polite guess / confirmation)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: think of でしょう as a TV weather forecast—polite, with falling intonation. でしょ with rising intonation is “I told you so!” in an argument. だろう is the less polite version used among friends.","nuance":"Pair each form with a typical context: weather forecast (でしょう↘), playful argument (でしょ↗), and chatting with friends (だろう). Intonation is the key: the same written form can change meaning with a different tone.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-087||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","patternReading":"じゃないか／じゃないですか","coreMeaning":"Right? / It really is / Oh, it's... (emotion + confirmation)","slug":"core","definition":"〜じゃないか / じゃないですか both confirm something (right?) and express emotion such as surprise, discovery, or mild objection. The speaker already has an opinion and wants the listener to notice or agree.","nuance":"Mostly conversational. じゃないか sounds masculine, casual, and somewhat strong; じゃないですか is polite and neutral; じゃん is youthful slang.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あ","r":"あ","m":"Oh"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん","m":"Mr./Ms."},{"t":"じゃないか","r":"じゃないか","m":"isn't it...?"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Oh, aren't you Mr. Tanaka!","scene":"Meeting someone unexpectedly"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ラーメン","r":"ラーメン","m":"ramen"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おいしい","r":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"じゃないですか","r":"じゃないですか","m":"isn't it...?"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"This ramen is really delicious!","scene":"Trying a new shop and finding it surprisingly good"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-087||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & attachment forms","sub":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","patternReading":"じゃないか／じゃないですか","coreMeaning":"Right? / It really is / Oh, it's... (emotion + confirmation)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches to the **plain form** of verbs, adjectives, and nouns. With nouns and な-adjectives, do not add だ because じゃない already contains the meaning of ではない.","nuance":"じゃないか is casual, masculine, and strong; じゃないですか is polite and natural for all genders; じゃん is very casual and mainly used among friends. ではないか is formal and literary.","structure":[{"lhs":"Plain form / N","rhs":"＋ じゃないか","note":"Casual, masculine, strong"},{"lhs":"Plain form / N","rhs":"＋ じゃないですか","note":"Polite, used by all genders"},{"lhs":"Plain form / N","rhs":"＋ じゃん","note":"Colloquial, youthful"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"come on"},{"t":"行こう","r":"いこう","m":"let’s go"},{"t":"じゃないか","r":"じゃないか","m":"shall we?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s go!","scene":"Inviting a friend to see a movie"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"十分","r":"じゅうぶん","m":"enough"},{"t":"じゃないですか","r":"じゃないですか","m":"isn't it enough?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This should be enough.","scene":"A boss asks whether preparations are finished"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"じゃん","r":"じゃん","m":"though"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Sure, sounds good!","scene":"Agreeing when a friend makes a suggestion"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-087||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","patternReading":"じゃないか／じゃないですか","coreMeaning":"Right? / It really is / Oh, it's... (emotion + confirmation)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main situations: ① sudden discovery, ② confirmation or seeking agreement, and ③ mild criticism. In the workplace, 〜んじゃないですか presents an opinion gently rather than imposing it.","nuance":"Tone depends heavily on intonation and context. With close friends, じゃないか can sound excited; with superiors, use じゃないですか with ん to soften the statement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あ","r":"あ","m":"Oh"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見つかった","r":"みつかった","m":"was found"},{"t":"じゃないか","r":"じゃないか","m":"look"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Oh, we found the wallet!","scene":"Searching for a wallet and spotting it under a chair"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","r":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"earlier"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"tell me"},{"t":"くれれば"},{"t":"よかった"},{"t":"じゃないですか","r":"じゃないですか","m":"you should have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should have told me sooner.","scene":"Mildly reproaching a coworker for reporting late"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"方法","r":"ほうほう","m":"method"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"better"},{"t":"んじゃないですか","r":"んじゃないですか","m":"seems better"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Wouldn’t this method be better?","scene":"Gently offering feedback in a team meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-087||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with でしょう／よ／ね","sub":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","patternReading":"じゃないか／じゃないですか","coreMeaning":"Right? / It really is / Oh, it's... (emotion + confirmation)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this pattern with easily confused forms: でしょう (soft confirmation), よ (announcement), ね (empathy), and ではないか (formal style). Each has its own core function.","nuance":"じゃないか is much stronger and more emotional; compared with ではないか, it is more casual.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"十分","r":"じゅうぶん","m":"enough"},{"t":"でしょう","r":"でしょう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This should be enough, right?","scene":"Politely confirming that preparations are complete with a customer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"十分","r":"じゅうぶん","m":"enough"},{"t":"じゃないか","r":"じゃないか","m":"isn't it?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is plenty!","scene":"Brushing aside a friend’s unnecessary worry"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜でしょう","point":"でしょう confirms gently and politely; じゃないか emphasizes surprise or mild criticism."},{"vs":"〜よ","point":"よ simply announces information without seeking agreement; じゃないか expects a response."},{"vs":"〜ね","point":"ね seeks gentle empathy; じゃないか sounds more like discovery or strong objection."},{"vs":"〜ではないか","point":"ではないか is formal and used in writing or speeches; じゃないか is casual and conversational."}]}},"n3-087||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","patternReading":"じゃないか／じゃないですか","coreMeaning":"Right? / It really is / Oh, it's... (emotion + confirmation)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using the wrong level of politeness, misusing intonation, attaching the pattern incorrectly to nouns, or using a masculine style in an unsuitable context.","nuance":"Rising ↗ intonation asks a genuine question; falling ↘ intonation makes an assertion or exclamation. Use the right tone to avoid misunderstanding.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"遅い","r":"おそい","m":"late"},{"t":"じゃないか","r":"じゃないか","m":"it’s already"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s late already—hurry up.","scene":"Checking the time and urging the group along"}],"pitfalls":["❌ すごいじゃないか先生！ → ✅ すごいじゃないですか！","❌ これ、おいしいじゃない？ (a genuine question) → ✅ A falling tone means 'It's delicious!'","❌ 学生だじゃないか → ✅ 学生じゃないか (no だ after a noun)","❌ 女性が上司に 'やるじゃないか' → ✅ やりましたね／やるじゃないですか"],"compare":null}},"n3-087||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","patternReading":"じゃないか／じゃないですか","coreMeaning":"Right? / It really is / Oh, it's... (emotion + confirmation)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: じゃないか means “isn’t it...?”, directing the statement toward the listener for a response. Imagine pointing and saying, “Isn’t this ~?”","nuance":"Intonation is the key: raise your voice to ask, lower it to exclaim. An accompanying surprised expression makes it sound natural.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-087||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜じゃないか／じゃないですか","patternReading":"じゃないか／じゃないですか","coreMeaning":"Right? / It really is / Oh, it's... (emotion + confirmation)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common forms in tests and daily life include んじゃないか (suggestion), たじゃないか (mild reproach), じゃない (shortened question), and じゃん (colloquial speech).","nuance":"〜んじゃないか is gentle like a suggestion; 〜たじゃないか reproaches or reminds; 〜じゃない？ is very casual and close to じゃん.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","r":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"すれば"},{"t":"上手に","r":"じょうずに","m":"skillfully"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"んじゃない","r":"んじゃない","m":"might become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you practice more, you’ll get better.","scene":"Encouraging a younger student"},{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"じゃないか","r":"じゃないか","m":"you did"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"You promised!","scene":"Reminding a friend about a promise to go out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-088||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調）","patternReading":"んです／んだ","coreMeaning":"The thing is... / Actually... (explaining, emphasizing)","slug":"core","definition":"〜んだ／んです is the short form of のだ／のです. It expresses an explanation of a reason, emphasizes the speaker's feelings or viewpoint, or asks for an explanation. It does more than state a neutral fact: it creates a pragmatic bridge, implying, 'I am saying this to explain or clarify the context.'","nuance":"The casual spoken form is んだ, the polite spoken form is んです, and the written form is のです. It is used with friends, family, and coworkers. The tone is stronger than a simple statement, carrying emotion and often implying 'therefore...'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れた","r":"おくれた","m":"was delayed"},{"t":"んです","m":"the thing is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The thing is, the train was delayed.","scene":"Apologizing for arriving late"},{"tokens":[{"t":"実は","r":"じつは","m":"actually"},{"t":"ずっと","m":"for a long time"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"したかった","m":"had wanted to study"},{"t":"んです","m":"the thing is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Actually, I've wanted to study Japanese for a long time.","scene":"Sharing a passion with friends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-088||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調）","patternReading":"んです／んだ","coreMeaning":"The thing is... / Actually... (explaining, emphasizing)","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach it to the **plain form** (普通形). With **nouns** and **na-adjectives**, add **な** before んです. Past and negative forms also use the plain form.","nuance":"んです is polite, んだ is casual, and のです is written or formal. In conversation, んですか is used to ask for a reason.","structure":[{"lhs":"動詞辞書形・ない形・た形","rhs":"+ んです"},{"lhs":"い形容詞","rhs":"+ んです"},{"lhs":"な形容詞・名詞","rhs":"+ なんです"},{"lhs":"な形容詞・名詞 (quá khứ)","rhs":"+ だったんです"},{"lhs":"〜んですか","rhs":"ask for an explanation"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"んです","m":"the thing is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The thing is, I'm a student.","scene":"Explaining why you don't work part-time"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"んです","m":"I am going"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm going tomorrow.","scene":"Telling family about your plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-088||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調）","patternReading":"んです／んだ","coreMeaning":"The thing is... / Actually... (explaining, emphasizing)","slug":"usage","definition":"In conversation, んです appears when the speaker wants to **explain a reason** (often with an apology), **emphasize** a condition or feeling, or **ask for an explanation**. In addition, んですが is a way to **begin a polite request**.","nuance":"Do not use it for a purely neutral announcement. If you answer 'Why are you late?' with an ordinary statement, it may sound blunt.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行けない","r":"いけない","m":"can't go"},{"t":"んです","m":"the thing is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm afraid I can't go today.","scene":"Turning down a friend's invitation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"泣いて","r":"ないて","m":"crying"},{"t":"いる","m":"be"},{"t":"んですか","m":"are you?"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Are you crying?","scene":"Noticing a friend seems sad"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-088||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish: です・から","sub":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調）","patternReading":"んです／んだ","coreMeaning":"The thing is... / Actually... (explaining, emphasizing)","slug":"compare","definition":"んです differs from です because it **explains and links context**. Unlike から, which simply states a reason, んです can also carry **emotional emphasis**. Unlike わけだ, which gives a conclusion based on logic, んです simply **explains the situation**.","nuance":"Use んです when the listener is wondering or needs context. Do not use it in academic writing; use のです instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"んです","m":"the thing is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Actually, I have a fever.","scene":"Explaining an absence"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜です（普通の文）","point":"です states a neutral fact (熱があります). んです explains the reason, implying 'that's why' (熱があるんです)."},{"vs":"〜から（理由）","point":"から explicitly links cause and effect (頭が痛いから行かない). んです packages the whole reason as a natural sentence (頭が痛いんです)."},{"vs":"〜わけだ","point":"わけだ gives a logical conclusion (彼は学生なわけだ = no wonder). んです simply explains a state; no inference is needed."}]}},"n3-088||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes / traps","sub":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調）","patternReading":"んです／んだ","coreMeaning":"The thing is... / Actually... (explaining, emphasizing)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three common mistakes: **overusing** んです in every sentence, **forgetting な** after nouns and na-adjectives, and using んです for **neutral objective news**.","nuance":"Use it only when explaining or emphasizing. For a simple statement, just use です.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"大丈夫","r":"だいじょうぶ","m":"okay"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"んです","m":"actually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Actually, I'm fine now.","scene":"Reassuring a friend"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 忙しいです。 (when explaining a reason) → ✅ 忙しいんです。","❌ 学生 ん です → ✅ 学生なんです。","❌ あの人は元気んです → ✅ あの人は元気なんです。"],"compare":null}},"n3-088||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調）","patternReading":"んです／んだ","coreMeaning":"The thing is... / Actually... (explaining, emphasizing)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember んです as an opening like '**The truth is...**' or '**Let me explain...**'. The ん (formerly の) creates a sense of background and reason.","nuance":"It does not mean exactly the same as 'because'. It is a pragmatic tool.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-088||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜んだ／んです（説明・強調）","patternReading":"んです／んだ","coreMeaning":"The thing is... / Actually... (explaining, emphasizing)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases appear often in exams and everyday life. **どうしたんですか** is a basic question asking for an explanation. **〜んだけど** opens a request. **実は〜んです** reveals a fact.","nuance":"〜んですよ emphasizes an announcement and is used when sharing surprising information.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"実は","r":"じつは","m":"actually"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お願い","r":"おねがい","m":"favor"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"んですが","m":"the thing is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Actually, I have a favor to ask.","scene":"Opening a polite request"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-089||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語）","patternReading":"とか","coreMeaning":"Colloquial: listing / hearsay / soft suggestions","slug":"core","definition":"〜とか is a versatile **colloquial particle** used for: (1) **incomplete listing** — AとかBとか = A, B, etc.; (2) **examples** — ~とか = such as ~; (3) **vague hearsay** — ~とか言っていた = I heard...; and sometimes (4) **soft suggestions** — 〜とかしない？ = Why don't we...?","nuance":"This pattern is **casual and colloquial**, common in daily conversation and messages between friends, but unsuitable for formal writing. It feels **flexible and noncommittal**: the speaker may avoid making a firm claim or listing everything. Use it sparingly with older people or superiors.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"休日","r":"きゅうじつ","m":"day off"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"遊ぶ","r":"あそぶ","m":"hang out"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On my days off, I watch movies, hang out with friends, and things like that.","scene":"Talking about how you spend your days off"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"辞める","r":"やめる","m":"quit"},{"t":"とか","m":"I hear"},{"t":"言ってた","r":"いってた","m":"said"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I heard he was quitting his job or something.","scene":"Sharing a rumor about someone you know"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-089||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語）","patternReading":"とか","coreMeaning":"Colloquial: listing / hearsay / soft suggestions","slug":"forms","definition":"とか attaches directly to a **noun** or the **plain form (普通形)** of a verb or adjective. When listing several items, repeat とか between them: AとかBとか. For hearsay, it often appears with speech verbs such as **言っていた** (said) or **聞いた** (heard) in colloquial form.","nuance":"There is no special conjugation, and it can refer to the present or past because tense is shown by the following verb. 〜とかって is a very common shortened form in messages, meaning 'I hear...' or 'something like that'.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"とか","note":"list noun examples"},{"lhs":"N1 + とか + N2 + とか","rhs":"〜","note":"list two examples"},{"lhs":"普通形 + とか","rhs":"〜","note":"list clause examples"},{"lhs":"普通形 + とか 言っていた","rhs":"〜","note":"quote imprecisely"},{"lhs":"〜とかって","rhs":"〜","note":"colloquial shortening of とかと"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"紅茶","r":"こうちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どっち","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"prefer"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Coffee or tea— which one would you like?","scene":"Asking a friend to choose a drink"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"girlfriend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いる","m":"have"},{"t":"とか","m":"I hear"},{"t":"言ってた","r":"いってた","m":"said"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I heard Tanaka has a new girlfriend or something.","scene":"Discussing someone you both know"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-089||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語）","patternReading":"とか","coreMeaning":"Colloquial: listing / hearsay / soft suggestions","slug":"usage","definition":"① **Listing examples**: give one or more representative examples, implying that others are left unstated. ② **Vague hearsay**: report information heard from someone else without guaranteeing its accuracy, often with 〜とか言っていた / 〜とかって. ③ **Soft suggestion**: present an action as one possible choice among several.","nuance":"In hearsay, とか gives an 'I heard somewhere' feeling, so the speaker does not take responsibility for the information. In suggestions, とか softens the proposal, similar to 〜でもどう？ In casual speech, young people also use it as a verbal habit (口癖), as in **明日とかどう？** instead of 明日はどうですか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"とか"},{"t":"行かない","r":"いかない","m":"not go"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"How about going shopping or something?","scene":"Inviting a friend out"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"とか","m":"I hear"},{"t":"言ってた","r":"いってた","m":"said"},{"t":"けど"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"true"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"I heard it will rain tomorrow—is that true?","scene":"Checking a weather rumor"},{"tokens":[{"t":"疲れた","r":"つかれた","m":"tired"},{"t":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"好きな","r":"すきな","m":"favorite"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞く","r":"きく","m":"listen to"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I'm tired, I listen to music or do something like that.","scene":"Sharing a way to relax"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-089||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with や・など・そうだ","sub":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語）","patternReading":"とか","coreMeaning":"Colloquial: listing / hearsay / soft suggestions","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜とか with easily confused listing and hearsay patterns. Each has its own profile: や is neutral, など is formal, そうだ reports information from a heard source, and ということだ presents the information as fact.","nuance":"Depending on context, とか contrasts with や/など in register or with そうだ/ということだ in certainty. とか consistently feels informal, relaxed, and incomplete.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"not come"},{"t":"とか","m":"I hear"},{"t":"言ってた","r":"いってた","m":"said"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He said he wasn't coming, or something like that.","scene":"Reporting something uncertain"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜や","point":"や lists items neutrally in ordinary speech and writing; とか is casual and has an easygoing 'etc.' feel. 例: リンゴやミカンを買う (neutral) vs リンゴとかミカンとか買う (casual)."},{"vs":"〜など","point":"など is formal and common in writing and reports; とか is colloquial. 例: 英語などの言語 (written) vs 英語とかの言語 (colloquial)."},{"vs":"〜そうだ（伝聞）","point":"そうだ reports information from a source without necessarily stating certainty; とか presents vague, uncertain information, often with '〜とか言っていた'."},{"vs":"〜ということだ","point":"ということだ is formal and presents reported content as confirmed fact; とか suggests 'I only heard something like that' and avoids responsibility."}]}},"n3-089||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語）","patternReading":"とか","coreMeaning":"Colloquial: listing / hearsay / soft suggestions","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes with とか, mainly involving **register** (using it in contexts that require formality) and **function** (confusing listing with hearsay).","nuance":"In workplaces, meetings, with superiors, and in emails or reports, replace とか with や/など or a more formal pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"お茶","r":"おちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"飲み物","r":"のみもの","m":"drinks"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"たくさん","m":"many"},{"t":"ある","m":"there are"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are lots of drinks, such as coffee, tea, and so on.","scene":"Listing drinks at a party"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 報告書とか書きました。 → ✅ 報告書などを書きました。 (use など in writing)","❌ 会議の資料とか準備しました。 → ✅ 会議の資料などを準備しました。 (formal workplace style)","彼は結婚するとか。 (At the end of a sentence, this is colloquial but may sound like an unfinished list.) → ✅ 彼は結婚するとか言っていましたよ。 (Add 〜とか言っていた to clearly mark hearsay.)","❌ 部長、食事とかどうですか？ → ✅ 部長、食事はいかがですか？ (Avoid とか with superiors)"],"compare":null}},"n3-089||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語）","patternReading":"とか","coreMeaning":"Colloquial: listing / hearsay / soft suggestions","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember とか as **'something like / for example'**. Picture a quick, casual list that does not need to be exact. In hearsay, **'or something'** conveys uncertainty.","nuance":"This memory image helps learners recall instantly that とか is spoken, informal, and incomplete.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-089||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜とか（例示・伝聞・口語）","patternReading":"とか","coreMeaning":"Colloquial: listing / hearsay / soft suggestions","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases with とか include repetition for listing, speech verbs for hearsay, the shortened form とかって, and the suggestion pattern とかどう？.","nuance":"All are colloquial and common in everyday conversation and messages. とかって and とかどう？ are especially casual and best used with peers and friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"とか","m":"for example"},{"t":"どう","m":"how about"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"How about tomorrow or something?","scene":"Inviting a friend without imposing a fixed schedule"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-090||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔）","patternReading":"てしまう ／ ちゃう","coreMeaning":"Already finished (regretfully) / Accidentally did","slug":"core","definition":"〜てしまう expresses an action that was **completed fully** or **done accidentally**, with a feeling that it cannot be undone. If the event was unwanted, it conveys regret; if it was completed intentionally, it conveys decisiveness or relief.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. てしまう/てしまった is neutral and slightly formal. ちゃう/ちゃった is casual, conversational, and lighter. じゃう/じゃった follows verbs ending in で. The accompanying feeling may be regret or emphasis on completion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"落として","r":"おとして","m":"drop"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"oh no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I accidentally dropped my wallet.","scene":"Just noticed the wallet is missing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やって","r":"やって","m":"do"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"finished"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ve finished my homework already.","scene":"Finished the homework and feels relieved"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-090||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔）","patternReading":"てしまう ／ ちゃう","coreMeaning":"Already finished (regretfully) / Accidentally did","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **Vて-form + しまう** (or ちゃう/じゃう). Tense, negation, and politeness are added to しまう. ちゃう/じゃう are shortened casual forms, very common in conversation.","nuance":"てしまいます is polite and suitable for work. ちゃった is casual and familiar. じゃう follows verbs ending in ぶ, む, or ぐ because their て-form ends in んで.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて + しまう","rhs":"finished / accidentally","note":"standard form"},{"lhs":"Vて + しまった","rhs":"finished / accidentally did it","note":"past"},{"lhs":"Vて + しまいます","rhs":"will finish / may accidentally do","note":"polite, future"},{"lhs":"Vて + しまいました","rhs":"finished / accidentally did it","note":"polite past"},{"lhs":"Vて + ちゃう / じゃう","rhs":"casual speech","note":"て→ちゃ, で→じゃ"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べちゃった","r":"たべちゃった","m":"ate it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That cake—I ate it.","scene":"Embarrassed confession about eating a friend’s portion"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう"},{"t":"寝て","r":"ねて","m":"sleep"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"fell asleep"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Did you fall asleep already?","scene":"A late message gets a reply afterward"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-090||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔）","patternReading":"てしまう ／ ちゃう","coreMeaning":"Already finished (regretfully) / Accidentally did","slug":"usage","definition":"Meaning 1: regret over an accidental or unwanted action. Meaning 2: decisive completion, sometimes with relief. Meaning 3: ちゃう/じゃう in casual conversation. Meaning 4: 〜てしまえ means “just do it—stop hesitating.”","nuance":"Context determines the meaning. A bad or unfavorable event suggests regret; a positive or neutral event suggests completion. ちゃう/じゃう is common among young speakers. てしまえ is rough and should be used with close friends or as self-talk.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"メール","m":"email"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"消して","r":"けして","m":"delete"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"deleted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I accidentally deleted the important email.","scene":"Just deleted the boss’s email by mistake"},{"tokens":[{"t":"夜中","r":"よなか","m":"midnight"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"all"},{"t":"見て","r":"みて","m":"watch"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"finished watching"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I watched the whole movie at midnight.","scene":"Stayed up engrossed in a movie"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あ"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"be late"},{"t":"しちゃう","r":"しちゃう","m":"will be late"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Oh no, I’m going to be late!","scene":"Looks at the clock and sees they’re running late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-090||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar forms","sub":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔）","patternReading":"てしまう ／ ちゃう","coreMeaning":"Already finished (regretfully) / Accidentally did","slug":"compare","definition":"The forms often confused with てしまう all involve states or completion. Use this quick comparison to choose the right pattern.","nuance":"てしまう always carries the speaker’s subjective feeling—regret or decisiveness—while てある, ておく, and 終わる are more objective descriptions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"ate it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ate it.","scene":"Someone asks where the cake went"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てみる","point":"てしまう = finish/accidentally do; てみる = try. 食べてしまった (ate it all/accidentally ate) ≠ 食べてみた (tried eating it)"},{"vs":"〜てある","point":"てある = a prepared state that remains; てしまう = an action completed. 書いてある (written and left there) ≠ 書いてしまった (finished writing/wrote by mistake)"},{"vs":"〜ておく","point":"ておく = do in advance for the future; てしまう = complete an action without emphasizing preparation. 予約しておく (book in advance) ≠ 予約してしまった (already booked it)"},{"vs":"〜終わる","point":"終わる = neutral completion; てしまう = completion with regret or decisiveness. 読み終わった (finished reading) ≠ 読んでしまった (finished reading/ended up reading)"}]}},"n3-090||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔）","patternReading":"てしまう ／ ちゃう","coreMeaning":"Already finished (regretfully) / Accidentally did","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include using ちゃう/じゃう in formal writing, confusing ちゃう with ては, or using てしまう for a simple intentional future plan.","nuance":"ちゃった sounds casual and does not fit formal emails or reports. ちゃう can also be a shortened form of ては, which expresses a condition or prohibition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忘れ","r":"わすれ","m":"forget"},{"t":"ちゃった","r":"ちゃった","m":"forgot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot.","scene":"Texting a friend to admit forgetting something"}],"pitfalls":["❌ Using ちゃった in a report: 書いちゃった → ✅ 書き終えました / 書いてしまいました","❌ Confusing ちゃう (てしまう) with ちゃう (ては): 食べちゃう (will eat/may eat) vs 食べちゃダメ (must not eat = 食べてはダメ)","❌ Using てしまう for an intentional future action: 明日、買ってしまう → sounds like “I’ll accidentally buy it tomorrow” → ✅ 明日、買うつもり / 買います"],"compare":null}},"n3-090||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔）","patternReading":"てしまう ／ ちゃう","coreMeaning":"Already finished (regretfully) / Accidentally did","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine a box: the action has been “put into the box,” finished, and the lid is closed. It cannot be taken back out. That is the feeling of てしまう.","nuance":"If putting it in the box was accidental, the nuance is regret. If it was intentional, the nuance is completion. One image, two feelings.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-090||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔） · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てしまう／ちゃう（完了・後悔）","patternReading":"てしまう ／ ちゃう","coreMeaning":"Already finished (regretfully) / Accidentally did","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases are extremely common in tests and everyday life. Memorizing them helps you use the pattern naturally and accurately.","nuance":"Phrases with ちゃう/ちゃった often appear in daily conversation, while てしまった/てしまいます are used in more formal speech or narration.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"秘密","r":"ひみつ","m":"secret"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"話し","r":"はなし","m":"tell"},{"t":"ちゃった","r":"ちゃった","m":"let slip"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Did you let the secret slip?","scene":"A friend innocently asks after information was revealed"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-091||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜なあ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜なあ","patternReading":"なあ","coreMeaning":"Right? / How... (exclamation, wish, self-talk)","slug":"core","definition":"〜なあ is a sentence-ending particle expressing an exclamation, wish, or thought to oneself. The speaker lets feelings spill out and does not need a response. It is close to “right?” or “How...” in English.","nuance":"Colloquial and casual, common in everyday conversation or inner monologue. Avoid it with superiors or in formal writing. Unlike ね, which seeks agreement, なあ focuses more on personal feelings.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"楽しかった","r":"たのしかった","m":"was fun"},{"t":"なあ","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Today was really fun.","scene":"Thinking back on a fun day while walking home from work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"かわいい","m":"cute"},{"t":"なあ","m":"right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person is cute.","scene":"Muttering after spotting someone adorable."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-091||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜なあ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜なあ","patternReading":"なあ","coreMeaning":"Right? / How... (exclamation, wish, self-talk)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜なあ attaches to the end of a plain-form sentence (普通形): a verb, adjective, noun, or **Vたい** form, to express feelings. With Aな and nouns, add **だ** before なあ: 「きれいだなあ」「学生だなあ」.","nuance":"This structure is very casual and everyday. なあ creates a drawn-out sound that shows strong feeling. Do not add だ after い-adjectives.","structure":[{"lhs":"動詞辞書形/ない形/た形","rhs":"なあ"},{"lhs":"い形容詞","rhs":"なあ"},{"lhs":"な形容詞 / 名詞","rhs":"だ + なあ"},{"lhs":"Vたい","rhs":"なあ"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"美味しい","r":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"なあ","m":"so"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is so delicious.","scene":"Blurting out praise after tasting a delicious cake."},{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"なあ","m":"really"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Being a student feels nostalgic.","scene":"Looking at old school photos and feeling nostalgic."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-091||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜なあ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜なあ","patternReading":"なあ","coreMeaning":"Right? / How... (exclamation, wish, self-talk)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜なあ is mainly used in three situations: **(1)** exclaiming at something beautiful or good; **(2)** muttering or sighing when things are difficult; **(3)** wishing for something that has not happened. All are subjective and emotional.","nuance":"It does not seek a response. You can say it alone even in a crowd. **Vたいなあ** especially expresses a wish, while **〜たらなあ** describes an unreal condition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"きれいだ","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"なあ","m":"so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"景色","r":"けしき","m":"view"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This view is so beautiful.","scene":"Admiring the sunset."},{"tokens":[{"t":"困った","r":"こまった","m":"what a problem"},{"t":"なあ","m":"really"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a problem.","scene":"Standing outside after forgetting the phone."},{"tokens":[{"t":"休みたい","r":"やすみたい","m":"want to rest"},{"t":"なあ","m":"if only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wish I could take a break.","scene":"Sighing during a tiring workday."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-091||compare":{"title":"⚖️ なあ vs other particles","sub":"〜なあ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜なあ","patternReading":"なあ","coreMeaning":"Right? / How... (exclamation, wish, self-talk)","slug":"compare","definition":"The sentence-final particles なあ, ね, よなあ, and な (prohibition) are easy to confuse. The key distinction is: **なあ = inner monologue**, **ね = seeks agreement**, **よなあ = assertion + exclamation**, and **short な after V-る = prohibition**.","nuance":"なあ is used when thinking alone or exclaiming without needing a response. ね is listener-oriented and can be polite. よなあ is more casual, both asserting something and seeking acknowledgment.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"楽しい","r":"たのしい","m":"fun"},{"t":"なあ","m":"really"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is fun. (talking to myself)","scene":"Enjoying a walk alone."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ね","point":"ね shares feelings and invites agreement; なあ is self-talk and needs no reply."},{"vs":"〜よなあ","point":"よなあ asserts and complains to the listener; なあ is entirely personal."},{"vs":"〜な (禁止)","point":"V-辞書形 + な (short) = prohibition; なあ (long) = exclamation or wish."}]}},"n3-091||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜なあ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜なあ","patternReading":"なあ","coreMeaning":"Right? / How... (exclamation, wish, self-talk)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The two biggest mistakes are: **(1)** using なあ with superiors, making the sentence too casual and disrespectful; **(2)** confusing short **な** (prohibition) with long **なあ** (exclamation).","nuance":"なあ is very casual and suits friends or self-talk. Avoid it at work, in restaurants, and with customers. In writing, use it only to recreate dialogue.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"帰りたい","r":"かえりたい","m":"want to go home"},{"t":"なあ","m":"if only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wish I could go home early.","scene":"Tired after work, talking to oneself."}],"pitfalls":["❌ To your boss: 疲れたなあ。 → ✅ Say: 疲れました。","❌ In an email: ありがたいなあ。 → ✅ Avoid なあ in formal writing.","❌ Confusing 行くな (prohibition: don’t go) with 行くなあ (exclamation: I have to go again) → ✅ Notice the final sound: short な = prohibition, long なあ = exclamation."],"compare":null}},"n3-091||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜なあ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜なあ","patternReading":"なあ","coreMeaning":"Right? / How... (exclamation, wish, self-talk)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine なあ as a **thought bubble** in a comic: the character sighs “right...” without speaking to anyone. The **long あ** also resembles a relieved exhale or sigh.","nuance":"When you see なあ, think “talking quietly to myself.” Read it aloud to feel the long sound at the end.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-091||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜なあ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜なあ","patternReading":"なあ","coreMeaning":"Right? / How... (exclamation, wish, self-talk)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Some なあ phrases appear frequently in conversation and songs: **〜たいなあ** (wish), **〜だなあ** (exclamation), **困ったなあ** (complaint), and **そうだなあ** (hesitant agreement).","nuance":"These phrases are almost formulaic, so memorize them for natural Japanese. そうだなあ often buys time while deciding, with the drawn-out sound creating thinking time.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そう","m":"that way"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"なあ","m":"I suppose"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let me think.","scene":"Hesitating when asked what to eat tonight."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-092||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜っけ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っけ","patternReading":"っけ","coreMeaning":"Was it...? (trying to recall something forgotten)","slug":"core","definition":"〜っけ is used when the speaker **already knew** some information but has **temporarily forgotten it** and is trying to retrieve it from memory. It can be **self-talk** while searching one’s memory or a **confirmation question** to someone who also knows. The key point is that the information **exists in memory**—unlike かな, which expresses uncertainty about something unknown.","nuance":"〜っけ is **casual spoken language**, used with friends, family, and close coworkers. With a superior or older person, it sounds **rough and impolite**. It also sounds **softer and more endearing** than saying 「忘れた」(I forgot); Japanese speakers often use っけ to ask again without losing face.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"だっけ","r":"だっけ","m":"what was it"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"What was her name again? (I know it, but I forgot.)","scene":"Suddenly forgetting an acquaintance’s name"},{"tokens":[{"t":"鍵","r":"かぎ","m":"key"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"置いた","r":"おいた","m":"put"},{"t":"っけ","r":"っけ","m":"again"},{"t":"…"}],"vi":"Where did I put the key again...? (I forgot.)","scene":"About to leave but unable to find the key"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-092||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜っけ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っけ","patternReading":"っけ","coreMeaning":"Was it...? (trying to recall something forgotten)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜っけ attaches to the **end of a sentence**, and is often used with the **past form** because the information being recalled was known or happened before. With nouns and な-adjectives, **だ must appear** before っけ: Nだ + っけ. The mildly polite form is でした + っけ, but it is still casual and should not be used with superiors.","nuance":"っけ itself does not change form—the verb or adjective before it does. The た-form is most natural because the speaker is asking about **previously known information**. The non-past form can sound slightly unusual, except when asking about a known habit or schedule.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（た形）","rhs":"っけ","note":"Was it...? (most common)"},{"lhs":"N + だ","rhs":"っけ","note":"Was it...? (recalling identity or event)"},{"lhs":"Aな + だ","rhs":"っけ","note":"な-adjective + だ + っけ"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"っけ","note":"い-adjective + っけ"},{"lhs":"でした","rhs":"っけ","note":"Mildly polite, still casual"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"だっけ","r":"だっけ","m":"again"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"What time did yesterday’s meeting start again?","scene":"An employee asks a coworker about the forgotten meeting time"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"もう"},{"t":"見た","r":"みた","m":"watched"},{"t":"っけ","r":"っけ","m":"again"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"That movie—have I seen it already?","scene":"Choosing a movie and suddenly unsure whether it was watched"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-092||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜っけ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っけ","patternReading":"っけ","coreMeaning":"Was it...? (trying to recall something forgotten)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜っけ appears most often in **everyday conversation** when the brain is searching for a memory. Situation ①: self-talk while forgetting—for example, saying 「財布どこだっけ」 while looking for your wallet. Situation ②: asking someone who also knows, creating a sense of familiarity and shared memory. Situation ③: asking about a past event—「前に会ったっけ？」.","nuance":"っけ often combines with **あれ/あれっ** when you suddenly realize you have forgotten: 「あれ、今日って何曜日だっけ？」. Do not use it in formal writing, essays, or with people you are not close to.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あれ"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"どこ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入れた","r":"いれた","m":"put"},{"t":"っけ","r":"っけ","m":"again"},{"t":"…"}],"vi":"Oh, where did I put my wallet...?","scene":"About to go out and unable to feel the wallet in a pocket"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ねえ"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"って"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"だっけ","r":"だっけ","m":"again"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Hey, was he a student?","scene":"Two friends discussing a mutual acquaintance"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"って"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"before"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会った","r":"あった","m":"met"},{"t":"っけ","r":"っけ","m":"again"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Mr. Tanaka—we’ve met before, haven’t we?","scene":"Seeing a familiar face but not recalling where you met"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-092||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from かな・ね・かしら","sub":"〜っけ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っけ","patternReading":"っけ","coreMeaning":"Was it...? (trying to recall something forgotten)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns 「っけ」「かな」「ね」 all occur at the end of a sentence to express uncertainty, but their **mental states differ**: っけ = I **knew it** but forgot; かな = I **don’t know**, so I wonder; ね = I **know it and just want confirmation**. かしら resembles かな but has a more feminine and slightly formal tone.","nuance":"っけ often goes with あれ/あれっ; かな can go with どうかな; ね is used when you are about 90% sure and want the other person to agree.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"って"},{"t":"何曜日","r":"なんようび","m":"what day"},{"t":"だっけ","r":"だっけ","m":"again"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"What day of the week is it today? (I forgot.)","scene":"Waking up and not remembering the day"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜かな","point":"かな = UNCERTAIN, UNKNOWN, and asking whether something is true. っけ = KNOWN but FORGOTTEN. 「今日は晴れるかな」 (I wonder if it will be sunny) → nobody knows yet; 「今日は何曜日だっけ」 (What day is it again?) → I knew it but forgot."},{"vs":"〜ね（確認）","point":"ね = CONFIRMING with the listener, when you are nearly sure. っけ = SEARCHING MEMORY because you forgot. 「会議は3時ですね？」 (The meeting is at 3, right?—please confirm); 「会議は3時だったっけ？」 (Was the meeting at 3?—I forgot)."},{"vs":"〜かしら","point":"かしら = feminine and slightly more polite, often used to wonder about something UNKNOWN. っけ = gender-neutral and always recalls something KNOWN but forgotten.「明日、雨が降るかしら」 (I wonder if it will rain tomorrow); 「明日、雨だったっけ？」 (Was it supposed to rain tomorrow?—I recall hearing that forecast)."}]}},"n3-092||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜っけ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っけ","patternReading":"っけ","coreMeaning":"Was it...? (trying to recall something forgotten)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners commonly make three mistakes: (1) **forgetting だ** before a noun or な-adjective and attaching っけ directly; (2) using っけ with **superiors**, since casual sentence-final particles can be hard to judge; (3) using it in **formal writing**, even though っけ is spoken language.","nuance":"The past form 「学生だったっけ」 is also common when asking about a past state. With superiors, use a polite question such as 「すみません、お名前は何でしたか」—not っけ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"単語","r":"たんご","m":"vocabulary word"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どういう"},{"t":"意味","r":"いみ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"だっけ","r":"だっけ","m":"again"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"What did this word mean again? (I learned it but forgot.)","scene":"Reviewing and encountering a forgotten vocabulary word"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は学生っけ？ → ✅ 彼は学生**だ**っけ？","❌ 先生、お名前は何だっけ？ (disrespectful) → ✅ すみません、お名前は何でしたか。","❌ この本は読むっけ？ (unclear meaning) → ✅ この本は読ん**だ**っけ？ (known but forgotten)"],"compare":null}},"n3-092||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜っけ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っけ","patternReading":"っけ","coreMeaning":"Was it...? (trying to recall something forgotten)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture someone **scratching their head** and saying 「あれ…なんだっけ？」. That is っけ—the feeling of *almost remembering but not quite*. Unlike かな, which is thoughtful uncertainty about something unknown, っけ is when **your brain gives up**: “I know it, but I can’t access it right now!”","nuance":"Extra tip: 「っけ」 sounds like a cue to “key” your memory back in. When あれ/あれっ appears at the start of a sentence, っけ is likely to follow.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-092||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜っけ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜っけ","patternReading":"っけ","coreMeaning":"Was it...? (trying to recall something forgotten)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common っけ phrases include 「何だっけ」「いつだっけ」「どこだっけ」「誰だっけ」「どうだったっけ」—all express trying to recall information. In JLPT questions, the most common forms are 「〜たっけ？」 and 「Nだっけ？」.","nuance":"「どこだっけ」 sounds more natural than 「どこでしたっけ」 in casual conversation. 「なんだっけ」 can be shortened in speech and used either as self-talk or with people nearby.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ごめん"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"君","r":"きみ","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"だっけ","r":"だっけ","m":"again"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Sorry, what was your name again?","scene":"Meeting an acquaintance again and asking after forgetting their name"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-093||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？","patternReading":"てくれない／てもらえる","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (soft request)","slug":"core","definition":"〜てくれない？ and 〜てもらえる？ are both used to **ask someone to do something for you** gently. Both are questions, not commands. くれない？ asks from the viewpoint “will you do it for me?”; もらえる？ asks “can I receive your help?”.","nuance":"Both are **casual spoken forms**. 〜てくれない？ is used with friends, subordinates, or peers and sounds more direct. 〜てもらえる？ is softer because the speaker presents themself as the **recipient of a favor**, so it also works with acquaintances and less-familiar coworkers. Polite forms add ませんか or use いただく to show respect.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"くれない？","m":"for me?"}],"vi":"Could you help me for a moment?","scene":"Asking a close friend for a small favor"},{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらえますか","m":"could you?"}],"vi":"Could you take a photo for me?","scene":"Asking a stranger to take a photo"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-093||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？","patternReading":"てくれない／てもらえる","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (soft request)","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: **Vて + a giving/receiving auxiliary in question form**. Politeness rises from くれない? to いただけませんか. In conversation, choosing the right form shows respect for the listener.","nuance":"Use the **negative question** (くれない？) or the **potential form** (もらえる？) to keep the request from sounding imposing. Adding ませんか makes it more formal. ていただく is the humble form of もらう and is used with superiors or customers.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くれない？","note":"casual, friends"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くれませんか","note":"polite, less-familiar people"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"もらえる？","note":"casual, gentle"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"もらえますか","note":"more polite"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただけませんか","note":"honorific, superiors"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"待って","r":"まって","m":"wait"},{"t":"くれない？","m":"for me?"}],"vi":"Could you wait for me a moment?","scene":"Asking a friend to wait"},{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けて","r":"あけて","m":"open"},{"t":"もらえますか","m":"could you?"}],"vi":"Could you open the window for me?","scene":"Asking a coworker to open the meeting-room window"},{"tokens":[{"t":"署名","r":"しょめい","m":"signature"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please?"}],"vi":"Could you sign this, please?","scene":"Asking a customer to sign for confirmation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-093||usage":{"title":"🎬 Choosing by situation","sub":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？","patternReading":"てくれない／てもらえる","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (soft request)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch shows **which form to choose** in real situations, from casual to formal, so your requests match the relationship.","nuance":"With close friends, use てくれない?; with acquaintances or coworkers, use てもらえる?; with customers or older people, use てもらえますか or ていただけませんか. Question intonation softens the force of the request.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"見て","r":"みて","m":"look at"},{"t":"くれない？","m":"for me?"}],"vi":"Could you take a quick look at this computer?","scene":"Asking a close friend to fix a computer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらえますか","m":"could you?"}],"vi":"Excuse me, could you take a photo for me?","scene":"Asking a stranger to take a souvenir photo"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"書類","r":"しょるい","m":"document"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"目","r":"め","m":"eye"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"通して","r":"とおして","m":"look over"},{"t":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please?"}],"vi":"Could you look over this document, please?","scene":"Asking your boss to check a document"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-093||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing similar forms","sub":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？","patternReading":"てくれない／てもらえる","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (soft request)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch compares 〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ with 〜てください, 〜てほしい, and the statement form てもらう. It clarifies differences in politeness, directness, and request nuance. Choosing the right form makes everyday speech with friends, coworkers, and superiors more natural and polite.","nuance":"てください is a direct request with a slightly commanding tone; てほしい states a wish without asking; てくれない/もらえる makes a softer request and gives the listener room to refuse.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"サイン","m":"sign"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"くれない？","m":"for me?"}],"vi":"Could you sign here?","scene":"Asking a friend to sign for confirmation instead of commanding"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てください","point":"てください: a request/command that does not ask for agreement (e.g., ここに書いてください). てくれない？: asks the listener’s willingness and sounds softer (e.g., ここに書いてくれない？)."},{"vs":"〜てほしい","point":"てほしい: simply states the speaker’s wish (e.g., 手伝ってほしい). てもらえる？: asks whether the listener is willing to help (e.g., 手伝ってもらえる？)."},{"vs":"〜てもらう","point":"てもらう is a statement that describes receiving help; てもらえる? is a potential question inviting the action."}]}},"n3-093||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？","patternReading":"てくれない／てもらえる","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (soft request)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with this pattern, especially choosing the wrong politeness level.","nuance":"Learners may use てください as their default, but てくれない? sounds rude with older people or superiors. Also remember that くれない is not a negative request when it has question intonation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please?"}],"vi":"Could you teach me this, please?","scene":"A student using honorifics to ask a teacher"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 部長、この書類を確認してくれない？ → ✅ 部長、この書類をご確認いただけませんか？","❌ Mistaking 〜てくれない? for 'won't you give it to me?' → ✅ It is a soft request, more polite than 〜てくれる?","❌ 先生、教えてくれない？ → ✅ 先生、教えていただけませんか？"],"compare":null}},"n3-093||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？","patternReading":"てくれない／てもらえる","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (soft request)","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick memory aid based on each verb’s core meaning: くれる = give to me (viewed from the giver), もらえる = I receive (viewed from the recipient), and いただける = I humbly receive from someone above me. The more the speaker lowers themself, the more polite the form becomes. Picture “you reach out to help me” for くれる and “I reach out to receive” for もらえる; with a boss, bow and receive with both hands—いただける. Mantra: “For requests, choose the right level.”","nuance":"Visual associations can make these forms easier to remember. When speaking, ask yourself: Who is the other person, and what is my position relative to them? Then choose the appropriate giving/receiving verb.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n3-093||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？ · N3","v2":{"pattern":"〜てくれない？／てもらえる？","patternReading":"てくれない／てもらえる","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (soft request)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are high-frequency request phrases that appear in JLPT questions and everyday life. Mastering them helps you respond quickly: ちょっと〜てくれない？ (close friends), 〜てもらえますか (common polite form), and 〜ていただけませんか (honorific). Learn each as a whole phrase for natural use.","nuance":"These phrases combine with many verbs, such as 待つ, 手伝う, 説明する, and 写真を撮る. Practice making sentences with familiar verbs to build natural fluency.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"待って","r":"まって","m":"wait"},{"t":"もらえますか","m":"could you?"}],"vi":"Excuse me, could you wait a moment?","scene":"Asking a receptionist to wait"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}}}}